const GALLERY_DATA = [{"id":"000","slug":"000_biologically_inspired_adaptive_learning_in_the_h","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Biologically-inspired adaptive learning in the Hopfield-network based self-optimization model","authors":"Aisha Belhadi¹","institutes":"¹Okinawa Institute of Science and Technology Graduate University","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=WjHYgEfXiV","code_url":"","hook":"The paper surveys adaptive mechanisms in biology — metaplasticity, homeostasis, inhibition, and forgetting — and proposes concrete ways to translate them into the SO model and Hopfield networks via adaptive learning rate, enhanced modularit…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nDeep learning attains high accuracy but relies on massive data, supervised or reward-based rules, and heavy energy, and it suffers catastrophic forgetting — unlike living systems that learn and adapt from sparse data with little energy.\n\n*Also —* Hopfield networks offer unsupervised, associative, one-shot learning, yet basic implementations store only about 0.14N patterns and few extensions draw on biologically-inspired mechanisms.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nLiving systems resolve the tension between integrating new information and staying stable through metaplasticity, homeostasis, inhibition, and resets — the same robustness-versus-flexibility balance that machine learning struggles to achieve.\n\n*Also —* The self-optimization (SO) model, a Hopfield network with Hebbian updates and periodic state resets, already echoes biological attractor dynamics, making it a natural substrate for further biological mechanisms.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper surveys adaptive mechanisms in biology — metaplasticity, homeostasis, inhibition, and forgetting — and proposes concrete ways to translate them into the SO model and Hopfield networks via adaptive learning rate, enhanced modularity, and modified activation rules.\n\n*Also —* It frames biological constraints not as opposites of flexibility but as a scaffold that lets degrees of freedom expand productively, and lays out a research agenda for algorithmically defining and testing these mechanisms.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nRather than adding parameters, the paper scales the network's degrees of freedom by exploiting existing components. Local metaplasticity is introduced by making the learning rate α a function of the connection weight, turning the fixed-rate Hebbian rule into a weight-dependent one; network-level metaplasticity makes α a function of the energy change ΔE, akin to gradient descent on the energy landscape.\n\n**Key idea.** $w_{ij}(t{+}1) = w_{ij}(t) + \\mathrm{d}w_{ij}(t), \\quad \\mathrm{d}w_{ij} = f(w_{ij})\\, s_i s_j, \\quad \\alpha = f(w_{ij})$ and energy-driven metaplasticity $\\alpha = f(\\Delta E)$\n\n*Also —* Homeostatic oscillation is mimicked through alternative (e.g. trigonometric) activation functions, and modular structure with adaptive intra- and inter-module connections and inhibition supports temporal integration, degeneracy, and continuous learning — echoing synaptic tagging and capture.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nThis is a conceptual proposal paper with no experiments or datasets; it grounds its ideas in prior computational work on the self-optimization model, including an SO model of the C. elegans connectome and SO models solving combinatorial satisfiability problems.\n\n*Also —* The theoretical target tasks are associative memory, optimization, and continuous/adaptive learning, where the proposed mechanisms would later be defined algorithmically and tested.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nThe paper's outcome is a coherent design framework — a set of concrete, biologically grounded modifications to the SO model's learning rate, modularity, and activation rule — rather than measured performance; these proposals are stated to still require algorithmic definition and testing.\n\n*Also —* The central conceptual claim is that biological constraints and increased degrees of freedom are complementary: constraints give shape to added flexibility, which would otherwise collapse into uninformative noise.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nNo ablation experiments are performed; the paper instead discusses design variants — different choices of the function f for the weight- or energy-dependent learning rate, and different activation functions — as directions to explore for modeling normal versus pathological behavioral modes.\n\n*Also —* It notes that linear versus nonlinear forms of f, and sigmoid, Heaviside, or trigonometric activations, would be expected to yield qualitatively different network dynamics worth systematic study.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nHopfield networks store approximately 0.14N patterns, where N is the number of nodes — the capacity limit that motivates the paper's biologically-inspired extensions.\n\n*Also —* The paper draws on a broad literature (roughly 67 references spanning neuroscience, molecular biology, and machine learning) but reports no experimental metrics of its own.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nMetaplasticity, homeostasis, and inhibition can be folded into Hopfield-network self-optimization by making the learning rate adaptive, enhancing modularity, and modifying activation — a route toward associative-memory models that learn adaptively like living systems.\n\n*Also —* Biological constraints are the scaffold that lets added degrees of freedom become useful flexibility rather than noise, offering a plausible computational path for modeling diverse adaptive systems.","poster_thumb":"posters/000_biologically_inspired_adaptive_learning_in_the_h.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/000_biologically_inspired_adaptive_learning_in_the_h/000_biologically_inspired_adaptive_learning_in_the_h/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/000_biologically_inspired_adaptive_learning_in_the_h/000_biologically_inspired_adaptive_learning_in_the_h/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/000_biologically_inspired_adaptive_learning_in_the_h/000_biologically_inspired_adaptive_learning_in_the_h/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/000_biologically_inspired_adaptive_learning_in_the_h/000_biologically_inspired_adaptive_learning_in_the_h/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/000_biologically_inspired_adaptive_learning_in_the_h/000_biologically_inspired_adaptive_learning_in_the_h/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/000_biologically_inspired_adaptive_learning_in_the_h/000_biologically_inspired_adaptive_learning_in_the_h/reel.html"}},{"id":"001","slug":"001_the_hessian_screening_rule","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"The Hessian Screening Rule","authors":"Johan Larsson¹, Jonas Wallin¹","institutes":"¹Department of Statistics, Lund University","venue":"NeurIPS 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.13026","code_url":"https://github.com/jolars/HessianScreening","hook":"The paper introduces the Hessian Screening Rule, which uses second-order (Hessian) information to produce a more accurate next-step gradient estimate for screening and, from the same Hessian, a much more accurate warm start; it also enables…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nFitting the lasso along a full regularization path is costly because the optimal penalty λ is unknown and must be tuned by cross-validation, requiring repeated refits over high-dimensional data.\n\n*Also —* Screening rules cut cost by discarding predictors before fitting, but existing heuristic and safe rules screen conservatively and degrade sharply when predictors are highly correlated.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nExisting sequential rules such as the strong rule and working-set strategy base their next-step estimate on first-order information, which over-screens under high correlation and forces costly KKT rechecks and re-optimization.\n\n*Also —* The authors observe that both the strong rule and the working-set strategy can be rewritten as estimates of the correlation (negative gradient) at the next path step, exposing a common weakness that second-order information can fix.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces the Hessian Screening Rule, which uses second-order (Hessian) information to produce a more accurate next-step gradient estimate for screening and, from the same Hessian, a much more accurate warm start; it also enables efficient Hessian updates and an approximate-homotopy path.\n\n*Also —* The rule extends beyond the standard lasso to general smooth convex losses such as ℓ1-regularized logistic regression, and ships as an open C++/R implementation.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nFor an interval where the active set is fixed, the lasso solution is linear in λ, so the Hessian yields a second-order estimate of the next-step correlation ĉ_H(λ_{k+1}); computation is restricted to the strong-rule set for efficiency, with a small unit-bound margin added. The same Hessian inverse gives a coefficient warm start that is exact when the active set stays constant.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\hat{c}_H(\\lambda_{k+1}) = c(\\lambda_k) + (\\lambda_{k+1}-\\lambda_k)\\, X^{\\mathsf{T}} X_{A_k}\\,(X_{A_k}^{\\mathsf{T}} X_{A_k})^{-1}\\,\\mathrm{sign}\\,\\hat{\\beta}(\\lambda_k)_{A_k}$ and the Hessian warm start $\\hat{\\beta}(\\lambda_{k+1})_{A_k} := \\hat{\\beta}(\\lambda_k)_{A_k} + (\\lambda_k-\\lambda_{k+1})\\,H_{A_k}^{-1}\\,\\mathrm{sign}\\,\\hat{\\beta}(\\lambda_k)_{A_k}$\n\n*Also —* Efficient low-rank updates maintain the Hessian and its inverse across active-set changes, and approximate homotopy adaptively places the λ grid; a preconditioner keeps the warm start stable.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluation uses simulated Gaussian designs in a low-dimensional setting (n=10000, p=100, s=5, SNR=1) and a high-dimensional setting (n=400, p=40000, s=20, SNR=2), each at correlations ρ ∈ {0, 0.4, 0.8}, plus twelve real data sets.\n\n*Also —* Real data include bcTCGA, e2006-log1p, e2006-tfidf, scheetz, YearPredictionMSD (least-squares) and arcene, colon-cancer, duke-breast-cancer, ijcnn1, madelon, news20, rcv1 (logistic), spanning p up to ~4.3 million.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nThe Hessian screening rule is the fastest in every simulated setting, with the largest margin in the high-correlation low-dimensional case, and wins on nearly all real data sets, in all but one least-squares case taking under half the runner-up's time.\n\n*Also —* On YearPredictionMSD it fits the path in 78.8 s versus 541 s for the working+ runner-up, and on e2006-tfidf in 14.3 s versus 143 s, roughly a 7× to 10× speedup.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nIsolating the warm start, Hessian warm starts sharply cut coordinate-descent passes versus standard warm starts, often needing only a single pass per step because the warm start is near-exact when the active set is stable (Figure 2).\n\n*Also —* The screening component alone keeps the number of screened predictors close to the true active-set minimum (Figure 1), whereas Celer, Blitz, Strong, EDPP, Gap Safe, and Sasvi screen orders of magnitude more, especially as correlation rises.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- YearPredictionMSD (least-squares): 78.8 s vs 541 s for working+ (≈6.9× faster)\n\n*Also —* - bcTCGA: 3.00 s vs 7.67 s (working+); madelon (logistic): 48.2 s vs 232 s\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nSecond-order Hessian information delivers both tighter screening and near-exact warm starts, making the Hessian Screening Rule the fastest way to fit lasso and ℓ1-logistic regularization paths, especially under high correlation.\n\n*Also —* The same Hessian machinery serves double duty (screening and warm starts) and generalizes cleanly to smooth convex losses beyond least-squares.","poster_thumb":"posters/001_the_hessian_screening_rule.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/001_the_hessian_screening_rule/001_the_hessian_screening_rule/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/001_the_hessian_screening_rule/001_the_hessian_screening_rule/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/001_the_hessian_screening_rule/001_the_hessian_screening_rule/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/001_the_hessian_screening_rule/001_the_hessian_screening_rule/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/001_the_hessian_screening_rule/001_the_hessian_screening_rule/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/001_the_hessian_screening_rule/001_the_hessian_screening_rule/reel.html"}},{"id":"002","slug":"002_transformers_parallel_computation_and_logarithmi","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Transformers, parallel computation, and logarithmic depth","authors":"Clayton Sanford¹, Daniel Hsu¹, Matus Telgarsky²","institutes":"¹Department of Computer Science, Columbia University; ²Courant Institute, New York University","venue":"ICML 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.09268","code_url":"https://github.com/chsanford/hop-induction-heads","hook":"(1) A two-way simulation showing logarithmic-depth transformers and constant-round MPC protocols capture exactly the same algorithmic capabilities.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nWhat fundamentally distinguishes transformers from other neural sequence models is unclear. Prior expressivity results are either about impractically large models or show basic algorithmic tasks are impossible at constant depth.\n\n*Also —* Existing fine-grained results fix depth and grow context length, a regime where matching parentheses or evaluating Boolean formulas are provably out of reach, revealing little about what transformers uniquely gain.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nUnlike recurrent models that process inputs serially, transformers let all tokens interact simultaneously through query-key inner products. This paper identifies that parallelism as the key distinguishing property and formalizes it.\n\n*Also —* Massively Parallel Computation (MPC), the theoretical model behind MapReduce-style distributed systems, is the natural formal language for describing what a small number of self-attention layers can compute.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\n(1) A two-way simulation showing logarithmic-depth transformers and constant-round MPC protocols capture exactly the same algorithmic capabilities. (2) The k-hop induction heads task, on which log-depth transformers succeed but recurrent, state-space, and sub-quadratic-attention models provably fail, confirmed empirically.\n\n*Also —* The MPC connection immediately yields log-depth transformers for graph problems such as connectivity, plus conditional near-optimality via a well-known MPC lower-bound conjecture.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nThe core technical step (Lemma 3.2) builds a single self-attention unit that implements MPC's simultaneous message routing, using multiple hashing and sparse propagation to keep query/key/value matrices tall-and-skinny. Stacking one such layer per round simulates an R-round protocol in depth R+1. For k-hop induction heads, a specialized construction gives depth L = ⌊log₂ k⌋ + 2 at constant width.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\mathrm{hop}_k(X)_i = X_{\\mathrm{find}^k_X(i)}$ $L = \\lfloor \\log_2 k \\rfloor + 2$ $\\text{$R$-round MPC} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{transformer of depth } L = R+1$\n\n*Also —* The reverse direction (Theorem 3.4) encodes each transformer layer as one MPC round, so depth-L transformers with mH = O(N) are no more powerful than O(L)-round protocols, yielding conditional lower bounds.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nThe k-hop induction heads task: a synthetic sequential benchmark generalizing standard induction heads by chaining k bigram completions. Evaluated with context length N = 100, alphabet size |Σ| = 4, and hop counts k ∈ {0, …, 16}.\n\n*Also —* It is a hard special case of pointer-chasing and related to the LEGO reasoning task; its k-fold composition structure is what forces a depth-vs-k trade-off that cleanly separates architectures.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nDepth L = Θ(log k) is both necessary and sufficient for transformers to solve k-hop induction heads. Trained transformers match this threshold: incrementing depth by one approximately doubles the largest learnable k, with the empirical boundary landing exactly at ⌊log₂ k⌋ + 2.\n\n*Also —* A six-layer transformer performs well on all k ≤ 16, five layers on k ≤ 8, four layers on k ≤ 4, and so on, an exponential growth of solvable k with depth that mirrors the constructive theory.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nVarying depth is the central ablation: depths {2, 3, 4, 5, 6} sweep the log-threshold, confirming the ⌊log₂ k⌋ + 2 boundary. Widening embedding dimension and heads (from m=128,H=4 to m=256,H=8) leaves the logarithmic depth dependence intact.\n\n*Also —* Finite-sample training (1000–3000 examples) shows deeper models generalize better, evidence of a favorable inductive bias; interpretability probes reveal attention heads computing the intermediate findⱼ pointers used in the proof.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Depth to solve k-hop induction heads: **L = ⌊log₂ k⌋ + 2** (matched by theory and experiment)\n\n*Also —* Trained models span 500K–5M parameters; MPC simulation uses embedding dimension m = O(n^{4δ} log n) and H = O(log log n) heads.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nParallelism is the defining strength of transformers: they are, up to logarithmic depth, equivalent to Massively Parallel Computation, letting them solve in log k depth what serial architectures need depth k to handle.\n\n*Also —* The k-hop induction heads task offers a crisp, empirically verified separation showing why depth and parallel structure, not raw scale, underlie transformers' algorithmic edge.","poster_thumb":"posters/002_transformers_parallel_computation_and_logarithmi.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/002_transformers_parallel_computation_and_logarithmi/002_transformers_parallel_computation_and_logarithmi/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/002_transformers_parallel_computation_and_logarithmi/002_transformers_parallel_computation_and_logarithmi/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/002_transformers_parallel_computation_and_logarithmi/002_transformers_parallel_computation_and_logarithmi/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/002_transformers_parallel_computation_and_logarithmi/002_transformers_parallel_computation_and_logarithmi/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/002_transformers_parallel_computation_and_logarithmi/002_transformers_parallel_computation_and_logarithmi/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/002_transformers_parallel_computation_and_logarithmi/002_transformers_parallel_computation_and_logarithmi/reel.html"}},{"id":"003","slug":"003_neural_tangent_kernels_for_axis_aligned_tree_ens","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"A Neural Tangent Kernel Perspective of Infinite Tree Ensembles","authors":"Ryuichi Kanoh¹², Mahito Sugiyama¹²","institutes":"¹National Institute of Informatics; ²The Graduate University for Advanced Studies, SOKENDAI","venue":"ICLR 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.04983","code_url":"","hook":"The paper introduces the Tree Neural Tangent Kernel (TNTK) for infinite soft-tree ensembles, gives its closed form at initialization, proves it stays constant during training (enabling kernel-regression analysis and global convergence), sho…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nSoft tree ensembles are trained by gradient descent and perform strongly on tabular data, yet little theory explains their training dynamics, generalization, or the empirical tricks used to train them.\n\n*Also —* Unlike hard decision trees, soft trees make splits differentiable and update all parameters jointly, but this gradient view has had no kernel-theoretic foundation.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nThe Neural Tangent Kernel has explained training and generalization for many neural architectures in the infinite-width limit, but had never been derived for tree models, leaving soft-tree behavior theoretically opaque.\n\n*Also —* Every architecture induces its own distinct NTK; deriving the tree-specific kernel is the missing piece needed to analyze infinite soft-tree ensembles.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces the Tree Neural Tangent Kernel (TNTK) for infinite soft-tree ensembles, gives its closed form at initialization, proves it stays constant during training (enabling kernel-regression analysis and global convergence), shows oblivious trees induce the same TNTK as ordinary ones, and identifies a degeneracy of the kernel for deep trees.\n\n*Also —* These four results together provide theoretical support for empirical techniques such as parameter sharing (NODE-style oblivious trees), adjusting split hardness, and limiting tree depth.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nThe authors consider the limit of infinitely many perfect binary soft trees (M → ∞) and derive the TNTK, a deterministic kernel with separate contributions from inner splitting nodes and from leaves. The soft split uses a scaled error (sigmoid-like) function whose hardness is controlled by a scaling parameter α, and the kernel is obtained in closed form independent of tree depth's recursion.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\Theta^{(d)}(x_i,x_j) = 2^d\\, d\\, \\Sigma(x_i,x_j)\\,\\big(T(x_i,x_j)\\big)^{d-1}\\dot{T}(x_i,x_j) + \\big(2T(x_i,x_j)\\big)^d$ where $T(x_i,x_j)=\\tfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\arcsin\\!\\frac{\\alpha^2\\Sigma(x_i,x_j)}{\\sqrt{(\\alpha^2\\Sigma(x_i,x_i)+0.5)(\\alpha^2\\Sigma(x_j,x_j)+0.5)}}+\\tfrac14$ and $\\Sigma(x_i,x_j)=x_i^\\top x_j$\n\n*Also —* Because the TNTK is fixed during training, learning dynamics reduce to kernel regression; positive definiteness of the TNTK yields global convergence, and the closed form makes computation independent of tree depth.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nGeneralization is evaluated by kernel regression with the TNTK on 90 real-world datasets, compared against the MLP-induced NTK and the RBF kernel. Convergence and dynamics are also studied on synthetic inputs.\n\n*Also —* For timing, a synthetic dataset of 300 samples with 10 i.i.d. Gaussian features is used; tree depth is tuned from d = 1 to 29 and the split-hardness α is swept from 0.5 to 64.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nThe theory holds empirically: the finite-ensemble kernel converges to the closed-form TNTK as trees increase, training dynamics match the kernel-regression prediction, and deep trees show the predicted degeneracy (accuracy first rises then falls with depth). On the 90 datasets the TNTK beats the MLP-induced NTK on more than 30% of them and is much faster to compute.\n\n*Also —* Harder decision functions (α > 0.5) consistently outperform the near-linear sigmoid case (α = 0.5), matching the theory that hardness makes the kernel nonlinear.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nSweeping the split-hardness α (Table 1) shows the win rate against the MLP-induced NTK rises from 13.6% at α = 0.5 to a peak of 34.9% at α = 32, versus 11.8% for the RBF kernel; sweeping depth reveals the rise-then-fall accuracy pattern predicted by degeneracy.\n\n*Also —* The oblivious-tree equivalence is verified: sharing splitting rules within a depth (NODE-style) yields the same kernel as non-oblivious trees, so this common architectural constraint costs nothing in the infinite limit.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- TNTK beats the MLP-induced NTK on **more than 30%** of the 90 real-world datasets.\n\n*Also —* Win rate rises monotonically from 13.6% (α = 0.5) to the 32–34.9% range for hard splits.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nThe Tree Neural Tangent Kernel gives the first kernel-theoretic account of infinite soft-tree ensembles, explaining their global convergence, why oblivious trees lose nothing, why deep trees degenerate, and why harder splits help.\n\n*Also —* It also offers a depth-free, fast-to-compute kernel that is competitive with, and on many datasets superior to, the MLP-induced NTK.","poster_thumb":"posters/003_neural_tangent_kernels_for_axis_aligned_tree_ens.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/003_neural_tangent_kernels_for_axis_aligned_tree_ens/003_neural_tangent_kernels_for_axis_aligned_tree_ens/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/003_neural_tangent_kernels_for_axis_aligned_tree_ens/003_neural_tangent_kernels_for_axis_aligned_tree_ens/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/003_neural_tangent_kernels_for_axis_aligned_tree_ens/003_neural_tangent_kernels_for_axis_aligned_tree_ens/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/003_neural_tangent_kernels_for_axis_aligned_tree_ens/003_neural_tangent_kernels_for_axis_aligned_tree_ens/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/003_neural_tangent_kernels_for_axis_aligned_tree_ens/003_neural_tangent_kernels_for_axis_aligned_tree_ens/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/003_neural_tangent_kernels_for_axis_aligned_tree_ens/003_neural_tangent_kernels_for_axis_aligned_tree_ens/reel.html"}},{"id":"004","slug":"004_cw_erm_improving_autonomous_driving_planning_wit","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"\"CW-ERM: Improving Autonomous Driving Planning with Closed-loop Weighted Empirical Risk Minimization\"","authors":"Eesha Kumar¹*, Yiming Zhang², Stefano Pini¹, Simon Stent¹, Ana Ferreira², Sergey Zagoruyko¹, Christian S. Perone¹*","institutes":"¹Woven Planet United Kingdom Limited; ²Woven Planet North America, Inc.","venue":"NeurIPS 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.02174","code_url":"https://github.com/wp-research-uk/cw-erm","hook":"The paper proposes CW-ERM, a simple training principle that uses closed-loop simulation of an identification policy to find failure scenes and then upsamples them when training the final policy, debiasing it toward closed-loop performance.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nBehavioral-cloning driving policies are trained open-loop with Empirical Risk Minimization, matching only per-step expert actions, so they perform poorly closed-loop where small errors compound into covariate shift and collisions.\n\n*Also —* Non-differentiable safety metrics like collisions are ignored by the surrogate loss, so open-loop training and closed-loop deployment optimize different objectives.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nExisting closed-loop training remedies, such as backpropagation-through-time in a differentiable simulator, are expensive, need differentiable metrics, and scale poorly; a simple, metric-agnostic alternative is missing.\n\n*Also —* Collecting on-policy data or adding human oracles is costly, and prior debiasing methods like LfF and JTT target classifier group robustness rather than closed-loop driving behavior.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper proposes CW-ERM, a simple training principle that uses closed-loop simulation of an identification policy to find failure scenes and then upsamples them when training the final policy, debiasing it toward closed-loop performance.\n\n*Also —* It evaluates CW-ERM on a challenging real-world urban driving dataset showing significant collision reductions, and draws a formal connection to covariate-shift correction via density-ratio estimation.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nCW-ERM runs in three stages: (1) train an identification policy with ordinary ERM; (2) roll it out in a closed-loop simulator and build an error set of scenes where a cost such as collisions is positive; (3) train the final policy with weighted ERM that upsamples the error-set scenes by a factor w.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\hat{\\pi}_{BC} = \\arg\\min_{\\pi\\in\\Pi}\\; \\mathbb{E}_{s\\sim d_{\\pi^*},\\, a\\sim\\pi^*(s)}[\\ell(s,a,\\pi)]$ ; error set $E_{\\hat{\\pi}_{ERM}} = \\{(s_i,a_i)\\ \\text{s.t.}\\ C(s_i,a_i) > 0\\}$ ; CW-ERM objective $\\hat{\\pi}_{CW\\text{-}ERM} = \\arg\\min_{\\pi\\in\\Pi}\\; \\mathbb{E}_{s\\sim d_{\\pi^*},\\, a\\sim\\pi^*(s)}[\\,w(E_{\\hat{\\pi}_{ERM}}, s)\\,\\ell(s,a,\\pi)\\,]$\n\n*Also —* The identification policy is deliberately stopped early (K epochs) to avoid depleting the error set; in practice scenes are upsampled by a fixed factor rather than reweighted, which is more stable, and any non-differentiable closed-loop metric can be used.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nCW-ERM is evaluated on a proprietary real-world urban driving dataset collected on San Francisco and Palo Alto roads, using 180 hours of training data and 60 hours each for validation and test.\n\n*Also —* Scenes are 11-13 seconds long (up to 30 seconds) and cover diverse urban scenarios including stopping behind lead vehicles, intersections, and dense traffic with pedestrians and cyclists; the simulator and metrics are released via the l5kit repository.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nAgainst the strongest baseline (ERM with perturbation), CW-ERM significantly reduces collisions across metrics, with improvements around 35% on some metrics; front collisions drop from 14 to 9 and side collisions from 55 to 47 when the matching metric is upsampled.\n\n*Also —* Improving one metric often helps related ones (upsampling side collisions also reduces rear collisions), and variance is lower than the baseline in several cases; multi-metric upsampling trades off metrics along a Pareto front.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nSingle-metric upsampling yields the largest gains on the targeted metric, while multi-metric upsampling balances across objectives; combining Front, Side, and Distance metrics helps, but adding rear collisions causes regression due to false positives from non-reactive log-replayed agents.\n\n*Also —* The identification-policy budget matters: K=10 works best for single-metric error sets and K=20 for multi-metric, and there is an upsampling-factor limit (around w=50) beyond which performance stops improving and degrades.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nFront collisions 14 → 9 (~36% reduction) and distance-to-reference failures 35 → 28 (~20% reduction) versus the best baseline; the paper reports up to ~35% improvement on some metrics.\n\n*Also —* Optimal upsampling factor w ≈ 50; 180 hours of training data with 60 hours each for validation and test; final policy trained for 40 epochs.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nSimply using a simulator to find and upsample a policy's own failure scenes, then retraining, meaningfully improves closed-loop driving safety without any differentiable simulator, extra human oracle, or added inference latency.\n\n*Also —* CW-ERM is metric-agnostic and connects reweighting-by-failure to density-ratio covariate-shift correction, pointing to a theoretically grounded, easy-to-adopt direction for imitation-learned planners.","poster_thumb":"posters/004_cw_erm_improving_autonomous_driving_planning_wit.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/004_cw_erm_improving_autonomous_driving_planning_wit/004_cw_erm_improving_autonomous_driving_planning_wit/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/004_cw_erm_improving_autonomous_driving_planning_wit/004_cw_erm_improving_autonomous_driving_planning_wit/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/004_cw_erm_improving_autonomous_driving_planning_wit/004_cw_erm_improving_autonomous_driving_planning_wit/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/004_cw_erm_improving_autonomous_driving_planning_wit/004_cw_erm_improving_autonomous_driving_planning_wit/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/004_cw_erm_improving_autonomous_driving_planning_wit/004_cw_erm_improving_autonomous_driving_planning_wit/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/004_cw_erm_improving_autonomous_driving_planning_wit/004_cw_erm_improving_autonomous_driving_planning_wit/reel.html"}},{"id":"005","slug":"005_identifying_the_context_shift_between_test_bench","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Identifying the Context Shift between Test Benchmarks and Production Data","authors":"Matthew Groh¹","institutes":"¹MIT Media Lab","venue":"","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.01059","code_url":"","hook":"The paper introduces context shift, a term for the semantically meaningful changes in the underlying data-generation process, and identifies three complementary methods for addressing it: human intuition and expert knowledge, dynamic benchm…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nMachine learning models achieve high accuracy on static benchmarks yet break on production data. The mismatch is usually called distribution shift, but that framing is simultaneously over-specified and under-specified.\n\n*Also —* Formal shift definitions (covariate, prior probability, concept shift) precisely compare two samples but say nothing about the data-generating process that caused the mismatch.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nDistribution shift is only a symptom of changes in how data are generated, not part of the generating process itself. The jargon is overlapping and confusing, and the literature around it is exploding.\n\n*Also —* Papers combining \"machine learning\" with covariate shift, concept drift, distribution shift, and dataset shift rose sharply from 2012 to 2021 (Figure 1), yet the terms remain conflated.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces context shift, a term for the semantically meaningful changes in the underlying data-generation process, and identifies three complementary methods for addressing it: human intuition and expert knowledge, dynamic benchmarking, and clarifying a model's limitations.\n\n*Also —* It grounds the framing in three \"model organism\" computer-vision domains, facial expression recognition, deepfake detection, and medical diagnosis, showing how implicit benchmark assumptions cause real-world errors.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nContext shift names the semantically meaningful upstream factors driving distribution shift, driven by three concepts: sample selection bias, adversarial perturbations, and non-stationarity. Three methods address it: (1) human intuition and expert knowledge to surface features on which models systematically fail, (2) dynamic benchmarking that evaluates models continually against a well-specified data-generation process to yield corroborated accuracy, and (3) clarifying a model's limitations via an explicit limitations section.\n\n*Also —* Unlike distribution shift, which can be measured between two datasets, context shift can only be fully addressed by understanding the whole population's data distribution and how it changes over space and time; non-stationarity is a special case of sample selection bias over features and labels that may differ in the future.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nThe paper analyzes three model-organism domains and their benchmarks: facial expression recognition (SFEW, MMI, DISFA, FER2013, FERA, CK+, MultiPie), deepfake detection (the DFDC dataset of 128,154 videos from 960 actors), and medical diagnosis (the Diverse Dermatology Images / DDI and Fitzpatrick 17k skin datasets).\n\n*Also —* It points to context-aware benchmarks as the way forward: BREEDS (subpopulation shift), WILDS (in-the-wild distribution shifts with sub-population labels), and Dynabench (dynamic NLP benchmarking).\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nA single AlexNet facial-expression model scores anywhere from 48.6% (SFEW) to 94.8% (MultiPie) across seven benchmarks, versus a 14.2% random baseline, showing that a single benchmark badly predicts real-world performance. Re-created ImageNet and CIFAR-10 test sets dropped state-of-the-art accuracy 3-15%, and a leading DFDC deepfake detector assigned only 2% and 8% likelihood to real deepfakes of world leaders.\n\n*Also —* In dermatology, evaluation benchmarks contained as little as 2.7% of people with the second-darkest Fitzpatrick skin type and a single person with the darkest, and the DDI dataset exposed systematically higher error rates on dark skin.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- 48.6% to 94.8%: accuracy range of one FER model across 7 benchmarks (random baseline 14.2%)\n\n*Also —* - 2.7%: share of the second-darkest Fitzpatrick skin type in one dermatology evaluation benchmark (1 person for the darkest)\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nReframe robustness around context shift, the semantically meaningful data-generation factors behind distribution shift, and close the benchmark-production gap by combining human intuition, dynamic benchmarking, and honest model limitations.\n\n*Also —* The most effective near-term fix is simply for researchers to clearly communicate the contexts a model was evaluated in and the contexts where its performance is unknown.","poster_thumb":"posters/005_identifying_the_context_shift_between_test_bench.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/005_identifying_the_context_shift_between_test_bench/005_identifying_the_context_shift_between_test_bench/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/005_identifying_the_context_shift_between_test_bench/005_identifying_the_context_shift_between_test_bench/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/005_identifying_the_context_shift_between_test_bench/005_identifying_the_context_shift_between_test_bench/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/005_identifying_the_context_shift_between_test_bench/005_identifying_the_context_shift_between_test_bench/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/005_identifying_the_context_shift_between_test_bench/005_identifying_the_context_shift_between_test_bench/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/005_identifying_the_context_shift_between_test_bench/005_identifying_the_context_shift_between_test_bench/reel.html"}},{"id":"006","slug":"006_neural_unbalanced_optimal_transport_via_cycle_co","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Neural Unbalanced Optimal Transport via Cycle-Consistent Semi-Couplings","authors":"Frederike Lübeck¹³, Charlotte Bunne¹, Gabriele Gut², Jacobo Sarabia del Castillo², Lucas Pelkmans², David Alvarez-Melis³","institutes":"¹ETH Zurich; ²University of Zurich; ³Microsoft Research","venue":"NeurIPS 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.15621","code_url":"https://github.com/FrederikeLuebeck/nubot","hook":"NubOT, a neural unbalanced OT formulation that (i) weaves the semi-coupling theory of unbalanced OT with a scalable neural OT map estimator, (ii) parameterizes it via convex dual potentials (ICNNs) plus learned reweighting functions, and (i…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nPredicting how an unpaired population evolves under an intervention when measurement is destructive; classical optimal transport assumes conserved mass, which fails when population size changes via cell proliferation or death.\n\n*Also —* In single-cell biology the same cell cannot be measured twice, so correspondences across time points must be inferred from unpaired replicas of the population.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nTo predict single-cell drug responses we need nonlinear maps that model both feature shifts and mass changes; prior neural OT methods either assume balance or only crudely rescale mass, missing per-cell proliferation and death.\n\n*Also —* Aggregate or mechanistic models cannot capture the heterogeneous, cell-type-specific responses that drive treatment outcomes.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nNubOT, a neural unbalanced OT formulation that (i) weaves the semi-coupling theory of unbalanced OT with a scalable neural OT map estimator, (ii) parameterizes it via convex dual potentials (ICNNs) plus learned reweighting functions, and (iii) trains it through a novel cycle-consistent alternating scheme that generalizes out-of-sample.\n\n*Also —* The formulation recovers interpretable semi-couplings and predicts per-cell mass changes for previously unseen samples.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nNubOT decomposes unbalanced OT into a mass-rescaling subproblem and a balanced feature-transport subproblem. It seeks reweighting fields η, ζ and forward/backward Monge maps T, S such that the rescaled proxy measures μ̃ = η·μ and ν̃ = ζ·ν are balanced and matched. Maps are learned as gradients of ICNN convex potentials (T = ∇g, S = ∇f); an alternating scheme updates η, ζ via unbalanced-Sinkhorn scaling and updates the potentials by min–max optimization.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\tilde\\mu = \\eta\\cdot\\mu,\\quad \\tilde\\nu = \\zeta\\cdot\\nu,\\quad T_\\sharp\\tilde\\mu = \\tilde\\nu,\\quad S_\\sharp\\tilde\\nu = \\tilde\\mu$ and the ICNN dual objective $\\mathcal{L}(f,g) = \\tfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n} e_i\\big[f(\\nabla g(x_i)) - \\langle x_i, \\nabla g(x_i)\\rangle\\big] - \\sum_{j=1}^{m} z_j\\, f(y_j)$, with new-sample transform $(x,u)\\mapsto(\\nabla g(x),\\ \\eta(x)\\cdot u\\cdot \\zeta(\\nabla g(x))^{-1})$.\n\n*Also —* The reweighting functions are fit as neural networks so mass changes can be predicted for new cells; from the learned components the original semi-couplings (γ₁, γ₂) can be recovered exactly. Cycle-consistency ties the forward and backward maps together.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nA single-cell melanoma drug-response dataset measured with 4i imaging over 25 drug treatments at t = 0h, 8h, 24h, comprising two co-cultured melanoma cell lines identified by mutually exclusive markers (MelA⁺ and Sox9⁺). A synthetic 2D three-cluster Gaussian-mixture benchmark with controlled cluster-proportion imbalance is also used.\n\n*Also —* Control cells (DMSO vehicle) form the source; perturbed populations at 8h and 24h form the targets. Proliferation and apoptosis markers (Ki67, ClCasp3) provide biological validation.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn the single-cell drug-response task NubOT outperforms all baselines (CellOT, the state-of-the-art ubOT GAN, Identity, Observed) in almost all drug perturbations under weighted kernel MMD, while accurately recovering per-subpopulation mass changes that the ubOT GAN only captures as a coarse trend.\n\n*Also —* On synthetic data NubOT maps clusters without transporting mass across non-corresponding clusters and predicts the correct per-cluster weights, whereas ubOT GAN gets locations right but misestimates the reweighting.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nPredicted weights are validated biologically: the summed predicted weights per subpopulation correlate strongly with observed cell-count fractions at 8h (R = 0.95, P = 2.2e-25), with correlation weakening at 24h (R = 0.44, P = 0.004) as severe drug-induced death shrinks observable populations.\n\n*Also —* Predicted weights spatially track the proliferation marker Ki67 (weights ≥ 1) and the apoptosis marker ClCasp3 (weights ≤ 1); e.g. under Ulixertinib, MelA⁺ cells proliferate and Sox9⁺ cells die by 24h, matching NubOT's predictions.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Outperforms all baselines in **almost all** of **25** drug perturbations (weighted kernel MMD).\n\n*Also —* - **2** co-cultured melanoma cell lines (MelA⁺, Sox9⁺); **3** timepoints (0h, 8h, 24h) via 4i imaging.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy recasting unbalanced OT through learnable semi-couplings and training with a cycle-consistent scheme, NubOT jointly predicts where single cells move and how their mass grows or dies, beating prior neural OT methods on drug-response forecasting while staying biologically interpretable.\n\n*Also —* Explicitly modeling proliferation and death at single-cell resolution is a step toward faithfully forecasting heterogeneous perturbation responses.","poster_thumb":"posters/006_neural_unbalanced_optimal_transport_via_cycle_co.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/006_neural_unbalanced_optimal_transport_via_cycle_co/006_neural_unbalanced_optimal_transport_via_cycle_co/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/006_neural_unbalanced_optimal_transport_via_cycle_co/006_neural_unbalanced_optimal_transport_via_cycle_co/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/006_neural_unbalanced_optimal_transport_via_cycle_co/006_neural_unbalanced_optimal_transport_via_cycle_co/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/006_neural_unbalanced_optimal_transport_via_cycle_co/006_neural_unbalanced_optimal_transport_via_cycle_co/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/006_neural_unbalanced_optimal_transport_via_cycle_co/006_neural_unbalanced_optimal_transport_via_cycle_co/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/006_neural_unbalanced_optimal_transport_via_cycle_co/006_neural_unbalanced_optimal_transport_via_cycle_co/reel.html"}},{"id":"007","slug":"007_inner_classifier_free_guidance_and_its_taylor_ex","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Inner Classifier-Free Guidance and Its Taylor Expansion for Diffusion Models","authors":"Shikun Sun¹², Longhui Wei³, Zhicai Wang⁴, Zixuan Wang¹², Junliang Xing¹², Jia Jia¹², Qi Tian³","institutes":"¹Tsinghua University; ²BNRist; ³Huawei Inc.; ⁴University of Science and Technology of China","venue":"ICLR 2024","paper_url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=0QAzIMq32X","code_url":"","hook":"The paper proposes Inner Classifier-Free Guidance (ICFG), showing that CFG is exactly the first-order case of a Taylor expansion of ICFG around guidance strength β = 1, and derives a second-order implementation that adds new information wit…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nClassifier-free guidance (CFG) balances diversity and fidelity in conditional diffusion models, but it imposes no structure on the condition space, so it underutilizes continuity when the condition is continuous.\n\n*Also —* For text-based models like Stable Diffusion, a structured continuous condition space could further improve the fidelity–diversity trade-off, yet CFG cannot exploit it.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nThe two dominant guidance approaches, external classifiers and classifier-free guidance, both ignore the geometry of a continuous condition space, leaving the benefits of continuity unexploited.\n\n*Also —* In Stable Diffusion the text encoder induces a rich continuous space; if that space has a \"cone\" structure, guidance could be extended along it to gain new, valuable information.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper proposes Inner Classifier-Free Guidance (ICFG), showing that CFG is exactly the first-order case of a Taylor expansion of ICFG around guidance strength β = 1, and derives a second-order implementation that adds new information without changing the training policy.\n\n*Also —* It contributes a training policy based on a correlation metric between condition and data, two second-order sampling algorithms (a strict and a practical non-strict version), and a convergence analysis of the Taylor expansion.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nICFG expresses the enhanced intermediate distribution as `$q^\\theta(x_t\\mid c,\\beta) \\propto q^\\theta(x_t)\\left[q^\\theta(x_t\\mid c)/q^\\theta(x_t)\\right]^{\\beta}$` with β = w+1, then Taylor-expands the score predictor εθ(xt|βc) around β = 1. The first-order term reproduces CFG; the second-order term, estimated using a middle point m, injects extra information at sampling time without altering training.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\epsilon^\\theta(x_t\\mid\\beta c)=\\epsilon^\\theta(x_t\\mid c)+\\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{k!}\\frac{\\partial^k \\epsilon^\\theta(x_t\\mid\\beta c)}{\\partial\\beta^k}\\Big|_{\\beta=1}(\\beta-1)^k+R_n(\\beta)$ ; second-order non-strict sampler: $\\epsilon_t=\\epsilon^\\theta(z_i,c)+w\\big(\\epsilon^\\theta(z_i,c)-\\epsilon^\\theta(z_i)\\big)+\\frac{v}{m(1-m)}\\big[(1-m)\\epsilon^\\theta(z_i)+m\\,\\epsilon^\\theta(z_i,c)-\\epsilon^\\theta(z_i,mc)\\big]$\n\n*Also —* A training policy (Algorithm 1) rescales the condition by a correlation metric r(x,c) so the model can assess guidance strength; a strict (Algorithm 2) and a practical non-strict (Algorithm 3) sampler realize second-order ICFG on pretrained Stable Diffusion using only a few extra score evaluations.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nExperiments evaluate text-to-image generation on the MS-COCO validation set, reporting FID for fidelity and CLIP Score for text–image alignment.\n\n*Also —* Guidance is tested on pretrained Stable Diffusion, with an additional class-conditional study on U-ViT and few-shot fine-tuning on Stable Diffusion v1.5.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nSecond-order ICFG improves the FID–CLIP-Score balance over CFG on MS-COCO: at w = 2.0 with v = 0.25 in the full condition space Call, it reaches FID 15.28 and CLIP Score 26.11, beating CFG's FID 15.42 / CLIP 25.80 at the same guidance strength.\n\n*Also —* The optimal balance is found at w = 2.0, v = 0.25; the best absolute FID appears in the noun subspace Cnouns, while Call offers a more favorable \"cone\" structure for balancing the two metrics. On U-ViT, ICFG lowers FID versus CFG across sampling budgets.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nVarying the middle point m (Table 2) yields a \"U\"-shaped FID, with the best FID of 15.42 at m = 1.1; points too close together miss long-term curvature, while points near 0 or 1 are hard for the model to score.\n\n*Also —* Varying sampling steps (Table 3) shows ICFG is already strong at few steps (FID 15.80 at T = 10, improving to 15.28 at T = 50 for Call), and varying w and v maps the fidelity–diversity trade-off across condition spaces Call and Cnouns.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Best balance: **FID 15.28 / CLIP 26.11** at w = 2.0, v = 0.25, C = Call (vs CFG's 15.42 / 25.80).\n\n*Also —* - On U-ViT, ICFG cuts FID from **34.23 → 24.69** at the 5k-step budget and reaches **7.92 vs CFG 8.10** at 80k steps.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nCFG is just the first-order Taylor term of inner classifier-free guidance; adding the second-order term gives Stable Diffusion a better fidelity–diversity balance for free, with no retraining.\n\n*Also —* By choosing a condition space with a well-defined \"cone\" structure, ICFG naturally extends to higher orders, offering a principled path beyond standard guidance.","poster_thumb":"posters/007_inner_classifier_free_guidance_and_its_taylor_ex.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/007_inner_classifier_free_guidance_and_its_taylor_ex/007_inner_classifier_free_guidance_and_its_taylor_ex/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/007_inner_classifier_free_guidance_and_its_taylor_ex/007_inner_classifier_free_guidance_and_its_taylor_ex/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/007_inner_classifier_free_guidance_and_its_taylor_ex/007_inner_classifier_free_guidance_and_its_taylor_ex/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/007_inner_classifier_free_guidance_and_its_taylor_ex/007_inner_classifier_free_guidance_and_its_taylor_ex/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/007_inner_classifier_free_guidance_and_its_taylor_ex/007_inner_classifier_free_guidance_and_its_taylor_ex/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/007_inner_classifier_free_guidance_and_its_taylor_ex/007_inner_classifier_free_guidance_and_its_taylor_ex/reel.html"}},{"id":"008","slug":"008_butterfly_effects_of_sgd_noise_error_amplificati","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Butterfly Effects of SGD Noise: Error Amplification in Behavior Cloning and Autoregression","authors":"Adam Block¹, Dylan J. Foster², Akshay Krishnamurthy², Max Simchowitz¹, Cyril Zhang²","institutes":"¹MIT; ²Microsoft Research NYC","venue":"ICLR 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11428","code_url":"","hook":"(1) Through 278 distinct interventions the paper diagnoses rollout oscillations and names the mechanism gradient variance amplification (GVA) — SGD noise propagating through closed-loop dynamics; (2) it shows GVA is algorithmic not statisti…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nWhen deep policies are behavior-cloned with minibatch SGD, long-horizon rollout reward oscillates violently across iterates even though the single-step behavior-cloning loss stays flat and stable.\n\n*Also —* Because evaluating true task reward at every checkpoint is impractical, these hidden oscillations mean any given trained checkpoint carries high risk of poor deployed performance.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nModels trained on surrogate objectives (next-token prediction, single-step imitation) ignore the feedback loops of the real task, so surrogate loss improving monotonically hides large swings in the metric that actually matters.\n\n*Also —* Prior imitation-learning work attributes the loss-vs-reward gap to error amplification, but the role of optimizer noise as the driver of iterate-to-iterate instability had not been isolated.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\n(1) Through 278 distinct interventions the paper diagnoses rollout oscillations and names the mechanism gradient variance amplification (GVA) — SGD noise propagating through closed-loop dynamics; (2) it shows GVA is algorithmic not statistical, and that exponential moving average (EMA) of iterates fixes it where most mitigations fail; (3) it demonstrates GVA and its EMA cure in both continuous control and autoregressive language generation, plus a convex theoretical vignette.\n\n*Also —* The paper argues EMA and related filters should be treated as first-class \"stabilizers\" in deep learning pipelines, on par with optimizers and schedulers.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nThe authors empirically disentangle statistical from computational (algorithmic) causes of reward oscillation, isolating GVA — the amplification of minibatch SGD gradient variance through unstable closed-loop rollouts. They then test variance-reduction remedies (LR decay, large batches via gradient accumulation, and iterate averaging), finding EMA of the optimization trajectory stabilizes rollouts with essentially no downside, and back this with a convex-optimization theory vignette on a discontinuous \"cliff loss\".\n\n**Key idea.** $\\tilde{\\theta}_\\gamma^{(t+1)} = (1-\\gamma_t)\\,\\tilde{\\theta}_\\gamma^{(t)} + \\gamma_t\\,\\theta^{(t+1)}$ and the error-amplification bound $\\sup_{\\Delta\\in B_\\delta}\\{J_H(\\pi_{\\theta^\\star}) - J_H(\\pi_{\\theta^\\star+\\Delta})\\} \\ge \\Omega(H)\\cdot\\left(e^{\\Omega(H\\delta)}-1\\right)$ while $\\sup_{\\Delta\\in B_\\delta}\\ell_{\\mathrm{BC}}(\\pi_{\\theta^\\star+\\Delta}) \\le O(H\\delta^2)$\n\n*Also —* EMA is recommended with an initial burn-in, a polynomial-decay schedule for the averaging rate, and updates at every iterate; in language modeling EMA further removes the need for full learning-rate decay, reducing overfitting.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nContinuous control uses MuJoCo locomotion tasks (Walker2d-v4 as the primary testbed, plus Hopper, HalfCheetah, Ant, Humanoid) with behavior cloning from SAC experts over horizon H = 1000. Language experiments use 270M-parameter Transformers trained on the TinyStories dataset.\n\n*Also —* Rollout reward is averaged over 20 initial conditions with per-episode rewards shown disaggregated; the BC setting uses N = H = 1000 so overfitting is not the confound.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nRollout reward oscillates dramatically across consecutive gradient iterates while BC loss is flat, revealing a fractal reward landscape invisible to the single-step objective; taking an EMA of iterates drastically damps these oscillations across architectures, dataset sizes, and tasks with no learning-rate or batch-size change.\n\n*Also —* The same \"butterfly effect\" appears in autoregressive language generation — nearby training iterates bifurcate in generated text — and EMA yields the lowest-perplexity model while enabling training without LR decay.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nOscillations persist across dataset sizes, architectures (4-layer MLP and 4-layer Transformer), model scales, and regularizers, and are only strongly mitigated by variance-reduction strategies. EMA ablations show it should update every iterate with a burn-in and a tuned decay; skipping burn-in or updating infrequently degrades stabilization.\n\n*Also —* Aggressive LR decay and large-batch gradient accumulation also reduce GVA but at large compute cost; shallower models are less susceptible. Some tasks (e.g. higher-dimensional Humanoid-v4) show more benign oscillations.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n278 distinct interventions in the continuous-control study; horizon H = 1000 rollouts; error amplification constant is exponential in the horizon.\n\n*Also —* 270M-parameter Transformers on TinyStories; rewards averaged over 20 initial conditions; theory bound grows as `$\\Omega(H)(e^{\\Omega(H\\delta)}-1)$` in reward gap versus `$O(H\\delta^2)$` in BC loss.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nSGD noise is harmless for the one-step training loss but, through feedback loops, gets amplified into chaotic long-horizon failures (GVA); averaging the weights with an EMA is a cheap, broadly effective stabilizer.\n\n*Also —* EMA deserves to be a standard \"stabilizer\" in any pipeline where a model acts inside a feedback loop, from robotic control to language generation.","poster_thumb":"posters/008_butterfly_effects_of_sgd_noise_error_amplificati.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/008_butterfly_effects_of_sgd_noise_error_amplificati/008_butterfly_effects_of_sgd_noise_error_amplificati/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/008_butterfly_effects_of_sgd_noise_error_amplificati/008_butterfly_effects_of_sgd_noise_error_amplificati/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/008_butterfly_effects_of_sgd_noise_error_amplificati/008_butterfly_effects_of_sgd_noise_error_amplificati/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/008_butterfly_effects_of_sgd_noise_error_amplificati/008_butterfly_effects_of_sgd_noise_error_amplificati/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/008_butterfly_effects_of_sgd_noise_error_amplificati/008_butterfly_effects_of_sgd_noise_error_amplificati/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/008_butterfly_effects_of_sgd_noise_error_amplificati/008_butterfly_effects_of_sgd_noise_error_amplificati/reel.html"}},{"id":"009","slug":"009_collective_certified_robustness_against_graph_in","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Collective Certified Robustness against Graph Injection Attacks","authors":"Yuni Lai*¹, Bailin Pan*², Kaihuang Chen², Yancheng Yuan², Kai Zhou¹","institutes":"¹Department of Computing, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University; ²Department of Applied Mathematics, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University","venue":"ICML 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.01423","code_url":"https://github.com/Yuni-Lai/CollectiveLPCert","hook":"The first collective certified robustness scheme for GNNs against graph injection attacks.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nGraph neural networks are vulnerable to graph injection attacks, where an adversary inserts malicious nodes. Existing certificates verify each node independently (sample-wise), yielding hopelessly pessimistic, near-zero certified performance.\n\n*Also —* Sample-wise certification unrealistically assumes the attacker can craft a fresh perturbed graph per target node; real attackers produce one graph to disrupt all targets at once.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nA single perturbed graph must simultaneously fool an entire target set, so certifying nodes jointly should be far less conservative than certifying them one by one.\n\n*Also —* The only prior collective scheme (Schuchardt et al., 2020) assumes a fixed receptive field and does not apply to GIA, which adds edges after injecting nodes and thereby expands the receptive field.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe first collective certified robustness scheme for GNNs against graph injection attacks. It formulates certification as a binary integer quadratic constrained linear program (BQCLP) and introduces a customized linearization that relaxes it to an efficiently solvable linear program.\n\n*Also —* The scheme is almost model-agnostic, applying to any message-passing GNN, and delivers large certified-ratio gains with minimal computational overhead.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nBuilt on node-aware bi-smoothing (edge- and node-deletion randomized smoothing), the method exploits GNN locality: an injected node only harms a target if at least one message-passing path survives smoothing. It upper-bounds the message-interference probability p(E_v), derives a certifying condition, and models the worst-case attacker who maximizes non-robust target nodes under budget ρ (injected nodes) and τ (edges per injected node). This budget-allocation problem is a BQCLP, relaxed to an LP for tractability.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\max_{A_1,A_2,m} M = t^\\top m \\quad \\text{s.t.} \\quad \\tilde{p}_1 A_1^\\top \\mathbf{1}_\\rho + \\tilde{p}_2 (A_1 A_0 + A_2 A_1)^\\top \\mathbf{1}_\\rho \\le C \\circ m,\\; A_1 \\mathbf{1}_n + A_2 \\mathbf{1}_\\rho \\le \\tau$ and the certifying condition $\\overline{p(E_v)} < [p_{v,y^*}(G) - \\max_{y \\ne y^*} p_{v,y}(G)]/2$\n\n*Also —* Two relaxations are proposed: Collective-LP1 uses a standard quadratic-term linearization (O(ρ²|T|) extra variables); Collective-LP2 is a customized reformulation that combines the quadratic term first, needing only O(ρ|T|) extra variables, improving both solution quality and speed. The LP always has a larger feasible region than the BQCLP, so its certified ratio is a sound lower bound.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on two standard citation-graph node-classification benchmarks: Cora-ML (2,810 nodes, 7,981 edges, 7 classes) and Citeseer (2,110 nodes, 3,668 edges, 6 classes), with GCN and GAT (hidden size 64) as base classifiers.\n\n*Also —* Injected-node budget ρ ∈ {20, 50, 80, 100, 120, 140, 160}; per-node degree τ set to the graph's average degree (6 for Cora-ML, 4 for Citeseer). Smoothed classifier estimated by Monte Carlo with N = 100,000 samples, α = 0.01, and LPs solved with MOSEK via CVXPY.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nBoth collective certificates dramatically outperform the sample-wise baseline as the injected-node count grows. On Citeseer at ρ = 140, Collective-LP1 and Collective-LP2 certify 73.0% and 81.2% of target nodes respectively, while the sample-wise approach certifies 0.0%.\n\n*Also —* The customized Collective-LP2 consistently matches or beats Collective-LP1 — on Cora-ML (pe=0.7, pn=0.9, ρ=140) it improves the certified ratio by 216% over LP1 — while running far faster, solving even ρ=140 in roughly 1 minute versus LP1's ~700+ seconds.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nComparing the relaxed LP against the exact integer BQCLP (feasible only for small ρ ≤ 12) shows Collective-LP2 loses only about 5% certified ratio from the relaxation, explaining its slight under-performance versus sample-wise at very small ρ.\n\n*Also —* Runtime comparison (Figure 4) shows Collective-LP1 scales super-linearly to over 1000 s at large ρ, whereas Collective-LP2 stays near 1 minute; clean-accuracy vs certified-ratio trade-off (Figure 3) confirms LP2 dominates LP1 at equal clean accuracy.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Citeseer certified ratio at ρ = 140: 0.0% (sample-wise) → 81.2% (Collective-LP2).\n\n*Also —* With 5% of the graph size injected, certified ratio rises from 0.0% to over 80.0% on both Cora-ML and Citeseer.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nCertifying a set of nodes jointly, rather than one at a time, turns a near-useless 0% guarantee into an 80%+ certified ratio against graph injection attacks, and a customized LP relaxation makes it solvable in about a minute.\n\n*Also —* This is a concrete step toward practical, provable defenses for message-passing GNNs, and it composes with existing sample-wise certificates to stay strong across both small and large attack budgets.","poster_thumb":"posters/009_collective_certified_robustness_against_graph_in.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/009_collective_certified_robustness_against_graph_in/009_collective_certified_robustness_against_graph_in/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/009_collective_certified_robustness_against_graph_in/009_collective_certified_robustness_against_graph_in/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/009_collective_certified_robustness_against_graph_in/009_collective_certified_robustness_against_graph_in/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/009_collective_certified_robustness_against_graph_in/009_collective_certified_robustness_against_graph_in/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/009_collective_certified_robustness_against_graph_in/009_collective_certified_robustness_against_graph_in/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/009_collective_certified_robustness_against_graph_in/009_collective_certified_robustness_against_graph_in/reel.html"}},{"id":"010","slug":"010_flexible_attention_based_multi_policy_fusion_for","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Flexible Attention-Based Multi-Policy Fusion for Efficient Deep Reinforcement Learning","authors":"Zih-Yun Chiu¹, Yi-Lin Tuan², William Yang Wang², Michael C. Yip¹","institutes":"¹University of California, San Diego; ²University of California, Santa Barbara","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03729","code_url":"https://github.com/Pascalson/KGRL.git","hook":"The paper proposes Knowledge-Grounded RL (KGRL), a paradigm for fusing multiple knowledge policies, and KIAN, an actor architecture that enables free knowledge rearrangement via embedding-based attentive action prediction.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nReinforcement learning agents are sample-inefficient. Prior methods that inject external knowledge policies cannot freely combine, rearrange, or replace those policies, blocking generalization and transfer.\n\n*Also —* Human learning is knowledge-acquirable, sample-efficient, generalizable, compositional, and incremental; RL agents lack the flexibility to reuse and update a knowledge set on the fly.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nTo approach human-like efficiency, an agent must treat external policies as a modular, reorderable set, so knowledge can be transferred to new tasks and expanded without relearning the whole model.\n\n*Also —* Prior approaches such as KoGuN and A2T tie policy fusion to fixed input orderings or per-policy parameters, so adding or removing a policy forces architectural changes or retraining.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper proposes Knowledge-Grounded RL (KGRL), a paradigm for fusing multiple knowledge policies, and KIAN, an actor architecture that enables free knowledge rearrangement via embedding-based attentive action prediction. It also identifies and resolves entropy imbalance in maximum-entropy KGRL.\n\n*Also —* It formalizes knowledge-acquirable, generalizable, compositional, and incremental agents, and shows KIAN's key-based design decouples each policy from the rest so the knowledge set can be updated without relearning KIAN.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nKIAN has three parts: an inner actor that learns the agent's own policy, a learnable key (embedding) for every knowledge policy, and a state-dependent query. A dot-product attention between the query and each key produces fusion weights, and the fused policy predicts the action.\n\n**Key idea.** $w_{t,in} = \\Phi(s_t;\\phi)\\cdot k_{in}/c_{t,in},\\quad w_{t,g_j} = \\Phi(s_t;\\phi)\\cdot k_{g_j}/c_{t,g_j}$ ; $[\\hat{w}_{t,in},\\hat{w}_{t,g_1},\\dots,\\hat{w}_{t,g_n}]^\\top = \\mathrm{softmax}([w_{t,in},w_{t,g_1},\\dots,w_{t,g_n}]^\\top)$ ; $\\pi(\\cdot|s_t) = \\hat{w}_{in}\\,\\pi_{in}(\\cdot|s_t) + \\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\hat{w}_{g_j}\\,\\pi_{g_j}(\\cdot|s_t)$\n\n*Also —* Because each policy is encoded as an independent key in a shared embedding space, keys are unordered and any policy can be swapped by swapping its key, with no changes elsewhere. For continuous control, one policy is selected via Gumbel-softmax and sampled with the reparameterization trick to keep KIAN differentiable.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nKIAN is evaluated on two benchmark suites: MiniGrid (discrete action spaces, tasks like Empty-Random, Unlock, DoorKey, Dynamic-Obstacles, LavaCrossing, MultiRoom, KeyCorridor) and OpenAI-Robotics (continuous control: Push, Slide, Pick-and-Place, Reach).\n\n*Also —* Every method shares the same initial external knowledge set of sub-optimal if-else programs (e.g. pickup_a_key, move_forward_to_the_goal) that cannot solve tasks alone. All runs use ten random seeds with 95% confidence-interval error bands.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nGiven a sub-optimal knowledge set, KIAN is the only method that succeeds across all environments and shows the best sample efficiency, with its advantage growing as tasks become harder. It also transfers best in zero-shot simple-to-complex and complex-to-simple generalization.\n\n*Also —* KIAN produces lower-variance results than the baselines (BC, RL, RL+BC, KoGuN, A2T) and remains stable in most environments; A2T barely succeeds in continuous control because it ignores entropy imbalance.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nAblating the entropy-imbalance fix (comparing KIAN with and without the modified policy distributions) shows the modifications are essential: without them performance drops sharply on harder tasks like Dynamic-Obstacles-16x16, MultiRoom-N4-S5, and continuous Pick-and-Place, Push, and Slide.\n\n*Also —* Compositional and incremental experiments show KIAN reuses previously learned knowledge keys and inner policies to learn two tasks sequentially with fewer samples than learning them separately.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Zero-shot generalization, Empty-Random-5x5 trained then tested at 16x16: KIAN 0.93 vs best baseline 0.53 (KoGuN).\n\n*Also —* KIAN is the only method to succeed in all tested environments given a sub-optimal initial knowledge set.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy encoding each policy as an independent learnable key and fusing them with query-key attention, KIAN makes external knowledge fully modular, and its entropy-imbalance fix restores efficient exploration, yielding more sample-efficient, generalizable RL.\n\n*Also —* The design lets an agent add, remove, and rearrange knowledge policies at any time without relearning most of the network.","poster_thumb":"posters/010_flexible_attention_based_multi_policy_fusion_for.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/010_flexible_attention_based_multi_policy_fusion_for/010_flexible_attention_based_multi_policy_fusion_for/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/010_flexible_attention_based_multi_policy_fusion_for/010_flexible_attention_based_multi_policy_fusion_for/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/010_flexible_attention_based_multi_policy_fusion_for/010_flexible_attention_based_multi_policy_fusion_for/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/010_flexible_attention_based_multi_policy_fusion_for/010_flexible_attention_based_multi_policy_fusion_for/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/010_flexible_attention_based_multi_policy_fusion_for/010_flexible_attention_based_multi_policy_fusion_for/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/010_flexible_attention_based_multi_policy_fusion_for/010_flexible_attention_based_multi_policy_fusion_for/reel.html"}},{"id":"011","slug":"011_g_2_n_2_weisfeiler_and_lehman_go_grammatical","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"G2N2: Weisfeiler and Lehman Go Grammatical","authors":"Jason Piquenot¹, Aldo Moscatelli¹, Maxime Bérar¹, Pierre Héroux¹, Jean-Yves Ramel², Romain Raveaux², Sébastien Adam¹","institutes":"¹LITIS Lab, University of Rouen Normandy; ²LIFAT Lab, University of Tours","venue":"ICLR 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.01590","code_url":"https://github.com/JPiquenot/Wesfeiler-and-Lehman-go-grammatical","hook":"(i) A generic framework that produces a GNN from any fragment of an algebraic language via context-free grammars; (ii) an instantiation on ML(L3) yielding G2N2, a provably 3-WL GNN; (iii) experimental validation of the grammar reduction / r…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nBuilding graph neural networks with guaranteed expressive power usually means proving a model matches the k-Weisfeiler-Lehman test after it is designed, and no systematic procedure exists to derive a GNN from a given expressive language fragment.\n\n*Also —* Existing 3-WL models like 3-IGN are memory-heavy (basis size grows as the 2k-th Bell number) and PPGN, though tractable, is only shown to mimic 2-FWL without a constructive language-to-model link.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nThe Weisfeiler-Lehman hierarchy characterizes GNN expressiveness, and prior work reformulated the 1-WL and 3-WL tests as fragments ML(L1) and ML(L3) of the matrix language MATLANG — but turning such a fragment into a provably equivalent GNN was still done case by case.\n\n*Also —* GNNML3, derived from the same MATLANG view, was only shown to be more expressive than 1-WL, not provably 3-WL, precisely because there was no systematic derivation procedure.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\n(i) A generic framework that produces a GNN from any fragment of an algebraic language via context-free grammars; (ii) an instantiation on ML(L3) yielding G2N2, a provably 3-WL GNN; (iii) experimental validation of the grammar reduction / rule set; (iv) extensive experiments showing G2N2 outperforms existing 3-WL GNNs across regression, classification, and spectral tasks.\n\n*Also —* The grammar-reduction step both preserves 3-WL expressiveness and exposes which operations matter, enabling informed pruning of the model.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nFrom the operation set L3 = {·, ᵀ, 1, diag, ⊙} the authors build an exhaustive Context-Free Grammar that generates the MATLANG fragment ML(L3), then reduce it to a compact grammar r-G(L3) that stays equivalent to 3-WL. The reduced grammar's variables become the GNN inputs (a node/edge feature tensor) and its production rules become the layer update functions, giving the Grammatical Graph Neural Network G2N2.\n\n**Key idea.** $C^{(l+1)} = \\mathrm{MLP}_M\\big(C^{(l)} \\,\\|\\, L_1(C^{(l)})\\!\\cdot\\!L_2(C^{(l)}) \\,\\|\\, L_3(C^{(l)})\\odot L_4(C^{(l)}) \\,\\|\\, \\mathrm{diag}(L_6(H^{(l)}))\\big)$ ; $H^{(l+1)} = \\mathrm{MLP}_{V_c}\\big(H^{(l)} \\,\\|\\, L_5(C^{(l)})\\!\\cdot\\!L_7(H^{(l)})\\big)$\n\n*Also —* A layer keeps an edge (matrix) memory C and a node (vector) memory H; learnable linear blocks L1–L7 combine tensor slices, the reduced rules (M⊙M), (MM), diag(Vc), (MVc) are computed, and MLPs merge them. Permutation-equivariant readouts on H and on the diagonal / off-diagonal of C feed a decision layer.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nQM9 (130K molecules, 12 regression targets, hardest target R²) for regression; the TUD benchmark (MUTAG, PTC, Proteins, NCI1, IMDB-B, IMDB-M) for graph classification; and a spectral node-regression dataset for band-pass filter learning.\n\n*Also —* QM9 is split 0.8 / 0.1 / 0.1 for train / val / test as in prior work; TUD uses the standard evaluation protocol; the spectral task follows the 900-node-graph protocol used to stress PPGN.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn QM9 regression G2N2 obtains the best MAE on every target while training faster than PPGN; on the hardest R² target it cuts MAE from PPGN's 3.78 to 0.342 (single-target) and 16.07 to 1.19 (all-targets-at-once).\n\n*Also —* On TUD it ranks better than 2nd on five of six datasets (e.g. MUTAG 92.5%, PTC 72.3%, Proteins 80.1%), and on spectral filtering it learns band-pass filters (R² 0.8206) where PPGN collapses (0.1041).\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nThe grammar-reduction study (Q1) shows G(L3), the intermediate grammar, and the reduced r-G(L3) reach comparable MAE on QM9 R², confirming reduction preserves expressiveness, while over-reducing (removing rules) degrades MAE — validating each rule's contribution.\n\n*Also —* Removing rules also reveals each operation's weight in the model, guiding principled pruning when full 3-WL expressiveness is not required for a task.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- QM9 R² MAE: 0.342 (G2N2) vs 3.78 (PPGN), single-target — a ~11× reduction.\n\n*Also —* - Spectral band-pass R²: 0.8206 (G2N2) vs 0.1041 (PPGN).\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy writing an expressive matrix-language fragment as a reduced context-free grammar and translating its rules into layers, G2N2 becomes a provably 3-WL GNN that is also faster and more accurate than prior 3-WL models.\n\n*Also —* The framework is generic — any algebraic language fragment can, in principle, be turned into a GNN with matching expressive power.","poster_thumb":"posters/011_g_2_n_2_weisfeiler_and_lehman_go_grammatical.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/011_g_2_n_2_weisfeiler_and_lehman_go_grammatical/011_g_2_n_2_weisfeiler_and_lehman_go_grammatical/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/011_g_2_n_2_weisfeiler_and_lehman_go_grammatical/011_g_2_n_2_weisfeiler_and_lehman_go_grammatical/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/011_g_2_n_2_weisfeiler_and_lehman_go_grammatical/011_g_2_n_2_weisfeiler_and_lehman_go_grammatical/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/011_g_2_n_2_weisfeiler_and_lehman_go_grammatical/011_g_2_n_2_weisfeiler_and_lehman_go_grammatical/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/011_g_2_n_2_weisfeiler_and_lehman_go_grammatical/011_g_2_n_2_weisfeiler_and_lehman_go_grammatical/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/011_g_2_n_2_weisfeiler_and_lehman_go_grammatical/011_g_2_n_2_weisfeiler_and_lehman_go_grammatical/reel.html"}},{"id":"012","slug":"012_privacy_preserving_machine_learning_for_collabor","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Privacy-Preserving Machine Learning for Collaborative Data Sharing via Auto-encoder Latent Space Embeddings","authors":"Ana María Quintero Ossa¹, Jesús Solano², Hernán García², David Zarruk³, Alejandro Correa-Bahnsen², Carlos Valencia¹","institutes":"¹Los Andes University; ²Rappi; ³Amazon","venue":"NeurIPS 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.05717","code_url":"","hook":"The paper proposes a framework that uses autoencoders to create privacy-preserving latent embeddings peers can share and join to train a collaborative downstream model without losing predictive power.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nOrganizations want to jointly train ML models on complementary data, but privacy policies and IP laws block sharing the raw sensitive features that would improve predictions.\n\n*Also —* Two peers holding different variables for the same users cannot merge datasets, so the collaboration, and the accuracy gain it promises, is cancelled.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nExisting privacy tools fall short: encryption is heavy to deploy, federated learning assumes peers share the same features, and differential privacy or linear dimensionality reduction distort or degrade the data.\n\n*Also —* These methods mainly protect data in transit, alter individual observation patterns, or carry high maintenance requirements, leaving room for a representation-learning solution.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper proposes a framework that uses autoencoders to create privacy-preserving latent embeddings peers can share and join to train a collaborative downstream model without losing predictive power.\n\n*Also —* It further defines and evaluates five scenarios, including non-naive multitask autoencoders, across three public benchmarks to demonstrate applicability.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nBefore merging data, each peer encodes its raw features into a latent representation with a fixed four-layer autoencoder, shares only that obfuscated embedding, and the peers join the embeddings by a common observation ID to train a shared downstream predictor.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\min_{\\theta,\\phi}\\; \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\big\\lVert x_i - g_\\phi\\!\\left(f_\\theta(x_i)\\right)\\big\\rVert_1,\\quad z_i = f_\\theta(x_i)$\n\n*Also —* The autoencoder uses encoder dimensions N→[128, 64, 40]→M and a mirrored decoder, ReLU activations, Mean Absolute Error loss, and an Adam optimizer with learning rate 0.0001. Non-naive scenarios (3 and 4) turn the autoencoder into a multitask network that also predicts the objective variable, so the latent space is shaped by the downstream task.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nThree public benchmarks span both task types and varied feature sizes: House Pricing (21,613 obs, 12 features, regression), MNIST Numbers (35,000 obs, 784 features, multiclass classification), and Buzz in Social Media (87,488 obs, 77 features, regression).\n\n*Also —* The datasets were chosen to vary downstream task, dimensionality, and feature type, testing the framework's robustness and scalability. Training used an Intel Xeon CPU (2.30 GHz, 2 cores), an Nvidia K80/T4 GPU with 12 GB memory, and 12 GB RAM.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nSwapping raw features for shared latent embeddings preserves downstream accuracy: on the House Pricing test set the R² only drops from 90.29% (raw baseline) to 89.33% with two individual autoencoders (Scenario 2), and Buzz test R² stays at 89–91% versus a 96.19% baseline.\n\n*Also —* MNIST classification test accuracy moves from 92% (baseline) to 84–88% under encoding, and the non-naive multitask variant (Scenario 3) recovers much of the gap, reaching 91% accuracy and 94.03% R² on Buzz.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nFive scenarios isolate the design choices: Scenario 0 raw baseline; Scenario 1 single shared autoencoder; Scenario 2 individual per-peer autoencoders; and non-naive Scenarios 3 and 4 that add multitask target prediction to Scenarios 1 and 2.\n\n*Also —* The non-naive multitask autoencoders (3, 4) consistently outperform their naive counterparts (1, 2): on Buzz, test R² rises from 91.55% (Scenario 1) to 94.03% (Scenario 3), and on MNIST accuracy rises from 88% to 91%.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- < 10pp performance drop when replacing raw features with shared latent embeddings\n\n*Also —* Encoding quality: shared autoencoders correctly estimate 96%–98% of observations per feature (≤5% MAPE); MNIST test accuracy 92% baseline → 91% (Scenario 3).\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nPeers can collaborate on machine learning by sharing autoencoder latent embeddings instead of raw data, keeping sensitive features private while losing less than ten percentage points of downstream performance.\n\n*Also —* Making the autoencoder task-aware (non-naive) narrows the gap further, pointing toward custom per-dataset encoders and explicit privacy-level measures as future work.","poster_thumb":"posters/012_privacy_preserving_machine_learning_for_collabor.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/012_privacy_preserving_machine_learning_for_collabor/012_privacy_preserving_machine_learning_for_collabor/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/012_privacy_preserving_machine_learning_for_collabor/012_privacy_preserving_machine_learning_for_collabor/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/012_privacy_preserving_machine_learning_for_collabor/012_privacy_preserving_machine_learning_for_collabor/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/012_privacy_preserving_machine_learning_for_collabor/012_privacy_preserving_machine_learning_for_collabor/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/012_privacy_preserving_machine_learning_for_collabor/012_privacy_preserving_machine_learning_for_collabor/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/012_privacy_preserving_machine_learning_for_collabor/012_privacy_preserving_machine_learning_for_collabor/reel.html"}},{"id":"013","slug":"013_differentially_private_cutmix_for_split_learning","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Differentially Private CutMix for Split Learning with Vision Transformer","authors":"Seungeun Oh¹, Sihun Baek¹, Hyelin Nam¹, Seong-Lyun Kim¹, Jihong Park², Praneeth Vepakomma³, Ramesh Raskar³, Mehdi Bennis⁴","institutes":"¹Yonsei University; ²Deakin University; ³Massachusetts Institute of Technology; ⁴University of Oulu","venue":"NeurIPS 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.15986","code_url":"","hook":"The paper proposes DP-CutMixSL, a differentially private split-learning framework built on DP-CutMix, a patch-level randomized inter-client CutMix that replaces randomly selected patches across clients' smashed data, and proves it amplifies…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nIn split learning with a vision transformer, the cut-layer \"smashed data\" stays highly similar to the raw input because ViT lacks pooling and convolution, leaking privacy and inflating communication cost.\n\n*Also —* Federated learning is the usual privacy-preserving alternative but must communicate ViT's large model, imposing excessive energy and communication burdens on edge devices.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nViT is displacing CNN in vision, so privacy-preserving distributed training must be rethought for transformer architectures whose patch-level, pooling-free design breaks the assumptions behind prior SL privacy.\n\n*Also —* ViT's three distinctive properties, low feature distortion at the cut-layer, global self-attention that is robust to localized noise, and patch-level operation, together point to one solution: a patch-scale regularizer.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper proposes DP-CutMixSL, a differentially private split-learning framework built on DP-CutMix, a patch-level randomized inter-client CutMix that replaces randomly selected patches across clients' smashed data, and proves it amplifies the Rényi-DP guarantee while empirically raising accuracy.\n\n*Also —* Unlike prior work that applies Cutout and CutMix at the pixel level for intra-dataset augmentation, this work uses them at the patch level for privacy-preserving inter-dataset interpolation across devices.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nA mixer generates pseudorandom binary masks M_i with Dirichlet mixing ratios λ_i (Σλ_i = 1). Each client masks its smashed data (Cutout), adds Gaussian noise (DP-Cutout smashed data), and uploads it; the server sums the complementary masked patches from different clients into DP-CutMix smashed data, then continues forward and backward propagation as in vanilla SL.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\bar{s}'_i = \\bar{s}_i + N_s = M_i \\odot s_i + N_s$ ; $\\tilde{s}'_{i,j} = \\bar{s}'_i + \\bar{s}'_j,\\quad \\tilde{y}'_{i,j} = \\lambda_i \\bar{y}'_i + \\lambda_j \\bar{y}'_j$ ; $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{Mix}}(\\alpha) \\le \\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{CutMix}}(\\alpha) \\le \\varepsilon_{o}(\\alpha)$ , with $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{CutMix}}(\\alpha) = \\frac{\\alpha(\\max_{i\\in C}\\lambda_i)}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\Delta^2 D_s}{\\sigma_s^2} + \\frac{D_y}{\\sigma_y^2}\\right)$\n\n*Also —* Because the per-client masks are mutually exclusive and collectively exhaustive at the patch level, the aggregated DP-CutMix smashed data has no blank patches; only a fraction of each client's noisy smashed data is ever shared with the server.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluation uses CIFAR-10 and Fashion-MNIST across three architectures spanning the CNN-to-transformer spectrum: ViT-Tiny, PiT-Tiny (a transformer with a pooling layer), and VGG-16, with |C| = 10 clients and the cut-layer placed after the embedding process.\n\n*Also —* RDP is computed with patch size N = 64, D_s = 20, D_y = 10, sensitivity ∆ = 0.2, uniform mixing λ_i = 1/n, and RDP order α = 2.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nIn a noiseless setting (Table 1), CutMixSL attains the highest top-1 accuracy in every case but one, reaching 73.77% on CIFAR-10 with ViT-Tiny versus 57.21% for plain SL and 67.88% for SplitFed, and 71.26% with PiT-Tiny versus 55.63% for SplitFed.\n\n*Also —* The single exception is CIFAR-10 with VGG-16, where SL with Mixup (68.20%) edges out CutMixSL (67.53%), consistent with CNNs suffering more information loss from patch replacement than transformers do.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nVarying noise variance (Fig. 3a), DP-CutMixSL gives the best accuracy at almost every level and a tighter RDP bound ε than DP-SL, but a larger ε than DP-MixSL, exposing an accuracy-privacy trade-off; enlarging the mixing group (Fig. 3b) lowers both accuracy and ε for both mixing methods.\n\n*Also —* A reconstruction-attack study (Table 2) ranks robustness, measured by reconstruction MSE, as Cutout > patch CutMix > Mixup > raw smashed data, and scalability (Fig. 3c) improves as clients grow from 2 to 10, further boosted by adding SplitFed-style weight averaging (CutMixSFL).\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- **73.77%** top-1 accuracy, CutMixSL on CIFAR-10 with ViT-Tiny (vs. 57.21% plain SL, +16.56 pp; vs. 67.88% SplitFed, +5.89 pp).\n\n*Also —* Reconstruction loss (MSE, 100% train data): patch CutMix 0.0434 vs. raw smashed data 0.0056, an ~8× harder inversion.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nApplying differentially private, patch-level randomized CutMix to a vision transformer's smashed data simultaneously strengthens the DP guarantee over vanilla split learning and improves accuracy, with the privacy gain upper-bounded by a Mixup-based counterpart.\n\n*Also —* The patch-level, global-attention nature of ViT is what makes patch CutMix work where it hurts CNNs, so the method is tailored to transformer-based distributed learning.","poster_thumb":"posters/013_differentially_private_cutmix_for_split_learning.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/013_differentially_private_cutmix_for_split_learning/013_differentially_private_cutmix_for_split_learning/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/013_differentially_private_cutmix_for_split_learning/013_differentially_private_cutmix_for_split_learning/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/013_differentially_private_cutmix_for_split_learning/013_differentially_private_cutmix_for_split_learning/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/013_differentially_private_cutmix_for_split_learning/013_differentially_private_cutmix_for_split_learning/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/013_differentially_private_cutmix_for_split_learning/013_differentially_private_cutmix_for_split_learning/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/013_differentially_private_cutmix_for_split_learning/013_differentially_private_cutmix_for_split_learning/reel.html"}},{"id":"014","slug":"014_light_schr_dinger_bridge","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Light Schrödinger Bridge","authors":"Alexander Korotin¹², Nikita Gushchin¹, Evgeny Burnaev¹²","institutes":"¹Skolkovo Institute of Science and Technology; ²Artificial Intelligence Research Institute","venue":"ICLR 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.01174","code_url":"https://github.com/ngushchin/LightSB","hook":"The paper proposes LightSB, a fast, simulation-free, non-minimax SB solver that combines Gaussian-mixture (sum-exp quadratic) parameterization of the Schrödinger potential with an energy-based view, giving a single straightforward objective…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nComputational Schrödinger Bridge (SB) solvers are heavy: they stack several neural networks and need complex optimization, so there is no simple, principled baseline solver, analogous to k-means, logistic regression, or Sinkhorn.\n\n*Also —* SB, the dynamic form of Entropic Optimal Transport (EOT), seeks a diffusion process between two distributions maximally similar to a Wiener prior; practitioners often just want the endpoint plan π*(x₁|x₀).\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nA user who just wants EOT/SB between moderate-dimensional distributions is forced into heavy neural solvers with min-max objectives, process simulation during training, and hours of GPU time plus fragile hyperparameter tuning.\n\n*Also —* In most real applications only the endpoints of the trajectories matter, so a solver that returns the conditional plan directly and cheaply would already cover the dominant use case.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper proposes LightSB, a fast, simulation-free, non-minimax SB solver that combines Gaussian-mixture (sum-exp quadratic) parameterization of the Schrödinger potential with an energy-based view, giving a single straightforward objective with closed-form plan and drift.\n\n*Also —* It also proves LightSB is a universal approximator of Schrödinger Bridges (the first such result) and analyzes its generalization error, showing it vanishes at the standard parametric rate.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nLightSB directly minimizes KL(π*‖π_θ) by parameterizing the adjusted Schrödinger potential v_θ as an unnormalized Gaussian mixture. This yields a tractable objective L(θ) plus closed-form conditional distributions π_θ(x₁|x₀) and normalization c_θ(x₀), optimized by plain stochastic gradient descent.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\mathrm{KL}(\\pi^*\\|\\pi_\\theta)\\to\\min_\\theta,\\quad \\pi_\\theta(x_1\\mid x_0)\\propto \\exp(\\langle x_0,x_1\\rangle/\\epsilon)\\,v_\\theta(x_1),\\quad v_\\theta(x_1)=\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\alpha_k\\,\\mathcal{N}(x_1\\mid r_k,\\epsilon S_k)$ and $L(\\theta)=\\int \\log c_\\theta(x_0)\\,p_0(x_0)\\,dx_0-\\int \\log v_\\theta(x_1)\\,p_1(x_1)\\,dx_1$\n\n*Also —* Because the KL reduces to a difference of expectations of log c_θ and log v_θ, it admits Monte-Carlo estimation from samples; the learned plan reconstructs the full SB drift g_θ in closed form, so no process simulation is needed during training.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on the Gushchin et al. (2023b) high-dimensional continuous EOT/SB benchmark (known ground-truth plans, D=2–128, ϵ=0.1,1,10), the Kaggle MSCI single-cell dataset (four donors, days 2/3/4/7; PCA DIM=50/100/1000), and 1024×1024 FFHQ faces in the 512-dim latent space of a pretrained ALAE autoencoder.\n\n*Also —* Also a 2D Gaussian→Swiss-roll toy showing how ϵ controls trajectory volatility, plus an embryonic stem-cell differentiation dataset in the appendix.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn the EOT/SB benchmark LightSB beats the best prior solver by a wide margin (cBW²₂-UVP of roughly 0.03–0.62% versus 1.04–18.05% for the best baseline). On MSCI it matches strong GPU solvers in energy distance while training in 65–146 seconds on 4 CPU cores instead of tens of minutes to over an hour on a V100 GPU.\n\n*Also —* For image translation, LightSB performs male↔female and adult↔child face translation in the ALAE latent space (D=512), converging in under one minute on 4 CPU cores.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nThe 2D Gaussian→Swiss-roll study varies ϵ (2·10⁻³, 10⁻², 10⁻¹): small ϵ gives nearly straight, near-deterministic trajectories, while larger ϵ increases trajectory volatility and disperses the conditional distributions π_θ(x₁|x₀), confirming the noise level behaves as expected.\n\n*Also —* Across benchmark settings, LightSB's advantage is largest where the data matches its sum-exp Gaussian-mixture inductive bias, and its accuracy holds across dimensions D=2–128 and ϵ=0.1–10.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Convergence in under 1 minute on 4 CPU cores (no GPU) for 512-dim latent image translation.\n\n*Also —* Training each MSCI setup takes 65–146 s on CPU across DIM=50/100/1000, versus 8–71 min for GPU baselines.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nLightSB is a simple, simulation-free, non-minimax Schrödinger Bridge solver, essentially a Gaussian-mixture model for the transport potential, that runs in minutes on a CPU, matches or beats heavy GPU solvers, and is provably a universal approximator, making it a natural default baseline for EOT/SB.\n\n*Also —* Its main limitation is that it targets moderate-dimensional distributions and is applied in latent rather than raw image space.","poster_thumb":"posters/014_light_schr_dinger_bridge.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/014_light_schr_dinger_bridge/014_light_schr_dinger_bridge/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/014_light_schr_dinger_bridge/014_light_schr_dinger_bridge/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/014_light_schr_dinger_bridge/014_light_schr_dinger_bridge/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/014_light_schr_dinger_bridge/014_light_schr_dinger_bridge/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/014_light_schr_dinger_bridge/014_light_schr_dinger_bridge/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/014_light_schr_dinger_bridge/014_light_schr_dinger_bridge/reel.html"}},{"id":"015","slug":"015_conditional_matrix_flows_for_gaussian_graphical","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Conditional Matrix Flows for Gaussian Graphical Models","authors":"Marcello Massimo Negri¹, Fabricio Arend Torres¹, Volker Roth¹","institutes":"¹University of Basel","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.07255","code_url":"https://github.com/marcello-negri/CMF","hook":"A general variational-inference framework for Bayesian GGMs using a matrix-variate normalizing flow defined directly over symmetric positive-definite matrices, conditioned on both λ and q, that jointly trains a continuum of sparse regressio…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nGaussian Graphical Models recover conditional independence by sparsifying the precision matrix Ω, but the ideal sub-l1 (q<1) penalties are highly non-convex, so methods default to the l1 norm and its over-shrinkage.\n\n*Also —* In high dimensions (d>n) the sample covariance is singular, so the precision matrix cannot be obtained by inversion and must be estimated under a sparsity penalty.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nFrequentist Graphical Lasso gives the l1 solution path over λ but not a posterior; Bayesian formulations give a posterior but rely on expensive Gibbs samplers that must be re-run for every λ and do not generalize to other priors.\n\n*Also —* Variational inference could unify both, but mean-field approximations assume independence, which is precisely the dependence structure a GGM aims to model, so they cannot be applied naively.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nA general variational-inference framework for Bayesian GGMs using a matrix-variate normalizing flow defined directly over symmetric positive-definite matrices, conditioned on both λ and q, that jointly trains a continuum of sparse regression models for all shrinkage strengths and all lq pseudo-norms in a single model.\n\n*Also —* Within one model it delivers (i) the posterior for any λ and any lq (pseudo-)norm, (ii) the marginal log-likelihood for model selection, and (iii) the frequentist solution path via simulated annealing in the MAP limit. It is the first unified framework for sub-l1 pseudo-norms that needs no surrogate penalties.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nA normalizing flow maps a base vector z ∈ R^{d(d+1)/2} through Sum-of-Sigmoids layers, then Fill-Triangular → Positive-Diagonal (softplus) → Cholesky-product (Ω = LLᵀ), guaranteeing Ω is symmetric positive-definite by construction. A hypernetwork conditions the flow on (λ, q), and training minimizes the KL divergence to the unnormalized posterior with a generalized-Normal prior. Cooling the temperature T→0 by simulated annealing recovers the MAP / frequentist path.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\hat{\\Omega}=\\arg\\max_{\\Omega\\succ0}\\{\\log\\det\\Omega-\\mathrm{Tr}(\\tfrac{1}{n}S\\Omega)-\\lambda\\lVert\\Omega\\rVert_0\\}$ ; $p(\\Omega\\mid\\lambda,q)\\propto\\mathcal{W}_d(d{+}1,\\lambda\\mathbf{1}_d)\\prod_{i<j}\\tfrac{q\\lambda^{1/q}}{2\\Gamma(1/q)}\\exp\\{-\\lambda|\\omega_{ij}|^{q}\\}\\,\\mathbb{I}[\\Omega\\succ0]$ ; $\\mathcal{L}(\\theta;\\lambda,q)=\\mathrm{KL}\\big(q_{\\theta(\\lambda,q)}(\\Omega)\\,\\|\\,p(\\Omega\\mid S,\\lambda,q)\\big)$ ; $p^{(i)}(\\Omega)=p(\\Omega\\mid S,\\lambda,q)^{1/T_i}$\n\n*Also —* The generalized-Normal prior exp{−λ|ω_ij|^q} recovers the Laplace prior (Lasso, BGL) at q=1 and the Normal prior (Ridge) at q=2. The marginal log-likelihood log p(S|λ,q) is available directly as the loss's normalization constant, at no extra cost. For real data the flow is defined only over the query sub-blocks Ω₁₁, Ω₁₂, avoiding the full precision matrix.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nSynthetic sparse precision matrices (Scikit-learn generator) with d=15 for the toy study and d=30 with n∈{15,25,35,45} for edge recovery, averaged over 10 ground-truth matrices. Real data is a colorectal-cancer cohort with 7 clinical variables and 312 gene-expression measurements from 260 patients.\n\n*Also —* After dropping the p53 mutation status variable and incomplete records, n=190 fully measured patients remain, with s=6 clinical variables and t=312 genes; only the Ω₁₁ and Ω₁₂ query sub-blocks are modeled.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nA single Conditional Matrix Flow reconstructs the frequentist l1 solution path with MSE = 0.052 and, in the low-sample regime (n<d), sub-l1 pseudo-norms give higher F1 edge-recovery than the Bayesian Graphical Lasso, with CMF at q=0.25 outperforming all competing frequentist l0-surrogate penalties (atan, selo, log, sica) across every sample size, most strongly at n=15.\n\n*Also —* Model selection via the CMF marginal likelihood picks λ*=3.52, matching the Graphical Lasso cross-validation choice of λ*=3.36. On real data the inferred network shows strong edges between cancer group stage (GS) and each TNM variable (T, N, M), and lower q yields visibly sparser graphs.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nVarying the pseudo-norm q ∈ {1, 0.75, 0.5, 0.25} shows the posterior median is progressively less shrunk toward zero as q decreases: at λ=0.3 the 95% credible interval excludes zero for q∈{0.25,0.5,0.75} but includes it for the BGL / q=1, directly demonstrating that sub-l1 norms reduce over-shrinkage.\n\n*Also —* Sweeping temperature T interpolates the same model between the Bayesian solution (T=1, matches BGL) and the frequentist MAP path (T=0.01); on real data q∈{1.0,0.9,0.8,0.7,0.6} traces increasing sparsity of the recovered graph.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Solution-path reconstruction error: MSE = 0.052 vs. the frequentist path.\n\n*Also —* Toy study d=15, α=90% sparsity, 10 000 epochs, T from 5 to 0.01 over 100 cooling steps; edge recovery d=30, n∈{15,25,35,45}, 10 matrices.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nOne conditional normalizing flow over positive-definite matrices unifies Bayesian and frequentist sparse GGM inference, giving posteriors, marginal-likelihood model selection, and frequentist solution paths for every λ and every lq pseudo-norm, including the non-convex sub-l1 regime, without surrogate penalties or per-λ resampling.\n\n*Also —* Because sub-l1 norms curb the over-shrinkage of the Lasso relaxation, the method recovers edges better in the hard low-sample regime while sampling orders of magnitude faster than Gibbs.","poster_thumb":"posters/015_conditional_matrix_flows_for_gaussian_graphical.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/015_conditional_matrix_flows_for_gaussian_graphical/015_conditional_matrix_flows_for_gaussian_graphical/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/015_conditional_matrix_flows_for_gaussian_graphical/015_conditional_matrix_flows_for_gaussian_graphical/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/015_conditional_matrix_flows_for_gaussian_graphical/015_conditional_matrix_flows_for_gaussian_graphical/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/015_conditional_matrix_flows_for_gaussian_graphical/015_conditional_matrix_flows_for_gaussian_graphical/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/015_conditional_matrix_flows_for_gaussian_graphical/015_conditional_matrix_flows_for_gaussian_graphical/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/015_conditional_matrix_flows_for_gaussian_graphical/015_conditional_matrix_flows_for_gaussian_graphical/reel.html"}},{"id":"016","slug":"016_a_context_integrated_transformer_based_neural_ne","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"A Context-Integrated Transformer-Based Neural Network for Auction Design","authors":"Zhijian Duan¹, Jingwu Tang¹, Yutong Yin¹, Zhe Feng², Xiang Yan³, Manzil Zaheer⁴, Xiaotie Deng¹","institutes":"¹Peking University; ²Google Research; ³Shanghai Jiao Tong University; ⁴Google DeepMind","venue":"ICML 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.12489","code_url":"https://github.com/zjduan/CITransNet","hook":"The paper (1) formalizes contextual auction design as a learning problem with a sample-complexity generalization bound, and (2) proposes CITransNet, a context-integrated transformer that is permutation-equivariant over bids and contexts yet…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nDesigning an incentive-compatible auction that maximizes the auctioneer's expected revenue is a central open problem; even the two-bidder two-item case is unsolved after four decades.\n\n*Also —* Recent deep-learning auction designers only handle a fixed set of bidders/items or force the auction to be symmetric, ignoring the rich public context of real markets.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nReal auctions carry public contextual features on bidders and items and vary in size from round to round, so a practical designer must ingest features and accept a changing number of bidders and items.\n\n*Also —* Prior neural designers (RegretNet, EquivariantNet) either fix the auction scale or ignore bidder/item identity, so they cannot exploit context or find the asymmetric mechanisms that context demands.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper (1) formalizes contextual auction design as a learning problem with a sample-complexity generalization bound, and (2) proposes CITransNet, a context-integrated transformer that is permutation-equivariant over bids and contexts yet can represent asymmetric mechanisms whose parameter count is independent of auction scale.\n\n*Also —* Because the parameter count does not depend on the number of bidders or items, CITransNet can be trained at one scale and evaluated at another, an ability the authors call out-of-setting generalization.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nCITransNet embeds bidder-contexts and item-contexts, assembles them with the bidding profile into a per-bidder-item representation, and passes it through one or more transformer-based interaction layers that model mutual influence across bidders and items, then an output layer emits the allocation and payment.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\min_{(g,p)\\in\\mathcal{M}} -\\mathbb{E}_{(v,x,y)\\sim D}\\left[\\sum_{i=1}^{n} p_i(v,x,y)\\right] \\quad \\text{s.t.} \\quad \\mathbb{E}_{(v,x,y)\\sim D}\\left[\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathrm{rgt}_i(v,x,y)\\right] = 0$ and $\\mathrm{rgt}_i(v,x,y) := \\max_{b_i\\in V_i} u_i(v_i,(b_i,v_{-i}),x,y) - u_i(v_i,v,x,y)$\n\n*Also —* Each interaction layer combines a global average, a transformer applied along each column, and a transformer applied along each row, keeping the mechanism permutation-equivariant; IR is guaranteed by architecture and DSIC is enforced as a regret constraint trained via the augmented-Lagrangian method of RegretNet.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nExperiments span nine synthetic contextual-auction settings: three single-item settings (A–C) with Myerson-optimal solutions, and six multi-item settings (D–I) up to 5 bidders × 10 items with discrete or continuous (R¹⁰) contexts.\n\n*Also —* The 5×10 setting is, to the authors' knowledge, the largest auction size considered in prior deep-learning auction-design literature; each result is averaged over 5 runs.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nIn single-item settings CITransNet essentially recovers the Myerson-optimal revenue (0.593 vs 0.594 optimal in Setting A) with regret below 0.001, and in every multi-item setting it beats the strong Item-wise Myerson baseline and the context-integrated CIRegretNet and CIEquivariantNet variants.\n\n*Also —* Gains over Item-wise Myerson reach roughly +9.9% (Setting G: 1.177 vs 1.071) and +5.6% (Setting E: 6.872 vs 6.509), while plain RegretNet and EquivariantNet, lacking context, fail to reach the optimum even on the simple settings.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nSwapping CITransNet's transformer interaction layers for RegretNet's fully-connected layers (CIRegretNet) or EquivariantNet's layers (CIEquivariantNet) lowers revenue in every multi-item setting, isolating the transformer as the source of the gain.\n\n*Also —* Removing context entirely (RegretNet, EquivariantNet) drops revenue even on single-item settings, and the symmetric EquivariantNet underperforms RegretNet, confirming asymmetric solutions matter in contextual auctions.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Single-item Setting A: revenue 0.593 vs Myerson-optimal 0.594, regret < 0.001 (near-exact recovery).\n\n*Also —* Largest auction studied is 5 bidders × 10 items; all numbers averaged over 5 runs.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy folding public context into a permutation-equivariant transformer, CITransNet learns near-optimal, asymmetric, incentive-compatible auctions and, because its size is scale-independent, generalizes to auctions with more bidders or items than it was trained on.\n\n*Also —* It is the first neural auction designer to combine contextual features, asymmetric mechanisms, and out-of-setting generalization in one architecture.","poster_thumb":"posters/016_a_context_integrated_transformer_based_neural_ne.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/016_a_context_integrated_transformer_based_neural_ne/016_a_context_integrated_transformer_based_neural_ne/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/016_a_context_integrated_transformer_based_neural_ne/016_a_context_integrated_transformer_based_neural_ne/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/016_a_context_integrated_transformer_based_neural_ne/016_a_context_integrated_transformer_based_neural_ne/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/016_a_context_integrated_transformer_based_neural_ne/016_a_context_integrated_transformer_based_neural_ne/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/016_a_context_integrated_transformer_based_neural_ne/016_a_context_integrated_transformer_based_neural_ne/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/016_a_context_integrated_transformer_based_neural_ne/016_a_context_integrated_transformer_based_neural_ne/reel.html"}},{"id":"017","slug":"017_dragonn_distributed_randomized_approximate_gradi","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"DRAGONN: Distributed Randomized Approximate Gradients of Neural Networks","authors":"Zhuang Wang¹, Zhaozhuo Xu¹, Xinyu Crystal Wu¹, Anshumali Shrivastava¹², T. S. Eugene Ng¹","institutes":"¹Rice University; ²ThirdAI Corp","venue":"ICML 2022","paper_url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v162/wang22aj.html","code_url":"https://github.com/zhuangwang93/dragonn","hook":"(1) DRAGONN, a randomized hashing algorithm for GS that reduces compression overhead while preserving iteration-wise accuracy, with theoretical bounds on compression and generalization error.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nIn data-parallel distributed training, gradient synchronization is the efficiency bottleneck. Gradient sparsification (GS) reduces traffic, but its compression overhead now outweighs the communication savings, becoming the new bottleneck.\n\n*Also —* For tensors above 2^24 bytes the compression overhead exceeds communication and other overheads; for small tensors GS can even be slower than full synchronization.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nState-of-the-art GS methods such as DGC extract approximate top-k gradients using exact parallel prefix sum, an inherently sequential O(log d) algorithm that scans the tensor multiple times, incurring roughly 7x more memory accesses than the lower bound.\n\n*Also —* The authors argue this is an exact-approximate mismatch: only approximate top-k gradients are needed, so paying for an exact selection algorithm is unnecessary overhead.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\n(1) DRAGONN, a randomized hashing algorithm for GS that reduces compression overhead while preserving iteration-wise accuracy, with theoretical bounds on compression and generalization error. (2) System-level optimizations: efficiency-aware tensor selection and sparse decoding. (3) Extensive vision and recommendation evaluation showing large end-to-end speedups.\n\n*Also —* DRAGONN supports massively parallel gradient extraction because independent threads can hash and write indices simultaneously without ordering dependencies.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nDRAGONN pre-allocates a compressed memory buffer and, for every gradient above the threshold, hashes its index directly into a memory slot instead of computing nonzero positions with prefix sum. Collisions overwrite prior entries and empty slots (-1) map to zero. Because memory writes are atomic on GPUs, many threads hash and write in parallel with no ordering dependency, so the algorithm needs only d comparisons plus l hashing operations, near the d-comparison lower bound.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\text{if } |G[i]| \\ge t:\\ j \\leftarrow h(i),\\ I[j] \\leftarrow i$ ; $T_{comp}(d) < T_{full}(d) - T_{spr}(d)$\n\n*Also —* DRAGONN is deployed in practice with efficiency-aware tensor selection (compress a tensor only when compression time is less than the communication savings) and a sparse decoding mechanism that batches the K received compressed tensors into a single decode, making decoding cost nearly constant in the number of GPUs.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on four models: ResNet50 on ImageNet-1K, ViT and MLP-Mixer fine-tuned on Cifar10 (pretrained on ImageNet-21k), and an extreme multi-label classification (XML) model on Wiki10-31K.\n\n*Also —* Testbed: 16 Nvidia Tesla V100-32GB GPUs (2 machines x 8 GPUs), 25Gbps network, CUDA 11.0, PyTorch 1.8.0, NCCL 2.7.8, Horovod 0.19.1, with memory-momentum error feedback.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nDRAGONN reduces compression time by up to 70% versus state-of-the-art GS, and reaches the same convergence with up to 3.52x speedup in total training throughput over DGC and up to 35.9x over full synchronization, while matching FULL's test accuracy.\n\n*Also —* The speedup of DRAGONN over DGC grows with the number of GPUs, showing better scalability; gains are expected to widen on faster networks where communication-to-compression ratio shifts toward compression.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nA component ablation isolates the three parts. The hashing compressor alone gives the base speedup; adding efficiency-aware tensor selection raises it (e.g. to 1.92x on one model), and adding sparse decoding pushes it further (up to 4.33x). Sparse decoding's speedup exceeds K-fold because it removes the linear-in-GPU decode cost.\n\n*Also —* Micro-benchmarks (Figure 5) confirm DRAGONN has the lowest encoding time across tensor sizes and that sparse decoding stays nearly flat as GPUs increase, whereas dense decoding grows linearly.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Up to 70% reduction in compression time vs SOTA GS\n\n*Also —* DGC's GPU compression incurs ~7x the memory accesses of the d-comparison lower bound; DRAGONN removes this via O(1)-dependency hashing.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBecause gradient sparsification only needs approximate top-k gradients, replacing exact parallel-prefix-sum selection with direct randomized hashing removes the compression bottleneck, making GS actually pay off in distributed training.\n\n*Also —* Hashing turns a sequential, dependency-heavy operation into an embarrassingly parallel one, and pairing it with tensor selection and sparse decoding delivers end-to-end scalable speedups without hurting accuracy.","poster_thumb":"posters/017_dragonn_distributed_randomized_approximate_gradi.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/017_dragonn_distributed_randomized_approximate_gradi/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/017_dragonn_distributed_randomized_approximate_gradi/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/017_dragonn_distributed_randomized_approximate_gradi/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/017_dragonn_distributed_randomized_approximate_gradi/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/017_dragonn_distributed_randomized_approximate_gradi/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/017_dragonn_distributed_randomized_approximate_gradi/reel.html"}},{"id":"018","slug":"018_learning_to_reach_goals_via_diffusion","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Learning to Reach Goals via Diffusion","authors":"Vineet Jain¹², Siamak Ravanbakhsh¹²","institutes":"¹McGill University; ²Mila - Quebec AI Institute","venue":"ICML 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.02505","code_url":"https://github.com/vineetjain96/merlin","hook":"The paper introduces Merlin, a goal-conditioned reinforcement learning method that frames goal-reaching as reverse diffusion in the state space and learns the reverse policy purely by goal-conditioned behavior cloning, with no separate valu…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nOffline goal-conditioned reinforcement learning must train agents to reach arbitrary goals from fixed datasets under sparse binary rewards, but most methods depend on a learned value function that becomes unreliable in this setting.\n\n*Also —* Out-of-distribution actions generated during training corrupt value estimates, and these errors compound over time and can cause the policy to diverge.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nDenoising diffusion models offer a value-free way to model a target distribution by learning to reverse a noising process, suggesting goal-reaching can be cast as reversing trajectories that walk away from goal states.\n\n*Also —* Prior attempts to stabilize offline learning add policy constraints or conservative value updates, which compromise performance and hurt generalization.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces Merlin, a goal-conditioned reinforcement learning method that frames goal-reaching as reverse diffusion in the state space and learns the reverse policy purely by goal-conditioned behavior cloning, with no separate value function.\n\n*Also —* Merlin is the first method to perform diffusion directly in the state space, requiring only one denoising iteration per environment step, and comes in three variants: a base version, a parametric forward model (Merlin-P), and a non-parametric forward model with trajectory stitching (Merlin-NP).\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nMerlin views an offline trajectory in reverse: starting from a goal state, a forward diffusion transformation pushes states away from the goal, and a goal-conditioned policy is trained to reverse these transitions, needing only a single denoising step per environment step at inference.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\theta^* = \\arg\\max_\\theta \\; \\mathbb{E}_{g \\sim p(g),\\, (s,a) \\sim \\mathcal{D}(g)} \\left[ \\log \\pi_\\theta(a \\mid s, g) \\right] \\;\\; \\text{maximizes a lower bound on} \\;\\; L = \\mathbb{E}_{g \\sim p(g),\\, s_T \\sim q(s_T \\mid g)} \\left[ \\log p_\\theta(s_T) \\right]$\n\n*Also —* A theorem shows that maximizing the log-likelihood of goal states under the reverse diffusion process reduces to behavior cloning with hindsight relabeling; the forward process is constructed either by a fixed rule, a parametric model (Merlin-P), or a non-parametric nearest-neighbor stitching in latent space (Merlin-NP).\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluation uses the offline goal-conditioned benchmark of Yang et al. (2021): 10 control tasks with maximum trajectory length T=50, a sparse binary reward, and both \"expert\" and \"random\" dataset settings.\n\n*Also —* The suite spans easier tasks (PointReach, PointRooms, Reacher, SawyerReach, SawyerDoor, FetchReach, 2000 trajectories each) and harder tasks (FetchPush, FetchPick, FetchSlide, HandReach, 40000 trajectories each), with both state-space and pixel-space observation variants.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nMerlin and its variants outperform state-of-the-art offline goal-conditioned and diffusion-based baselines on most tasks, with Merlin-NP achieving the best overall average rank, while training and running roughly an order of magnitude faster than other diffusion methods.\n\n*Also —* Against GCRL baselines (GCSL, WGCSL, AM, GoFAR) and diffusion baselines (Decision Diffuser, g-DQL, BESO), Merlin-NP reaches an average rank near 1.7 on state inputs and around 1.25 on pixel inputs among ten methods; the speed advantage grows even larger with high-dimensional pixel observations.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nAblations over the evaluation time horizon show that conditioning on a time horizon consistently beats not conditioning, and the optimal horizon depends on task difficulty, with easier tasks favoring short horizons (h=1 or h=5) and harder tasks favoring longer ones.\n\n*Also —* The hindsight relabeling ratio and time horizon are the two tuned hyperparameters; HandReach is especially sensitive, performing far better at h=1 than at longer horizons or with no horizon conditioning.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Best overall average rank ~1.7 on state observations and ~1.25 on pixel observations for Merlin-NP among 10 competing methods\n\n*Also —* - Evaluated on 10 goal-conditioned tasks, averaged over 10 seeds, with maximum trajectory length T=50\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nReframing goal-conditioned reinforcement learning as reverse diffusion turns goal-reaching into simple, value-free behavior cloning that matches or beats state-of-the-art methods while being far more computationally efficient.\n\n*Also —* Performing diffusion in the state space with a single denoising step per environment step makes diffusion for reinforcement learning both simple and scalable.","poster_thumb":"posters/018_learning_to_reach_goals_via_diffusion.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/018_learning_to_reach_goals_via_diffusion/018_learning_to_reach_goals_via_diffusion/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/018_learning_to_reach_goals_via_diffusion/018_learning_to_reach_goals_via_diffusion/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/018_learning_to_reach_goals_via_diffusion/018_learning_to_reach_goals_via_diffusion/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/018_learning_to_reach_goals_via_diffusion/018_learning_to_reach_goals_via_diffusion/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/018_learning_to_reach_goals_via_diffusion/018_learning_to_reach_goals_via_diffusion/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/018_learning_to_reach_goals_via_diffusion/018_learning_to_reach_goals_via_diffusion/reel.html"}},{"id":"019","slug":"019_georeasoner_geo_localization_with_reasoning_in_s","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"GeoReasoner: Geo-localization with Reasoning in Street Views using a Large Vision-Language Model","authors":"Ling Li¹, Yu Ye², Yao Zhou³, Bingchuan Jiang⁴, Wei Zeng¹⁵","institutes":"¹The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology (Guangzhou); ²Tongji University; ³Independent Researcher; ⁴Information Engineering University; ⁵The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology","venue":"ICML 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18572","code_url":"https://github.com/lingli1996/GeoReasoner","hook":"The paper (1) presents a new paradigm that pairs an LVLM with human inference knowledge from geo-localization games for geo-localization with reasoning, (2) introduces the concept of locatability and a CLIP-based network to quantify it, and…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nStreet-view geo-localization models are black boxes that predict a location without any reasoning, and the street-view datasets used to train them are polluted with low-quality images that carry no visual clues.\n\n*Also —* Retrieval-based methods depend on hard-to-curate geo-tagged galleries; classification-based methods ignore semantic cues like signboard text and cannot explain their predictions.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nLarge vision-language models excel at joint visual and textual reasoning, and reasoning is known to boost model capability, so an LVLM augmented with human inference knowledge is a natural fit for interpretable geo-localization.\n\n*Also —* Online geo-localization games like GeoGuessr and Tuxun contain rich, human-curated textual clues that encode exactly the domain knowledge a model needs but no prior street-view dataset provides in image-text pair form.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper (1) presents a new paradigm that pairs an LVLM with human inference knowledge from geo-localization games for geo-localization with reasoning, (2) introduces the concept of locatability and a CLIP-based network to quantify it, and (3) proposes GeoReasoner, an LVLM that outperforms existing geo-localization models while providing explanations.\n\n*Also —* Together these yield a curated dataset of over 70K highly locatable geo-tagged street views plus 3K reasoned text-image pairs, and a two-stage fine-tuning recipe (reasoning tuning then location tuning) built on Qwen-VL with stacked LoRA adapters.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nGeoReasoner is built on Qwen-VL with three modules (Vision Encoder, VL Adapter, Pre-trained LLM) and is fine-tuned in two stages with stacked LoRA adapters: reasoning tuning on 3K game-sourced text-image pairs teaches country-level reasoning, then location tuning on 70K high-locatability GSV images teaches fine-grained city-level prediction. Data quality is enforced upstream by a locatability metric that scores each street view via a CLIP-based visual-text pairing network.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\mathrm{locatability}(I_{seg}, w_{loc}) = \\sum_{k=1}^{n} I_{seg}(k)\\cdot w_k^{loc}$\n\n*Also —* The locatability network uses MaskFormer to segment each image into class-area ratios I_seg, uses Sentence-BERT to weight each semantic class by its relevance to geo-localization clues (yielding w_loc), and multiplies-and-sums the two; images above a threshold of 0.4 are kept as high-locatability, filtering over 130K raw GSV images down to 70K.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nThe authors curate two new resources: over 130K geo-tagged GSV images from 72 cities across 48 countries (filtered to 70K high-locatability images at threshold 0.4), and over 3K reasoned text-image clue pairs harvested from the GeoGuessr and Tuxun communities and cleaned with BERT-based NER.\n\n*Also —* Evaluation uses a held-out set of 1K GSV images for country/city accuracy, plus the open Im2GPS and Im2GPS3k Flickr benchmarks for generalizability, where GeoReasoner is fine-tuned on only 10K Flickr images.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nGeoReasoner outperforms the best counterpart LVLM (Qwen-VL) by 25.02% at country-level and 38.61% at city-level geo-localization in F1, reaching country F1 of 0.9033 and city F1 of 0.8585, and slightly surpasses the geo-specialist StreetCLIP (0.8854 / 0.8543) despite training on 70K street views versus StreetCLIP's 1.1 million.\n\n*Also —* Higher proportions of high-locatability training images monotonically improve accuracy, rising from 0.63/0.47 (country/city) with 0% high-locatability data to 0.72/0.51 with 100%, confirming that data quality, not just quantity, drives performance.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nRemoving either fine-tuning stage hurts: starting from the Qwen-VL baseline (country/city F1 0.7225 / 0.5270), reasoning tuning alone reaches 0.8215 / 0.5813 and location tuning alone reaches 0.8766 / 0.8255, but the full two-stage GeoReasoner is best at 0.9033 / 0.8585.\n\n*Also —* Location tuning is the dominant driver of fine-grained city-level accuracy, while reasoning tuning adds a consistent further lift, confirming the two stages are complementary rather than redundant.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- +25.02% country-level and +38.61% city-level F1 over the best counterpart LVLM (Qwen-VL)\n\n*Also —* - 130K+ raw GSV images from 72 cities / 48 countries, filtered to 70K at locatability threshold 0.4\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nCurating high-locatability street views and injecting human reasoning knowledge from geo-games lets a two-stage fine-tuned LVLM both beat prior geo-localization models and explain its predictions, using a fraction of the training data.\n\n*Also —* Data quality plus human-derived reasoning can substitute for sheer data scale, pointing toward interpretable, resource-efficient geo-localization.","poster_thumb":"posters/019_georeasoner_geo_localization_with_reasoning_in_s.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/019_georeasoner_geo_localization_with_reasoning_in_s/019_georeasoner_geo_localization_with_reasoning_in_s/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/019_georeasoner_geo_localization_with_reasoning_in_s/019_georeasoner_geo_localization_with_reasoning_in_s/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/019_georeasoner_geo_localization_with_reasoning_in_s/019_georeasoner_geo_localization_with_reasoning_in_s/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/019_georeasoner_geo_localization_with_reasoning_in_s/019_georeasoner_geo_localization_with_reasoning_in_s/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/019_georeasoner_geo_localization_with_reasoning_in_s/019_georeasoner_geo_localization_with_reasoning_in_s/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/019_georeasoner_geo_localization_with_reasoning_in_s/019_georeasoner_geo_localization_with_reasoning_in_s/reel.html"}},{"id":"020","slug":"020_matrix_compression_via_randomized_low_rank_and_l","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Matrix Compression via Randomized Low Rank and Low Precision Factorization","authors":"Rajarshi Saha¹, Varun Srivastava¹, Mert Pilanci¹","institutes":"¹Stanford University","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11028","code_url":"https://github.com/pilancilab/matrix-compressor","hook":"The paper introduces LPLR, a randomized algorithm that jointly produces a low-rank and low-precision factorization A ≈ LR, derives upper bounds on its approximation error as a function of target rank and bit budget, and demonstrates it on i…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nModern matrices can contain billions of entries, making storage and computation costly, even though such matrices are often approximately low rank.\n\n*Also —* Low-rank approximation alone or low-precision quantization alone each leaves compression on the table; the two structures are rarely exploited jointly with error guarantees.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nCompressing a matrix by both factorizing it into low-rank factors and quantizing those factors can multiply the savings, but naive combinations lose accuracy fast at aggressive bit budgets.\n\n*Also —* Computing a full SVD is O(nd²) and infeasible for very large matrices on current GPUs, so a cheaper randomized route to low-rank-plus-low-precision factors is needed.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces LPLR, a randomized algorithm that jointly produces a low-rank and low-precision factorization A ≈ LR, derives upper bounds on its approximation error as a function of target rank and bit budget, and demonstrates it on images, embeddings, and LLM weights.\n\n*Also —* It also proposes LPLR-SVD (LSVD), an SVD-based variant, and characterizes when direct-SVD quantization versus LPLR is preferable through the row-norm versus entry-bounded regimes (Tables 1 and 2).\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nLPLR sketches the columns of A with a Gaussian matrix S to get an approximate range basis AS, quantizes it to Q(AS), then solves a least-squares projection W* = argmin ‖Q(AS)W − A‖²_F and quantizes W* to Q'(W*), returning L = Q(AS) and R = Q'(W*).\n\n**Key idea.** $W^\\star = \\arg\\min_{W}\\ \\lVert Q(AS)\\,W - A\\rVert_F^2,\\quad L = Q(AS),\\ R = Q'(W^\\star)$ ; error bound $\\mathbb{E}\\,\\lVert LR - A\\rVert_F^2 \\le \\left(1 + \\tfrac{k}{m-k-1}\\right)\\lVert A_k - A\\rVert_F^2 + \\epsilon$ ; equalization $\\mathbb{E}\\,\\lVert \\hat{x} - x\\rVert_2^2 = O(1),\\ \\hat{x} = S^\\top Q(Sx)$\n\n*Also —* Because S is a Gaussian JL embedding, uniform quantization enjoys an equalization property: the vector quantization error of x̂ = SᵀQ(Sx) stays O(1) rather than growing as O(d), which is why LPLR tolerates aggressive bit budgets, even 1-bit sign quantization.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluation spans the Shepp-Logan phantom and other test images, CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 embeddings from MobileNetV3, IMDB and Emotion text embeddings from BERT, and the weight matrices of LlaMa-7b.\n\n*Also —* For embeddings, a k-nearest-neighbor classifier measures downstream accuracy and weighted F1; matrix reconstruction quality is measured by relative Frobenius norm error under matched (parity) bit budgets across methods.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nLPLR compresses matrices to as low as one bit per coordinate while matching or surpassing baselines: on CIFAR-10 embeddings at Bnq = 1 bit it retains 92% accuracy (unquantized 91%) versus 11% for naive quantization, and on LlaMa-7b weights LPLR-SVD achieves 0.537 mean relative Frobenius error versus 0.836 for naive quant.\n\n*Also —* On the Shepp-Logan phantom LPLR shows the least visual distortion at aggressive budgets, preserving fine semantic features (the small ellipses) at an average of ~1 bit per pixel where naive 2-bit quantization visibly degrades.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nSweeping the bit-budget triplet (B, B′, Bnq) at fixed compression ratio (Table 3) shows LPLR and LPLR-SVD steadily lower Frobenius error as the low-rank budget B shrinks and Bnq grows, outperforming naive quant in the low-Bnq regime.\n\n*Also —* Increasing Bnq from 1 to 4 bits moves LPLR from clearly beating direct-SVD and naive quant toward performance parity, isolating the separate contributions of low-rank structure and quantization precision.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- ~1 bit per matrix coordinate compression while maintaining task performance.\n\n*Also —* LPLR computational cost is O(ndm) versus O(nd²) for direct-SVD; error bound scales as (1 + k/(m−k−1))·‖A_k − A‖²_F + ε.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nJointly exploiting low rank and low precision through a randomized Gaussian sketch, LPLR compresses matrices to roughly one bit per entry with provable error bounds, matching or beating standard compression on images, embeddings, and LLM weights at a fraction of the cost of an SVD.\n\n*Also —* The equalization property of Gaussian embeddings is the key enabler, keeping quantization error bounded even at 1-bit precision.","poster_thumb":"posters/020_matrix_compression_via_randomized_low_rank_and_l.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/020_matrix_compression_via_randomized_low_rank_and_l/020_matrix_compression_via_randomized_low_rank_and_l/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/020_matrix_compression_via_randomized_low_rank_and_l/020_matrix_compression_via_randomized_low_rank_and_l/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/020_matrix_compression_via_randomized_low_rank_and_l/020_matrix_compression_via_randomized_low_rank_and_l/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/020_matrix_compression_via_randomized_low_rank_and_l/020_matrix_compression_via_randomized_low_rank_and_l/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/020_matrix_compression_via_randomized_low_rank_and_l/020_matrix_compression_via_randomized_low_rank_and_l/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/020_matrix_compression_via_randomized_low_rank_and_l/020_matrix_compression_via_randomized_low_rank_and_l/reel.html"}},{"id":"021","slug":"021_all_in_a_row_compressed_convolution_networks_for","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"All in a Row: Compressed Convolution Networks for Graphs","authors":"Junshu Sun¹², Shuhui Wang¹³, Xinzhe Han¹², Zhe Xue⁴, Qingming Huang¹²³","institutes":"¹Key Laboratory of Intelligent Information Processing, Institute of Computing Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences; ²School of Computer Science and Technology, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences; ³Peng Cheng Laboratory; ⁴Beijing Key Laboratory of Intelligent Telecommunication Software and Multimedia, School of Computer Science, Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications","venue":"ICML 2023","paper_url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/sun23k.html","code_url":"https://github.com/sunjss/CoCN","hook":"The paper introduces a differentiable graph-regularization method that applies learnable permutations to constrain all nodes into a row with permutation invariance, and builds Compressed Convolution Network (CoCN), which applies CNN-style d…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nConvolutional GNNs built on graph polynomials are weakly expressive under limited parameters and cannot perform hierarchical representation learning without extra node clustering or node-drop pooling.\n\n*Also —* Euclidean CNNs escape both limits via local feature learning and global parameter sharing, but their convolution cannot be applied to irregular, non-grid graph structures.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nGeneralizing Euclidean convolution to graphs is hard: convolution is sensitive to spatial order, yet GNNs must stay permutation invariant, and prior graph-regularization methods are decoupled from the convolution and cannot be optimized per task.\n\n*Also —* Earlier node-sequence-selection schemes produce fixed, less informative regularized graphs because their ordering is independent of the downstream learning objective.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces a differentiable graph-regularization method that applies learnable permutations to constrain all nodes into a row with permutation invariance, and builds Compressed Convolution Network (CoCN), which applies CNN-style diagonal convolution and pooling for end-to-end hierarchical graph representation learning.\n\n*Also —* CoCN is validated across node and graph classification benchmarks, beating competitive convolutional GNNs and graph-pooling models while cutting permutation-modeling cost from O(n!) to O(n²).\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nCoCN has four modules: input, permutation generation, convolution, and output. Permutation generation regresses node positions and converts them into a relaxed, differentiable permutation matrix; diagonal convolution then slides a kernel along the diagonal of the permuted node-feature and edge-feature matrices, while compressed pooling shrinks the structure matrix from the anti-diagonal direction and transposed convolution up-samples for node classification.\n\n**Key idea.** $r_A = \\tilde{A}^{t}\\,\\mathrm{MLP}(X)$ (position regression, with $\\tilde{A}=D^{-1/2}AD^{-1/2}$); $\\hat{P} = \\exp\\!\\big(-\\tau\\,|m\\mathbf{1}^\\top - \\mathbf{1}r^\\top| + n\\big)\\ (\\mathrm{mod}\\ n)$ (relaxed differentiable permutation matrix); $K_i(\\hat{A},\\hat{X},k) = \\sum_{p=0}^{k-1}\\sum_{q=0}^{k-1} w_{p,q}\\hat{A}_{i+p,i+q} + \\sum_{p=0}^{k-1}\\sum_{t=0}^{d-1} v_{p,t}\\hat{X}_{i+p,t}$ (unified diagonal convolution).\n\n*Also —* Positions are computed with an MLP smoothed by the graph Laplacian so that similar or nearby nodes land in adjacent positions; multiple permutation heads generate diverse arrangements, and the learnable permutation reduces permutation modeling from O(n!) to O(n²), enabling mini-batch training on large graphs.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nGraph classification uses six TU datasets — MUTAG, PROTEINS, NCI1, IMDB-BINARY, IMDB-MULTI, COLLAB — and node classification uses six heterophilic datasets — Chameleon, Squirrel, Cornell, Texas, Wisconsin, Actor.\n\n*Also —* The authors additionally evaluate on de-duplicated filtered Chameleon/Squirrel and on three large-scale graphs (questions, amazon-ratings, genius) with mini-batch Cluster-GCN training to test scalability.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn node classification CoCN attains the best average rank of 1.83 over six datasets, topping Chameleon (79.17%), Squirrel (72.95%) and Cornell (86.22%); on graph classification it wins five of six datasets, including COLLAB (86.15%), IMDB-MULTI (56.32%) and NCI1 (82.89%).\n\n*Also —* On the filtered Chameleon and Squirrel datasets CoCN ranks first among 18 models (79.61% / 73.27%), showing it is far less sensitive to node-duplicate leakage than the baselines.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nIncreasing the number of learnable permutations steadily improves accuracy on MUTAG, Chameleon, Cornell and Wisconsin, and enlarging the diagonal-convolution kernel size and receptive field further boosts performance without degradation.\n\n*Also —* A graph-reconstruction test with an autoencoder shows the relaxed permutations cause almost no information loss even at moderate relaxation factors, confirming the regularization is nearly lossless.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Node classification average rank **1.83** across six datasets (best among all baselines).\n\n*Also —* Filtered Chameleon / Squirrel first place out of 18 models (79.61% / 73.27%).\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy learning a task-specific, differentiable permutation that lines up all graph nodes in a row, CoCN brings standard CNN-style diagonal convolution and hierarchical pooling to graphs and achieves state-of-the-art node and graph classification.\n\n*Also —* The permutation is provably convergent and permutation invariant, and its O(n²) cost makes CNN-on-graphs practical at scale.","poster_thumb":"posters/021_all_in_a_row_compressed_convolution_networks_for.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/021_all_in_a_row_compressed_convolution_networks_for/021_all_in_a_row_compressed_convolution_networks_for/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/021_all_in_a_row_compressed_convolution_networks_for/021_all_in_a_row_compressed_convolution_networks_for/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/021_all_in_a_row_compressed_convolution_networks_for/021_all_in_a_row_compressed_convolution_networks_for/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/021_all_in_a_row_compressed_convolution_networks_for/021_all_in_a_row_compressed_convolution_networks_for/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/021_all_in_a_row_compressed_convolution_networks_for/021_all_in_a_row_compressed_convolution_networks_for/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/021_all_in_a_row_compressed_convolution_networks_for/021_all_in_a_row_compressed_convolution_networks_for/reel.html"}},{"id":"022","slug":"022_inverse_approximation_theory_for_nonlinear_recur","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Inverse Approximation Theory for Nonlinear Recurrent Neural Networks","authors":"Zhong Li¹, Shida Wang², Qianxiao Li²³","institutes":"¹Microsoft Research Asia; ²National University of Singapore; ³Institute for Functional Intelligent Materials","venue":"ICLR 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.19190","code_url":"","hook":"The paper (1) generalizes the memory function from linear to nonlinear functional sequences in a numerically queryable form, (2) introduces a notion of stable approximation tied to perturbation continuity, and (3) proves the first Bernstein…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nRecurrent neural networks struggle to learn sequence relationships with long-term memory, but it was unknown whether this is merely an optimization failure or a fundamental limit of the model class.\n\n*Also —* Prior approximation guarantees were forward (Jackson-type) and, for the one existing inverse result, restricted to purely linear RNNs and linear targets.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nIn machine learning most approximation results are forward theorems that bound achievable error; the reverse question, what a target must look like to be efficiently and stably learnable, is under-explored and far more revealing of architectural limits.\n\n*Also —* A prior Bernstein-type result showed linear-RNN-approximable targets must have exponentially decaying memory, but its linear-only scope left open whether nonlinearity removes the limitation.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper (1) generalizes the memory function from linear to nonlinear functional sequences in a numerically queryable form, (2) introduces a notion of stable approximation tied to perturbation continuity, and (3) proves the first Bernstein-type inverse approximation theorem for nonlinear RNNs.\n\n*Also —* It further proposes stable reparameterization as a principled remedy and validates it numerically, including on sentiment analysis and MNIST.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nFor nonlinear functional sequences the authors define a memory function via the derivative of the response to Heaviside inputs, and a stable-approximation framework via the perturbation error under bounded parameter perturbations. Their main theorem shows that if a target is stably approximated by RNNs with uniformly bounded weights and uniformly decaying perturbed memories, the target's memory must decay exponentially. To escape this, they replace the recurrent weight with a stable reparameterization mapping into the negative-real-part matrices.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\mathcal{M}(H)(t) := \\sup_{x\\neq 0}\\frac{1}{|x|_\\infty}\\left|\\frac{d}{dt}H_t(u_x)\\right|$, $\\lim_{t\\to\\infty} e^{\\beta t}\\,\\mathcal{M}(H)(t) = 0,\\ \\ \\beta < \\beta_0$, $g:\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times m}\\to\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times m,-},\\ \\ g(M)=W,\\quad g(M)=-e^{M},\\ \\ g(M)=-\\log(1+e^{M})$\n\n*Also —* The proof uses that stability forces hidden-state derivatives toward zero and applies the Hartman-Grobman theorem to bound the eigenvalues of the recurrent matrices; the analysis extends toward GRU and LSTM.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nClaims are validated on synthetic linear and nonlinear functional targets with exponential vs. polynomial decaying memory, on randomly-initialized RNN teacher-student setups (hidden dimension up to m=256), and on real tasks: IMDB sentiment analysis and MNIST image classification.\n\n*Also —* Perturbation-error curves are swept over hidden dimensions m from 2 to 64 to expose the shifting stability radius.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nThe main theorem proves that any target stably approximated by nonlinear RNNs must have exponentially decaying memory, so nonlinearity does not remove the curse of memory. Numerically, polynomial-memory targets show no stability radius (intersection curves shift left as m grows), and filtering teacher models by stability leaves only those with exponential memory.\n\n*Also —* Targets with slower-than-exponential memory need about 1000 epochs to approximate versus roughly 10 epochs for exponential-memory targets, and even then lack perturbation stability.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nComparing recurrent-weight parameterizations on MNIST, the three stable reparameterizations (softplus, exp, inverse) all outperform the direct/unstable parameterization g(M)=M in optimization speed and test accuracy, while final capacity is comparable.\n\n*Also —* Reparameterization is also demonstrated with exp and softplus maps on linear RNNs for polynomial-decay targets (Figure 4), restoring a continuous limiting perturbation curve.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nOn MNIST (10 epochs, 3 repeats), softplus stable reparameterization reaches 71.36% test accuracy vs. 68.47% for the direct unstable baseline, a gain of about 2.9 points; exp 70.55% and inverse 70.77% also beat direct.\n\n*Also —* Exponential-memory targets are approximated in ~10 epochs vs. ~1000 epochs for polynomial-memory targets; teacher models use hidden dimension m=256.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nNonlinear RNNs can only stably learn targets whose memory decays exponentially, so their trouble with long-term dependencies is a fundamental architectural limit, but a principled stable reparameterization provably and empirically relaxes it.\n\n*Also —* The memory function and stable-approximation framework are general tools that also extend toward GRU and LSTM.","poster_thumb":"posters/022_inverse_approximation_theory_for_nonlinear_recur.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/022_inverse_approximation_theory_for_nonlinear_recur/022_inverse_approximation_theory_for_nonlinear_recur/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/022_inverse_approximation_theory_for_nonlinear_recur/022_inverse_approximation_theory_for_nonlinear_recur/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/022_inverse_approximation_theory_for_nonlinear_recur/022_inverse_approximation_theory_for_nonlinear_recur/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/022_inverse_approximation_theory_for_nonlinear_recur/022_inverse_approximation_theory_for_nonlinear_recur/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/022_inverse_approximation_theory_for_nonlinear_recur/022_inverse_approximation_theory_for_nonlinear_recur/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/022_inverse_approximation_theory_for_nonlinear_recur/022_inverse_approximation_theory_for_nonlinear_recur/reel.html"}},{"id":"023","slug":"023_crop_certifying_robust_policies_for_reinforcemen","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"\"CROP: Certifying Robust Policies for Reinforcement Learning through Functional Smoothing\"","authors":"Fan Wu¹, Linyi Li¹, Zijian Huang¹, Yevgeniy Vorobeychik², Ding Zhao³, Bo Li¹","institutes":"¹University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign; ²Washington University in St. Louis; ³Carnegie Mellon University","venue":"ICLR 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09292","code_url":"","hook":"CROP is the first unified framework to certify RL robustness at both the per-state action level and the cumulative-reward level, with three concrete algorithms and an evaluation of nine robust RL methods across four environments.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nReinforcement learning agents deployed in safety-critical settings are vulnerable to adversarial perturbations of their input states, yet no method could certify their robustness with theoretical guarantees.\n\n*Also —* Prior defenses (adversarial training, smoothness regularization) only offer empirical robustness, which stronger adaptive attacks repeatedly break.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nEmpirical robustness offers no guarantees, so a policy that resists known attacks may still fail against a stronger, unseen adversary; safety-critical RL needs provable certificates.\n\n*Also —* Randomized smoothing gives certified robustness in classification, but Q-learning breaks its assumptions: the output range is unbounded and outputs are not probabilities.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nCROP is the first unified framework to certify RL robustness at both the per-state action level and the cumulative-reward level, with three concrete algorithms and an evaluation of nine robust RL methods across four environments.\n\n*Also —* It contributes a local smoothing algorithm for action certification, a global smoothing algorithm for a reward lower bound, and a novel adaptive local-smoothing search (CROP-LoRe) for tighter reward certificates, plus a public leaderboard.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nFor each state, CROP draws Gaussian noise to smooth the trained Q-function; the smoothed value function is Lipschitz continuous, which yields a certified radius on the action from the margin between the top two smoothed action-values. For reward, global smoothing bounds the expected and percentile reward, and an adaptive local-smoothing tree search certifies an absolute lower bound.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\tilde{Q}^\\pi(s_t,a) := \\mathbb{E}_{\\Delta_t \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,\\sigma^2 I_N)}\\, Q^\\pi(s_t+\\Delta_t, a)$, $r_t = \\frac{\\sigma}{2}\\left(\\Phi^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\tilde{Q}^\\pi(s_t,a_1)-V_{min}}{V_{max}-V_{min}}\\right) - \\Phi^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\tilde{Q}^\\pi(s_t,a_2)-V_{min}}{V_{max}-V_{min}}\\right)\\right)$, $J(\\pi) := \\sum_{t=0}^{\\infty} \\gamma^t R(s_t, \\pi(s_t))$\n\n*Also —* Monte Carlo sampling estimates the smoothed values; the Q-output range [Vmin, Vmax] is pre-estimated on a state subset, and Hoeffding's inequality bounds the top-two smoothed values to certify a positive radius.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nCROP is evaluated on four environments: two high-dimensional Atari games (Pong and Freeway), the low-dimensional CartPole control task, and an autonomous-driving Highway environment.\n\n*Also —* Nine RL methods are certified: StdTrain, GaussAug, AdvTrain, SA-MDP (PGD), SA-MDP (CVX), RadialRL, CARRL, NoisyNet, and GradDQN.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nCROP certifies and ranks the nine methods: RadialRL is consistently the most certifiably robust on Freeway, while SA-MDP (CVX) is most robust on Pong, and the certified rankings largely match empirical robustness observations.\n\n*Also —* All methods exhibit periodic patterns in the per-state certified radius on Pong, tied to \"confident states\" such as when the ball approaches the paddle.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nVarying the smoothing parameter σ shows a trade-off: on Freeway larger σ (up to 1.0) steadily raises certified robustness for SA-MDP and RadialRL, while on Pong a moderate σ around 0.01 to 0.03 is best for almost all methods.\n\n*Also —* Among the reward bounds, the percentile bound Jp is much tighter than the loose expectation bound JE, and the absolute lower bound J shows a zero gap to empirical PGD results over a wide range of attack magnitudes.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- First unified robustness certification framework for RL, covering both action and cumulative-reward levels\n\n*Also —* - Smoothing variance σ certified up to 1.0 on Freeway\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nCROP shows that functional smoothing can give reinforcement learning policies provable robustness certificates, at both the per-state action and cumulative-reward levels, and those certificates are often tight.\n\n*Also —* It establishes a common, provable benchmark for robust RL and an open leaderboard for future methods and environments.","poster_thumb":"posters/023_crop_certifying_robust_policies_for_reinforcemen.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/023_crop_certifying_robust_policies_for_reinforcemen/023_crop_certifying_robust_policies_for_reinforcemen/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/023_crop_certifying_robust_policies_for_reinforcemen/023_crop_certifying_robust_policies_for_reinforcemen/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/023_crop_certifying_robust_policies_for_reinforcemen/023_crop_certifying_robust_policies_for_reinforcemen/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/023_crop_certifying_robust_policies_for_reinforcemen/023_crop_certifying_robust_policies_for_reinforcemen/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/023_crop_certifying_robust_policies_for_reinforcemen/023_crop_certifying_robust_policies_for_reinforcemen/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/023_crop_certifying_robust_policies_for_reinforcemen/023_crop_certifying_robust_policies_for_reinforcemen/reel.html"}},{"id":"024","slug":"024_rna_protein_interaction_classification_via_seque","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"RNA-Protein Interaction Classification via Sequence Embeddings","authors":"Dominika Matus¹, Frederic Runge¹, Jörg K.H. Franke¹, Lars Gerne¹, Michael Uhl¹, Frank Hutter²¹, Rolf Backofen¹","institutes":"¹University of Freiburg; ²ELLIS Institute Tübingen","venue":"ICLR 2024","paper_url":"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.11.08.622607v1","code_url":"","hook":"The paper delivers (1) RNAInterAct, a large curated ncRNA-protein interaction dataset with rigorously homology-separated train/test splits; (2) RPIembeddor, an attention-based classifier built on two foundation-model embeddings that outperf…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nPredicting whether an arbitrary non-coding RNA and protein interact, from sequence alone, is largely unsolved: most methods are protein-specific and need large per-protein interaction datasets that exist for only a few hundred of the ~2,000 human RNA-binding proteins.\n\n*Also —* Experimental assays like SELEX and CLIP-seq are time-consuming and expensive, and RNA structural features that drive interactions are not available at scale.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nA sequence-only classifier that works across diverse RNA and protein types would unlock the vast, unexplored space of RNA-protein interactions without per-protein training data, but meta-learning across interaction types for this task remains largely unexplored.\n\n*Also —* Foundation models trained on massive unlabeled corpora can inject structural and functional priors that are otherwise unavailable for RNAs at scale.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper delivers (1) RNAInterAct, a large curated ncRNA-protein interaction dataset with rigorously homology-separated train/test splits; (2) RPIembeddor, an attention-based classifier built on two foundation-model embeddings that outperforms state-of-the-art methods and generalizes to unseen distributions; and (3) an ablation confirming both embeddings are essential.\n\n*Also —* Train and test sets are split by RNA family to eliminate homology bias, giving a true test of generalization rather than the optimistic estimates from random splits.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nRPIembeddor embeds RNA sequences with RNA-FM and protein sequences with the 150M-parameter ESM-2 (both 640-dimensional), normalizes them through parallel feed-forward layers, then processes them symmetrically in attention-based encoder layers so each modality has equal influence. The latent representations are concatenated, passed through feed-forward layers, and a linear layer with sigmoid activation outputs the interaction probability.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\hat{p} = \\sigma\\big(\\mathbf{W}\\,\\mathbf{h} + b\\big)$  <!-- interaction probability: a linear layer with sigmoid activation over the concatenated latent representation h; trained with a binary (cross-entropy) loss -->\n\n*Also —* The resulting model has only 1.4M parameters and is trained with a binary loss and the AdamW optimizer using linear warm-up plus cosine annealing; ESM-2 is chosen over AlphaFold because it needs no multiple sequence alignments.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nRNAInterAct is built from the RNAInter database (over 47 million RNA interactions), cross-referenced with NCBI, UniProt and Ensembl for sequences and annotated with Rfam families and Pfam clans to generate biologically plausible negatives. It totals 122,217 ncRNA-protein interactions at a 1:2 positive-to-negative ratio, split by RNA family into TRinter (training) and TSfam (test) with no family overlap; models are additionally tested on the external, positives-only RPI2825 set.\n\n*Also —* Curation applies a 1024 nucleotide/amino-acid length cutoff, caps interactions per interactor at 150, and excludes mRNAs. TRinter holds 109,214 interactions over 976 RNA families; TSfam holds 13,003 over 172 families; RPI2825 holds 871 positive interactions.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn the homology-separated TSfam test set RPIembeddor clearly outperforms prior methods, reaching F1 0.586 (±0.010) and accuracy 0.667 (±0.009) with ROC AUC 0.70 (±0.01), versus AUC 0.48 for XRPI and 0.50 for IPMiner — the competitors perform at or below chance.\n\n*Also —* RPIembeddor correctly classifies 2,971 of 4,887 positive and 5,586 of 8,116 negative TSfam interactions, while XRPI collapses to predicting ~91% of pairs as positive despite ~62% being negative.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nReplacing either the RNA-FM or ESM-2 embedding with a random embedding, or both with one-hot encodings, causes the model to collapse into a negative-only classifier: precision, recall and F1 all drop to 0.0, with accuracy stuck at 0.624 (merely the negative class fraction). The full two-embedding model instead reaches F1 0.605 and accuracy 0.678.\n\n*Also —* This shows both foundation-model embeddings are individually necessary — neither structural/functional signal can be dropped or replaced by a simpler representation without breaking the model.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- ROC AUC 0.70 on TSfam, versus 0.48 (XRPI) and 0.50 (IPMiner)\n\n*Also —* Second-best F1 of 0.8 on the external positives-only RPI2825 set, demonstrating cross-distribution generalization.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nFeeding RNA and protein foundation-model embeddings into a small attention-based classifier yields the first strong sequence-only predictor that generalizes across unseen RNA families, and the released RNAInterAct dataset provides the homology-aware benchmark to drive further progress.\n\n*Also —* Both embeddings are indispensable; future work aims to add RNA-structure models and lift the sequence-length limit.","poster_thumb":"posters/024_rna_protein_interaction_classification_via_seque.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/024_rna_protein_interaction_classification_via_seque/024_rna_protein_interaction_classification_via_seque/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/024_rna_protein_interaction_classification_via_seque/024_rna_protein_interaction_classification_via_seque/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/024_rna_protein_interaction_classification_via_seque/024_rna_protein_interaction_classification_via_seque/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/024_rna_protein_interaction_classification_via_seque/024_rna_protein_interaction_classification_via_seque/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/024_rna_protein_interaction_classification_via_seque/024_rna_protein_interaction_classification_via_seque/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/024_rna_protein_interaction_classification_via_seque/024_rna_protein_interaction_classification_via_seque/reel.html"}},{"id":"025","slug":"025_neuroformer_multimodal_and_multitask_generative","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Neuroformer: Multimodal and Multitask Generative Pretraining for Brain Data","authors":"Antonis Antoniades¹, Yiyi Yu¹, Joseph Canzano¹, William Wang¹, Spencer LaVere Smith¹","institutes":"¹University of California, Santa Barbara","venue":"ICLR 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.00136","code_url":"https://github.com/a-antoniades/Neuroformer","hook":"Neuroformer is a multimodal, multitask generative pretrained transformer for neural data that scales linearly with feature size, ingests an arbitrary number of modalities, recovers directed circuit connectivity, and transfers to behavior pr…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nModern systems-neuroscience experiments yield large-scale, multimodal, cellular-resolution spiking data whose scale and heterogeneity outstrip the classical analysis tools built for smaller, single-modality recordings.\n\n*Also —* Experiments now co-acquire neuronal activity, visual stimuli, and behavior (eye position, body movement), but linking circuit dynamics to behavior across these modalities remains hard.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nLarge pretrained transformers transformed vision and language by learning general representations from raw data; the authors argue the same generative, self-supervised recipe can unlock scalable analysis of neural spiking data.\n\n*Also —* Prior spike models are often task-specific, single-modality, or scale poorly with the number of neurons and features, limiting transfer to new downstream tasks.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nNeuroformer is a multimodal, multitask generative pretrained transformer for neural data that scales linearly with feature size, ingests an arbitrary number of modalities, recovers directed circuit connectivity, and transfers to behavior prediction with few-shot fine-tuning.\n\n*Also —* All training is self-supervised and label-free; the paper validates the model on both a ground-truth simulated network and real two-photon imaging data from mouse visual cortex.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nNeuroformer tokenizes each neuron's spikes into Current and Past State windows, optionally encodes video and behavior, aligns the modalities with a multimodal contrastive objective, fuses them via cascading cross-attention, and decodes spikes autoregressively with a causally masked transformer that predicts both which neuron fires and its sub-interval time bin.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\mathcal{L} = \\gamma\\,\\mathcal{L}_{vnc} + \\mu\\,\\mathcal{L}_{ce(I)} + (1-\\gamma-\\mu)\\,\\mathcal{L}_{ce(dt)}$, $\\mathcal{L}_{vna} = \\tfrac{1}{3}\\,\\mathbb{E}_{(F,I,A)\\in d}\\big[H(y^{fi},p^{fi}) + H(y^{ia},p^{ia}) + H(y^{af},p^{af})\\big]$, $p^{mn}_k = \\dfrac{\\exp(s(m,n_k)/\\tau)}{\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\exp(s(m,n_k)/\\tau)}$\n\n*Also —* Cross-attention between the small Current State array and the larger feature arrays reduces attention cost from O(Tf²) to O(Tc·Tf), scaling linearly when Tc≪Tf; inference uses nucleus sampling, and downstream tasks attach a mean-pooled head fine-tuned with cross-entropy or MSE.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nThe model is validated on (1) a simulated spiking neural network with known ground-truth connectivity and (2) real large field-of-view two-photon calcium imaging from mouse visual areas V1 and AL (386 reliable neurons) responding to gratings and naturalistic videos, plus a Visnav virtual-navigation dataset with simultaneous speed/behavior.\n\n*Also —* V1+AL and Visnav Neuroformer models use ~40M and ~100M parameters respectively; 8 layers, 8 heads, embedding dimension 256, 80/20 train/test split, AdamW at learning rate 2e-4.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn ground-truth simulations Neuroformer's attention recovers directed connectivity and hub neurons that Pearson correlation cannot; on real data it predicts population responses more accurately than a GLM (t-test p=0.0196) and decodes mouse behavior with pretrained few-shot models reaching Pearson r up to 0.97.\n\n*Also —* A model pretrained on neural responses and fine-tuned on just 1% of behavior data outperforms a non-pretrained model trained on 10% of the data, evidence of transferable, behaviorally meaningful representations.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nAdding each architectural component (Current State, Past State, Video, Behavior, and the Contrastive objective) consistently improves predictive performance across datasets and brain regions; joint training on neural responses and behavior outperforms training on either alone.\n\n*Also —* The contrastive alignment objective is especially helpful in low-data settings, mitigating over-fitting on variable neural signals.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Behavior prediction Pearson r up to **0.97** (Neuroformer) vs 0.73 for Lasso regression on the same split.\n\n*Also —* 386 reliable neurons imaged across mouse visual areas V1 and AL; three \"hub\" neurons recovered by attention in the simulated ground-truth network.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nTreating neural spiking data as an autoregressive, multimodal generation problem lets a single self-supervised transformer recover circuit connectivity and transfer to behavior decoding with few-shot fine-tuning, bringing the pretraining paradigm to systems neuroscience.\n\n*Also —* Neuroformer paves the way for scaling neuroscience foundation models and potentially connecting them to large language models.","poster_thumb":"posters/025_neuroformer_multimodal_and_multitask_generative.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/025_neuroformer_multimodal_and_multitask_generative/025_neuroformer_multimodal_and_multitask_generative/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/025_neuroformer_multimodal_and_multitask_generative/025_neuroformer_multimodal_and_multitask_generative/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/025_neuroformer_multimodal_and_multitask_generative/025_neuroformer_multimodal_and_multitask_generative/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/025_neuroformer_multimodal_and_multitask_generative/025_neuroformer_multimodal_and_multitask_generative/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/025_neuroformer_multimodal_and_multitask_generative/025_neuroformer_multimodal_and_multitask_generative/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/025_neuroformer_multimodal_and_multitask_generative/025_neuroformer_multimodal_and_multitask_generative/reel.html"}},{"id":"026","slug":"026_deepjoint_robust_survival_modelling_under_clinic","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"DeepJoint: Robust Survival Modelling Under Clinical Presence Shift","authors":"Vincent Jeanselme¹, Glen Martin², Niels Peek², Matthew Sperrin², Brian Tom¹, Jessica Barrett¹","institutes":"¹University of Cambridge (MRC Biostatistics Unit); ²University of Manchester (Health e-Research Centre)","venue":"NeurIPS 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.13481","code_url":"https://github.com/Jeanselme/MultitaskTransportability","hook":"A single multi-task recurrent architecture that jointly models three clinical-presence dimensions, longitudinal values, inter-observation timing, and missingness, alongside the survival outcome, trained end-to-end with a dynamically weighte…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nClinical data are sampled through an informative process, when, which, and how often tests are ordered, yet survival models treat this \"clinical presence\" as noise, hurting accuracy and transportability.\n\n*Also —* Because observation practices vary across sites and time, models fit to one observation regime break when deployed under a shifted one.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nThe same patient population presents differently under different observation processes, so a model that overfits one clinical-presence regime fails when practice changes, a real risk as medicine evolves.\n\n*Also —* The well-documented \"weekend effect\", different testing intensity and mortality for weekend versus weekday admissions, gives a natural, controllable observation-process shift to test robustness.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nA single multi-task recurrent architecture that jointly models three clinical-presence dimensions, longitudinal values, inter-observation timing, and missingness, alongside the survival outcome, trained end-to-end with a dynamically weighted likelihood.\n\n*Also —* Empirical evidence that this joint modelling both improves discrimination and regularises the embedding to be robust under observation-process shift.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nAn LSTM encodes each patient's irregular test sequence into an embedding h. Three MLP heads model the longitudinal (Gaussian), missingness (Bernoulli), and inter-observation timing (cumulative-intensity) processes; a DeepSurv Cox head models survival. All losses are averaged with a dynamic weighting-average scheme and back-propagated jointly.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\ell(e) = (1-\\alpha)\\,\\ell_S + \\alpha \\sum_{\\text{task}\\in\\{L,I,M\\}} w_{\\text{task}}(e)\\,\\ell_{\\text{task}}(e)$\n\n*Also —* Variants: DeepJoint (embedding only), DeepJointFeature (adds clinical-presence features), and DeepJointFineTune (full fine-tune of the survival head after joint pre-training). Loss weights are normalised across tasks by a temperature-controlled Softmax.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nMIMIC-III ICU laboratory tests: 38,597 patients at Beth Israel Deaconess (2001–2012), filtered to 30,834 patients surviving the first 24 hours after admission. Survival is predicted from the embedding at the last observation in that 24-hour window.\n\n*Also —* A 90%–10% patient-level train–test split; evaluation by time-dependent C-index and Brier score at horizons of 1, 7, and 14 days.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nModelling clinical presence gives state-of-the-art discrimination on short horizons, DeepJoint variants top the C-index at 1 day (up to 0.878), beating input-matched baselines including GRU-D. The edge fades at longer horizons as clinical presence chiefly signals short-term instability.\n\n*Also —* Even DeepJoint using only laboratory values (no missingness inputs) beats the \"Ignore\" LSTM and GRU-D, showing the observation process improves the embedding without being fed as an input.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nComparing variants: DeepJointFeature improves over DeepJoint and matches the feature-augmented baseline; DeepJointFineTune adds discriminative power but overfits under shift, while DeepJointFeature stays closest to the diagonal, best robustness with strong discrimination.\n\n*Also —* Baselines span Last, Count, Ignore, Resample, Feature, and GRU-D, isolating the effect of each way of using (or ignoring) clinical presence.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nC-index 0.878 at the 1-day horizon; 30,834 MIMIC-III patients; 3 clinical-presence processes modelled jointly with survival; horizons of 1 / 7 / 14 days.\n\n*Also —* DeepJointFeature is the most robust model under the weekday↔weekend observation-process shift.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nExplicitly modelling how clinical data are observed, not just their values, yields survival models that are both more accurate and far more transportable when clinical practice shifts.\n\n*Also —* Clinical presence should be treated as signal to be modelled, not noise to be ignored.","poster_thumb":"posters/026_deepjoint_robust_survival_modelling_under_clinic.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/026_deepjoint_robust_survival_modelling_under_clinic/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/026_deepjoint_robust_survival_modelling_under_clinic/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/026_deepjoint_robust_survival_modelling_under_clinic/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/026_deepjoint_robust_survival_modelling_under_clinic/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/026_deepjoint_robust_survival_modelling_under_clinic/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/026_deepjoint_robust_survival_modelling_under_clinic/reel.html"}},{"id":"027","slug":"027_fast_kinematics_modeling_for_conjunction_with_le","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Fast kinematics modeling for conjunction with lens image modeling","authors":"Matthew R. Gomer¹, Luca Biggio², Sebastian Ertl³⁴, Han Wang³⁴, Aymeric Galan⁵, Lyne Van de Vyvere¹, Dominique Sluse¹, Georgios Vernardos⁵, Sherry Suyu³⁴⁶","institutes":"¹Université de Liège; ²ETH Zürich; ³Max-Planck-Institut für Astrophysik; ⁴Technische Universität München; ⁵École Polytechnique Fédérale de Lausanne (EPFL); ⁶Academia Sinica Institute of Astronomy and Astrophysics (ASIAA)","venue":"NeurIPS 2022","paper_url":"https://neurips.cc/virtual/2022/57003","code_url":"https://github.com/mattgomer/SKiNN","hook":"The paper introduces SKiNN (Stellar Kinematics Neural Network), the first application of neural-network emulation to the kinematic-modeling piece of a gravitational lens modeling framework, delivering roughly 300× speedup with sub-percent a…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nGalaxy kinematics modeling is the computational bottleneck in joint gravitational lensing + kinematics modeling, because the physics code (JAM) must be recalled for every likelihood evaluation inside an MCMC.\n\n*Also —* Kinematic constraints are required to break lensing mass-sheet degeneracies and recover a precise Hubble constant, but existing joint frameworks remain too expensive to explore both parameter spaces simultaneously.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nSpatially resolved kinematics from telescopes like JWST are now available, but the axisymmetric physics code (JAM) needed to model them self-consistently with elliptical lens mass models is far too slow for joint likelihood sampling.\n\n*Also —* Prior joint-modeling frameworks either fit lens and kinematics separately, were built for galaxy-structure research rather than H0, or expanded JAM but remained computationally expensive.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces SKiNN (Stellar Kinematics Neural Network), the first application of neural-network emulation to the kinematic-modeling piece of a gravitational lens modeling framework, delivering roughly 300× speedup with sub-percent accuracy.\n\n*Also —* By confining the machine-learning component to emulating JAM only, the approach exploits the speed of neural networks while retaining the physical fidelity of the full model, and demonstrates a strategy generalizable to other fields.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nSKiNN is a convolutional network Ψθ mapping an 8-dimensional galaxy-parameter vector to a d×d velocity-dispersion (vrms) image. It has 5 blocks, each with two 2D convolutional layers, upsampling, and ReLU, trained with mean-squared-error loss and the Adam optimizer on JAM-generated data.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\Psi_\\theta : \\mathbb{R}^8 \\to \\mathbb{R}^{d\\times d}$, $\\theta^* = \\arg\\min_\\theta \\mathcal{L}(\\Psi_\\theta, \\mathcal{D}),\\quad \\mathcal{L} = \\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\lVert \\Psi_\\theta(x_i) - y_i \\rVert^2$\n\n*Also —* The 8 inputs describe a Power-law Elliptical Mass Distribution (PEMD) plus elliptical Sérsic light profile (6 shape parameters) together with inclination i and anisotropy β. Training data is built with GLEE + JAM; the final model has about 7.07 million trainable parameters.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nA dataset of 5000 input-output pairs was generated with JAM: 4000 for training, 500 for validation, and 500 for testing. Each vrms image is 551×551 pixels, and a single JAM image takes about 15 seconds to create.\n\n*Also —* Parameter ranges are physically motivated. The network is implemented in PyTorch with PyTorch Lightning and trained on 5 Tesla P100 GPUs (16 GB each) in about one day.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOnce trained, SKiNN generates a vrms image in about 50 ms on a GPU, roughly 300× faster than JAM, while achieving relative error within ±1% for almost all pixels inside the scientifically important innermost two-arcsecond region.\n\n*Also —* Across the 500 test images, the median absolute error averaged over an entire image is about 0.47% and the 90th percentile about 1.1%, both well below the typical ≳2% systematic uncertainty in velocity measurements.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nThe paper is a proof-of-concept and reports no formal ablation table; performance is characterized by the per-pixel error distribution across the 500-image test set (median ~0.47%, 90th percentile ~1.1%) and by contrasting GPU vs CPU inference.\n\n*Also —* GPU inference is roughly 10× faster than CPU; the accuracy analysis distinguishes the constrained innermost two-arcsecond region (±1% error) from outer regions where errors can reach a few percent.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- ~300× faster than JAM (≈50 ms per vrms image on GPU vs ~15 s for JAM image creation)\n\n*Also —* - 8-dimensional input → d×d vrms image; ~7,065,451 trainable parameters\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nA neural network can emulate slow physics-based galaxy kinematics (JAM) at ~300× speed and sub-percent accuracy, making joint lensing + spatially-resolved-kinematics modeling feasible for precise Hubble-constant measurements.\n\n*Also —* The strategy of replacing an expensive-but-calculable model component with a trained neural network while keeping the rest of the physics is broadly transferable to other modeling domains.","poster_thumb":"posters/027_fast_kinematics_modeling_for_conjunction_with_le.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/027_fast_kinematics_modeling_for_conjunction_with_le/027_fast_kinematics_modeling_for_conjunction_with_le/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/027_fast_kinematics_modeling_for_conjunction_with_le/027_fast_kinematics_modeling_for_conjunction_with_le/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/027_fast_kinematics_modeling_for_conjunction_with_le/027_fast_kinematics_modeling_for_conjunction_with_le/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/027_fast_kinematics_modeling_for_conjunction_with_le/027_fast_kinematics_modeling_for_conjunction_with_le/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/027_fast_kinematics_modeling_for_conjunction_with_le/027_fast_kinematics_modeling_for_conjunction_with_le/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/027_fast_kinematics_modeling_for_conjunction_with_le/027_fast_kinematics_modeling_for_conjunction_with_le/reel.html"}},{"id":"028","slug":"028_diving_into_underwater_segment_anything_model_gu","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Diving into Underwater: Segment Anything Model Guided Underwater Salient Instance Segmentation and A Large-scale Dataset","authors":"Shijie Lian¹, Ziyi Zhang², Hua Li¹³, Wenjie Li¹, Laurence Tianruo Yang¹⁴⁵, Sam Kwong⁶, Runmin Cong⁷⁸","institutes":"¹Hainan University; ²The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology (Guangzhou); ³Key Laboratory of New Generation Artificial Intelligence Technology, Southeast University; ⁴Huazhong University of Science and Technology; ⁵St. Francis Xavier University; ⁶Lingnan University; ⁷Shandong University; ⁸Key Laboratory of Machine Intelligence and System Control, Ministry of Education","venue":"ICML 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06039","code_url":"https://github.com/LiamLian0727/USIS10K","hook":"The paper introduces USIS10K, the first large-scale underwater salient instance segmentation dataset (10,632 images, pixel-level annotations, 7 categories), and USIS-SAM, a SAM-based architecture with an Underwater Adaptive ViT encoder and…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nUnderwater salient instance segmentation suffers from low accuracy because complex underwater conditions distort images and no large-scale pixel-level salient dataset exists to train modern models.\n\n*Also —* Segment Anything Model (SAM) generalizes poorly underwater and needs explicit point or box prompts, which are unavailable in an automatic salient-segmentation setting.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nFoundation models like SAM show strong segmentation potential, but without underwater-specific adaptation and salient data they underperform on the domain, so both a dataset and an adapted architecture are needed.\n\n*Also —* Prior salient instance datasets are small and class-agnostic, limiting both training and the study of multi-class underwater saliency.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces USIS10K, the first large-scale underwater salient instance segmentation dataset (10,632 images, pixel-level annotations, 7 categories), and USIS-SAM, a SAM-based architecture with an Underwater Adaptive ViT encoder and an automatic Salient Feature Prompt Generator.\n\n*Also —* USIS-SAM achieves state-of-the-art results on both class-agnostic and multi-class underwater salient instance segmentation, and datasets and code are publicly released.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nUSIS-SAM freezes SAM and adds an Underwater Adaptive ViT (UA-ViT) encoder that injects underwater visual prompts through channel and multi-scale-convolution adapters, plus a Salient Feature Prompt Generator (SFPG) that fuses multi-layer features to automatically produce salient prompt embeddings fed to SAM's mask decoder for end-to-end segmentation.\n\n**Key idea.** $F_i^m = \\sum_{s \\in \\{3,5,7\\}} \\mathrm{Conv}_{s \\times s}(\\mathrm{CA}(F_i))$ ; $F_i^a = \\lambda F_i^m + (1-\\lambda)\\,\\mathrm{Avg}(F_i^m)$ ; $P = \\mathrm{MLP}_{out}(\\sigma(\\mathrm{MLP}_{prompt}(F)))$ ; $L = L_{rpn} + \\sum_i (L_{cls}^i + L_{reg}^i + L_{seg}^i)$\n\n*Also —* The UA-ViT uses a channel adapter and multi-scale convolutions (3×3, 5×5, 7×7) balanced by average residuals; the SFPG's Salient Feature Fusion Module aggregates UA-ViT features so the decoder can focus attention on salient regions.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nUSIS10K contains 10,632 underwater images with pixel-level salient instance annotations across 7 categories, making it the first and largest underwater salient instance segmentation dataset.\n\n*Also —* Images span diverse underwater scenes; the paper analyzes per-category instance counts, instances-per-image distribution, and global/local color contrast versus the prior SIS10K, and reports an average salient instance size of 34,336 pixels.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn USIS10K, USIS-SAM reaches 59.7 mAP class-agnostic and 43.1 mAP multi-class, surpassing every prior method, including a 1.5 mAP gain over the SAM-based RSPrompter and a 0.7 mAP gain over the underwater method WaterMask on the class-agnostic task.\n\n*Also —* In the multi-class setting USIS-SAM leads WaterMask and RSPrompter by 4.4 and 5.1 mAP respectively and beats the SIS baseline OQTR by 3.1 mAP class-agnostic; it also generalizes, improving over prior methods when retrained on the land SIS10K dataset.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nRemoving the Underwater Adaptive ViT encoder drops multi-class performance by 1.6 mAP (43.1 to 41.5), and replacing the Salient Feature Prompt Generator with a Multi-scale Feature Enhancer drops it by 0.9 mAP (43.1 to 42.2), confirming both modules contribute.\n\n*Also —* Further ablations show freezing the image encoder costs about 1.4 mAP, and simplifying the multi-scale convolution reduces performance, validating the specific adapter design choices.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- 10,632 underwater images with pixel-level annotations across 7 categories (USIS10K)\n\n*Also —* Trained 24 epochs on 6 NVIDIA 3090 GPUs with a ViT-H backbone; class-agnostic scores 81.6 AP50 and 67.7 AP75.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy pairing the first large-scale underwater salient dataset (USIS10K) with a SAM adapted through underwater visual prompts and automatic salient prompting, USIS-SAM sets a new state of the art for segmenting salient objects underwater.\n\n*Also —* Both the dataset and code are publicly released, giving the community a foundation for future underwater vision research.","poster_thumb":"posters/028_diving_into_underwater_segment_anything_model_gu.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/028_diving_into_underwater_segment_anything_model_gu/028_diving_into_underwater_segment_anything_model_gu/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/028_diving_into_underwater_segment_anything_model_gu/028_diving_into_underwater_segment_anything_model_gu/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/028_diving_into_underwater_segment_anything_model_gu/028_diving_into_underwater_segment_anything_model_gu/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/028_diving_into_underwater_segment_anything_model_gu/028_diving_into_underwater_segment_anything_model_gu/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/028_diving_into_underwater_segment_anything_model_gu/028_diving_into_underwater_segment_anything_model_gu/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/028_diving_into_underwater_segment_anything_model_gu/028_diving_into_underwater_segment_anything_model_gu/reel.html"}},{"id":"029","slug":"029_musc_zero_shot_industrial_anomaly_classification","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"MuSc: Zero-Shot Industrial Anomaly Classification and Segmentation with Mutual Scoring of the Unlabeled Images","authors":"Xurui Li¹, Ziming Huang¹, Feng Xue³, Yu Zhou¹²","institutes":"¹School of Electronic Information and Communications, Huazhong University of Science and Technology; ²Artificial Intelligence Research Institute, Wuhan JingCe Electronic Group Co., LTD; ³Department of Information Engineering and Computer Science, University of Trento","venue":"ICLR 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.16753","code_url":"https://github.com/xrli-U/MuSc","hook":"(1) A novel training- and prompt-free zero-shot AC/AS method, MuSc, that lets unlabeled test images mutually score each other.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nIndustrial anomaly classification and segmentation usually need many normal reference images (one-class methods) or hand-crafted text prompts (CLIP-based zero-shot methods), both of which limit real-world deployment.\n\n*Also —* In a true zero-shot setting no training data, prompts, or normal references are available, yet defects still must be localized at the pixel level and flagged at the image level.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nThe abundant normal and abnormal cues implicit in the unlabeled test images themselves are ignored by prior methods, yet they are enough to determine anomalies without any labels or prompts.\n\n*Also —* Key observation: a normal image patch finds many similar patches across other unlabeled images, while an abnormal patch finds only a few, giving a discriminative, training-free signal.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\n(1) A novel training- and prompt-free zero-shot AC/AS method, MuSc, that lets unlabeled test images mutually score each other. (2) A Local Neighborhood Aggregation with Multiple Degrees (LNAMD) module and a Mutual Scoring Mechanism (MSM) for patch-level scoring. (3) A Re-scoring with Constrained Image-level Neighborhood (RsCIN) module that refines image-level classification and also boosts existing methods.\n\n*Also —* MuSc requires no training, no prompts, and no normal reference images, and is a plug-in improvement for other AC methods via RsCIN.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nMuSc has three parts. LNAMD aggregates patch tokens from a frozen ViT at multiple degrees r to represent anomalies of different sizes. MSM lets every unlabeled test image assign anomaly scores to every other image's patches (mutual scoring), averaging only the minimum-interval scores to sharpen the normal/abnormal separation. RsCIN then refines the image-level classification score using a constrained neighborhood graph over class tokens with a multi-window mask.\n\n**Key idea.** $a^{m,r}_{i,l}(I_j) = \\min_{n} \\lVert \\hat{p}^{m,r}_{i,l} - \\hat{p}^{n,r}_{j,l} \\rVert_2$ ; $a^{m,r}_{i,l} = \\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{k\\in[1,K]} a^{m,r}_{i,l}(I_k)$ ; $\\hat{C} = \\Big(\\sum_{M_k\\in\\mathcal{M}} D^{-1}(M_k \\odot W)C + C\\Big)/(K+1)$\n\n*Also —* Backbone is a frozen ViT-L/14-336 (OpenAI CLIP), 24 layers in 4 stages; patch tokens from each stage feed LNAMD, and the last-layer class token feeds RsCIN. Inputs are scaled to 518×518; MSM keeps the minimum 30% interval; RsCIN uses multi-window {2,3} on MVTec AD and {8,9} on VisA.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on two standard industrial benchmarks: MVTec AD (15 categories, 10 objects + 5 textures) and VisA (12 objects). Both contain normal and anomalous test images.\n\n*Also —* Classification is scored with AUROC, AP, and F1-max; segmentation with pixel-wise AUROC, F1-max, AP, and Per-Region Overlap (PRO).\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nMuSc sets a new zero-shot state of the art. On MVTec AD it reaches 97.8% image-AUROC and improves segmentation PRO by +21.1% and AP by +21.9% over the second-best zero-shot method. On VisA it gains over +10% in both classification and segmentation versus the second-best zero-shot method.\n\n*Also —* Without any labels, MuSc surpasses most 4-shot methods, beats 32-shot RegAD, is competitive with 8-shot GraphCore, and rivals full-shot methods like CutPaste, IGD, and NSA.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nUsing all three aggregation degrees r∈{1,3,5} in LNAMD gives the best combined AC/AS (small r for small defects on VisA, large r for large defects on MVTec AD). For MSM, averaging the minimum 30% interval (30% + mean) is optimal, reaching 97.8% AC on MVTec AD versus 83.8% for a max strategy. RsCIN lifts VisA image-AUROC from 90.0% to 92.8%.\n\n*Also —* Figure 7 confirms the minimum 30% interval selection gives the best comprehensive AC and AS across both datasets.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- MVTec AD: 97.8% image-AUROC (AC), 97.3% pixel-AUROC (AS), zero-shot.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nUnlabeled industrial test images can score each other to detect anomalies, so MuSc achieves state-of-the-art zero-shot classification and segmentation with no training, no prompts, and no reference images.\n\n*Also —* Because normal patches recur across images while abnormal ones do not, mutual scoring turns a batch of unlabeled test images into its own supervision signal.","poster_thumb":"posters/029_musc_zero_shot_industrial_anomaly_classification.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/029_musc_zero_shot_industrial_anomaly_classification/029_musc_zero_shot_industrial_anomaly_classification/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/029_musc_zero_shot_industrial_anomaly_classification/029_musc_zero_shot_industrial_anomaly_classification/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/029_musc_zero_shot_industrial_anomaly_classification/029_musc_zero_shot_industrial_anomaly_classification/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/029_musc_zero_shot_industrial_anomaly_classification/029_musc_zero_shot_industrial_anomaly_classification/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/029_musc_zero_shot_industrial_anomaly_classification/029_musc_zero_shot_industrial_anomaly_classification/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/029_musc_zero_shot_industrial_anomaly_classification/029_musc_zero_shot_industrial_anomaly_classification/reel.html"}},{"id":"030","slug":"030_input_gradient_space_particle_inference_for_neur","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Input-gradient space particle inference for neural network ensembles","authors":"Trung Trinh¹, Markus Heinonen¹, Luigi Acerbi², Samuel Kaski¹³","institutes":"¹Aalto University, Finland; ²University of Helsinki, Finland; ³University of Manchester, United Kingdom","venue":"ICLR 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.02775","code_url":"https://github.com/AaltoPML/FoRDE","hook":"The paper proposes FoRDE, a particle-based ensemble method that performs repulsion in first-order input-gradient space, and derives a practical input-gradient kernel with a data-driven PCA lengthscale selection scheme.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nDeep ensembles gain accuracy and calibration from functional diversity, but weight-space repulsion is inefficient under over-parameterization and function-space repulsion barely improves over plain deep ensembles.\n\n*Also —* Comparing whole functions requires an intractable function kernel; prior work compared functions only on training inputs, which causes underfitting and no real gains over vanilla deep ensembles.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nA function can be represented up to a translation by its first-order input gradients, which are much smaller than the weight vector, so repelling in input-gradient space guarantees functional difference cheaply.\n\n*Also —* Diversifying input gradients pushes each member to rely on different input features, which is expected to improve robustness of the ensemble under input perturbations.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper proposes FoRDE, a particle-based ensemble method that performs repulsion in first-order input-gradient space, and derives a practical input-gradient kernel with a data-driven PCA lengthscale selection scheme.\n\n*Also —* It shows both theoretically and empirically that input-gradient repulsion increases functional diversity, and that the PCA kernel connects to feature-robustness priors such as EmpCov.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nFoRDE uses Wasserstein gradient descent where each particle update combines a driving force toward the posterior with a repulsion force from a kernel that compares members' normalized true-label input gradients, using an RBF base kernel on the unit sphere with PCA-derived lengthscales.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\theta^{(t+1)} = \\theta^{(t)} + \\eta_t\\left(\\nabla_{\\theta^{(t)}}\\log\\pi(\\theta^{(t)}) - \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{M}\\nabla_{\\theta^{(t)}}k(\\theta^{(t)},\\theta_i^{(t)})}{\\sum_{i=1}^{M}k(\\theta^{(t)},\\theta_i^{(t)})}\\right)$ , $k(\\theta_i,\\theta_j) = \\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\\sim p(x,y)}\\big[\\kappa(\\nabla_x f(x;\\theta_i)_y, \\nabla_x f(x;\\theta_j)_y)\\big]$ , $\\kappa(s,s';\\Sigma) = \\exp\\!\\left(-\\tfrac{1}{2}(s-s')^\\top \\Sigma^{-1}(s-s')\\right),\\; s = \\frac{\\nabla_x f(x;\\theta)_y}{\\lVert\\nabla_x f(x;\\theta)_y\\rVert_2}$\n\n*Also —* The kernel compares only the gradient of the true-label logit (not the full Jacobian) for linear-cost training; a global bandwidth and PCA lengthscales control how strongly each input dimension is repelled.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100 and TinyImageNet image classification, with corrupted variants CIFAR-10-C, CIFAR-100-C and TinyImageNet-C covering 19 corruption types across 5 severity levels, plus 1D/2D toy tasks and a transfer-learning setup.\n\n*Also —* Backbones are ResNet18 for CIFAR, PreActResNet18 for TinyImageNet, and WideResNet16x4 for the ensemble-size study; metrics are accuracy, NLL and expected calibration error (ECE), with corrupted counterparts cA, cNLL and cECE.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nFoRDE with PCA lengthscales outperforms deep ensembles and other repulsive-ensemble and BNN baselines under input corruptions across all metrics, with roughly a +2.4% accuracy gain on CIFAR-10-C and +1.3% on CIFAR-100-C over the second-best method, while staying competitive on clean data.\n\n*Also —* In 1D/2D toy tasks FoRDE captures higher predictive uncertainty outside the training data than baselines, confirming greater functional diversity.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nComparing lengthscale choices, FoRDE-PCA is most robust to corruptions, FoRDE-Identity has the best accuracy on CIFAR-100 and best NLL on CIFAR-10 but is slightly less corruption-robust, and FoRDE-Tuned (a weighted average of the two) does best on both clean and corrupted data in most cases.\n\n*Also —* Under varying ensemble size on WideResNet16x4, a 10-member FoRDE matches or beats the corruption robustness of a 30-member deep ensemble, though it slightly trails deep ensembles on clean data.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- +2.4% accuracy on CIFAR-10-C over the second-best method (FoRDE-PCA)\n\n*Also —* Ensemble size 10 with ResNet18 backbone; input-gradient kernel evaluated with linear cost in the number of training samples.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nRepelling ensemble members in the low-dimensional space of their input gradients guarantees functional diversity cheaply and, with PCA-based lengthscales, produces ensembles that are substantially more robust and better calibrated under input corruptions than deep ensembles.\n\n*Also —* Choosing where to apply repulsion, not just how much, is the key lever: input gradients are the sweet spot between over-parameterized weights and intractable function comparisons.","poster_thumb":"posters/030_input_gradient_space_particle_inference_for_neur.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/030_input_gradient_space_particle_inference_for_neur/030_input_gradient_space_particle_inference_for_neur/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/030_input_gradient_space_particle_inference_for_neur/030_input_gradient_space_particle_inference_for_neur/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/030_input_gradient_space_particle_inference_for_neur/030_input_gradient_space_particle_inference_for_neur/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/030_input_gradient_space_particle_inference_for_neur/030_input_gradient_space_particle_inference_for_neur/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/030_input_gradient_space_particle_inference_for_neur/030_input_gradient_space_particle_inference_for_neur/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/030_input_gradient_space_particle_inference_for_neur/030_input_gradient_space_particle_inference_for_neur/reel.html"}},{"id":"031","slug":"031_a_robust_test_for_the_stationarity_assumption_in","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"A Robust Test for the Stationarity Assumption in Sequential Decision Making","authors":"Jitao Wang¹, Chengchun Shi², Zhenke Wu¹","institutes":"¹Department of Biostatistics, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor; ²Department of Statistics, London School of Economics and Political Science","venue":"ICML 2023","paper_url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/wang23ai/wang23ai.pdf","code_url":"https://github.com/jtwang95/Double_CUSUM_RL","hook":"A model-based doubly robust procedure that tests the stationarity assumption and detects change points in offline RL, controlling type-I error as long as either the transition function or the marginal state-action distribution is correctly…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nMost RL algorithms assume a stationary MDP with time-invariant transition and reward functions, but real offline systems drift over time, so policies learned under stationarity become suboptimal or harmful.\n\n*Also —* Detecting whether and where the dynamics change is essential before trusting any learned policy, yet valid inference is hard in high-dimensional, complex environments.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nNon-stationarity is pervasive in mobile health, traffic signal control, and robotics — e.g. waning intervention effects in the Intern Health Study — where ignoring drift sends inopportune prompts and erodes long-term reward.\n\n*Also —* Existing stationarity tests either need known MDP models (Hadoux et al.), rely on linear approximation that fails in high dimensions (Padakandla et al.; Li et al.), or learn a policy per time step and waste samples when the process is piecewise homogeneous.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nA model-based doubly robust procedure that tests the stationarity assumption and detects change points in offline RL, controlling type-I error as long as either the transition function or the marginal state-action distribution is correctly specified.\n\n*Also —* Theoretical guarantees for size and double robustness under a bidirectional asymptotic framework (either the number of trajectories N or the horizon T may diverge), plus a Gaussian multiplier bootstrap for valid p-values.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nThe test compares pooled transition functions before and after a candidate change point t via a CUSUM-type statistic. To avoid heavy plug-in bias from ML estimators, the authors augment the naive estimator with a mean-zero correction term, yielding a doubly robust estimating function ψ that lets flexible models (neural nets, random forests, lasso) estimate the nuisance transition and state-action distributions.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\hat{\\Gamma} = \\max_{\\epsilon T \\le t \\le (1-\\epsilon)T}\\ \\max_{h}\\ \\sqrt{t(T-t)/T^2}\\,\\hat{S}(t,\\hat{h}),\\qquad \\hat{S}(t,h)=\\hat{\\sigma}(t,h)^{-1}\\frac{1}{|I_2|}\\sum_{i\\in I_2}\\hat{\\psi}_i,\\qquad E\\psi_i=0 \\text{ under } H_0$\n\n*Also —* Sample splitting and cross-fitting normalize the statistic across candidate t and test functions h; a Gaussian multiplier bootstrap over Q samples approximates the null distribution to compute the p-value. Non-pathwise-differentiability from the CUSUM absolute value blocks the classical efficient-influence-function route, so the estimator is built directly from an outcome-regression plus propensity-score pair.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nFour numerical studies — a discrete-state toy example, high-dimensional synthetic data (state dim dS ∈ {1,10,20,30}), a 4×4 grid world, and a batch-online semi-synthetic study — plus a real-world Intern Health Study (IHS) micro-randomized mobile-health trial spanning 21 weeks.\n\n*Also —* Synthetic settings fix N=100, T=50, α=0.05, true change point t_cpt=25; baselines are ODCP (Padakandla et al., 2020) and CUSUM-RL (Li et al., 2022, integral and normalized variants).\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nThe proposed test controls type-I error in every setting and correctly identifies the true change point even when the state dimension dS ≥ 10, whereas CUSUM-RL only succeeds at dS=1 and ODCP fails to control type-I error in high dimensions.\n\n*Also —* In the toy example the test holds nominal size and power as long as either M1 (transition) or M2 (state-action distribution) is correctly specified, with highest power when both are correct.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nVarying model misspecification level λ ∈ {0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7,0.9} over 500 replications shows size stays at the nominal level whenever at least one of the two nuisance models is correct — a direct demonstration of the double robustness property.\n\n*Also —* Sweeping κ (the tested interval length) locates the single change point; correct type-I control persists across all κ ≤ 25 (null true) and power rises for κ > 25 (alternative true).\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Correct change-point detection sustained up to state dimension dS = 30, versus dS = 1 for CUSUM-RL.\n\n*Also —* ε = 0.1 boundary removal; 5000 bootstrap samples (B=1000 for real data); results aggregated over 100–500 replications.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nA doubly robust CUSUM test brings modern ML flexibility and valid statistical inference together to reliably flag when an offline RL environment stops being stationary — even in high dimensions — so policies can be relearned on the right data segment.\n\n*Also —* When homogeneity holds, detecting and adapting to change points recovers near-oracle reward that stationary or sliding-window policies leave on the table.","poster_thumb":"posters/031_a_robust_test_for_the_stationarity_assumption_in.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/031_a_robust_test_for_the_stationarity_assumption_in/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/031_a_robust_test_for_the_stationarity_assumption_in/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/031_a_robust_test_for_the_stationarity_assumption_in/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/031_a_robust_test_for_the_stationarity_assumption_in/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/031_a_robust_test_for_the_stationarity_assumption_in/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/031_a_robust_test_for_the_stationarity_assumption_in/reel.html"}},{"id":"032","slug":"032_infinitygan_towards_infinite_pixel_image_synthes","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"InfinityGAN: Towards Infinite-Pixel Image Synthesis","authors":"Chieh Hubert Lin¹, Hsin-Ying Lee², Yen-Chi Cheng³, Sergey Tulyakov², Ming-Hsuan Yang¹⁴⁵","institutes":"¹UC Merced; ²Snap Inc.; ³Carnegie Mellon University; ⁴Yonsei University; ⁵Google Research","venue":"ICLR 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.03963","code_url":"https://github.com/hubert0527/infinityGAN","hook":"InfinityGAN is a framework for arbitrary-sized image synthesis that disentangles global appearance, local structure, and texture, trains and infers patch-by-patch with constant memory, and enables seamless composition of independently gener…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nGenerating arbitrarily large, even infinite-pixel, images is bottlenecked because existing high-resolution GANs tie computation, memory, and training-data field-of-view directly to the output resolution.\n\n*Also —* State-of-the-art generators cap out around 1024×1024 and cannot extend beyond their training resolution without losing global structure or exhausting memory.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nLarge images should be locally and globally consistent, avoid repetitive patterns, and look realistic, yet prior generators rely on zero-padding for positional cues that break down once the output size differs from training.\n\n*Also —* Because the image border is effectively infinitely far away when synthesizing infinite-pixel images, padding-based position encoding cannot generalize, producing repetitive textures in image centers.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nInfinityGAN is a framework for arbitrary-sized image synthesis that disentangles global appearance, local structure, and texture, trains and infers patch-by-patch with constant memory, and enables seamless composition of independently generated patches.\n\n*Also —* It introduces a coordinate-conditioned neural-implicit structure synthesizer, a padding-free fully-convolutional texture synthesizer, a ScaleInv FID evaluation metric, and unlocks spatial style fusion, multi-modal outpainting, and arbitrary-length inbetweening.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nThe generator G splits into a structure synthesizer G_S, a coordinate-driven neural implicit function producing structural features z_S from a global latent z_g, a local latent z_l, and a coordinate grid c, and a padding-free fully-convolutional texture synthesizer G_T that renders a patch p_c from z_S, z_g, and noise z_n.\n\n**Key idea.** $z_S = G_S(z_g, z_l, c)$ , $p_c = G_T(z_S, z_g, z_n)$ , $\\mathcal{L}_{ar} = \\lVert \\hat{c}_y - \\bar{c}_y \\rVert_1$ , $\\min_G -\\mathcal{L}_{adv} + \\lambda_{ar}\\mathcal{L}_{ar} + \\lambda_{div}\\mathcal{L}_{div} + \\lambda_{path}\\mathcal{L}_{path}$\n\n*Also —* Coordinates use sine/cosine positional encoding horizontally and tanh saturation vertically; a mode-seeking diversity loss prevents repetition, feature unfolding widens the receptive field, and removing all padding guarantees consistent, independently-composable patches with constant O(1) memory.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nArbitrary-size synthesis is evaluated on Flickr-Landscape (450,000 high-quality landscape images); outpainting is evaluated on scenery subsets of Places365 (62,500 images) and Flickr-Scenery (54,710 images).\n\n*Also —* All models train on 101×101 patches cropped from 197×197 real images; outpainting data is split 80/10/10 for train/validation/test.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nInfinityGAN surpasses the strongest baseline beyond 4× extension, reaching 61.41 ScaleInv FID at 4× versus 79.83 for StyleGAN2+NCI and 121.18 versus 189.65 at 8×, while maintaining constant O(1) memory as image size grows.\n\n*Also —* A user study with 50 participants shows over 90% preference for InfinityGAN over every baseline; StyleGAN2 runs out of memory at the 16× setting whereas InfinityGAN composes a 1024×1024 image from 121 seamless independently-synthesized patches.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nRemoving all padding from StyleGAN2+NCI to form StyleGAN2+NCI+PFG destroys positional information and collapses structure quality across all FID settings; adding the implicit structure synthesizer G_S restores it by supplying position through z_S.\n\n*Also —* This confirms the hypothesis that conventional generators depend on zero-padding for position, and that a coordinate-driven implicit function is a valid, extensible replacement.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- 4× ScaleInv FID: 61.41 (InfinityGAN) vs 79.83 (best baseline); 8×: 121.18 vs 189.65\n\n*Also —* Constant O(1) memory at any output size; trained on 101×101 patches on a single GTX TITAN X GPU; 1024×1024 image composed from 121 seamless patches.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy disentangling global appearance, structure, and texture and removing all padding, InfinityGAN synthesizes seamless, globally-consistent images of arbitrary or infinite size from small patches at constant memory, with parallelizable inference.\n\n*Also —* The same design unlocks spatial style fusion, multi-modal outpainting, and arbitrary-length cyclic panorama and inbetweening.","poster_thumb":"posters/032_infinitygan_towards_infinite_pixel_image_synthes.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/032_infinitygan_towards_infinite_pixel_image_synthes/032_infinitygan_towards_infinite_pixel_image_synthes/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/032_infinitygan_towards_infinite_pixel_image_synthes/032_infinitygan_towards_infinite_pixel_image_synthes/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/032_infinitygan_towards_infinite_pixel_image_synthes/032_infinitygan_towards_infinite_pixel_image_synthes/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/032_infinitygan_towards_infinite_pixel_image_synthes/032_infinitygan_towards_infinite_pixel_image_synthes/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/032_infinitygan_towards_infinite_pixel_image_synthes/032_infinitygan_towards_infinite_pixel_image_synthes/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/032_infinitygan_towards_infinite_pixel_image_synthes/032_infinitygan_towards_infinite_pixel_image_synthes/reel.html"}},{"id":"033","slug":"033_parallel_sampling_of_diffusion_models","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Parallel Sampling of Diffusion Models","authors":"Andy Shih¹, Suneel Belkhale¹, Stefano Ermon¹, Dorsa Sadigh¹, Nima Anari¹","institutes":"¹Stanford University","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.16317","code_url":"https://github.com/AndyShih12/paradigms","hook":"The paper introduces ParaDiGMS, the first diffusion sampling method that trades compute for speed by denoising multiple steps in parallel, and it is complementary to existing fast samplers such as DDIM and DPMSolver.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nDiffusion models generate high-quality samples but sample slowly, often requiring up to 1000 sequential denoising steps to produce a single sample.\n\n*Also —* Prior accelerators like DDIM and DPMSolver reduce the number of denoising steps, but fewer steps degrades sample quality, trading quality for speed.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nRather than trading quality for speed by reducing steps, the authors ask whether additional parallel compute can perform the same number of denoising steps in less wall-clock time.\n\n*Also —* Sampling latency, not throughput, is the bottleneck; naive parallelism only raises throughput because denoising proceeds sequentially, so cutting single-sample latency looks hard.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces ParaDiGMS, the first diffusion sampling method that trades compute for speed by denoising multiple steps in parallel, and it is complementary to existing fast samplers such as DDIM and DPMSolver.\n\n*Also —* ParaDiGMS combines with prior methods to form ParaDDPM, ParaDDIM, and ParaDPMSolver, trading both compute and quality for speed, and works with classifier-free guidance.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nParaDiGMS reframes denoising as solving an ODE by Picard iteration: it guesses the entire denoising trajectory, then iteratively refines every timestep in parallel until the fixed-point iteration converges, which empirically needs far fewer iterations than there are steps.\n\n**Key idea.** $x_t^{k+1} = x_0^k + \\int_0^t s(x_u^k, u)\\, du \\;\\approx\\; x_0^k + \\tfrac{1}{T}\\sum_{i=0}^{t-1} s(x_i^k, i/T)$\n\n*Also —* To fit GPU memory it processes a sliding batch window of size p, updating each point from the cumulative drift over the window and sliding forward as soon as the leading timesteps converge; for SDEs the noise is sampled up front so the resulting ODE stays Lipschitz.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluation spans robotics policies (Robosuite Square, PushT, FrankaKitchen) and image generation (StableDiffusion-v2 on COCO2017 captions, and pixel-space LSUN Church/Bedroom).\n\n*Also —* Robotics tasks use DiffusionPolicy models measured by task reward over 200 episodes; image models are measured by CLIP score on ViT-g-14 and FID on 5000 samples.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nParaDiGMS delivers a consistent 2-4x sampling speedup across all tasks and samplers with no measurable loss in task reward, FID, or CLIP score, reaching 0.2s per sample on 100-step DiffusionPolicy and 14.6s on 1000-step StableDiffusion-v2.\n\n*Also —* On robotics, ParaDDPM gives 3.7x (Square), 3.9x (PushT), and 3.4x (FrankaKitchen); on StableDiffusion-v2, ParaDDPM cuts 50.0s to 14.6s (3.4x) while ParaDDIM/ParaDPMSolver reach 4.0x, all at unchanged CLIP score.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nLowering the convergence tolerance trades speed for fidelity; a relaxed tolerance still preserves quality while a tolerance that is too high starts to degrade samples, confirming the tolerance as the key speed-quality knob.\n\n*Also —* On LSUN Church, ParaDDPM matches DDPM FID (12.8 vs 12.9) at 3.9x speedup, while 500-step DDIM alone gives a noticeably worse FID, showing the speedup does not come from simply cutting steps.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n2-4x sampling speedup; StableDiffusion-v2 50.0s to 14.6s (3.4x); LSUN Church FID 12.8 to 12.9 at 3.9x; parallel iterations up to 20x fewer than sequential steps.\n\n*Also —* 100-step DiffusionPolicy reaches 0.2s per sample; ParaDDIM and ParaDPMSolver reach 4.0x on StableDiffusion-v2 at unchanged CLIP score of 31.7-31.9.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy reformulating denoising as parallel Picard iteration, ParaDiGMS trades extra parallel compute for 2-4x lower diffusion sampling latency with no quality loss, and layers on top of existing fast samplers.\n\n*Also —* As GPUs get better at large parallel batches, the wall-clock cost of sampling will be bounded only by the small number of parallel iterations, promising even larger future speedups.","poster_thumb":"posters/033_parallel_sampling_of_diffusion_models.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/033_parallel_sampling_of_diffusion_models/033_parallel_sampling_of_diffusion_models/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/033_parallel_sampling_of_diffusion_models/033_parallel_sampling_of_diffusion_models/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/033_parallel_sampling_of_diffusion_models/033_parallel_sampling_of_diffusion_models/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/033_parallel_sampling_of_diffusion_models/033_parallel_sampling_of_diffusion_models/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/033_parallel_sampling_of_diffusion_models/033_parallel_sampling_of_diffusion_models/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/033_parallel_sampling_of_diffusion_models/033_parallel_sampling_of_diffusion_models/reel.html"}},{"id":"034","slug":"034_mechanistic_mode_connectivity","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Mechanistic Mode Connectivity","authors":"Ekdeep Singh Lubana¹²³, Eric J. Bigelow², Robert P. Dick¹, David Krueger⁴, Hidenori Tanaka²³","institutes":"¹EECS Department, University of Michigan; ²Center for Brain Science, Harvard University; ³Physics & Informatics Laboratories, NTT Research, Inc.; ⁴University of Cambridge","venue":"ICML 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.08422","code_url":"https://github.com/EkdeepSLubana/MMC","hook":"The paper (1) defines mechanistic similarity via shared invariances to interventions on the data-generating process; (2) proves that lack of linear connectivity implies mechanistic dissimilarity; and (3) introduces Connectivity-Based Fine-T…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nPrior mode-connectivity work ignores *what mechanisms* the connected minimizers use, so it cannot tell whether two low-loss solutions rely on the same or different input attributes for prediction.\n\n*Also —* In vision datasets, backgrounds correlate with object categories, so models can succeed via either spurious cues or robust shape features, yet both are equally reachable minimizers.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nIf mechanistically dissimilar minimizers were freely connected, naive fine-tuning could silently preserve a model's reliance on spurious attributes, undermining robustness even when clean data is used.\n\n*Also —* Fine-tuned models often stay linearly connected to their pretraining solution, hinting that fine-tuning may fail to change the underlying mechanism at all.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper (1) defines mechanistic similarity via shared invariances to interventions on the data-generating process; (2) proves that lack of linear connectivity implies mechanistic dissimilarity; and (3) introduces Connectivity-Based Fine-Tuning (CBFT) to deliberately alter a model's mechanisms.\n\n*Also —* It also contributes synthetic cue-augmented benchmarks (CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, Dominoes) with counterfactual test sets for measuring mechanism reliance.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nMechanistic similarity is defined through invariance to unit interventions on latent factors of the data-generating process; the core result links linear loss barriers to differing invariances. CBFT then minimizes cross-entropy on cue-free data plus a barrier loss that raises loss along the linear path to the cue-relying solution, plus an invariance loss aligning representations across counterfactuals.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{CBFT}} = \\mathcal{L}_{CE}(f(D_{NC};\\theta),y) + \\mathcal{L}_B + \\tfrac{1}{K}\\mathcal{L}_I$ where $\\mathcal{L}_B = \\mathbb{E}_{t\\sim\\mathcal{N}_{Tr}}\\,\\big|\\lambda_B - \\mathcal{L}_{CE}(f(D_C;\\gamma_{\\theta\\to\\theta_C}(t)),y)\\big|$ and $\\mathcal{L}_I = \\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\big\\|\\mathbb{E}_{x\\in D_C^k}(f_r(x;\\theta)) - \\mathbb{E}_{\\tilde{x}\\in D_{NC}^k}(f_r(\\tilde{x};\\theta))\\big\\|_2^2$\n\n*Also —* Counterfactual datasets (removing, keeping, randomizing the cue, or randomizing the natural image) probe how much a model relies on natural versus spurious attributes; quadratic paths can still connect dissimilar models while linear paths cannot.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nThree synthetic cue-augmented benchmarks: CIFAR-10 with 3×3 box cues located by label, CIFAR-100 with box cues colored and located by label digits, and Dominoes concatenating CIFAR-10 with class-matched Fashion-MNIST. Cue proportion is varied from 60% to 100%.\n\n*Also —* Each benchmark ships counterfactual test splits, No Cue, With Cue, Randomized Cue, Randomized Image, so reliance on spurious versus natural attributes is directly measurable.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nLack of linear mode connectivity provably and empirically signals mechanistic dissimilarity: ResNet-18 minimizers trained with versus without cues cannot be linearly connected even after permutation, yet quadratic paths connect them easily.\n\n*Also —* Naive fine-tuning keeps models linearly connected to pretraining and preserves cue reliance, whereas CBFT breaks this connectivity and removes it.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nAblating CBFT's two auxiliary losses shows both are essential: the barrier loss $\\mathcal{L}_B$ induces a mechanistically dissimilar model, while the invariance loss $\\mathcal{L}_I$ selects the desired cue-invariant mechanism.\n\n*Also —* Learning rate and cue-correlation strength affect whether large-learning-rate or perfectly correlated cues change linear connectivity after permutation.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nOn CIFAR-10 with 60% cue data, CBFT keeps accuracy nearly constant across No-Cue, With-Cue, and Randomized-Cue tests (74.1 / 71.5 / 73.4%), whereas fine-tuning collapses from 98.7% with cue to 17.5% on randomized cue, evidence of cue reliance.\n\n*Also —* Across CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100 and Dominoes (mean of three seeds), only CBFT drives Randomized-Image accuracy toward chance (e.g. 8.75% on CIFAR-10), confirming it stops predicting from the cue.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nWhether two low-loss models are linearly connected tells you whether they share mechanisms, and you can exploit this to deliberately edit a model's mechanisms and strip out spurious-attribute reliance.\n\n*Also —* Mode connectivity is not just a geometric curiosity; it is a practical lever for robustness and targeted model editing.","poster_thumb":"posters/034_mechanistic_mode_connectivity.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/034_mechanistic_mode_connectivity/034_mechanistic_mode_connectivity/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/034_mechanistic_mode_connectivity/034_mechanistic_mode_connectivity/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/034_mechanistic_mode_connectivity/034_mechanistic_mode_connectivity/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/034_mechanistic_mode_connectivity/034_mechanistic_mode_connectivity/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/034_mechanistic_mode_connectivity/034_mechanistic_mode_connectivity/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/034_mechanistic_mode_connectivity/034_mechanistic_mode_connectivity/reel.html"}},{"id":"035","slug":"035_patchrot_a_self_supervised_technique_for_trainin","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"PatchRot: A Self-Supervised Technique for Training Vision Transformers","authors":"Sachin Chhabra¹, Prabal Bijoy Dutta¹, Hemanth Venkateswara², Baoxin Li¹","institutes":"¹Arizona State University; ²Georgia State University","venue":"NeurIPS 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.15722","code_url":"https://github.com/s-chh/PatchRot","hook":"The paper proposes PatchRot, a self-supervised technique tailored to ViTs that trains the network to predict the rotation angle of both the whole image (via the class token) and every individual patch (via new per-patch MLP heads), learning…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nVision transformers (ViTs) lack the inductive biases of ConvNets and only outperform them when huge labeled datasets are available; labeling data at that scale is expensive and time-consuming.\n\n*Also —* Existing self-supervised pretext tasks (jigsaw, colorization, image-rotation) were designed for ConvNets, which emit a single feature map, and do not exploit the patch-token structure that makes ViTs distinctive.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nViTs process an image as a sequence of patch tokens with self-attention, so they can produce an output for every patch, not just one global output like a ConvNet. A self-supervised task should exploit this to learn both global and local features.\n\n*Also —* RotNet showed that predicting a single image-level rotation angle yields strong ConvNet features; extending rotation prediction to the patch level is a natural fit for a token-based architecture but has not been explored.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper proposes PatchRot, a self-supervised technique tailored to ViTs that trains the network to predict the rotation angle of both the whole image (via the class token) and every individual patch (via new per-patch MLP heads), learning global and local features jointly.\n\n*Also —* It introduces a buffer-gap patch-partitioning trick that prevents the network from cheating on patch rotations through edge continuity, and shows PatchRot outperforms supervised-from-scratch and RotNet across four datasets plus transfer-learning and semi-supervised settings.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nGiven an image, PatchRot either rotates the whole image or rotates each patch by a random multiple of 90° (0/90/180/270) and trains the ViT to predict every rotation angle as a 4-way classification. The class token's MLP head predicts the image rotation (global); new added MLP heads on each patch token predict that patch's rotation (local). After pretraining, the extra heads are discarded and only the class-token head is fine-tuned on the downstream task.\n\n**Key idea.** $R(x;\\iota),\\ \\theta = 90^\\circ\\cdot\\iota,\\ \\iota\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\},\\quad y=\\iota,\\quad N=\\tfrac{H}{P}\\cdot\\tfrac{W}{P}$\n\n*Also —* To stop the model from solving patch rotation via edge continuity, patches are cut from a larger grid of size P+B and randomly cropped, leaving a random 0-to-2B pixel buffer gap. Self-supervised training runs at a reduced resolution and downstream fine-tuning uses the original resolution with interpolated positional embeddings. Image and patch rotations are applied in separate forward passes (not simultaneously), and the two passes share encoder weights.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, FashionMNIST (32×32), and Tiny-ImageNet (64×64), plus SVHN and MNIST for rotation-invariant objects. A compact ViT (6 encoder blocks, 256 embedding dim, 512 expansion) is used with patch size 4 (CIFAR/FashionMNIST) or 8 (Tiny-ImageNet).\n\n*Also —* Buffer B is one quarter of the patch size. Training uses Adam (lr and weight decay 5e-4 and 3e-2), batch size 128 (effective 128×5), 300 self-supervised epochs and 200 supervised epochs. Transfer learning (CIFAR100↔CIFAR10) and semi-supervised CIFAR-10 (250-10000 labels) are also tested.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nPatchRot pretraining consistently beats both supervised-from-scratch and the RotNet baseline on every dataset. On CIFAR-10 (full fine-tuning) it reaches 92.6% top-1 versus 83.9% supervised; on CIFAR-100 it reaches 70.6% top-1 (90.2% top-5) versus 50.2% supervised; on FashionMNIST 94.1% versus 89.8%.\n\n*Also —* Even linear probing (freezing the whole network, MLP head only) approaches supervised accuracy, and fine-tuning just one encoder block plus the head already surpasses supervised-from-scratch. PatchRot features also transfer better than supervised pretraining and give large gains in the low-label semi-supervised regime.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nOn CIFAR-10, removing either signal hurts: predicting only patch rotations (\"No ImageRot\", 91.8%) or only image rotation (\"No PatchRot\", 91.0%) both trail full PatchRot (92.6%), confirming global and local signals are complementary.\n\n*Also —* Rotating image and patches together in one pass (\"Rotate Img & Patch\", 90.7%), training at the original size instead of reduced size (\"Original Size\", 92.1%), and reusing the class-token head instead of new patch heads (\"Reuse MLP head\", 91.1%) all underperform the full method, validating each design choice.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- CIFAR-10 top-1: 92.6% (PatchRot) vs 83.9% (supervised from scratch), +8.7 points\n\n*Also —* - Semi-supervised CIFAR-10 with only 4000 labels: 81.1% (vs 53.7% supervised on same labels)\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nPredicting the rotation of both an image and its individual patches is a simple, ViT-native self-supervised task that learns global and local features and reliably beats supervised-from-scratch and RotNet across datasets, transfer, and low-label settings.\n\n*Also —* The buffer-gap trick and reduced-resolution pretraining are cheap, general components that make patch-level rotation prediction work in practice.","poster_thumb":"posters/035_patchrot_a_self_supervised_technique_for_trainin.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/035_patchrot_a_self_supervised_technique_for_trainin/035_patchrot_a_self_supervised_technique_for_trainin/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/035_patchrot_a_self_supervised_technique_for_trainin/035_patchrot_a_self_supervised_technique_for_trainin/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/035_patchrot_a_self_supervised_technique_for_trainin/035_patchrot_a_self_supervised_technique_for_trainin/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/035_patchrot_a_self_supervised_technique_for_trainin/035_patchrot_a_self_supervised_technique_for_trainin/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/035_patchrot_a_self_supervised_technique_for_trainin/035_patchrot_a_self_supervised_technique_for_trainin/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/035_patchrot_a_self_supervised_technique_for_trainin/035_patchrot_a_self_supervised_technique_for_trainin/reel.html"}},{"id":"036","slug":"036_an_infinite_width_analysis_on_the_jacobian_regul","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"An Infinite-Width Analysis on the Jacobian-Regularised Training of a Neural Network","authors":"Taeyoung Kim¹, Hongseok Yang¹","institutes":"¹School of Computing, KAIST","venue":"ICML 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03386","code_url":"","hook":"The paper (1) proves an MLP and its Jacobian jointly converge to a zero-mean Gaussian process at initialisation and characterises its kernel (the Jacobian NNGP kernel); (2) defines the Jacobian NTK (JNTK), proving it becomes deterministic a…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nInfinite-width theory (NNGP, NTK) explains the training of a network's output, but nothing is known about the limiting behaviour of its input-output Jacobian, or of training that regularises that Jacobian.\n\n*Also —* The Jacobian encodes a network's smoothness and is widely used to measure and enforce robustness to input noise and adversarial attacks.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nJacobian regularisation is a simple and strong defence against adversarial attacks, yet why it works is not understood theoretically.\n\n*Also —* The powerful infinite-width tools that answered analogous questions for standard training had never been extended to the Jacobian or to Jacobian-regularised (\"robust\") training.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper (1) proves an MLP and its Jacobian jointly converge to a zero-mean Gaussian process at initialisation and characterises its kernel (the Jacobian NNGP kernel); (2) defines the Jacobian NTK (JNTK), proving it becomes deterministic at init and stays constant during robust training in the infinite-width limit; (3) derives the linear first-order ODE governing robust training and its analytic kernel-regression solution; (4) validates the theory and analyses the accuracy/robustness of the solution.\n\n*Also —* Proofs use the Tensor-Program framework and its Master theorem; analogous results are shown to hold for gradient descent, not just gradient flow.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nRobust training minimises a mean-squared loss plus a coefficient λ times a penalty on the network's input-output Jacobian at each training point. The authors translate the joint convergence of the MLP and its Jacobian into a tensor program and apply the Master theorem to prove GP convergence, then define the finite JNTK from inner products of the parameter-gradients of the output and of the Jacobian components. Showing the JNTK stays constant in the wide limit linearises the training ODE, whose t→∞ solution is a kernel regressor fntk built from the limiting JNTK Θ rescaled by a diagonal matrix Λ.\n\n**Key idea.** $L(\\theta) := \\frac{1}{2N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\big(f_{d,\\theta}(x^{(i)})-y^{(i)}\\big)^2 + \\lambda \\sum_{\\alpha\\in[d_0]}\\big(J(f_{d,\\theta})(x^{(i)})_{\\alpha-1}\\big)^2$ ; $f_{\\mathrm{ntk}}(x) = \\Theta_\\lambda(x,x^{(1:N)})_0^\\top\\,\\Theta_\\lambda(x^{(1:N)},x^{(1:N)})^{-1}\\,y_{(1:N)}$ ; $\\Theta_\\lambda(x,x') := \\Lambda\\,\\Theta(x,x')\\,\\Lambda,\\ \\ \\Lambda_{00}=1,\\ \\Lambda_{ii}=\\sqrt{\\lambda}$\n\n*Also —* The rescaled kernel is Θλ = Λ Θ Λ with Λ₀₀ = 1 and Λᵢᵢ = √λ; bounding parameter movement uses the 1-, 2-, and ∞-norms (the standard NTK proof needs only the 2-norm) because of the Jacobian regulariser.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nKernel-convergence tests use a synthetic 256-point dataset in R⁴ approximating an ε-net of the sphere S³, over all width-depth combinations in {2⁶,…,2¹³} × {1,2,3}. The constancy-during-training test uses the Algerian forest fire dataset (UCI, 224 points, input dimension 11) treated as a ±1 regression task.\n\n*Also —* Robust and standard training solutions are compared on the same setups; an 11-layer GeLU MLP is used for the kernel-regression robustness study.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nAs MLP width grows, the finite Jacobian NNGP kernel converges to its deterministic limit Σ⁽ᴸ⁾ (Figure 1) and the (1/κ²)-scaled finite JNTK converges to the limiting JNTK Θ (Figure 2), confirming Theorems 3.1 and 4.3. During robust training the JNTK's deviation from Θ shrinks monotonically with width at every training step (Figure 3), supporting the constancy claim of Theorem 4.5.\n\n*Also —* The max-norm error at width 8192 is still above the smallest Gram-matrix eigenvalue, but its steady decrease with width indicates full validation would need wider networks than compute allowed.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nUnder Jacobian regularisation the eigenfeatures of the kernel-regression solution show accuracy and robustness moving together — high test accuracy implies robustness (Figure 4). Standard training shows little to no such correlation (Figure 5), and no highly-accurate-but-fragile eigenfeatures appear, unlike the finite-NTK finding of Tsilivis & Kempe (2022).\n\n*Also —* The smallest eigenvalue (Assumption 4.4) is far smaller for the JNTK than the NTK and needs deeper networks to become positive — depth ≥11 for GeLU versus ≥6 for erf activation (Figure 6).\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nWidths swept from 64 to 8192 (2⁶–2¹³); each experiment repeated 10× with 95% bootstrap confidence intervals; covariance estimates use one million Monte-Carlo samples.\n\n*Also —* Robust-training hyperparameters λ = 0.01, κ = 0.1, learning rate 1; full-rank JNTK (Assumption 4.4) first holds at depth 11 (GeLU) and depth 6 (erf); required width estimate F = O(N²(log N)¹²ᴸ).\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nInfinite-width theory extends to a network's Jacobian: robust (Jacobian-regularised) training of a wide MLP behaves like kernel regression with a Jacobian NTK, and this regularisation makes accuracy and robustness align rather than trade off.\n\n*Also —* The work supplies the first infinite-width characterisation of why Jacobian regularisation improves adversarial robustness, plus practical tests for when its key full-rank assumption holds.","poster_thumb":"posters/036_an_infinite_width_analysis_on_the_jacobian_regul.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/036_an_infinite_width_analysis_on_the_jacobian_regul/036_an_infinite_width_analysis_on_the_jacobian_regul/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/036_an_infinite_width_analysis_on_the_jacobian_regul/036_an_infinite_width_analysis_on_the_jacobian_regul/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/036_an_infinite_width_analysis_on_the_jacobian_regul/036_an_infinite_width_analysis_on_the_jacobian_regul/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/036_an_infinite_width_analysis_on_the_jacobian_regul/036_an_infinite_width_analysis_on_the_jacobian_regul/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/036_an_infinite_width_analysis_on_the_jacobian_regul/036_an_infinite_width_analysis_on_the_jacobian_regul/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/036_an_infinite_width_analysis_on_the_jacobian_regul/036_an_infinite_width_analysis_on_the_jacobian_regul/reel.html"}},{"id":"037","slug":"037_dartformer_finding_the_best_type_of_attention","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"DARTFormer: Finding the Best Type of Attention","authors":"Jason Ross Brown¹, Yiren Zhao²¹, Ilia Shumailov³, Robert D Mullins¹","institutes":"¹University of Cambridge; ²Imperial College London; ³University of Oxford","venue":"","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.00641","code_url":"","hook":"(1) A DARTS-like NAS framework that efficiently finds the best attention for a task; (2) an extension that searches for and builds heterogeneous (multi-attention) Transformers; (3) empirical evidence that heterogeneous Transformers cannot b…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nThere are many efficient Transformer attention mechanisms and no single one is best across tasks, so practitioners lack an efficient way to pick the right attention for a given long-range task.\n\n*Also —* Prior work (Tay et al., Long Range Arena) shows attention performance is highly task-dependent when models are trained from scratch without pretraining, leaving the choice unclear.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nIt is widely hypothesized that different attention heads learn different relational biases, suggesting a mixture of attention types might outperform any single one; this paper tests whether that intuition holds.\n\n*Also —* Trying every attention mechanism (and every combination) by brute-force training is prohibitively expensive, motivating a cheap search-based selection method.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\n(1) A DARTS-like NAS framework that efficiently finds the best attention for a task; (2) an extension that searches for and builds heterogeneous (multi-attention) Transformers; (3) empirical evidence that heterogeneous Transformers cannot beat the best homogeneous Transformer on the tested long-range NLP tasks.\n\n*Also —* Two concrete search procedures are introduced — \"NAS Prune\" (iterative removal of the worst block) and the far cheaper \"NAS One-shot\" (top-scoring blocks in a single pass).\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nA single-layer supernetwork holds one candidate block per efficient attention type. Using a DARTS-style \"fixed α\" scheme, the candidate block outputs are simply averaged (no learnable softmax edge weights and no bi-level optimization), so the supernetwork can be trained to convergence. Each attention type is then scored by its masked validation accuracy drop — how much validation accuracy falls when that block is masked out.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\bar{o} = \\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N} o_i$ (fixed-α supernetwork averages the $N$ candidate attention block outputs instead of softmax-weighting them); block score $s_j = \\mathrm{Acc}_{\\mathrm{val}} - \\mathrm{Acc}_{\\mathrm{val}}^{(-j)}$ (masked validation accuracy drop when block $j$ is removed).\n\n*Also —* For homogeneous selection, the highest-scoring block is picked and used in a full Transformer. For heterogeneous models, blocks are pruned worst-first with fine-tuning (\"NAS Prune\") or the top four are taken directly (\"NAS One-shot\"), then stacked layer-wise. Searching over multi-head attention *blocks* (rather than the bare attention op) lets mechanisms like Reformer keep their own projections.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nThree tasks from Long Range Arena (LRA): byte-level binary text classification on IMDb (1k sequence length), ListOps 10-way classification (2k), and byte-level document matching (4k).\n\n*Also —* Nine candidate attention mechanisms are searched: Bigbird, Linear Transformer, Linformer, Local, Longformer, Performer, Reformer, Sparse Transformer, and Synthesizer. Hyperparameters largely follow LRA; each homogeneous model is trained three times and averaged.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nThe masked-validation-drop search reliably identifies the best homogeneous attention for text classification and ListOps, but every heterogeneous Transformer (NAS Prune and NAS One-shot) fails to beat the best homogeneous model on all three tasks.\n\n*Also —* On ListOps the search is decisive — Reformer, the best model (37.8% accuracy), receives a masked-validation-drop score of 11.85 versus under 0.5 for every other mechanism. On document matching (lower-magnitude scores) the single layer fails to place a true top-3 attention in its top-3 picks.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nComparing NAS Prune against NAS One-shot shows the expensive iterative pruning gives no consistent advantage over the far cheaper one-shot top-4 selection when good attentions are correctly identified.\n\n*Also —* Variants that weight the mixture toward the best homogeneous attentions or add more heads of stronger mechanisms gave no consistent improvement over NAS One-shot.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- ListOps: Reformer masked-validation-drop score = 11.85 vs < 0.5 for all eight other attentions — a clean, decisive selection signal.\n\n*Also —* 9 candidate attention mechanisms searched across 3 LRA tasks with sequence lengths of 1k / 2k / 4k.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nA cheap DARTS-like search with a masked-validation-drop metric reliably finds the single best attention for a task, but combining multiple attention types yields Transformers that beat the average homogeneous model yet never the best one.\n\n*Also —* The finding challenges the assumption that diverse attention biases compound; for these long-range NLP tasks, picking one well-suited attention beats mixing many.","poster_thumb":"posters/037_dartformer_finding_the_best_type_of_attention.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/037_dartformer_finding_the_best_type_of_attention/037_dartformer_finding_the_best_type_of_attention/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/037_dartformer_finding_the_best_type_of_attention/037_dartformer_finding_the_best_type_of_attention/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/037_dartformer_finding_the_best_type_of_attention/037_dartformer_finding_the_best_type_of_attention/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/037_dartformer_finding_the_best_type_of_attention/037_dartformer_finding_the_best_type_of_attention/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/037_dartformer_finding_the_best_type_of_attention/037_dartformer_finding_the_best_type_of_attention/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/037_dartformer_finding_the_best_type_of_attention/037_dartformer_finding_the_best_type_of_attention/reel.html"}},{"id":"038","slug":"038_color_equivariant_convolutional_networks","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Color Equivariant Convolutional Networks","authors":"Attila Lengyel¹, Ombretta Strafforello¹, Robert-Jan Bruintjes¹, Alexander Gielisse¹, Jan van Gemert¹","institutes":"¹Computer Vision Lab, Delft University of Technology","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.19368","code_url":"https://github.com/Attila94/CEConv","hook":"The paper introduces Color Equivariant Convolutions (CEConvs), a novel building block enforcing equivariance to discrete hue shifts that shares shape features across colors while retaining color, and drops into existing architectures such a…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nCNNs exploit color for recognition but fail under train-test color distribution shifts; color invariance fixes robustness only by discarding all color, sacrificing discriminative power.\n\n*Also —* Balanced training data covering every color variation is impossible due to the long tail of real-world appearance, so underrepresented colors are systematically misclassified.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nEquivariance has been extended to many geometric transformations, but photometric transformations like hue shifts remain largely unexplored, despite early CNN layers learning highly color-selective filters.\n\n*Also —* Prior work either applies color invariants as preprocessing or uses quaternion/offset-equivariant networks, none of which share features across colors while retaining color information.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces Color Equivariant Convolutions (CEConvs), a novel building block enforcing equivariance to discrete hue shifts that shares shape features across colors while retaining color, and drops into existing architectures such as ResNets.\n\n*Also —* It also shows CNNs benefit from color yet are not robust to color-based domain shifts, and demonstrates CEConvs improve robustness to train-test color shifts, complementing color augmentations.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nCEConvs define color equivariance as equivariance to the group Hn of discrete 360/n-degree rotations about the [1,1,1] diagonal in RGB space, sharing parameters across hue-transformed copies of each filter and storing color in an extra feature-map dimension.\n\n**Key idea.** $[f \\star \\psi^i](x,k) = \\sum_{y\\in\\mathbb{Z}^2}\\sum_{c=1}^{C^l} f_c(y)\\cdot H_n(k)\\psi_c^i(y-x)$\n\n*Also —* Building on Group Equivariant Convolutions, the input-layer filter is rotated by the hue matrix Hn(k) while hidden layers use cyclically permuted filter copies; hybrid networks apply CEConvs only in early ResNet stages and pool over the color dimension to introduce early color invariance and control the parameter cost.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nTwo controlled MNIST variants (long-tailed ColorMNIST, 30 classes; biased ColorMNIST, 10 classes) plus eight natural-image classification benchmarks: CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, Flowers-102, STL-10, Oxford-IIIT Pet, Caltech-101, Stanford Cars, and ImageNet.\n\n*Also —* Robustness is measured on hue-shifted test sets spanning -180° to 180°; ResNet-18 is used for high-resolution datasets and ResNet-44 for CIFAR, all trained on a single NVIDIA A40 GPU.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn the hue-shifted test sets CE-ResNets are far more robust than vanilla ResNets while matching them on the original data; e.g. on Flowers-102 average hue-shift accuracy rises from 13.41% (baseline) to 33.33% (CEConv), and on CIFAR-100 from 47.01% to 62.11% (CEConv-2).\n\n*Also —* In the long-tailed ColorMNIST toy setting CECNN reaches 91.35±0.40% versus 71.59±0.61% for the vanilla CNN, with the largest gains on the rarest classes; on Flowers-102 the CE-CNN accuracy peaks at -120°, 0°, and 120° hue shifts.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nThree ablations show a) more hue rotations increase test-time hue-shift robustness at a slight capacity cost, b) removing group coset pooling breaks hue invariance, and c) hue-equivariant networks need weaker color-jitter augmentation to match the same robustness and accuracy.\n\n*Also —* Hybrid CE-ResNets using CEConvs in only one or two early stages give the best accuracy-robustness trade-off on most datasets, and color equivariance benefits datasets whose neurons are more color-selective (e.g. Flowers-102, color selectivity 0.70).\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Flowers-102 hue-shifted accuracy: 13.41% (baseline) -> 33.33% (CEConv), roughly 2.5x.\n\n*Also —* Compute overhead is modest: MACs increase by only |Hn|/k² + |Hn| and parameters by |Hn|/k² + 1 thanks to the spatial/pointwise filter decomposition.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy making convolutions equivariant to hue shifts, networks can share shape features across colors and stay robust to color distribution shifts while keeping, not discarding, discriminative color information.\n\n*Also —* CEConvs drop into existing architectures like ResNet, complement color augmentation, and help most on color-selective datasets, with hybrid early-stage variants giving the best trade-off.","poster_thumb":"posters/038_color_equivariant_convolutional_networks.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/038_color_equivariant_convolutional_networks/038_color_equivariant_convolutional_networks/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/038_color_equivariant_convolutional_networks/038_color_equivariant_convolutional_networks/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/038_color_equivariant_convolutional_networks/038_color_equivariant_convolutional_networks/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/038_color_equivariant_convolutional_networks/038_color_equivariant_convolutional_networks/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/038_color_equivariant_convolutional_networks/038_color_equivariant_convolutional_networks/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/038_color_equivariant_convolutional_networks/038_color_equivariant_convolutional_networks/reel.html"}},{"id":"039","slug":"039_langevin_autoencoders_for_learning_deep_latent_v","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Langevin Autoencoders for Learning Deep Latent Variable Models","authors":"Shohei Taniguchi¹, Yusuke Iwasawa¹, Wataru Kumagai¹, Yutaka Matsuo¹","institutes":"¹The University of Tokyo","venue":"NeurIPS 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.07036","code_url":"https://github.com/iShohei220/LAE","hook":"(1) Amortized Langevin Dynamics (ALD), which entirely replaces datapoint-wise MCMC with Langevin updates of an encoder's parameters, proven to keep the true posterior as its stationary distribution; (2) the Langevin Autoencoder (LAE), a dee…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nLangevin-dynamics MCMC can approximate the intractable posteriors of deep latent variable models, but its costly per-datapoint sampling iterations and slow convergence make it impractical for training.\n\n*Also —* Gradient-based maximum-likelihood learning needs an expectation over the posterior p(z|x), which has no closed form and must be Monte-Carlo estimated separately for every data point.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nAmortized variational inference (the VAE) is efficient because an encoder predicts latents for all data, but its accuracy is capped by the tractable variational family; MCMC is flexible yet has never been truly amortized.\n\n*Also —* Prior encoder-plus-MCMC hybrids (e.g. Hoffman 2017) only use the encoder to initialize sampling, so they still pay for datapoint-wise Langevin iterations at train and test time.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\n(1) Amortized Langevin Dynamics (ALD), which entirely replaces datapoint-wise MCMC with Langevin updates of an encoder's parameters, proven to keep the true posterior as its stationary distribution; (2) the Langevin Autoencoder (LAE), a deep latent-variable model realized as a small modification of a traditional autoencoder.\n\n*Also —* Traditional Langevin dynamics is shown to be a special case of ALD, and the standard autoencoder is a special case of the LAE.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nInstead of simulating the latent SDE per datapoint, ALD defines a deterministic encoder f(x;φ) and runs a Langevin SDE on its parameters φ; the encoder's outputs are taken directly as posterior samples. When the encoder has the form f(x;Φ)=Φg(x) with a fixed feature extractor and a trainable last linear layer whose width exceeds the batch size, Theorem 1 guarantees the induced latent samples converge to the true posterior. The LAE trains a decoder by running T ALD steps on the encoder's last layer before each parameter update, with an optional Metropolis-Hastings rejection step to remove discretization error.\n\n**Key idea.** $d\\phi = -\\nabla_\\phi V(\\phi)\\,dt + \\sqrt{2}\\,dB, \\quad V(\\phi) := \\sum_{i=1}^{n} U\\!\\left(x^{(i)}, f_{z|x}(x^{(i)};\\phi);\\theta\\right)$ and the discretized update $q(\\phi' \\mid \\phi) := \\mathcal{N}\\!\\left(\\phi';\\, \\phi - \\eta\\nabla_\\phi V(\\phi),\\, 2\\eta I\\right)$ with encoder form $f_{z|x}(x;\\Phi)=\\Phi\\, g(x)$.\n\n*Also —* ALD amortizes MCMC across all data through the shared encoder, so a warmed-up encoder can also accelerate sampling for new test data.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nToy studies use a conjugate bivariate-Gaussian latent-variable model and a neural-network (neural-likelihood) posterior; image generation is evaluated on MNIST, SVHN, CIFAR-10, and CelebA.\n\n*Also —* All methods share the same fully-connected networks; test performance is measured by the negative evidence lower bound (ELBO) per data dimension over three seeds.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nThe LAE consistently achieves the best (lowest) negative ELBO per dimension on all four image datasets, beating the VAE, VAE-flow, and Hoffman (2017), showing that ALD's more accurate posterior sampling yields better DLVM training.\n\n*Also —* On toy posteriors, ALD captures multimodal, correlated posteriors that mean-field and full VI cannot, matching the ground-truth density.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nThe Metropolis-Hastings rejection step is important for stabilizing training, while the number of ALD iterations T has little effect once T ≥ 2. The encoder's last-layer dimensionality must satisfy d ≥ n (batch size) for samples to converge to the true posterior.\n\n*Also —* With d < n some datapoints' samples collapse to a small region, empirically confirming the rank condition of Theorem 1.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- MNIST negative ELBO/dim: 1.177 (LAE) vs 1.189 (VAE)\n\n*Also —* Only T = 2 ALD iterations are used in the image experiments; LAE and Hoffman (2017) have nearly identical training speed.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nReplacing per-datapoint MCMC with a Langevin update of a shared encoder's parameters gives efficient yet flexible posterior sampling, turning a plain autoencoder into a provably valid MCMC-based generative model that outperforms VAEs on test likelihood.\n\n*Also —* The LAE reframes the traditional autoencoder as a bias-corrected, sampling-based alternative to variational inference.","poster_thumb":"posters/039_langevin_autoencoders_for_learning_deep_latent_v.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/039_langevin_autoencoders_for_learning_deep_latent_v/039_langevin_autoencoders_for_learning_deep_latent_v/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/039_langevin_autoencoders_for_learning_deep_latent_v/039_langevin_autoencoders_for_learning_deep_latent_v/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/039_langevin_autoencoders_for_learning_deep_latent_v/039_langevin_autoencoders_for_learning_deep_latent_v/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/039_langevin_autoencoders_for_learning_deep_latent_v/039_langevin_autoencoders_for_learning_deep_latent_v/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/039_langevin_autoencoders_for_learning_deep_latent_v/039_langevin_autoencoders_for_learning_deep_latent_v/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/039_langevin_autoencoders_for_learning_deep_latent_v/039_langevin_autoencoders_for_learning_deep_latent_v/reel.html"}},{"id":"040","slug":"040_bisimulation_makes_analogies_in_goal_conditioned","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Bisimulation Makes Analogies in Goal-Conditioned Reinforcement Learning","authors":"Philippe Hansen-Estruch¹, Amy Zhang¹², Ashvin Nair¹, Patrick Yin¹, Sergey Levine¹","institutes":"¹University of California, Berkeley; ²Meta AI Research","venue":"ICML 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.13060","code_url":"","hook":"The paper introduces goal-conditioned bisimulation (GCB), a state abstraction that captures functional equivariance across tasks, learned via a metric objective that enables goal specification through latent-space arithmetic over analogies.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nGoal-conditioned RL usually assumes the agent is handed the exact goal configuration, which is often unrealistic since the precise goal state is unknown before the task is attempted.\n\n*Also —* A more scalable interface would let a user provide an example of an analogous task and have the agent infer the corresponding goal for its own current state.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nHumans solve families of tasks, like dicing any vegetable, because their goal representation is invariant to irrelevant factors and equivariant to the factors that differ between analogous tasks.\n\n*Also —* Prior bisimulation and representation-learning methods focus on robustness to distractors for a single task, not on transferring skills across analogous goals.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces goal-conditioned bisimulation (GCB), a state abstraction that captures functional equivariance across tasks, learned via a metric objective that enables goal specification through latent-space arithmetic over analogies.\n\n*Also —* It proves the learned representation is sufficient not only for goal-conditioned tasks but for any downstream task described by a state-only reward function, and validates GCB on simulated manipulation tasks.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nGCB learns two encoders: a paired state-goal encoder φ trained so that ℓ₁ distance matches an on-policy goal-conditioned bisimulation metric, and a state encoder ψ trained so that the difference ψ(g) − ψ(s) equals φ(s, g), which makes goals composable by arithmetic. At test time an analogous pair (sₐ, gₐ) is added to the current state via ψ(s) + φ(sₐ, gₐ), and the nearest neighbor in ψ space yields the inferred goal.\n\n**Key idea.** $L_\\phi = \\Big(\\lVert \\phi(s_i,g_i)-\\phi(s_j,g_j)\\rVert_1 - \\lVert r_i-r_j\\rVert_2 - \\gamma\\lVert \\bar\\phi(s_i',g_i)-\\bar\\phi(s_j',g_j)\\rVert_2\\Big)^2$ ; $\\psi(g_i)-\\psi(s_i)=\\phi(s_i,g_i)$ ; $L_\\psi = \\big\\lVert(\\bar\\phi(s_i,g_i)-\\bar\\phi(g_i,g_i))-(\\psi(g_i)-\\psi(s_i))\\big\\rVert^2$\n\n*Also —* Both encoders are six-layer CNNs mapping to 256-dim latent spaces; the policy π(ψ(s), φ(s, g)) is trained on top of Implicit Q-Learning (IQL) in an offline RL setting, with φ, ψ, and the policy learned concurrently.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nGCB is evaluated in a PyBullet simulated manipulation suite of randomly generated workspaces with 84 object geometries, spanning Drawer, Button-and-Drawer (BD), and Analogy tasks, each also tested with added Video Distractors (VD).\n\n*Also —* The offline setting uses a demonstration policy that reaches the goal in roughly 80% of episodes; success is measured against the true goal over 5 seeds.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nGCB achieves the best final success rate on 5 of 6 task settings, and is the strongest method on every distractor-augmented task, e.g. 0.448 on Drawer+VD, 0.322 on BD+VD, and 0.403 / 0.303 on Analogy / Analogy+VD.\n\n*Also —* On the analogy tasks GCB is the only representation that supports goal inference from an analogous pair, roughly doubling the next-best baseline (CPV) on Analogy (0.403 vs 0.176).\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nAblations show that adding the grounding point φ(gᵢ, gᵢ) as a normalizing constant in ψ's objective improves performance, and that the ℓ₁ metric loss for φ outperforms ℓ₂.\n\n*Also —* Varying the latent dimensionality of φ and ψ does not significantly affect downstream control performance on the Drawer task.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Drawer+VD success: **0.448 ± 0.033** (GCB), best of all methods\n\n*Also —* Analogy+VD: 0.303 ± 0.027 (GCB, best); encoders are six-layer CNNs mapping to 256-dim latent spaces.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy making behaviorally equivalent state-goal pairs map to the same representation, goal-conditioned bisimulation lets an agent specify goals through analogy and latent-space arithmetic, generalizing skills to new, unseen goals.\n\n*Also —* The abstraction is provably sufficient for goal-conditioned control and for any state-only reward task, pointing toward more flexible task interfaces for RL.","poster_thumb":"posters/040_bisimulation_makes_analogies_in_goal_conditioned.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/040_bisimulation_makes_analogies_in_goal_conditioned/040_bisimulation_makes_analogies_in_goal_conditioned/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/040_bisimulation_makes_analogies_in_goal_conditioned/040_bisimulation_makes_analogies_in_goal_conditioned/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/040_bisimulation_makes_analogies_in_goal_conditioned/040_bisimulation_makes_analogies_in_goal_conditioned/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/040_bisimulation_makes_analogies_in_goal_conditioned/040_bisimulation_makes_analogies_in_goal_conditioned/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/040_bisimulation_makes_analogies_in_goal_conditioned/040_bisimulation_makes_analogies_in_goal_conditioned/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/040_bisimulation_makes_analogies_in_goal_conditioned/040_bisimulation_makes_analogies_in_goal_conditioned/reel.html"}},{"id":"041","slug":"041_ds_1000_a_natural_and_reliable_benchmark_for_dat","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"DS-1000: A Natural and Reliable Benchmark for Data Science Code Generation","authors":"Yuhang Lai¹, Chengxi Li¹, Yiming Wang¹², Tianyi Zhang³, Ruiqi Zhong⁴, Luke Zettlemoyer⁵⁶, Scott Wen-tau Yih⁶, Daniel Fried⁷, Sida Wang⁶, Tao Yu¹⁵","institutes":"¹The University of Hong Kong; ²Peking University; ³Stanford University; ⁴UC Berkeley; ⁵University of Washington; ⁶Meta AI; ⁷Carnegie Mellon University","venue":"ICML 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.11501","code_url":"https://ds1000-code-gen.github.io/","hook":"DS-1000 introduces a thousand realistic data science problems over seven libraries with reliable multi-criteria execution-based evaluation and a proactive perturbation strategy that defends against memorization.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nExisting code benchmarks target competition or interview-style puzzles, lack naturalistic data science intent and context, and often rely on unreliable surface-form metrics like BLEU.\n\n*Also —* No prior benchmark combines naturally occurring, diverse-context problems with reliable execution-based evaluation for everyday data science coding.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nReal user problems, such as those on StackOverflow, carry diverse contexts including buggy code, error messages, and input-output examples that prior benchmarks omit, and reliable metrics are needed as models grow stronger.\n\n*Also —* Surface-form metrics increasingly diverge from programmer intent as model capability improves, so execution-based evaluation becomes essential.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nDS-1000 introduces a thousand realistic data science problems over seven libraries with reliable multi-criteria execution-based evaluation and a proactive perturbation strategy that defends against memorization.\n\n*Also —* The authors also benchmark five pre-trained code models across three families, quantify evaluation reliability, and release the benchmark publicly.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nA five-stage pipeline builds each problem: manually select and modify high-quality StackOverflow posts, add code context with insertion markers, implement automatic tests, perturb the original problem to prevent memorization, and red-team the evaluation. Correctness combines execution test cases with surface-form (API/keyword) constraints.\n\n*Also —* Problems use an official insertion (infilling) prompt format with left and right context; surface perturbations keep the reference solution unchanged while semantic perturbations change it, plus difficult rewrites raise the bar.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nDS-1000 has 1000 problems from 451 unique StackOverflow posts spanning seven libraries (NumPy, Pandas, TensorFlow, PyTorch, SciPy, Scikit-learn, Matplotlib), including 152 surface perturbations, 235 semantic perturbations, and 162 difficult rewrites.\n\n*Also —* Each problem averages 1.6 test cases, 140 words, and a 3.6-line reference solution; 19.4% carry surface-form constraints. Its 140 average words far exceed data science datasets like DSP (71.9) and CoNaLa (13.8).\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nThe best model, Codex-002 in Insertion format, reaches only 43.3% average accuracy, and models span 7.4% to 43.3%, showing the benchmark separates capabilities and leaves ample room for improvement.\n\n*Also —* Insertion (infilling) format gives Codex-002 a 4.1% average accuracy gain over Completion format; smaller models like InCoder-6B and CodeGen-6B score under 5% on some libraries.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nOn the numpy-100 probe, Codex-002 accuracy drops from 72.5% to 40.6% after perturbation (50.8% surface, 23.6% semantic), and in 36% of semantic cases the model still emits the original answer, exposing memorization on web-sourced problems.\n\n*Also —* On DS-1000 itself the perturbation drop is milder (3.4% surface, 9.0% semantic), indicating less memorization because these problems are less repeated online than numpy-100.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- 1000 problems across 7 Python data science libraries, from 451 unique StackOverflow posts\n\n*Also —* 0.5% false omission rate; 5.7% problem-level false positive percentage (vs 60% reported on APPS); 19.4% of problems carry surface-form constraints.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nDS-1000 is a realistic, reliably-evaluated, memorization-resistant benchmark on which even the best code model reaches only 43.3%, exposing a large gap in data science code generation.\n\n*Also —* Its execution-based multi-criteria metric and perturbation strategy make it a trustworthy, hard-to-game standard for future models.","poster_thumb":"posters/041_ds_1000_a_natural_and_reliable_benchmark_for_dat.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/041_ds_1000_a_natural_and_reliable_benchmark_for_dat/041_ds_1000_a_natural_and_reliable_benchmark_for_dat/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/041_ds_1000_a_natural_and_reliable_benchmark_for_dat/041_ds_1000_a_natural_and_reliable_benchmark_for_dat/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/041_ds_1000_a_natural_and_reliable_benchmark_for_dat/041_ds_1000_a_natural_and_reliable_benchmark_for_dat/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/041_ds_1000_a_natural_and_reliable_benchmark_for_dat/041_ds_1000_a_natural_and_reliable_benchmark_for_dat/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/041_ds_1000_a_natural_and_reliable_benchmark_for_dat/041_ds_1000_a_natural_and_reliable_benchmark_for_dat/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/041_ds_1000_a_natural_and_reliable_benchmark_for_dat/041_ds_1000_a_natural_and_reliable_benchmark_for_dat/reel.html"}},{"id":"042","slug":"042_videocomposer_compositional_video_synthesis_with","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"\"VideoComposer: Compositional Video Synthesis with Motion Controllability\"","authors":"Xiang Wang¹*, Hangjie Yuan¹*, Shiwei Zhang¹*, Dayou Chen¹*, Jiuniu Wang¹, Yingya Zhang¹, Yujun Shen², Deli Zhao¹, Jingren Zhou¹","institutes":"¹Alibaba Group; ²Ant Group","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.02018","code_url":"https://github.com/damo-vilab/videocomposer","hook":"(1) VideoComposer, a compositional framework that generates video from arbitrary combinations of textual, spatial, and temporal conditions; (2) the use of compressed-video motion vectors as an explicit temporal control signal; (3) the Spati…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nControllable video synthesis is far harder than controllable image synthesis: temporal dynamics vary widely and generated frames must stay temporally consistent, so image-style controls do not transfer directly.\n\n*Also —* Prior progress in customizable image generation gives spatial control, but offers no principled way to steer how content moves and evolves over time.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nTo give users explicit control over motion, the paper introduces motion vectors from compressed video as a temporal condition, providing direct guidance on temporal dynamics that other conditions lack.\n\n*Also —* Feeding many heterogeneous conditions naively breaks cross-frame consistency, motivating a single encoder that captures space-time relations across all sequential inputs.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\n(1) VideoComposer, a compositional framework that generates video from arbitrary combinations of textual, spatial, and temporal conditions; (2) the use of compressed-video motion vectors as an explicit temporal control signal; (3) the Spatio-Temporal Condition encoder (STC-encoder), a unified interface that embeds diverse sequential conditions while improving inter-frame consistency.\n\n*Also —* VideoComposer supports text, single image, single sketch, motion vectors, depth sequences, mask sequences, sketch sequences, and style, and can even animate simple hand-crafted strokes.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nVideoComposer is built on a Video Latent Diffusion Model. Each video is decomposed into textual, spatial, and temporal conditions; sequential conditions pass through the STC-encoder (two 2D convolutions, average pooling, then a temporal Transformer), are fused by element-wise addition, and concatenated with the noisy latent along the channel dimension, while text and style are injected via cross-attention. A two-stage strategy first pre-trains text-to-video, then trains compositionally; DDIM with classifier-free guidance is used at inference.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\mathcal{L}_{VLDM} = \\mathbb{E}_{\\mathcal{E}(x),\\,\\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,1),\\,c,\\,t}\\big[\\lVert \\epsilon - \\epsilon_\\theta(z_t, c, t)\\rVert_2^2\\big]$\n\n*Also —* Motion vectors are extracted in standard formats to encode inter-frame variation; single-image and single-sketch spatial conditions are repeated along time to align with the temporal conditions before fusion.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nTraining uses two public datasets: WebVid10M (10.3M video-caption pairs scraped from the web) and LAION-400M (a CLIP-filtered image-caption dataset). Text-to-video quality is evaluated on the MSR-VTT benchmark.\n\n*Also —* Motion controllability is measured on 1000 randomly selected caption-video pairs using a dedicated motion-control error metric, with FVD and CLIPSIM for generation quality.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn MSR-VTT, VideoComposer reaches an FVD of 580 and a CLIPSIM of 0.2932 in the zero-shot setting, competitive with state-of-the-art text-to-video methods and better than its own first-stage text-to-video pre-training (FVD 803), showing compositional training does not sacrifice text-to-video quality.\n\n*Also —* Qualitatively, VideoComposer produces videos that faithfully follow supplied sketches, depth sequences, motion vectors, masks, and reference styles, and can animate two simple strokes into coherent motion and shape.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nOn motion controllability, adding motion vectors as a condition lowers the motion-control error from 4.03 (text only) to 2.67, and the STC-encoder lowers it further to 2.18, confirming that both the motion-vector signal and the STC-encoder contribute to temporal control.\n\n*Also —* The qualitative ablation shows that removing the STC-encoder degrades adherence to the specified temporal structure and inter-frame consistency.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- MSR-VTT text-to-video: **FVD 580**, **CLIPSIM 0.2932** (zero-shot), vs first-stage pre-training FVD 803.\n\n*Also —* Supports 8+ condition types (text, single image, single sketch, motion vectors, depth sequence, mask sequence, sketch sequence, style) that can be composed arbitrarily.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy treating a video as a composition of textual, spatial, and temporal conditions, and by pairing compressed-video motion vectors with a unified STC-encoder, VideoComposer delivers flexible, controllable video synthesis with strong inter-frame consistency and no loss in text-to-video quality.\n\n*Also —* The same model can be driven by text, sketches, depth, masks, reference video, or even two hand-drawn strokes, making motion an explicit, user-editable dimension of video generation.","poster_thumb":"posters/042_videocomposer_compositional_video_synthesis_with.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/042_videocomposer_compositional_video_synthesis_with/042_videocomposer_compositional_video_synthesis_with/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/042_videocomposer_compositional_video_synthesis_with/042_videocomposer_compositional_video_synthesis_with/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/042_videocomposer_compositional_video_synthesis_with/042_videocomposer_compositional_video_synthesis_with/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/042_videocomposer_compositional_video_synthesis_with/042_videocomposer_compositional_video_synthesis_with/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/042_videocomposer_compositional_video_synthesis_with/042_videocomposer_compositional_video_synthesis_with/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/042_videocomposer_compositional_video_synthesis_with/042_videocomposer_compositional_video_synthesis_with/reel.html"}},{"id":"043","slug":"043_quantifying_network_similarity_using_graph_cumul","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Quantifying Network Similarity using Graph Cumulants","authors":"Gecia Bravo-Hermsdorff¹, Lee M. Gunderson¹, Pierre-André Maugis², Carey E. Priebe³","institutes":"¹University College London; ²Google Research; ³Johns Hopkins University","venue":"JMLR 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.11403","code_url":"","hook":"The paper introduces a simple two-sample test for networks built on graph cumulants and shows, via theory, simulation, and real data, that it has substantially greater statistical power than the analogous graph-moment test at identical comp…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nGiven two samples of graphs drawn i.i.d. from unknown distributions, decide whether the two distributions differ, using only statistics of their exchangeable, unlabeled edges.\n\n*Also —* Nodes are indistinguishable, so tests cannot exploit a node-to-node correspondence; only subgraph-count statistics of pairwise interactions are available.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nNetwork analysis routinely characterizes structure with subgraph or motif densities, but these raw densities are strongly correlated across orders and yield statistically weak comparisons, especially with few observed graphs.\n\n*Also —* Prior tests often require expensive sampling from fitted models (configuration, exponential-random-graph, geometric models) to assess significance; this work computes significance analytically instead.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces a simple two-sample test for networks built on graph cumulants and shows, via theory, simulation, and real data, that it has substantially greater statistical power than the analogous graph-moment test at identical computational complexity.\n\n*Also —* It demonstrates that the cumulant test statistic closely follows a known chi-squared null distribution even for very few graphs per sample, enabling analytic control of false-positive rates, and that it remains applicable even when only a single graph is observed per sample.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nBoth tests convert subgraph counts into a vector of statistics (graph moments, i.e. injective-homomorphism subgraph densities, versus graph cumulants) and measure the difference between two samples with the squared Mahalanobis distance using analytically computed covariance matrices. Graph cumulants are obtained from graph moments through a combinatorial Möbius transform over partitions of a subgraph's edges that respects connectivity, quantifying the \"excess propensity\" of a substructure beyond what smaller substructures predict.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\mu_g(\\mathcal{G}) = \\sum_{\\pi \\in P_{E(g)}} \\prod_{b \\in \\pi} \\kappa_{g_b}(\\mathcal{G})$ and the test statistic $\\hat{d}^2_\\kappa(\\mathbf{G}_A,\\mathbf{G}_B) = (\\hat{\\kappa}_A - \\hat{\\kappa}_B)^\\top \\big(\\hat{\\Sigma}^{(\\kappa)}_A + \\hat{\\Sigma}^{(\\kappa)}_B\\big)^{-1} (\\hat{\\kappa}_A - \\hat{\\kappa}_B)$\n\n*Also —* The authors use connected subgraphs with at most r = 3 edges; computing the covariances requires counting connected subgraphs with up to 2r edges, giving both tests the same complexity, dominated by O(n^ω) matrix operations. Significance is computed analytically, with no sampling from inferred models.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nSynthetic experiments use pairs of two-community Stochastic Block Models, one heterogeneous (parameter ε_h) and one assortative (parameter ε_a), matched in edge density, with n = 128 and n = 256 nodes. Real experiments use genetic-interaction networks from the FunCoup repository (Persson et al., 2021).\n\n*Also —* Real networks compared include Arabidopsis (~1.3×10⁴ nodes, 7.9×10⁵ edges) versus Mouse (~1.4×10⁴ nodes, 9.2×10⁵ edges), and Human (~1.6×10⁴ nodes, 13.9×10⁵ edges) versus Rat (~1.1×10⁴ nodes, 9.0×10⁵ edges), each adjusted to equal edge density so that low-order tests cannot trivially separate them.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nAcross synthetic SBM comparisons, the cumulant test achieves consistently higher AUC than the moment test, with the largest gains when the number of graphs per sample s is small; the moment test fails entirely for s < 4 (singular covariance), whereas the cumulant test works even for s = 1.\n\n*Also —* On real genetic-interaction networks with matched edge density, including more subgraphs (r = 2 then r = 3) steadily improves the cumulant test but causes the moment test to overfit and degrade.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nVarying the maximum subgraph order r shows the divergence clearly: at r = 1 neither test can separate the density-matched real networks; at r = 2 cumulants already outperform moments; at r = 3 cumulants keep improving while moments perform worse.\n\n*Also —* Sweeping the SBM parameters ε_h and ε_a and the sample size s confirms the qualitative advantage of cumulants is robust across regimes.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nMoment test requires s ≥ 4 graphs per sample to be defined; cumulant test works down to s = 1. Both tests use connected subgraphs up to r = 3 edges at identical O(n^ω) complexity. The cumulant test statistic closely matches a χ² distribution (5 degrees of freedom) even for small s, while the moment statistic deviates.\n\n*Also —* Real-network experiments span organisms with ~1.1×10⁴ to ~1.6×10⁴ nodes and ~7.9×10⁵ to ~1.39×10⁶ edges, all density-matched.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nWhen comparing networks with subgraph or motif densities, convert them to graph cumulants: you gain substantially more statistical power, an accurate analytic χ² null, and the ability to test even a single observed graph, all at no extra computational cost.","poster_thumb":"posters/043_quantifying_network_similarity_using_graph_cumul.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/043_quantifying_network_similarity_using_graph_cumul/043_quantifying_network_similarity_using_graph_cumul/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/043_quantifying_network_similarity_using_graph_cumul/043_quantifying_network_similarity_using_graph_cumul/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/043_quantifying_network_similarity_using_graph_cumul/043_quantifying_network_similarity_using_graph_cumul/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/043_quantifying_network_similarity_using_graph_cumul/043_quantifying_network_similarity_using_graph_cumul/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/043_quantifying_network_similarity_using_graph_cumul/043_quantifying_network_similarity_using_graph_cumul/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/043_quantifying_network_similarity_using_graph_cumul/043_quantifying_network_similarity_using_graph_cumul/reel.html"}},{"id":"044","slug":"044_self_supervised_detection_of_atmospheric_phenome","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Self-supervised detection of atmospheric phenomena from remotely sensed synthetic aperture radar imagery","authors":"Yannik Glaser¹, Peter Sadowski¹, Justin E. Stopa²","institutes":"¹Information and Computer Sciences, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa; ²Ocean Engineering Department, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa","venue":"NeurIPS 2022","paper_url":"https://ml4physicalsciences.github.io/2022/files/NeurIPS_ML4PS_2022_158.pdf","code_url":"","hook":"The authors compile a new unbiased, multi-label, expert-annotated SAR dataset, apply the SwAV contrastive framework to three million unlabeled Sentinel-1 images, and benchmark self-supervised embeddings against ImageNet transfer learning an…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nMachine learning on Sentinel-1 SAR ocean imagery is bottlenecked by sparse expert labels, and prior classifiers used biased, single-label datasets that misrepresent the true image population.\n\n*Also —* Labeling SAR vignettes requires trained experts, so datasets beyond a few thousand images are infeasible, limiting supervised deep learning for atmospheric phenomena detection.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nSelf-supervised contrastive learning can exploit vast unlabeled imagery to build reusable embeddings, and has never been widely tested on remote-sensing SAR data despite its success on natural and medical images.\n\n*Also —* Sentinel-1 collects nearly 120,000 Wave-mode images every month, an enormous unlabeled resource that contrastive learning could turn into a strong SAR representation.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe authors compile a new unbiased, multi-label, expert-annotated SAR dataset, apply the SwAV contrastive framework to three million unlabeled Sentinel-1 images, and benchmark self-supervised embeddings against ImageNet transfer learning and the prior CmWV model.\n\n*Also —* They evaluate the learned representation under three protocols (weighted kNN, linear evaluation, and full fine-tuning) to isolate the value of self-supervised pretraining.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nA ResNet-50 backbone is pretrained with SwAV, a clustering-based contrastive framework that predicts swapped cluster assignments between augmented views and works with modest batch sizes via a feature queue. The embedding is then evaluated by weighted kNN, linear probing, and end-to-end fine-tuning on the labeled stability-classification task.\n\n*Also —* SwAV was trained with batch size 1024 across eight NVIDIA V100 32GB GPUs, a queue of 16 batches, 1000 cluster centroids, for 65 epochs over about 10 days. Fine-tuning ran on a single V100 with batch size 128.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nThe unlabeled pretraining set holds 2,943,550 Sentinel-1 A/B Wave-mode images from 2017-2019 (each 20 km × 20 km at 5 m resolution). The labeled benchmark has 2,300 expert-consensus, multi-label vignettes over four classes, MC, WS, NV, and OT, split 60/20/20 for train, validation, and test.\n\n*Also —* Images are freely available from ESA's Sentinel Open Access Hub with no licensing restrictions; unlabeled images are zero-padded to 450×450 pixels with no further preprocessing.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nFine-tuned models achieve a best micro-averaged AUROC of 0.93, and both the contrastive and ImageNet models dramatically outperform the previous CmWV classifier on the wind-streak and convection-cell classes.\n\n*Also —* On WS, fine-tuned SwAV reaches 0.831 and ImageNet 0.850 versus CmWV's 0.727; on MC, 0.872 and 0.873 versus 0.793; the three models tie on NV around 0.95.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nComparing evaluation protocols, contrastive weights beat ImageNet weights slightly under kNN (0.864 vs 0.859) and linear evaluation (0.841 vs 0.836), but this reverses under full fine-tuning, where ImageNet edges ahead (0.931 vs 0.929).\n\n*Also —* The advantage of self-supervised pretraining only appears when the backbone is frozen; once all weights are fine-tuned, the initialization barely matters.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- 0.93 best micro-averaged AUROC (fine-tuned model)\n\n*Also —* SwAV trained 65 epochs / ~10 days on 8× V100 GPUs; images 20×20 km at 5 m resolution.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nSelf-supervised contrastive pretraining on massive unlabeled SAR imagery matches but does not beat plain ImageNet transfer learning here, though both yield a large jump over the prior state of the art for detecting ocean-surface atmospheric phenomena.\n\n*Also —* The authors caution these are preliminary results; longer training, better hyperparameters, and remote-sensing-specific pretext tasks may yet unlock self-supervised gains.","poster_thumb":"posters/044_self_supervised_detection_of_atmospheric_phenome.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/044_self_supervised_detection_of_atmospheric_phenome/044_self_supervised_detection_of_atmospheric_phenome/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/044_self_supervised_detection_of_atmospheric_phenome/044_self_supervised_detection_of_atmospheric_phenome/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/044_self_supervised_detection_of_atmospheric_phenome/044_self_supervised_detection_of_atmospheric_phenome/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/044_self_supervised_detection_of_atmospheric_phenome/044_self_supervised_detection_of_atmospheric_phenome/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/044_self_supervised_detection_of_atmospheric_phenome/044_self_supervised_detection_of_atmospheric_phenome/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/044_self_supervised_detection_of_atmospheric_phenome/044_self_supervised_detection_of_atmospheric_phenome/reel.html"}},{"id":"045","slug":"045_demonstration_free_autonomous_reinforcement_lear","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Demonstration-free Autonomous Reinforcement Learning via Implicit and Bidirectional Curriculum","authors":"Jigang Kim¹², Daesol Cho¹², H. Jin Kim¹³","institutes":"¹Seoul National University; ²Artificial Intelligence Institute of Seoul National University (AIIS); ³Automation and Systems Research Institute (ASRI)","venue":"ICML 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.09943","code_url":"https://github.com/snu-larr/ibc_official","hook":"The paper introduces IBC, to the best of the authors' knowledge the first non-episodic RL algorithm that consistently learns without manual resets and without demonstrations, by combining a conditionally activated auxiliary agent (an implic…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nReinforcement learning assumes cheap resets to a fixed initial state after each episode, but real-world autonomous agents cannot reset on demand, so learning must proceed from continual, non-episodic interaction.\n\n*Also —* Existing autonomous RL (ARL) methods lean on prior data such as expert or sub-optimal demonstrations and fail when task-relevant interactions are sparse and unlikely to occur by chance.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nA truly autonomous agent should learn from scratch without external interventions or prior data, yet naive RL fails in the non-episodic setting because pre-convergence rollouts leave the agent in arbitrary states, causing highly variable initial conditions and unstable learning.\n\n*Also —* Prior reset-free methods either still require occasional manual resets, only work where task interactions occur by chance, or (like VaPRL and MEDAL) depend on demonstrations to build subgoal curricula or to define the backward objective.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces IBC, to the best of the authors' knowledge the first non-episodic RL algorithm that consistently learns without manual resets and without demonstrations, by combining a conditionally activated auxiliary agent (an implicit curriculum) with a bidirectional goal curriculum based on optimal transport (an explicit curriculum).\n\n*Also —* Across six sparse-reward manipulation and locomotion environments, IBC reaches state-of-the-art performance, outperforming baselines that use expert demonstrations and approaching the episodic oracle RL upper bound.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nIBC alternates between a forward agent π_f pursuing the task and an auxiliary agent π_a that returns it toward target initial states; the auxiliary agent is conditionally activated only when the forward agent fails, so its intervention fades as an implicit curriculum. A bidirectional goal curriculum casts curriculum generation as a Wasserstein Barycenter problem over particles sampled from the replay buffer, solved by bipartite matching via Minimum Cost Maximum Flow, jointly proposing K forward and K auxiliary curriculum goals.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\max_{\\mathcal{T},\\pi}\\big[V^{\\pi}(\\mathcal{T}) - L\\cdot D(\\mathcal{T},\\mathcal{T}^{*})\\big]$ ; $D(\\mathcal{T},\\mathcal{T}')=\\inf_{\\mu\\in\\Gamma(\\mathcal{T},\\mathcal{T}')}\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}\\big[d((s_0,g),(s_0',g'))\\big]$ ; $w(s_0^{*},s_{g^{*}}^{i},\\tau^{i}):=c\\,\\lVert\\phi_a(s_0^{*,i})-\\phi_a(s_0^{i})\\rVert_2+\\min_t\\big(\\lVert\\phi_f(s_{g^{*}}^{i})-\\phi_f(s_t^{i})\\rVert_2-\\tfrac{1}{L}V^{\\pi_f}(s_0^{i},\\phi_f(s_t^{i}))\\big)$\n\n*Also —* The surrogate objective relaxes the original RL objective using a Lipschitz-continuity assumption on the goal-conditioned value function; a diversity constraint allows at most one state per past trajectory to be selected, and off-the-shelf SAC optimizes the policies. Specifying the target distribution ρ_tar(s) needs only ~10 samples (sometimes a single example) from ρ_0(s) and the evaluation goal, no demonstrations.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nIBC is evaluated on six sparse-reward environments: Tabletop Manipulation and Sawyer Door from the established EARL autonomous-RL benchmark, plus four MuJoCo-based OpenAI Gym environments modified for the ARL setting, Fetch Pick&Place, Fetch Push, Fetch Reach, and Point-U-Maze.\n\n*Also —* These cover a mixture of robotic manipulation and locomotion tasks; evaluation follows the EARL protocol, reporting the deployed-policy evaluation return in 10k-step intervals with occasional resets only after hundreds of thousands of steps, averaged over 5 seeds.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nAs shown in Figure 4, IBC achieves state-of-the-art evaluation success rates across the environments without any demonstration data, outperforming demonstration-using baselines VaPRL and MEDAL and reaching success rates comparable to the episodic oracle RL upper bound.\n\n*Also —* VaPRL and MEDAL struggle where task-relevant interactions are very sparse or where evaluation goals are spread over a region rather than a few points (e.g., the Fetch environments); a demonstration-free version of VaPRL performs noticeably worse than the original.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nRemoving the bidirectional curriculum (IBC w/o Bidirectional) consistently degrades performance, and additionally removing the auxiliary agent (IBC w/o Bidirectional & Auxiliary, i.e., naive reset-free RL) degrades it further, especially in object-manipulation environments, confirming both components are necessary.\n\n*Also —* The effect varies by task: bidirectional curriculum helps little in the simple Tabletop Manipulation state space, and the auxiliary agent is less effective in Point-U-Maze where the initial state is far from the goals. The auxiliary agent's episode ratio drops toward zero as the forward agent converges (Figure 6), confirming the implicit curriculum vanishes on schedule.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- **6** sparse-reward environments (2 from the EARL benchmark + 4 modified MuJoCo/Gym tasks), evaluated over **5** random seeds.\n\n*Also —* K curriculum candidates (default 50 trajectories) are matched into K forward + K auxiliary goals; the curriculum buffer holds up to 1000 trajectories and updates once every 20 episodes.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy pairing a self-fading auxiliary agent with an optimal-transport-based bidirectional goal curriculum, IBC is the first method to learn robotic tasks autonomously with no resets and no demonstrations, matching approaches that rely on expert data.\n\n*Also —* The approach is limited to reversible (ergodic) environments and still needs minimal human input to specify sparse rewards; a reward-free variant using C-learning shows early promise as future work.","poster_thumb":"posters/045_demonstration_free_autonomous_reinforcement_lear.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/045_demonstration_free_autonomous_reinforcement_lear/045_demonstration_free_autonomous_reinforcement_lear/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/045_demonstration_free_autonomous_reinforcement_lear/045_demonstration_free_autonomous_reinforcement_lear/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/045_demonstration_free_autonomous_reinforcement_lear/045_demonstration_free_autonomous_reinforcement_lear/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/045_demonstration_free_autonomous_reinforcement_lear/045_demonstration_free_autonomous_reinforcement_lear/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/045_demonstration_free_autonomous_reinforcement_lear/045_demonstration_free_autonomous_reinforcement_lear/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/045_demonstration_free_autonomous_reinforcement_lear/045_demonstration_free_autonomous_reinforcement_lear/reel.html"}},{"id":"046","slug":"046_neuro_symbolic_language_modeling_with_automaton","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Neuro-Symbolic Language Modeling with Automaton-augmented Retrieval","authors":"Uri Alon¹, Frank F. Xu¹, Junxian He¹, Sudipta Sengupta², Dan Roth³, Graham Neubig¹","institutes":"¹Language Technologies Institute, Carnegie Mellon University; ²Amazon AWS; ³AWS AI Labs","venue":"ICML 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.12431","code_url":"https://github.com/neulab/retomaton","hook":"RetoMaton builds an unsupervised weighted finite automaton over any datastore by (1) saving a pointer from each entry to its successor in the text and (2) clustering entries with close keys into automaton states that share pointers.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nRetrieval-based language models improve quality by searching an external datastore, but that nearest-neighbor search runs as often as every time step and is the dominant computational bottleneck at inference.\n\n*Also —* The search is far slower than the LM's forward pass, which blocks retrieval LMs from practical deployment despite their accuracy, domain-adaptability, and provenance benefits.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nkNN-LM treats the datastore as a flat list and searches it token by token, ignoring that consecutive retrieved entries are highly correlated in time and that nearby keys behave alike.\n\n*Also —* Prior retrieval-saving work (AdaptRet) trains an MLP to skip searches, but when it skips it backs off to the base LM alone, losing the retrieval signal exactly when it helps most.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nRetoMaton builds an unsupervised weighted finite automaton over any datastore by (1) saving a pointer from each entry to its successor in the text and (2) clustering entries with close keys into automaton states that share pointers.\n\n*Also —* Traversed in parallel with LM inference, the automaton approximates the next nearest neighbors so most searches are skipped; it needs no training data and can be built from the training corpus or a new domain.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nAt inference the model traverses the automaton in parallel with the LM, visiting a set of states each step. It follows pointers of matching-value entries to predict the next states cheaply; only when the number of valid transitions falls below a threshold τ does it launch a fresh kNN search and restart the traversal.\n\n**Key idea.** $p(w \\mid c, S) = \\lambda\\, p_{\\text{auto}}(w \\mid c, S) + (1-\\lambda)\\, p_{\\text{LM}}(w \\mid c)$ ; $p_{\\text{auto}}(w \\mid c, S) \\propto \\sum_{q \\in S} \\varphi(q, c, w)$ ; $\\varphi(q, c, w) = \\sum_{(k_i, w_i, \\cdot)\\in \\pi^{-1}(q)} \\mathbb{1}_{w=w_i}\\exp(-\\text{dist}(f(c), k_i))$\n\n*Also —* Transition weights are dynamic, computed from distances between the current LM hidden state and the entries in each state, then interpolated with the base LM. τ trades off accuracy (frequent restarts, more searches) against speed (rare searches). Clustering can use k-means or a cheaper greedy merge.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nIn-domain modeling uses WikiText-103 (103M training tokens, 250K validation and test tokens) with a 247M-parameter Transformer base LM. Domain adaptation uses the English side of Law-MT (19M tokens) with a 656M-parameter Transformer base LM.\n\n*Also —* For WikiText-103 the datastore holds 103M entries clustered into 1M states; for Law-MT the datastore holds 19M entries clustered into 200K states, keeping an average cluster size near 100 in both. Baselines are the original kNN-LM and AdaptRet.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn WikiText-103, RetoMaton saves 81% of kNN searches while matching kNN-LM perplexity, and even at zero saved searches it lowers perplexity from 16.65 (kNN-LM) and 16.35 (AdaptRet) to 16.08. On Law-MT domain adaptation it cuts perplexity from 12.34 (kNN-LM) to 10.49 at FoSS=0 and degrades only gently as searches are saved, while kNN-LM's perplexity rises sharply.\n\n*Also —* Across the paper the automaton reduces perplexity by up to 1.85, or saves up to 83% of nearest-neighbor searches without hurting perplexity. Fraction of Saved Searches (FoSS) is used as a hardware-independent proxy for wall-clock savings.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nPointers versus clustering: a pointers-only variant (no clustering) already beats all baselines, reaching 16.12 perplexity at FoSS=0 and matching kNN-LM while saving over 60% of searches, showing pointers drive most of the gain at low FoSS; clustering adds its benefit mainly at high FoSS (≥0.7) by enabling longer search-free runs.\n\n*Also —* Clustering granularity: on WikiText-103, k=500K and k=1M means give similar perplexity while k=100K is too coarse; the cheaper greedy merge wins at FoSS=0 but degrades as FoSS grows. 98% of validation tokens fall in automaton-continued n-grams with n>1.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Up to 83% of nearest-neighbor searches saved with no perplexity loss.\n\n*Also —* - Law-MT: perplexity 12.34 → 10.49 at FoSS=0.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nTurning a retrieval datastore into a pointer-and-cluster automaton lets a language model reuse retrieval across time steps, so it skips most costly nearest-neighbor searches while matching or beating perplexity.\n\n*Also —* The construction is unsupervised, model-agnostic, and works across domains, generalizing token, chunk, and sequence retrieval within a single dynamic mechanism.","poster_thumb":"posters/046_neuro_symbolic_language_modeling_with_automaton.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/046_neuro_symbolic_language_modeling_with_automaton/046_neuro_symbolic_language_modeling_with_automaton/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/046_neuro_symbolic_language_modeling_with_automaton/046_neuro_symbolic_language_modeling_with_automaton/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/046_neuro_symbolic_language_modeling_with_automaton/046_neuro_symbolic_language_modeling_with_automaton/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/046_neuro_symbolic_language_modeling_with_automaton/046_neuro_symbolic_language_modeling_with_automaton/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/046_neuro_symbolic_language_modeling_with_automaton/046_neuro_symbolic_language_modeling_with_automaton/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/046_neuro_symbolic_language_modeling_with_automaton/046_neuro_symbolic_language_modeling_with_automaton/reel.html"}},{"id":"047","slug":"047_agnostic_learnability_of_halfspaces_via_logistic","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Agnostic Learnability of Halfspaces via Logistic Loss","authors":"Ziwei Ji¹, Kwangjun Ahn², Pranjal Awasthi³, Satyen Kale³, Stefani Karp³⁴","institutes":"¹University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign; ²Massachusetts Institute of Technology; ³Google Research; ⁴Carnegie Mellon University","venue":"ICML 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.13419","code_url":"","hook":"The paper (1) proves a matching Ω(√OPT) lower bound for the logistic-risk minimizer via an explicit 2-D distribution, (2) shows an added radial-Lipschitzness assumption yields Õ(OPT) risk, and (3) gives a simple two-phase (logistic + percep…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nHow good is plain logistic regression for agnostically learning homogeneous halfspaces, where an adversary corrupts an OPT fraction of labels and we must compete with the best linear classifier of zero-one risk OPT?\n\n*Also —* Prior work left a wide gap between an Ω̃(OPT) lower bound for all convex surrogates and an Õ(√OPT) upper bound achieved by logistic regression on well-behaved distributions.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nLogistic regression is a default algorithm in practice, but its worst-case behavior is alarming: Ben-David et al. (2012) show its minimizer can suffer zero-one risk as bad as 1−OPT on adversarial distributions.\n\n*Also —* Restricting to \"well-behaved\" (e.g. isotropic log-concave) distributions makes the problem tractable, but the exact rate logistic regression achieves there was open, blocking any principled recommendation to use it.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper (1) proves a matching Ω(√OPT) lower bound for the logistic-risk minimizer via an explicit 2-D distribution, (2) shows an added radial-Lipschitzness assumption yields Õ(OPT) risk, and (3) gives a simple two-phase (logistic + perceptron) algorithm achieving Õ(OPT) for all well-behaved distributions.\n\n*Also —* The two-phase algorithm needs only one extra convex minimization and Õ(d/ε²) samples, versus prior methods that solve O(log(1/OPT)) minimization problems.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nThe analysis is built on the logistic loss and the hinge loss and their population risks. The lower bound comes from a 4-part distribution Q on R² that fools the logistic minimizer; the upper bound uses radial-Lipschitzness; the algorithm chains projected gradient descent on the logistic risk with projected SGD (perceptron) on the hinge loss over a bounded domain, warm-started at the logistic solution.\n\n**Key idea.** logistic loss $\\ell_{\\log}(z)=\\ln(1+e^{-z})$ and hinge loss $\\ell_{h}(z)=\\max\\{-z,0\\}$; empirical risk $\\widehat{R}_{\\ell}(w)=\\tfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\ell\\!\\left(y_i\\langle w,x_i\\rangle\\right)$; Phase 1 projected GD $w_{t+1}=\\Pi_{B(1/\\sqrt{\\epsilon})}\\!\\left[w_t-\\eta\\nabla\\widehat{R}_{\\log}(w_t)\\right]$; Phase 2 projected hinge SGD $w_{t+1}=\\Pi_{D}\\!\\left[w_t-\\eta\\,\\ell_h'(y_t\\langle w_t,x_t\\rangle)\\,y_t x_t\\right]$.\n\n*Also —* Phase 1 finds a unit vector v within angle Õ(√OPT+ε) of the target ū; Phase 2 boosts this to Õ(OPT) error using SGD on the hinge loss with step size Θ(ε) and T = Ω(1/ε²) steps (bounded case).\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nThis is a theoretical paper with no empirical datasets; guarantees are proven for the family of \"well-behaved\" distributions (isotropic, bounded, satisfying the soft-margin and sub-exponential regularity conditions of Diakonikolas et al. 2020d and Frei et al. 2021b).\n\n*Also —* The lower-bound instance is an explicit distribution Q over R²×{−1,+1} with four parts Q₁–Q₄ (illustrated in Figure 1); the upper bounds cover both bounded (‖x‖≤B) and (α₁,α₂)-sub-exponential feature distributions.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nThe logistic-risk minimizer w* on the constructed Q provably has zero-one risk R₀₋₁(w*) ≥ √OPT/(60π), matching Frei et al.'s Õ(√OPT) upper bound and proving it tight. Under radial-Lipschitzness, logistic regression instead achieves R₀₋₁(w*) = O((1+Cκ)OPT), i.e. Õ(OPT).\n\n*Also —* The two-phase algorithm attains E[min_t R₀₋₁(w_t)] = O(OPT+ε) for bounded and O(OPT·ln(1/OPT)+ε) for sub-exponential distributions.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nThe upper-bound analysis is deliberately versatile: the same argument recovers Frei et al.'s Õ(√OPT) guarantee for general well-behaved distributions and also applies to the hinge loss, which is precisely what makes the two-phase algorithm possible.\n\n*Also —* Theorem 3.4 separates the two regimes: bounded distributions give O((1+Cκ)OPT) with Cκ=∫₀ᴮκ(r)dr, while sub-exponential ones give O((1+Cκ)OPT·ln(1/OPT)) with the integral taken to 3α₂ln(1/OPT), isolating the ln(1/OPT) factor as the sole cost of heavier tails.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Lower bound: R₀₋₁(w*) ≥ √OPT / (60π) for the logistic minimizer (valid when OPT ≤ 1/100).\n\n*Also —* Prior O(OPT+ε) algorithms solve O(log(1/OPT)) minimization problems; this method needs only 2 convex steps (logistic regression + one perceptron phase).\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nPlain logistic regression is stuck at √OPT error for agnostically learning halfspaces, and that rate is tight; but a single extra convex step, a perceptron phase warm-started from the logistic solution, provably restores near-optimal Õ(OPT) error on any well-behaved distribution.\n\n*Also —* Radial-Lipschitzness of the density is the clean structural condition that lets logistic regression alone already reach Õ(OPT).","poster_thumb":"posters/047_agnostic_learnability_of_halfspaces_via_logistic.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/047_agnostic_learnability_of_halfspaces_via_logistic/047_agnostic_learnability_of_halfspaces_via_logistic/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/047_agnostic_learnability_of_halfspaces_via_logistic/047_agnostic_learnability_of_halfspaces_via_logistic/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/047_agnostic_learnability_of_halfspaces_via_logistic/047_agnostic_learnability_of_halfspaces_via_logistic/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/047_agnostic_learnability_of_halfspaces_via_logistic/047_agnostic_learnability_of_halfspaces_via_logistic/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/047_agnostic_learnability_of_halfspaces_via_logistic/047_agnostic_learnability_of_halfspaces_via_logistic/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/047_agnostic_learnability_of_halfspaces_via_logistic/047_agnostic_learnability_of_halfspaces_via_logistic/reel.html"}},{"id":"048","slug":"048_on_the_convergence_of_msgd_and_adagrad_for_stoch","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"On the Convergence of mSGD and AdaGrad for Stochastic Optimization","authors":"Ruinan Jin¹², Yu Xing³, Xingkang He³⁴","institutes":"¹Academy of Mathematics and Systems Science, Chinese Academy of Sciences; ²University of Chinese Academy of Sciences; ³KTH Royal Institute of Technology; ⁴University of Notre Dame","venue":"","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.11204","code_url":"","hook":"The paper proves almost-sure asymptotic convergence of mSGD to a connected set of stationary points, quantifies its convergence rate to explain the acceleration over SGD, and proves almost-sure asymptotic convergence of standard norm-form A…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nMomentum SGD (mSGD) and AdaGrad are empirically superior to SGD, yet their theoretical convergence for smooth, possibly non-convex loss functions remains poorly established.\n\n*Also —* Most prior results only guarantee subsequence convergence or convergence of time averages, which is weaker than the almost-sure asymptotic convergence practitioners rely on.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nA rigorous convergence theory for static-momentum mSGD and standard AdaGrad, without convexity, bounded-iterate, or uniformly bounded-gradient-noise assumptions, is still missing.\n\n*Also —* Prior work handles only time-varying momentum or modified AdaGrad variants; the widely used static momentum coefficient and the norm-form AdaGrad in practice fall outside those conditions.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper proves almost-sure asymptotic convergence of mSGD to a connected set of stationary points, quantifies its convergence rate to explain the acceleration over SGD, and proves almost-sure asymptotic convergence of standard norm-form AdaGrad.\n\n*Also —* Results hold for possibly non-convex loss functions under relaxed noise assumptions, extending subsequence and time-average convergence results in the literature to the stronger asymptotic sense.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nUsing stochastic approximation techniques, the authors treat mSGD and AdaGrad as noisy dynamical systems and show their iterates track a limiting ODE toward the stationary set. A stability lemma bounds the expected loss, and a relaxed noise condition controls gradient fluctuations without requiring a uniform bound over the whole space.\n\n**Key idea.** $v_n = \\alpha v_{n-1} + \\varepsilon_n \\nabla_{\\theta_n} g(\\theta_n,\\xi_n),\\quad \\theta_{n+1}=\\theta_n - v_n$ (mSGD); $S_n = S_{n-1} + \\|\\nabla_{\\theta_n} g(\\theta_n,\\xi_n)\\|^2,\\quad \\theta_{n+1}=\\theta_n - \\tfrac{\\alpha_0}{\\sqrt{S_n}}\\nabla_{\\theta_n} g(\\theta_n,\\xi_n)$ (AdaGrad, norm form); $\\mathbb{E}\\|\\nabla_{\\theta_n} g(\\theta_n)\\|^2 = O\\!\\left(e^{-\\sum_{i=1}^n \\frac{s\\varepsilon_i}{p(1-\\alpha)^2}}\\right)$ (mSGD rate)\n\n*Also —* For mSGD, a decreasing step size satisfying the Robbins-Monro conditions counteracts noise while the momentum coefficient stays static; for AdaGrad, the adaptive (random, gradient-dependent) step size is handled by carefully bounding the conditionally dependent terms that block naive conditional-expectation arguments.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nThis is a purely theoretical paper: there are no datasets, benchmarks, or empirical experiments. All results are mathematical theorems proven under stated assumptions.\n\n*Also —* The loss functions considered are general smooth (continuously differentiable), non-negative, possibly non-convex functions whose gradients are Lipschitz; example families satisfying the assumptions include periodic and polynomial functions such as sin²(x) and (x−1)(x−2)(x−3)(x−4).\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nUnder mild assumptions, mSGD iterates converge almost surely to a connected set of stationary points (Theorem 1), and standard norm-form AdaGrad iterates do the same (Theorem 3), both for non-convex losses.\n\n*Also —* These almost-sure asymptotic results are strictly stronger than the subsequence-convergence and time-average results they extend from prior literature.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nAs a theory paper there are no experimental ablations; instead the analysis isolates the effect of the momentum coefficient α by deriving the convergence rate as a function of α.\n\n*Also —* Setting α = 0 recovers the SGD rate exactly, and increasing α toward one reduces the rate coefficient, showing momentum's acceleration analytically rather than empirically.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nmSGD mean-square-gradient rate is $O(e^{-\\sum_{i=1}^n s\\varepsilon_i / (p(1-\\alpha)^2)})$; with α = 0 this equals the SGD rate, and as α→1 the time-average rate reaches order $O(1/T)$.\n\n*Also —* Results require only Robbins-Monro step sizes (∑εₙ = ∞, ∑εₙ² < ∞, e.g. εₙ = 1/n) and a relaxed noise bound E‖∇g(θ) − ∇g(θ,ξ)‖² ≤ M(1 + g(θ)), with no convexity or bounded-iterate assumptions.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nWith probability one, both static-momentum mSGD and standard AdaGrad converge to stationary points of general non-convex losses, and momentum provably accelerates SGD.\n\n*Also —* The proofs relax convexity, bounded-iterate, and uniform noise assumptions, closing much of the gap between the theory and the practice of these ubiquitous optimizers.","poster_thumb":"posters/048_on_the_convergence_of_msgd_and_adagrad_for_stoch.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/048_on_the_convergence_of_msgd_and_adagrad_for_stoch/048_on_the_convergence_of_msgd_and_adagrad_for_stoch/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/048_on_the_convergence_of_msgd_and_adagrad_for_stoch/048_on_the_convergence_of_msgd_and_adagrad_for_stoch/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/048_on_the_convergence_of_msgd_and_adagrad_for_stoch/048_on_the_convergence_of_msgd_and_adagrad_for_stoch/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/048_on_the_convergence_of_msgd_and_adagrad_for_stoch/048_on_the_convergence_of_msgd_and_adagrad_for_stoch/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/048_on_the_convergence_of_msgd_and_adagrad_for_stoch/048_on_the_convergence_of_msgd_and_adagrad_for_stoch/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/048_on_the_convergence_of_msgd_and_adagrad_for_stoch/048_on_the_convergence_of_msgd_and_adagrad_for_stoch/reel.html"}},{"id":"049","slug":"049_towards_unbiased_gravitational_wave_parameter_es","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Towards Unbiased Gravitational-Wave Parameter Estimation using Score-Based Likelihood Characterization","authors":"Ronan Legin¹²³, Maximiliano Isi⁴, Kaze Wong⁴, Alexandre Adam¹²³, Laurence Perreault-Levasseur¹²³⁴⁵, Yashar Hezaveh¹²³⁴⁵","institutes":"¹Department of Physics, Université de Montréal; ²Mila - Quebec Artificial Intelligence Institute; ³Ciela - Montreal Institute for Astrophysical Data Analysis and Machine Learning; ⁴Center for Computational Astrophysics, Flatiron Institute; ⁵Perimeter Institute for Theoretical Physics","venue":"ICML 2023","paper_url":"https://ml4astro.github.io/icml2023/assets/70.pdf","code_url":"https://github.com/RonanLegin/slicgw","hook":"The paper adapts Score-based Likelihood Characterization (SLIC) to gravitational waves: a score-based diffusion model learns the empirical detector-noise distribution, which is combined with a differentiable waveform model to form an unbias…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nGravitational-wave parameter estimation conventionally assumes Gaussian, stationary detector noise, but real LIGO, Virgo, and KAGRA noise deviates strongly from this, biasing inference.\n\n*Also —* Real data carry transient non-Gaussian glitches, drifting narrow spectral lines, and subthreshold astrophysical signals that must otherwise be handled by expensive, bias-prone bespoke treatments.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nAs detector catalogs grow, even small departures from the Gaussian assumption accumulate into systematic biases, so a likelihood that handles real noise without special-casing each artifact is increasingly essential.\n\n*Also —* Next-generation observatories such as Cosmic Explorer and the Einstein Telescope will produce far more events, making a scalable, assumption-free noise model a foundational requirement.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper adapts Score-based Likelihood Characterization (SLIC) to gravitational waves: a score-based diffusion model learns the empirical detector-noise distribution, which is combined with a differentiable waveform model to form an unbiased likelihood for parameter estimation.\n\n*Also —* It demonstrates end-to-end that the learned model reproduces real LIGO noise (including fine spectral lines) and recovers true injected source parameters, validating the framework on real detector data.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nFor additive-noise inverse problems, a score-based diffusion model learns the noise score ∇ₓ log Q(x) via denoising score matching; combined with the Jacobian of a differentiable waveform model M(θ), it yields the likelihood score, which is sampled with a Metropolis-adjusted Langevin algorithm (MALA).\n\n**Key idea.** $\\nabla_{\\theta} \\log p(x_O \\mid \\theta) = -\\nabla_x \\log Q(x)\\, \\nabla_{\\theta} M(\\theta), \\quad x_O = M(\\theta) + x$\n\n*Also —* The forward model uses the differentiable IMRPhenomD waveform from the ripple package, enabling automatic differentiation for the required Jacobian; the score network is trained in the Fourier domain on Tukey-windowed 4 s segments.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nThe score network is trained on 11 hours of real LIGO-Hanford data around GW150914 (sampled at 4096 Hz, split into 4 s segments), with the segment containing the true signal discarded.\n\n*Also —* Testing injects a simulated GW150914-like signal (chirp mass M = 29 M⊙, η = 0.2495, χ₁ = χ₂ = 0, dL = 400 Mpc) into held-out real LIGO noise after the training window; training is done in the Fourier domain after a Tukey window (αT = 0.1), with likelihood integration starting at 20 Hz.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nSLIC recovers the five varied source parameters of the injected signal with high credibility, and its posterior has smaller variance than the standard Gaussian-likelihood posterior; the model also reproduces the real LIGO PSD, including narrow spectral lines.\n\n*Also —* From 1024 sampled noise realizations, SLIC's power spectral density closely matches the Welch estimate from real LIGO data and resolves spectral lines more finely thanks to exposure to more data (11 h vs 1 h).\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nAs a baseline comparison, inference is repeated with a standard Gaussian likelihood using a Welch PSD; on a glitch-free segment both methods recover the true values, but SLIC yields a tighter posterior.\n\n*Also —* This head-to-head against the conventional Gaussian likelihood isolates the effect of the learned non-Gaussian noise model on posterior width and bias.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- 11 hours of real LIGO-Hanford noise used to train the score-based model\n\n*Also —* - Data sampled at 4096 Hz, split into 4 s segments; likelihood integration from 20 Hz\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy learning detector noise with a score-based diffusion model and coupling it to a differentiable waveform, SLIC delivers unbiased gravitational-wave likelihoods without assuming Gaussian, stationary noise, a step toward scalable, artifact-robust inference.\n\n*Also —* Current limits are the 4 s segment length and single-detector, MALA-based sampling; extending to longer segments, multi-detector coherent analysis, and advanced samplers like flowMC is the clear path to real high-dimensional GW events.","poster_thumb":"posters/049_towards_unbiased_gravitational_wave_parameter_es.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/049_towards_unbiased_gravitational_wave_parameter_es/049_towards_unbiased_gravitational_wave_parameter_es/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/049_towards_unbiased_gravitational_wave_parameter_es/049_towards_unbiased_gravitational_wave_parameter_es/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/049_towards_unbiased_gravitational_wave_parameter_es/049_towards_unbiased_gravitational_wave_parameter_es/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/049_towards_unbiased_gravitational_wave_parameter_es/049_towards_unbiased_gravitational_wave_parameter_es/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/049_towards_unbiased_gravitational_wave_parameter_es/049_towards_unbiased_gravitational_wave_parameter_es/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/049_towards_unbiased_gravitational_wave_parameter_es/049_towards_unbiased_gravitational_wave_parameter_es/reel.html"}},{"id":"050","slug":"050_deconvolving_detector_effects_for_distribution_m","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Deconvolving Detector Effects for Distribution Moments","authors":"Krish Desai¹, Benjamin Nachman², Jesse Thaler³⁴","institutes":"¹University of California, Berkeley; ²Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory; ³Massachusetts Institute of Technology; ⁴NSF AI Institute for Artificial Intelligence and Fundamental Interactions","venue":"NeurIPS 2022","paper_url":"https://ml4physicalsciences.github.io/2022/files/NeurIPS_ML4PS_2022_43.pdf","code_url":"https://github.com/hep-lbdl/MomentUnfolding","hook":"The paper introduces Moment Unfolding, a dedicated machine-learning technique that directly unfolds statistical moments of an observable in an unbinned, non-iterative way, using a reweighting function whose parameters are identified with th…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nDeconvolving ('unfolding') detector distortions is critical for comparing cross-section measurements with theory, yet most unfolding methods require histogram binning even though many theoretical predictions are stated at the level of moments.\n\n*Also —* Binning the two-dimensional (X, Y) support to compute moments of X in bins of Y introduces discretization artifacts that limit precision.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nStatistical moments of observables, such as their energy-scale dependence, are often predictable from theory even when the full probability density is not, so a method that targets moments directly is valuable.\n\n*Also —* Existing unbinned unfolding methods unfold whole spectra generically, which can compromise precision for any specific aspect like a small set of moments.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces Moment Unfolding, a dedicated machine-learning technique that directly unfolds statistical moments of an observable in an unbinned, non-iterative way, using a reweighting function whose parameters are identified with the moments.\n\n*Also —* The generator's form is inspired by the Boltzmann equation and the training structure is adapted from a Generative Adversarial Network; unlike iterative methods such as OmniFold, one pair of networks is trained only once.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nEach synthetic event is a pair (X_T, X_D) of pre-detector 'generation' and post-detector 'simulation'. A generator g reweights the generation so that, after detector emulation, the reweighted simulation becomes statistically indistinguishable from the observed data, as judged by a discriminator d trained on a weighted binary cross-entropy loss.\n\n**Key idea.** $g(x) = e^{\\lambda_1 x + \\lambda_2 x^2 + \\cdots + \\lambda_n x^n}$ and $L[g,d] = -\\frac{1}{N_x}\\left[\\sum_{\\text{data}} \\log d(x_{\\text{data}}) + \\sum_{(x_{\\text{gen}}, x_{\\text{sim}})} g(x_{\\text{gen}}) \\log\\bigl(1 - d(x_{\\text{sim}})\\bigr)\\right]$\n\n*Also —* g is parametrized as the exponential of a degree-n polynomial with only n trainable parameters {λ_i} identified with the moments; d has three hidden layers of 50 ReLU nodes with a sigmoid output. The generator maximizes the loss while the discriminator minimizes it, mirroring a GAN, and detector emulation is run only once because reweighting changes importance weights rather than features.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nTwo case studies: a Gaussian toy example (truth N(0,1), generation N(−0.5,1), Gaussian detector noise N(0,5), 10⁶ samples, 3:1 train–test split) and hadronic jet substructure from LHC simulations using the jet width observable w.\n\n*Also —* The jet data are the Pythia/Herwig + Delphes samples from the OmniFold study, with one simulation acting as 'data' and the other as the synthetic dataset; two moments of the jet width are unfolded.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn the jet substructure task the predicted moments, read off as the peaks of the loss-function scans, coincide with the known true moments (the vertical dotted lines) with a mean absolute error of ≤ 0.02%.\n\n*Also —* On the Gaussian example the maximum of the loss function sits at the true mean, verifying the procedure, and the discriminator converges within 10 epochs of training.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nEven though the first and second moments of the reweighted generation match the truth well, the full jet-width distributions remain not statistically identical, because higher moments differ between truth and generation and are not constrained by unfolding only two moments.\n\n*Also —* This highlights the intended scope of the method: it targets a chosen small set of moments rather than the entire density, so residual differences appear in the uncontrolled higher moments.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nPredicted-vs-true moment mean absolute error ≤ 0.02%; 2 moments unfolded simultaneously for jet width; discriminator converges within 10 epochs.\n\n*Also —* 10⁶ samples with a 3:1 train–test split in the Gaussian study; the full analysis reproduces in under 5 minutes on a single Nvidia RTX6000 GPU.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nMoment Unfolding is a novel unbinned, non-iterative, GAN-inspired reweighting method that directly unfolds a chosen set of distribution moments to sub-percent accuracy, avoiding the discretization artifacts of binned unfolding.\n\n*Also —* It is dataset-agnostic, so beyond collider physics it could serve deconvolution problems in fields from medical devices and seismology to spectral astronomy and computer vision.","poster_thumb":"posters/050_deconvolving_detector_effects_for_distribution_m.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/050_deconvolving_detector_effects_for_distribution_m/050_deconvolving_detector_effects_for_distribution_m/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/050_deconvolving_detector_effects_for_distribution_m/050_deconvolving_detector_effects_for_distribution_m/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/050_deconvolving_detector_effects_for_distribution_m/050_deconvolving_detector_effects_for_distribution_m/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/050_deconvolving_detector_effects_for_distribution_m/050_deconvolving_detector_effects_for_distribution_m/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/050_deconvolving_detector_effects_for_distribution_m/050_deconvolving_detector_effects_for_distribution_m/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/050_deconvolving_detector_effects_for_distribution_m/050_deconvolving_detector_effects_for_distribution_m/reel.html"}},{"id":"051","slug":"051_conformal_semantic_keypoint_detection_with_stati","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Object Pose Estimation with Statistical Guarantees: Conformal Keypoint Detection and Geometric Uncertainty Propagation","authors":"Heng Yang¹, Marco Pavone¹","institutes":"¹NVIDIA Research","venue":"CVPR 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.12246","code_url":"https://github.com/NVlabs/ConformalKeypoint","hook":"The first object pose estimator endowing an estimate with provable and computable worst-case error bounds, via two additions to the two-stage paradigm: conformal keypoint detection and geometric uncertainty propagation.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nTwo-stage object pose estimators (detect keypoints, then solve PnP) perform well on benchmarks but offer no provable guarantee on the quality or uncertainty of the estimated 6D pose.\n\n*Also —* Safety-critical uses (autonomous driving, robotic manipulation, space robotics) need provably correct estimates with formal worst-case error bounds, which prior pipelines lack.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nNeural keypoint detectors can be arbitrarily wrong (outliers), robust back-ends like RANSAC give no optimality guarantee and can fail silently, and no prior method certifies a worst-case error bound between the estimate and the groundtruth.\n\n*Also —* Three challenges: (C1) keypoints may be far from groundtruth, (C2) outlier rejection is nonconvex and heuristics lack global optimality, (C3) no provably correct uncertainty quantification exists.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe first object pose estimator endowing an estimate with provable and computable worst-case error bounds, via two additions to the two-stage paradigm: conformal keypoint detection and geometric uncertainty propagation.\n\n*Also —* Introduces the Pose Uncertainty Set (PURSE), the RANdom SAmple averaGing (RANSAG) sampler for computing an average pose, and semidefinite relaxations that upper-bound the worst-case rotation and translation errors.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nInductive conformal prediction calibrates heatmap nonconformity scores on a calibration set to build per-keypoint prediction sets (balls or ellipses) with 1−ε coverage. These constraints define the nonconvex PURSE over SE(3); RANSAG samples keypoints, solves P3P/PnP, and averages accepted poses into an average pose. A semidefinite relaxation maximizes pose-to-PURSE distance to certify worst-case rotation and translation bounds.\n\n**Key idea.** $P[y_{l+1} \\in F^{\\epsilon}(x_{l+1})] \\ge 1-\\epsilon$ ; $\\varphi_{\\text{peak}}(y_k, f(x)_k) = p_k \\lVert y_k - q_k \\rVert$ ; $\\varphi_{\\text{cov}}(y_k, f(x)_k) = (y_k - \\bar{q}_k)^{\\top} \\Sigma_k^{-1} (y_k - \\bar{q}_k)$ ; $d^2_{\\epsilon,\\lambda} = \\max_{(R,t)\\in S^{\\epsilon}} \\lambda \\lVert R - \\bar{R} \\rVert_F^2 + (1-\\lambda)\\lVert t - \\bar{t} \\rVert^2$ ; $\\lVert \\bar{R} - R_{gt} \\rVert_F \\le d_{\\epsilon,1},\\ \\lVert \\bar{t} - t_{gt} \\rVert \\le d_{\\epsilon,0}$\n\n*Also —* Two nonconformity designs — peak (scaled peak-to-true error) and covariance (Mahalanobis distance over top-J detections) — yield circular vs elliptical sets. The worst-case bound problem is nonconvex; its SDP relaxation is either exact or gives a valid upper bound.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on the LineMOD Occlusion (LM-O) dataset: 1214 test images of 8 objects on a table; the 200 BOP-selected images serve as the calibration set and all 1214 images for testing.\n\n*Also —* Four setups combine detection source (groundtruth or Faster-RCNN bounding boxes) with prediction-set shape (ball or ellipse): gt-ball, gt-ellipse, frcnn-ball, frcnn-ellipse. The dataset's uniform pose sampling supports the exchangeability assumption conformal prediction requires.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn LM-O the prediction sets attain their target coverage (empirical coverage near 90% at ε=0.1), the worst-case error bounds are always correct whenever PURSE contains the groundtruth pose, and the average pose matches or beats strong baselines on the 2D-projection success metric.\n\n*Also —* In the coverage-vs-bound plots, points covered by PURSE (blue circles) never cross the y=x diagonal, confirming the bounds upper-bound the true error as guaranteed with probability 1−ε.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nVarying the error rate ε (0.1 vs 0.4) trades coverage for tightness: larger ε shrinks the prediction sets and improves average-pose success but lowers coverage. Elliptical (covariance) sets generally match or beat circular (ball) sets, and accuracy rises with ε.\n\n*Also —* A first-order SDP relaxation gives looser but faster bounds than the second-order relaxation; solving the bound for multiple RANSAG samples and taking the minimum slightly tightens the translation bounds (Remark 4).\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Target coverage 90% (ε=0.1) is empirically attained on LM-O, with as few as n=200 calibration images.\n\n*Also —* - LM-O: 1214 test images, 8 objects; 200 calibration images.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy marrying conformal prediction with geometric uncertainty propagation, the two-stage pose pipeline becomes the first to output an estimate carrying a provable, computable worst-case error bound, without sacrificing accuracy.\n\n*Also —* The framework is detector-agnostic (wraps any heatmap keypoint network) and points toward statistically guaranteed perception for other geometric vision problems.","poster_thumb":"posters/051_conformal_semantic_keypoint_detection_with_stati.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/051_conformal_semantic_keypoint_detection_with_stati/051_conformal_semantic_keypoint_detection_with_stati/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/051_conformal_semantic_keypoint_detection_with_stati/051_conformal_semantic_keypoint_detection_with_stati/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/051_conformal_semantic_keypoint_detection_with_stati/051_conformal_semantic_keypoint_detection_with_stati/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/051_conformal_semantic_keypoint_detection_with_stati/051_conformal_semantic_keypoint_detection_with_stati/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/051_conformal_semantic_keypoint_detection_with_stati/051_conformal_semantic_keypoint_detection_with_stati/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/051_conformal_semantic_keypoint_detection_with_stati/051_conformal_semantic_keypoint_detection_with_stati/reel.html"}},{"id":"052","slug":"052_equivariant_polynomials_for_graph_neural_network","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Equivariant Polynomials for Graph Neural Networks","authors":"Omri Puny¹, Derek Lim², Bobak T. Kiani², Haggai Maron³, Yaron Lipman¹⁴","institutes":"¹Weizmann Institute of Science; ²MIT CSAIL; ³NVIDIA Research; ⁴Meta AI Research","venue":"ICML 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.11556","code_url":"https://github.com/omri1348/Equivariant-Polynomaials","hook":"The paper (1) gives a full characterization of all equivariant graph polynomials via a concrete basis, where each basis element is a multi-graph and its evaluation is a tensor contraction; (2) provides algorithmic tools (tensor-contraction…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nGraph neural networks have inherently limited expressive power, and the Weisfeiler-Lehman (WL) hierarchy used to measure it has a complex, hard-to-act-on definition and is too coarse to separate current GNNs.\n\n*Also —* WL gives no direct guidance for improving a model, so practitioners lack a constructive target for building more powerful architectures.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nA good expressiveness measure should be interpretable and constructive, pointing directly at operations a model is missing, rather than only certifying pass or fail on an isomorphism test.\n\n*Also —* Prior work characterized only low-order equivariant maps or leaned on WL, leaving a gap for a complete, computation-friendly description of what a GNN should be able to output.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper (1) gives a full characterization of all equivariant graph polynomials via a concrete basis, where each basis element is a multi-graph and its evaluation is a tensor contraction; (2) provides algorithmic tools (tensor-contraction sequences plus a linear-time decision algorithm) to compute the exact polynomial expressive power of popular GNNs; and (3) uses the theory to enhance common architectures with polynomial features and new aggregations, yielding PPGN++.\n\n*Also —* This defines a new expressiveness hierarchy based on the degree of equivariant polynomials a model can compute, refining and connecting to the WL hierarchy.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nEquivariant graph polynomials of degree at most d are characterized by a basis indexed by directed multi-graphs H with bounded vertices and edges; each basis element P_H is computed as a tensor contraction (einsum) over the input matrix, and is obtained by symmetrizing monomials with the Reynolds operator. A model is analyzed as a contraction bank F, and Algorithm 1 decides in linear time whether P_H is computable by node-based (F_n) or edge-based (F_e) banks. The theory then motivates PPGN++, a matrix-multiplication network augmented with additional primitive operations and precomputed polynomial features.\n\n**Key idea.** $Q_H(X) = \\sum_{g \\in S_n} g \\cdot M_H\\!\\left(g^{-1}\\cdot X,\\ \\delta_{g(a),g(b)}\\right) = \\sum_{g \\in S_n} M_{g\\cdot H}\\!\\left(X, \\delta_{a,b}\\right)$ ; PPGN++ layer: $Z^{(k+1)} = \\bar{m}_3\\!\\left[\\ \\bar{m}_1\\!\\left(Z^{(k)}, Z^{(k)\\top}\\right) \\circledast \\bar{m}_2\\!\\left(Z^{(k)}\\right),\\ Z^{(k)}\\right]$\n\n*Also —* Node-based contractions recover 1-WL, edge-based contractions recover 2-FWL / 3-WL, and adding higher-degree polynomial features pushes expressive power strictly beyond 3-WL.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluation uses three molecular graph regression benchmarks, ZINC-12K, ZINC-full, and Alchemy, plus a Strongly Regular (SR) graph collection for testing the ability to distinguish hard non-isomorphic graphs.\n\n*Also —* ZINC-12K has a 10000/1000/1000 train/val/test split and ZINC-full has about 220011/24445/5000; all trained models respect a 500K parameter budget, following the protocols of Dwivedi et al. (2020) and Lim et al. (2022).\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nPPGN++ with high-degree polynomial features sets state of the art on all three regression benchmarks, reaching test MAE 0.071 on ZINC-12K, 0.020 on ZINC-full, and 0.109 on Alchemy, and precomputing polynomial features gives provably strictly better than 3-WL expressive power while remaining efficient.\n\n*Also —* Polynomial features also sharply improve weaker backbones: GatedGCN's ZINC-12K MAE drops from 0.265 to 0.106 once degree-6 polynomial features are added.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nIncreasing the degree of the added polynomial features monotonically improves results: on ZINC-12K PPGN++ goes from 0.076 to 0.072 (degree 5) to 0.071 (degree 6), and on the SR-graph task the distinguishing failure rate falls by orders of magnitude as feature degree rises from 5 to 7 (Figure 10).\n\n*Also —* Table 1 quantifies the theory: the edge-based model F_e leaves far fewer polynomials non-computable than the node-based F_n (for example 0/18 vs 2/8 at degree 3), matching that F_e is 5-node- and 4-edge-polynomial-exact while F_n is only 2-node-polynomial-exact.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- ZINC-12K test MAE: 0.071 ± 0.001 (PPGN++ degree 6), best in class (vs CIN 0.079, GIN 0.163).\n\n*Also —* All models stay within a 500K parameter budget; the edge-based model leaves only 0/18 degree-3 polynomials non-computable versus 2/8 for the node-based model.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nGrading and building graph networks by the equivariant polynomials they can compute, each a multi-graph evaluated as a tensor contraction, yields a finer expressiveness hierarchy than WL and, in practice, precomputed polynomial features push standard GNNs beyond 3-WL and to state-of-the-art accuracy on molecular benchmarks.\n\n*Also —* The basis plus the linear-time computability algorithm turn \"how expressive is this GNN?\" into a concrete, checkable question with an actionable answer.","poster_thumb":"posters/052_equivariant_polynomials_for_graph_neural_network.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/052_equivariant_polynomials_for_graph_neural_network/052_equivariant_polynomials_for_graph_neural_network/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/052_equivariant_polynomials_for_graph_neural_network/052_equivariant_polynomials_for_graph_neural_network/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/052_equivariant_polynomials_for_graph_neural_network/052_equivariant_polynomials_for_graph_neural_network/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/052_equivariant_polynomials_for_graph_neural_network/052_equivariant_polynomials_for_graph_neural_network/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/052_equivariant_polynomials_for_graph_neural_network/052_equivariant_polynomials_for_graph_neural_network/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/052_equivariant_polynomials_for_graph_neural_network/052_equivariant_polynomials_for_graph_neural_network/reel.html"}},{"id":"053","slug":"053_sit_dataset_socially_interactive_pedestrian_traj","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"SiT Dataset: Socially Interactive Pedestrian Trajectory Dataset for Social Navigation Robots","authors":"Jongwook Bae¹, Jungho Kim¹, Junyong Yun¹, Changwon Kang¹, Jeongseon Choi¹, Chanhyeok Kim¹, Junho Lee¹, Jungwook Choi¹, Jun Won Choi¹","institutes":"¹Hanyang University","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/hash/4d6a000c216974f59e597bc878cd6325-Abstract-Datasets_and_Benchmarks.html","code_url":"https://github.com/SPALaboratory/SiT-Dataset","hook":"SiT is a large-scale real-world pedestrian trajectory dataset collected by a robot navigating crowded indoor and outdoor scenes, providing synchronized LiDAR, camera, IMU, and RTK raw data, 2D and 3D annotations, multi-layered semantic maps…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nSocial navigation robots must perceive and predict nearby pedestrian trajectories to move safely in shared spaces, but existing trajectory datasets do not capture the close, dynamic human-robot interaction a navigating robot actually experiences.\n\n*Also —* Prior datasets are either static top-view camera recordings (ETH, UCY, SDD) or autonomous-driving data (nuScenes, Waymo, Argoverse) where vehicles and pedestrians occupy separate roads.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nA robot sharing the same path and space as pedestrians provokes interactive walking behaviors that only appear at close range, so training data must be collected from a moving robot inside real crowds, with fully synchronized multi-modal sensors.\n\n*Also —* Prior robot-collected sets fall short: STCrowd used fixed-viewpoint sensors, and JRDB did not arrange data in trajectory form nor fully time-synchronize its sensors.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nSiT is a large-scale real-world pedestrian trajectory dataset collected by a robot navigating crowded indoor and outdoor scenes, providing synchronized LiDAR, camera, IMU, and RTK raw data, 2D and 3D annotations, multi-layered semantic maps, and a unified benchmark for 3D detection, tracking, trajectory prediction, and end-to-end motion prediction.\n\n*Also —* Precise time synchronization via a centralized PPS sensor-triggering method enables efficient sensor fusion; the dataset, development kit, and trained models are publicly released.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nData was collected with a remotely operated Clearpath Husky UGV equipped with two Velodyne VLP-16 3D LiDARs, five Basler cameras giving a 360-degree view, two IMUs, and RTK; a pulse-per-second signal generator triggers the LiDARs and cameras for precise time synchronization.\n\n*Also —* Robot pose comes from RTK outdoors and LiDAR-inertial SLAM indoors; twelve-layer semantic maps are built from LiDAR point clouds and manually segmented; expert-labeled 3D cuboids at 5 Hz are interpolated to 10 Hz and 2D boxes are derived from the 3D cuboids sharing object IDs. Faces and license plates are blurred for privacy.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nSiT comprises 60 scenes totaling roughly 60K images and 12K point cloud frames, with about 470K 2D annotations and 320K 3D annotations; each clip is 20 seconds of 10 Hz sequential data yielding 9 seconds of trajectory. It defines four benchmarks: 3D pedestrian detection, 3D tracking, trajectory prediction, and end-to-end motion prediction.\n\n*Also —* Detection uses distance-based AP at 0.25/0.5/1.0/2.0 m; tracking uses sAMOTA/AMOTA/AMOTP/MOTA/MOTP/IDS; prediction uses ADE and FDE over K modes with a 2-second input and 7-second future horizon; annotated classes extend beyond pedestrians to car, bus, truck, cyclist, and motorcyclist. Indoor and outdoor scenes in downtown Seoul.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nAnalysis confirms SiT captures the close human-robot interaction absent from driving datasets: pedestrians are concentrated near the robot, and SiT has far more instances satisfying both space-sharing and within-2-meter proximity conditions than nuScenes, Waymo Open, or Argoverse2. Using the provided semantic maps consistently improves trajectory prediction.\n\n*Also —* For trajectory prediction, NSP-SFM with the semantic map achieves the best accuracy (ADE20 0.517, FDE20 0.925), improving over its no-map variant (ADE20 0.634, FDE20 1.087).\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nAdding the semantic map as scene context improves every trajectory-prediction model that can use it: Y-Net improves from ADE20 0.836 / FDE20 1.878 to 0.675 / 1.547, and NSP-SFM from 0.634 / 1.087 to 0.517 / 0.925.\n\n*Also —* On detection, camera-LiDAR fusion beats single-modality models, with TransFusion-V (0.531 mAP) over CenterPoint-V (0.518) and camera-only FCOS3D (0.244); voxel backbones (SECOND) outperform PointPillar backbones.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- 60 scenes; about 60K images and 12K point cloud frames\n\n*Also —* Best tracking CenterPoint tracker sAMOTA 0.6070 / AMOTA 0.2007; sensors: 2 LiDARs, 5 cameras (360°), 2 IMUs, RTK; 20 s clips at 10 Hz, 9 s trajectories.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nSiT is the first trajectory dataset recorded by a robot moving through real crowds with fully synchronized multi-modal sensors, semantic maps, and a unified perception-to-prediction benchmark, giving social navigation research the close human-robot interaction data that driving and fixed-camera datasets lack.\n\n*Also —* Everything, including the development kit and trained baselines, is publicly available.","poster_thumb":"posters/053_sit_dataset_socially_interactive_pedestrian_traj.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/053_sit_dataset_socially_interactive_pedestrian_traj/053_sit_dataset_socially_interactive_pedestrian_traj/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/053_sit_dataset_socially_interactive_pedestrian_traj/053_sit_dataset_socially_interactive_pedestrian_traj/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/053_sit_dataset_socially_interactive_pedestrian_traj/053_sit_dataset_socially_interactive_pedestrian_traj/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/053_sit_dataset_socially_interactive_pedestrian_traj/053_sit_dataset_socially_interactive_pedestrian_traj/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/053_sit_dataset_socially_interactive_pedestrian_traj/053_sit_dataset_socially_interactive_pedestrian_traj/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/053_sit_dataset_socially_interactive_pedestrian_traj/053_sit_dataset_socially_interactive_pedestrian_traj/reel.html"}},{"id":"054","slug":"054_automatic_clipping_differentially_private_deep_l","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Automatic Clipping: Differentially Private Deep Learning Made Easier and Stronger","authors":"Zhiqi Bu¹, Yu-Xiang Wang¹², Sheng Zha¹, George Karypis¹","institutes":"¹AWS AI; ²UC Santa Barbara","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.07136","code_url":"https://github.com/awslabs/fast-differential-privacy","hook":"The paper introduces automatic clipping (AUTO-V and AUTO-S) that removes the clipping threshold from any DP optimizer, gives a non-convex convergence theorem matching standard SGD, and demonstrates state-of-the-art results on vision and lan…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nDifferentially private deep learning depends on per-example gradient clipping, but the clipping threshold R is a fragile, task-specific hyperparameter whose value strongly determines final accuracy.\n\n*Also —* On ImageNet, ResNet18 accuracy can fall from 45% to 31% if R is set 2x too large; SOTA results often need very small R (e.g. R=1 or R=0.1), found only by expensive search.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nTuning the pair (R, learning rate) for large models takes days to months of compute and, because it touches private data, also consumes extra privacy budget, making DP training far harder than standard training.\n\n*Also —* In practice the threshold is set so small that essentially all per-sample gradients are clipped at every iteration, so the exact value of R stops carrying useful magnitude information.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces automatic clipping (AUTO-V and AUTO-S) that removes the clipping threshold from any DP optimizer, gives a non-convex convergence theorem matching standard SGD, and demonstrates state-of-the-art results on vision and language tasks with a one-line code change.\n\n*Also —* It shows any constant R is equivalent to R=1, sets a default stability constant gamma=0.01, and integrates into existing libraries (Opacus, ObJAX) by replacing Abadi's clipping.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nAutomatic clipping replaces Abadi's clipping factor min(R/||g||, 1) with a pure normalization R/||g||, called AUTO-V. To preserve gradient magnitude and escape the resulting \"lazy region,\" AUTO-S divides by ||g|| + gamma with a small stability constant gamma. Because any constant R rescales into the learning rate, R is fixed to 1, leaving a threshold-free optimizer.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\text{Clip}_{\\text{AUTO-V}}(g_i;R)=R/\\lVert g_i\\rVert$ ; $\\text{Clip}_{\\text{AUTO-S}}(g_i;R)=R/(\\lVert g_i\\rVert+\\gamma)$ ; $\\hat g_t=\\sum_i \\frac{g_{t,i}}{\\lVert g_{t,i}\\rVert+\\gamma}+\\sigma\\cdot N(0,I)$\n\n*Also —* AUTO-V maximizes dot-product similarity between the private and true gradient but is scale-invariant, so small gradients cannot vanish; the gamma in AUTO-S restores magnitude so clipped gradients approach g/gamma as g -> 0, enabling convergence to zero gradient norm.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on language tasks with RoBERTa-base/large (MNLI, QQP, QNLI, SST-2) and GPT2/GPT2-medium/large text generation (E2E, DART), plus vision tasks (CIFAR10 with SimCLRv2, ImageNette with ResNet9, ImageNet with ResNet18).\n\n*Also —* Uses standard privacy budgets epsilon=3 and epsilon=8, the exact hyperparameters of prior SOTA (Li et al.), and reports 95% confidence intervals over 5 runs on image tasks.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nAutomatic clipping (AUTO-S) matches or beats state-of-the-art on every task without tuning R: e.g. GPT2 on E2E reaches BLEU 64.18 at epsilon=3 (vs 63.85 prior), and RoBERTa-base SST-2 reaches 92.32% vs 91.86%.\n\n*Also —* On CIFAR10 with SimCLRv2, AUTO-S reaches 92.70% at epsilon=2; RoBERTa-large SST-2 hits 94.61% at epsilon=8; searching only the learning rate (not the (R, learning rate) grid) cuts tuning cost roughly 5x.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nAUTO-S consistently outperforms AUTO-V once magnitude information is preserved, and the method is insensitive to the stability constant gamma: any gamma > 0 yields the same asymptotic convergence rate, justifying the fixed default gamma=0.01.\n\n*Also —* Heatmaps over (R, learning rate) show the AUTO-S column matching the best hand-tuned R across SST2, QNLI, and MNLI, confirming R can be safely eliminated rather than tuned.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- GPT2 on E2E: BLEU 64.18 at epsilon=3 (AUTO-S), vs 63.85 prior SOTA\n\n*Also —* - RoBERTa-large SST-2: 94.61% at epsilon=8 (AUTO-S)\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nPer-sample gradient clipping in DP training does not need a tuned threshold: normalize each gradient and add a tiny stability constant, and you get a threshold-free optimizer that is as private, as fast, and as accurate as the best hand-tuned DP methods.\n\n*Also —* Automatic clipping is a one-line change in existing DP libraries with rigorous convergence guarantees, making DP training as easy as non-private training.","poster_thumb":"posters/054_automatic_clipping_differentially_private_deep_l.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/054_automatic_clipping_differentially_private_deep_l/054_automatic_clipping_differentially_private_deep_l/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/054_automatic_clipping_differentially_private_deep_l/054_automatic_clipping_differentially_private_deep_l/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/054_automatic_clipping_differentially_private_deep_l/054_automatic_clipping_differentially_private_deep_l/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/054_automatic_clipping_differentially_private_deep_l/054_automatic_clipping_differentially_private_deep_l/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/054_automatic_clipping_differentially_private_deep_l/054_automatic_clipping_differentially_private_deep_l/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/054_automatic_clipping_differentially_private_deep_l/054_automatic_clipping_differentially_private_deep_l/reel.html"}},{"id":"055","slug":"055_accurate_and_scalable_estimation_of_epistemic_un","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Accurate and Scalable Estimation of Epistemic Uncertainty for Graph Neural Networks","authors":"Puja Trivedi¹, Mark Heimann², Rushil Anirudh², Danai Koutra¹, Jayaraman J. Thiagarajan²","institutes":"¹University of Michigan; ²Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory","venue":"ICLR 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.10976","code_url":"https://github.com/pujacomputes/gduq","hook":"(1) A case study showing GNN expressivity does not fix confidence-indicator calibration under shift; (2) G-∆UQ, a new single-model UQ method extending stochastic centering to graphs with partial stochasticity, including a pretrained-model v…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nUnder distribution shift, graph neural networks produce unreliable confidence indicators (miscalibrated softmax / entropy scores), yet how and why GNN confidence degrades is far less studied than in vision.\n\n*Also —* Safety-critical GNN deployment depends on these confidence indicators for calibration, OOD rejection, and generalization-gap estimates, so their degradation directly undermines safe use.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nA natural assumption is that more expressive architectures fix calibration; the authors show graph transformers and positional encodings do NOT reliably improve calibration under controlled shifts, motivating an explicit uncertainty-quantification approach instead.\n\n*Also —* Increasing model width or depth can even worsen calibration, so scaling expressivity is not a substitute for principled epistemic uncertainty estimation.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\n(1) A case study showing GNN expressivity does not fix confidence-indicator calibration under shift; (2) G-∆UQ, a new single-model UQ method extending stochastic centering to graphs with partial stochasticity, including a pretrained-model variant; (3) extensive evaluation across three shift types and three safety tasks.\n\n*Also —* The method supports three anchoring variants (node-feature, intermediate MPNN, and READOUT), trading off the level of stochasticity and enabling reuse of pretrained backbones.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nG-∆UQ adapts stochastic centering (anchoring) to graphs: each input is transformed into a relative representation against a random anchor drawn from the training distribution, `[X − c ‖ c]`. Anchoring can be applied at the node features, an intermediate MPNN layer, or after READOUT; a pretrained variant freezes the backbone and only trains an anchored classifier head, giving cheap partial stochasticity.\n\n**Key idea.** Anchored prediction $\\mu(y\\mid\\mathcal{G}_i)=\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{k=1}^{K} f_\\theta([\\mathcal{G}_i, c_k])$; epistemic uncertainty $\\sigma(y\\mid\\mathcal{G}_i)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{K-1}\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\big(f_\\theta([\\mathcal{G}_i, c_k])-\\mu\\big)^2}$; calibrated prediction $\\mu_{\\text{calib}}=\\mu\\,(1-\\sigma)$, with node anchor $c_n \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\sigma)$.\n\n*Also —* At inference, predictions are averaged over K anchors to yield an epistemic-uncertainty estimate; because a graph anchor would introduce spurious edges, anchoring is done in node/hidden-representation space rather than on adjacency structure.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on a controlled Rotated Super-pixel MNIST structural-distortion benchmark, size-generalization datasets (D&D, NCI1, NCI109, PROTEINS with GCN/GIN/PNA backbones), and the GOOD benchmark suite (GOODCMNIST-color, GOODMotif-basis, GOODMotif-size, GOODSST2-length) under both concept and covariate shifts.\n\n*Also —* Baselines include Vanilla, Deep Ensembles (DEns), Temperature scaling (Temp), and Monte-Carlo Dropout (MCD); tasks span calibration, OOD detection (AUROC), and generalization-gap prediction (MAE).\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nG-∆UQ substantially improves expected calibration error under structural distortion without significantly sacrificing accuracy, and produces better-calibrated confidence indicators than popular single-model UQ methods across size, concept, and covariate shifts.\n\n*Also —* When the calibrated confidence indicators are used downstream, G-∆UQ is competitive-to-best for both generalization-gap prediction (lowest MAE among single-model estimators) and OOD detection; the pretrained variant is often the strongest, notably on covariate shift.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nThe choice of anchoring layer matters: on the severe D&D size shift, anchoring after the READOUT layer (last layer) dramatically improves both accuracy and calibration as depth increases, whereas earlier-layer anchoring converges worse; last-layer anchoring is recommended for size shifts.\n\n*Also —* Partial stochasticity (the pretrained, classifier-head-only variant) is shown to be both effective and scalable, and NTK analysis confirms that constant shifts at intermediate layers genuinely change the GNN's function.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nEvaluated across 3 distribution-shift types (size, concept, covariate) and 3 safety tasks (calibration, generalization-gap prediction, OOD detection), against 4 UQ baselines (Vanilla, DEns, Temp, MCD).\n\n*Also —* 3 anchoring variants (node-feature, intermediate-MPNN, READOUT) plus a pretrained partial-stochasticity variant; single-model method (no ensemble needed).\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBigger or more expressive GNNs do not fix confidence calibration under shift; instead, extending stochastic centering to graphs with partial stochasticity (G-∆UQ) yields a scalable, single-model way to get reliable, well-calibrated uncertainty across diverse shifts and safety tasks.\n\n*Also —* A pretrained-backbone variant makes the approach cheap to adopt on existing models, improving practicality.","poster_thumb":"posters/055_accurate_and_scalable_estimation_of_epistemic_un.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/055_accurate_and_scalable_estimation_of_epistemic_un/055_accurate_and_scalable_estimation_of_epistemic_un/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/055_accurate_and_scalable_estimation_of_epistemic_un/055_accurate_and_scalable_estimation_of_epistemic_un/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/055_accurate_and_scalable_estimation_of_epistemic_un/055_accurate_and_scalable_estimation_of_epistemic_un/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/055_accurate_and_scalable_estimation_of_epistemic_un/055_accurate_and_scalable_estimation_of_epistemic_un/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/055_accurate_and_scalable_estimation_of_epistemic_un/055_accurate_and_scalable_estimation_of_epistemic_un/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/055_accurate_and_scalable_estimation_of_epistemic_un/055_accurate_and_scalable_estimation_of_epistemic_un/reel.html"}},{"id":"056","slug":"056_unitabe_a_universal_pretraining_protocol_for_tab","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"UniTabE: A Universal Pretraining Protocol for Tabular Foundation Model in Data Science","authors":"Yazheng Yang¹, YuQi Wang¹, Guang Liu², Ledell Wu³, Qi Liu¹","institutes":"¹The University of Hong Kong; ²Beijing Academy of Artificial Intelligence; ³Creatify AI","venue":"ICLR 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.09249","code_url":"","hook":"The paper introduces UniTabE, a schema-agnostic architecture with per-cell TabUnit feature processing and free-form prompts; curates a 7TB, ~13-billion-sample tabular pretraining corpus; and empirically demonstrates transferable pretraining…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nPretraining has revolutionized NLP and vision, but tabular prediction in data science lacks a universal foundation model because every task carries a different table schema, blocking cross-task knowledge transfer.\n\n*Also —* Prior methods either textualize tables, discarding numerical semantics, or require identical column structure between training and inference, so they cannot generalize across heterogeneous or evolving tables.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nIf a single protocol could ingest arbitrarily structured tables and be pretrained at scale, the resulting model would transfer knowledge across tasks, handle missing values, generalize zero-shot, and absorb newly added columns.\n\n*Also —* Self-supervised objectives similar to masked language modeling have proven that unlabeled data teaches useful representations; the open question is how to apply that recipe to structure-varied tables.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces UniTabE, a schema-agnostic architecture with per-cell TabUnit feature processing and free-form prompts; curates a 7TB, ~13-billion-sample tabular pretraining corpus; and empirically demonstrates transferable pretraining that surpasses XGBoost and prior tabular models.\n\n*Also —* It couples a Transformer encoder with a deliberately shallow autoregressive decoder so most learned knowledge stays in the reusable encoder, and validates the approach on missing-value filling, zero-shot prediction, and incremental-column adaptation.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nEach cell is modeled by TabUnit as a key-value pair: a fuse layer gates data-type information into the column-name vector, a linking layer weaves that column vector into each value token, and the concatenated cell vectors, prefixed by a [CLS] token, are refined by a Transformer encoder. A shallow LSTM decoder, conditioned on the [CLS] state and a free-form prompt, autoregressively generates the target, unifying pretraining and finetuning.\n\n**Key idea.** $v_{cn} = (1-g_{dt})\\,x_{cn} + g_{dt}\\,x_{dt}$ , $v_{cv}^{i} = x_{cv}^{i} + \\alpha \\cdot v_{cn}$ , $g_{dt} = \\mathrm{Sigmoid}(v_{fl}\\,\\mathrm{ReLU}(w_{fl}^{\\top} x_{dt} + b_{fl}))$\n\n*Also —* Pretraining combines two objectives: multi-cell-masking (randomly masking whole cells and predicting their content via maximum log-likelihood) and contrastive learning over row-block anchors/positives/negatives. Finetuning reframes any task as predicting a masked target column with a task-specific prompt.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nThe authors curated a 7TB tabular pretraining corpus from Kaggle spanning 303 domains, 283K tables, and roughly 13 billion examples (averaging 28.7 numerical, 0.4 categorical, and 7.7 textual columns per table). Evaluation uses 12 held-out Kaggle tasks (6 classification, 6 regression) plus 7 standard public tabular benchmarks.\n\n*Also —* The top domains include Investing (71K tables, 1B examples), Time Series (65K), Finance (52K), Economics (47K), and Games (32K); the Kaggle evaluation tasks were explicitly excluded from pretraining to avoid leakage.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn the 7 public tabular benchmarks UniTabE finetune reaches an average AUC of 0.83, surpassing strong baselines such as Tapas (0.81), FT-Transformer (0.80), and XGBoost (0.79); across the 12 held-out Kaggle tasks it outperforms XGBoost and TransTab-LSTM on both classification (AUC) and regression (R²).\n\n*Also —* UniTabE also shows strong zero-shot classification (e.g. 0.94 AUC on IO, 0.89 on CA without target-data finetuning) and the smallest degradation under incremental columns.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nRemoving the linking layer drops average AUC from 0.83 to 0.75 and removing the fuse layer to 0.77; dropping both falls to 0.72, and removing the multi-cell-masking or contrastive-learning objective lowers it to 0.81 and 0.79 respectively, confirming every component contributes.\n\n*Also —* A 1-layer decoder outperforms 3- and 6-layer decoders on downstream AUC, validating the shallow-decoder design, even though deeper decoders store more generative knowledge (higher BLEU).\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- ~13B pretraining samples across 283K tables and 303 domains (7TB corpus)\n\n*Also —* Zero-shot AUC up to 0.94 (IO) without target-data finetuning; UniTabE-large chosen as the balanced model size.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nA schema-agnostic cell encoder plus free-form prompts, pretrained on 13 billion tabular samples, yields the first broadly transferable tabular foundation model that beats XGBoost and adapts to missing values, zero-shot tasks, and growing schemas.\n\n*Also —* UniTabE shows that the pretrain-then-transfer paradigm from NLP is viable for structured tabular data when feature processing respects cell-level structure rather than textualizing it.","poster_thumb":"posters/056_unitabe_a_universal_pretraining_protocol_for_tab.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/056_unitabe_a_universal_pretraining_protocol_for_tab/056_unitabe_a_universal_pretraining_protocol_for_tab/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/056_unitabe_a_universal_pretraining_protocol_for_tab/056_unitabe_a_universal_pretraining_protocol_for_tab/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/056_unitabe_a_universal_pretraining_protocol_for_tab/056_unitabe_a_universal_pretraining_protocol_for_tab/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/056_unitabe_a_universal_pretraining_protocol_for_tab/056_unitabe_a_universal_pretraining_protocol_for_tab/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/056_unitabe_a_universal_pretraining_protocol_for_tab/056_unitabe_a_universal_pretraining_protocol_for_tab/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/056_unitabe_a_universal_pretraining_protocol_for_tab/056_unitabe_a_universal_pretraining_protocol_for_tab/reel.html"}},{"id":"057","slug":"057_high_performance_temporal_reversible_spiking_neu","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"High-Performance Temporal Reversible Spiking Neural Networks with O(L) Training Memory and O(1) Inference Cost","authors":"Jiakui Hu¹, Man Yao², Xuerui Qiu³, Yuhong Chou⁴, Yuxuan Cai⁵, Ning Qiao⁶, Yonghong Tian¹⁷, Bo Xu², Guoqi Li²⁸","institutes":"¹Peking University; ²Institute of Automation, Chinese Academy of Sciences; ³School of Future Technology, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences; ⁴Department of Computing, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University; ⁵01.AI; ⁶SynSense AG Corporation; ⁷Peng Cheng Laboratory; ⁸Key Laboratory of Brain Cognition and Brain-inspired Intelligence Technology","venue":"ICML 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.16466","code_url":"https://github.com/BICLab/T-RevSNN","hook":"They propose T-RevSNN, altering SNN forward propagation so only key spiking neurons carry temporal information via multi-level temporal-reversible connections, giving O(L) training memory; combined with once-only input encoding and a groupe…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nMulti-timestep simulation of SNNs costs O(L×T) training memory and O(T) inference energy; existing training methods reduce one but never both at once.\n\n*Also —* Training a spiking ResNet-19 at 10 timesteps needs about 20× the memory of a plain ResNet-19, blocking SNNs from scaling to large models.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nThe authors observe that temporal gradients in SNNs are largely unimportant, so the temporal dynamics of most neurons can be switched off without hurting accuracy, freeing both memory and compute.\n\n*Also —* Prior work decouples training from timestep (OTTT/SLTT) to cut memory, or fine-tunes to fewer inference steps, but each tackles only the training OR the inference dilemma, never both simultaneously.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThey propose T-RevSNN, altering SNN forward propagation so only key spiking neurons carry temporal information via multi-level temporal-reversible connections, giving O(L) training memory; combined with once-only input encoding and a grouped sub-network organization, inference becomes O(1).\n\n*Also —* They redesign the basic SNN block (ConvNeXt-style, BN-free, depth-wise/point-wise convolutions) and add a scaled residual connection to make sparse temporal information interaction effective.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nThe image is encoded once, split into T groups fed one per timestep, and the network is divided into T parameter-sharing sub-networks; most neurons are temporally \"turned off\" (no temporal state), while a few key \"turned-on\" neurons pass information through multi-level temporal-reversible connections so activations can be recomputed instead of stored.\n\n**Key idea.** $V^{l}[t+1]=\\sum_{i=l}^{L}\\left(1-\\tfrac{1}{\\tau_i}\\right)V^{i}[t]+\\tfrac{1}{\\tau_m}W_l S^{l-1}[t+1]$ ; $V^{l}[t]=\\left(1-\\tfrac{1}{\\tau_m}\\right)^{-1}\\sum_{i=l+1}^{L}\\left(1-\\tfrac{1}{\\tau_i}\\right)\\left(V^{i}[t+1]-\\tfrac{1}{\\tau_m}W_l S^{l-1}[t+1]\\right)$\n\n*Also —* Reversibility means only the last-timestep membrane potentials must be kept during backward, yielding O(L) memory; feature fusion across stages (with up/down-sampling alignment) builds the multi-level temporal interaction.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on static ImageNet-1K (224×224) and neuromorphic CIFAR10-DVS and DVS128 Gesture, with training memory and time measured on 6 NVIDIA A100-40GB GPUs under float16 mixed precision.\n\n*Also —* Memory is reported as peak GPU memory per image (MB/img) following S-RevSNN; baselines span spiking ResNets, spiking Transformers, and training-optimization SNNs (OTTT/SLTT/S-RevSNN).\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn ImageNet-1K, T-RevSNN (ResNet-18, 512 ch, 29.8M params, T=4) reaches 73.2% top-1 at 85.7 MB/img and 2.8 mJ, the best accuracy among CNN-based spiking ResNets with the lowest memory, training time, and inference energy.\n\n*Also —* Versus the Spike-driven Transformer (74.6%), T-RevSNN improves memory efficiency 8.6×, training-time 2.0×, and inference energy 1.6×, at comparable accuracy; a smaller 15.2M variant hits 69.8% at just 57.5 MB/img.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nApplying T-RevSNN to an MS-ResNet-34 baseline cuts training memory from 267.1 to 88.1 MB/img and epoch time from 11.2 to 7.4 min with only a 1.6% accuracy change (68.3%→66.7%); multi-level temporal fusion is worth 1.2% accuracy (68.6% vs 67.4%).\n\n*Also —* Timestep T trades accuracy for cost (T=8 gives 69.8% at 49.8 MB/img); the scaled residual connection speeds convergence (25 vs 32 epochs to 60% accuracy); temporal and spatial reversibility are orthogonal and combine.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n8.6× memory efficiency, 2.0× training-time acceleration, and 1.6× inference-energy efficiency versus a leading spiking Transformer.\n\n*Also —* 73.2% ImageNet-1K top-1 accuracy; O(L) training memory and O(1) inference cost; 85.7 MB/img training memory at 2.8 mJ inference energy.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBecause most temporal gradients in SNNs don't matter, turning off most neurons' temporal dynamics and making the rest reversible delivers O(L) training memory and O(1) inference cost while keeping high accuracy.\n\n*Also —* T-RevSNN removes the memory/training-time bottleneck that has kept SNNs from scaling, pointing toward larger, more practical spiking networks.","poster_thumb":"posters/057_high_performance_temporal_reversible_spiking_neu.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/057_high_performance_temporal_reversible_spiking_neu/057_high_performance_temporal_reversible_spiking_neu/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/057_high_performance_temporal_reversible_spiking_neu/057_high_performance_temporal_reversible_spiking_neu/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/057_high_performance_temporal_reversible_spiking_neu/057_high_performance_temporal_reversible_spiking_neu/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/057_high_performance_temporal_reversible_spiking_neu/057_high_performance_temporal_reversible_spiking_neu/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/057_high_performance_temporal_reversible_spiking_neu/057_high_performance_temporal_reversible_spiking_neu/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/057_high_performance_temporal_reversible_spiking_neu/057_high_performance_temporal_reversible_spiking_neu/reel.html"}},{"id":"058","slug":"058_unveiling_privacy_memorization_and_input_curvatu","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Unveiling Privacy, Memorization, and Input Curvature Links","authors":"Deepak Ravikumar¹, Efstathia Soufleri¹, Abolfazl Hashemi¹, Kaushik Roy¹","institutes":"¹Purdue University","venue":"ICML 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.18726","code_url":"","hook":"Three theorems: (1) input loss curvature upper-bounds memorization; (2) the differential-privacy parameter upper-bounds average input loss curvature; (3) privacy upper-bounds memorization, all validated empirically.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nInput loss curvature is used as a cheap proxy for memorization in deep nets, but there is no theory explaining why the two are linked, or how either relates to differential privacy.\n\n*Also —* Feldman's stability-based memorization score is principled but computationally prohibitive, motivating proxies whose validity was purely empirical until now.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nA rigorous curvature-memorization-privacy link would justify using cheap curvature as a memorization proxy and reveal how privacy mechanisms suppress memorization.\n\n*Also —* Influence functions, the usual counterfactual tool, assume Hessian convexity and positive-definiteness, conditions that fail for deep nets; curvature needs no such assumptions.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThree theorems: (1) input loss curvature upper-bounds memorization; (2) the differential-privacy parameter upper-bounds average input loss curvature; (3) privacy upper-bounds memorization, all validated empirically.\n\n*Also —* The analysis makes no assumptions about Hessian convexity or definiteness, unlike influence-function theory, making it suitable for deep networks.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nUnder error-stability, generalization, uniform-model-bias, and dataset adjacency assumptions with bounded loss, a second-order (Nesterov-Polyak) expansion of the loss plus DP definitions yield the three bounds. Curvature is estimated efficiently via Hutchinson's trace estimator.\n\n**Key idea.** $|\\mathrm{mem}(A,S,i)| \\le \\tfrac{1}{L}\\,\\mathbb{E}_\\phi[\\mathrm{Curv}_\\phi(z_i,S^{\\setminus i})] + c_1$ and $\\mathbb{E}_{z,\\phi}[\\mathrm{Curv}_\\phi(z,S)] \\le L(m+1)(1-e^{-\\epsilon}) + c_2$\n\n*Also —* Bounds are validated using Feldman & Zhang's precomputed memorization scores and models (1000 for CIFAR100, 100 for ImageNet); privacy models are trained with DP-SGD at several epsilon budgets. Curvature uses step h=1e-3 and n=10 Rademacher vectors.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nStandard image classification benchmarks: CIFAR10, CIFAR100, and ImageNet, using Feldman & Zhang (2020) precomputed memorization scores and their released model ensembles.\n\n*Also —* Architectures include Small Inception and ResNet50 (CIFAR100 / ImageNet memorization ensembles) and ResNet18 trained with DP-SGD for the privacy experiments.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nAll three theoretical bounds hold empirically: memorization vs. curvature shows a strong linear trend on CIFAR100 and ImageNet, and stronger privacy (smaller epsilon) produces measurably lower curvature and memorization, matching the predicted best-fit curves.\n\n*Also —* The linear memorization-curvature relationship is especially pronounced on ImageNet; as the DP budget epsilon increases, memorization of the top-500 most-memorized examples rises as predicted, staying below the Theorem 5.4 bound.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nCurvature is estimated with Hutchinson's trace estimator (h=1e-3, n=10 Rademacher vectors); privacy is swept over epsilon = 1, 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 with delta = 1e-5, and curvature is averaged over 10 seeds per budget.\n\n*Also —* The top-500-most-memorized study splits CIFAR100 to isolate high-memorization samples, showing their memorization scores fall sharply once DP is applied and rise back as epsilon grows.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Input loss curvature is ~3 orders of magnitude more efficient to compute than Feldman's memorization score.\n\n*Also —* Three theorems (5.1, 5.3, 5.4) link curvature, privacy, and memorization; DP-SGD gradient clipping norm 1.0, learning rate 0.001, batch size 128.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nInput loss curvature provably upper-bounds memorization and is itself bounded by the differential-privacy parameter, giving a rigorous, assumption-light theory that justifies curvature as a cheap memorization proxy and links both to privacy.\n\n*Also —* The framework needs no Hessian-convexity assumptions, so it applies directly to deep networks, and its predictions match CIFAR and ImageNet experiments closely.","poster_thumb":"posters/058_unveiling_privacy_memorization_and_input_curvatu.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/058_unveiling_privacy_memorization_and_input_curvatu/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/058_unveiling_privacy_memorization_and_input_curvatu/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/058_unveiling_privacy_memorization_and_input_curvatu/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/058_unveiling_privacy_memorization_and_input_curvatu/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/058_unveiling_privacy_memorization_and_input_curvatu/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/058_unveiling_privacy_memorization_and_input_curvatu/reel.html"}},{"id":"059","slug":"059_inverse_transition_learning_for_characterizing_n","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Bayesian Inverse Transition Learning for Offline Settings","authors":"Leo Benac¹, Sonali Parbhoo², Finale Doshi-Velez¹","institutes":"¹Harvard University; ²Imperial College London","venue":"ICML 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.05075","code_url":"","hook":"The paper introduces Inverse Transition Learning (ITL): a gradient-free, constraint-based approach that turns a near-optimal expert policy into constraints on T, then clips a Bayesian posterior over T so every sampled dynamics yields a safe…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nIn offline RL for healthcare and education, the transition dynamics T must be estimated from fixed batch data, but standard MLE estimates yield high-variance policies that take unsafe actions wherever the data gives poor state-action coverage.\n\n*Also —* The number of dynamics parameters grows with the state and action space, and offline settings forbid gathering new data to fill the gaps left by the users who produced the batch.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nUsers who generate offline trajectories (e.g. clinicians) are usually near-optimal, so their demonstrations carry information about which actions are good that a plain MLE of T completely ignores.\n\n*Also —* Prior gradient-based inverse RL methods estimate an expert's belief of the dynamics but never relate the estimated T to the true T, and they inherit the instabilities of gradient optimization in tabular MDPs.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces Inverse Transition Learning (ITL): a gradient-free, constraint-based approach that turns a near-optimal expert policy into constraints on T, then clips a Bayesian posterior over T so every sampled dynamics yields a safe, high-performing policy.\n\n*Also —* It also delivers a comprehensive analysis of when and why MLE of T fails under uneven coverage, and shows how coupling constraints with uncertainty produces an informative ranking of actions in uncertain states.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nGiven rewards R, an ε-optimal expert policy πϵ, and batch data D, the method builds constraints (from the closed-form Bellman equations) requiring that expert actions out-value never-taken actions, so any T satisfying them recovers the expert's \"ε-ball\" structure. It places a Dirichlet-Multinomial posterior P(T|D) over the dynamics and uses rejection sampling to keep only samples that satisfy the constraints, giving the clipped posterior P(T|D, πϵ).\n\n**Key idea.** $V^{\\pi} = R_{\\pi} + \\gamma T_{\\pi} V^{\\pi} = (I-\\gamma T_{\\pi})^{-1} R_{\\pi}$, $T_{a^*}(I-\\gamma \\hat{T}_{\\pi_\\epsilon})^{-1} R_{\\pi_\\epsilon} = \\tfrac{1}{\\gamma}\\big((I-\\gamma \\hat{T}_{\\pi_\\epsilon})^{-1} R_{\\pi_\\epsilon} - R_{a^*}\\big)$, $Q^*_{metric}(\\hat{\\pi}) = \\sum_{s\\in S}\\big(Q^*(s,a^*) - \\sum_{a\\in A}\\hat{\\pi}(a|s)Q^*(s,a)\\big)$\n\n*Also —* Separate constraint sets handle a fully optimal expert and a sub-optimal (stochastic-policy) expert; a tunable slack δ per state-action enforces the ε-ball property even where action non-linearity would otherwise break it (Algorithm 1).\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nExperiments use a synthetic tabular MDP with 15 states (+1 terminal) and 6 actions, discount γ=0.95, with dynamics that are sometimes uniform and sometimes highly skewed to create varied behavior.\n\n*Also —* Two coverage regimes (low data K=15 episodes, high data K=300) are crossed with three optimality levels ε=0 (0 stochastic states), ε=3 (3 stochastic states), ε=4 (6 stochastic states); every result is averaged over 1000 datasets.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nThe clipped posterior P(T|D, πϵ) enforces 100% ± 0 accuracy on deterministic-policy states in every setting (vs MLE's 67-92%) and never picks an action outside the expert's ε-ball, so it dominates both MLE and the un-clipped posterior P(T|D) on the Q*metric.\n\n*Also —* On stochastic-policy states the constraints, though silent about which action to take, still yield higher accuracy than MLE and P(T|D), showing the constraints implicitly transfer uncertainty structure.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nVarying data volume shows more data does NOT rescue MLE: it keeps making \"bad mistakes\" (actions outside the ε-ball) because large regions of the state-action space stay unexplored under expert-only data (aleatoric uncertainty).\n\n*Also —* Sweeping ε=0/3/4 confirms the method holds across degrees of expert optimality, recovering policies that can beat the expert while keeping near-zero variance.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nAt ε=0 the method reaches Q*metric = 0 ± 0 in both low and high data, versus MLE 59.75 ± 51.99 (low) and P(T|D) 142.17 ± 8.66.\n\n*Also —* At ε=4 high data it scores 8.79 ± 0.6 versus MLE 20.11 ± 9.85 while roughly matching the expert's 9.4; deterministic-state accuracy is a constant 100% ± 0, and policy standard deviation collapses from ±51.99 (MLE) to near ±0.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy clipping a Bayesian posterior over transition dynamics with expert-derived constraints, you get gradient-free offline policies that are provably safe, can outperform the expert, and have dramatically lower variance than MLE.\n\n*Also —* The same constraints-plus-uncertainty recipe yields an action ranking in uncertain states, making the learned policies more informative for high-stakes planning such as healthcare.","poster_thumb":"posters/059_inverse_transition_learning_for_characterizing_n.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/059_inverse_transition_learning_for_characterizing_n/059_inverse_transition_learning_for_characterizing_n/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/059_inverse_transition_learning_for_characterizing_n/059_inverse_transition_learning_for_characterizing_n/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/059_inverse_transition_learning_for_characterizing_n/059_inverse_transition_learning_for_characterizing_n/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/059_inverse_transition_learning_for_characterizing_n/059_inverse_transition_learning_for_characterizing_n/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/059_inverse_transition_learning_for_characterizing_n/059_inverse_transition_learning_for_characterizing_n/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/059_inverse_transition_learning_for_characterizing_n/059_inverse_transition_learning_for_characterizing_n/reel.html"}},{"id":"060","slug":"060_active_learning_with_table_language_models","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Active Learning with Tabular Language Models","authors":"Martin Ringsquandl¹, Aneta Koleva¹²","institutes":"¹Siemens AG; ²LMU Munich","venue":"NeurIPS 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.04128","code_url":"","hook":"The paper presents the first industrial NER use case pairing tabular language models with active learning, outlines the novel problems tabular data raises for active learning, and adapts well-known acquisition functions to tables with an em…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nFine-tuning tabular language models for industrial tasks needs many labels, but only experts can annotate the highly technical, domain-specific spreadsheet tables, making label acquisition prohibitively expensive.\n\n*Also —* Sub-cell named entity recognition on tables is a novel *nested multi-instance* problem: each table holds many cells, and each cell carries multiple token labels, so it is unclear whether to acquire at the token, cell, or table level.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nSettings with abundant unlabeled data and costly expert labels are a natural fit for active learning, yet no prior work combines active learning with tabular language models.\n\n*Also —* Large transformers require batch-based acquisition (not one-by-one selection) and train for multiple epochs per round, so naive uncertainty sampling picks highly correlated, redundant cells that add little value.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper presents the first industrial NER use case pairing tabular language models with active learning, outlines the novel problems tabular data raises for active learning, and adapts well-known acquisition functions to tables with an empirical evaluation on a real-world dataset.\n\n*Also —* It argues that a cell-level acquisition function is sufficient because the tabular language model already encodes full-table context, avoiding the need for a separate hierarchical table-then-cell selection.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nA table-biased transformer encoder (using a row-column visibility matrix and within-cell positional encoding) produces context-sensitive token representations, and a classification decoder assigns IO NER tags over the entity types TAG, EQ, QUANT, and UoM. Pool-based active learning then iteratively selects batches of the most informative cells for an oracle to label.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\arg\\min_{X_S}\\ \\mathbb{E}_{x\\sim P}\\left[\\ell(f_\\theta(x), y)\\right]$ ; $f_{acq}^{MNLP}(c_{i,j};f_\\theta)=\\frac{1}{t}\\sum_{z=1}^{t}\\log y^{*}_{i,j,z}$ ; $f_{acq}^{BADGE}(c_{i,j};f_\\theta)=\\text{k-MEANS++}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\sum_z g_{i,j,z}\\right)$\n\n*Also —* Four cell-level acquisition functions are compared: MNLP (pure uncertainty via maximized normalized log-probability), MNLP+ (MNLP plus a forced round-robin maximum-table-diversity constraint), BADGE (k-MEANS++ over per-cell gradient embeddings for batch diversity), and Rand (uniform random cells) as a baseline.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nA real-world industrial dataset of downsampled spreadsheets from multiple plants (max 5 rows per table), expert-annotated cell-by-cell with prodi.gy; a random split yields 55 training tables and 24 test tables.\n\n*Also —* The 55 training tables hold 4,774 cells but only 1,112 NER span labels (0.23 labels per cell), so roughly 77% of cells contain only O-tagged tokens, an extreme class imbalance typical of industrial settings.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nBADGE is the only acquisition function that clearly beats random selection and even exceeds full-training performance, reaching the ceiling with far fewer labels; MNLP only slightly edges Rand after about 500 labels.\n\n*Also —* MNLP+ (forced maximum table diversity) is by far the worst, showing that over-enforcing table diversity is detrimental; MNLP also shows higher variance, indicating greater sensitivity to the initial seed in this high-imbalance regime.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nComparing MNLP, MNLP+, BADGE, and Rand isolates the effect of diversity: built-in batch diversity (BADGE) helps, while forced maximum table diversity (MNLP+) is detrimental, dropping well below the random baseline.\n\n*Also —* A table-diversity metric (number of distinct tables sampled per iteration) shows MNLP fixates on few tables, MNLP+ spreads across as many tables as the budget allows, and BADGE sits in between, balancing table diversity against cell uncertainty.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- **55** training tables, **24** test tables\n\n*Also —* BADGE exceeds the full-training F1 ceiling; MNLP only marginally beats Rand after ~500 labels; backbone is bert-base-uncased, up to 30 epochs per iteration.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nFor active learning with tabular language models, cell-level acquisition with built-in batch diversity (BADGE) can hit full-training performance with far fewer expert labels, but forcing maximum table diversity backfires: balancing diversity against uncertainty is the key.\n\n*Also —* This is the first study of active learning for tabular language models, leaving open questions about computational efficiency and the burden that table-diverse acquisition places on human annotators.","poster_thumb":"posters/060_active_learning_with_table_language_models.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/060_active_learning_with_table_language_models/060_active_learning_with_table_language_models/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/060_active_learning_with_table_language_models/060_active_learning_with_table_language_models/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/060_active_learning_with_table_language_models/060_active_learning_with_table_language_models/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/060_active_learning_with_table_language_models/060_active_learning_with_table_language_models/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/060_active_learning_with_table_language_models/060_active_learning_with_table_language_models/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/060_active_learning_with_table_language_models/060_active_learning_with_table_language_models/reel.html"}},{"id":"061","slug":"061_unimax_fairer_and_more_effective_language_sampli","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"UniMax: Fairer and More Effective Language Sampling for Large-Scale Multilingual Pretraining","authors":"Hyung Won Chung¹, Noah Constant¹, Xavier Garcia¹, Adam Roberts¹, Yi Tay¹, Sharan Narang¹, Orhan Firat¹","institutes":"¹Google Research","venue":"ICLR 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.09151","code_url":"https://github.com/google-research/t5x","hook":"(1) UniMax, a simple sampling method that allocates budget uniformly while capping per-language repeats; (2) an extensive ablation of sampling strategies across model scales; (3) a refreshed mC4 corpus of 29 trillion characters across 107 l…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nMultilingual pretraining faces severe data imbalance: in mC4, English has ~9.7 trillion characters, over 92,000× the lowest-resource language, Yoruba. How to balance the languages is an open, expensive question.\n\n*Also —* The dominant fix, temperature sampling with hyperparameter τ, has never been systematically evaluated across model scales.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nTemperature sampling tuned for head languages over-repeats the tail: at τ=3.33 with a trillion-token budget, the lowest-resource languages are repeated over 100 times.\n\n*Also —* Excessive repetition causes overfitting that degrades downstream tasks, raises the risk of memorizing private content, and wastes compute on duplicate examples, and these harms worsen as models scale.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\n(1) UniMax, a simple sampling method that allocates budget uniformly while capping per-language repeats; (2) an extensive ablation of sampling strategies across model scales; (3) a refreshed mC4 corpus of 29 trillion characters across 107 languages; (4) released umT5 checkpoints trained with UniMax.\n\n*Also —* Analysis of how sampling choice reshapes the learned vocabulary's capacity allocation across language scripts.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nUniMax (uniform + max) starts from a fixed character budget C and distributes it as uniformly as possible across languages, processed from lowest to highest resource. At each step it checks whether the remaining per-language budget can be split uniformly; if a language would exceed N epochs over its corpus, it is capped at N epochs and the freed budget is redistributed uniformly among the rest.\n\n**Key idea.** $U_l = \\min\\left(N \\cdot D_l,\\ \\dfrac{B}{|L| - i}\\right),\\qquad B \\leftarrow B - U_l$ <!-- languages sorted ascending by size; D_l = characters in language l, B = remaining budget, i = index of current language, N = max epochs -->\n\n*Also —* This delivers more uniform coverage of head languages than temperature sampling while preventing tail languages from being repeated more than N times. Default N=1 disallows any repeats.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nPretraining uses a refreshed mC4 corpus (29 trillion characters, 107 languages). Evaluation spans TyDi QA GoldP, WMT21 multilingual translation, XNLI, XQuAD, MLQA, and PAWS-X.\n\n*Also —* Sampling strategies are compared while varying model scale from Small up to XXL, isolating whether benefits persist with size.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nUniMax consistently outperforms τ=1 and τ=3.33 on average TyDi QA across all three model sizes, and outperforms temperature sampling on WMT21 across all scales, with the majority of language pairs benefiting.\n\n*Also —* On high-resource TyDi languages UniMax beats τ=3.33 and only trails τ=1 at the largest scale; it wins on low-resource languages, e.g. outperforming τ=3.33 on Swahili despite seeing fewer Swahili examples.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nAblating the max-epoch parameter N∈{1,5,10} on Large models gives TyDi QA of 82.2 (N=1), 81.5 (N=5), 81.8 (N=10) — best when disallowing repeats entirely, though the effect is small. At a 4× larger (1/2) budget, UniMax scores 83.1 vs 82.8 (τ=3.33) and 81.2 (τ=1).\n\n*Also —* Loss curves show overfitting under high temperature grows more severe with model size, while UniMax stays stable; the optimal N depends on the character budget.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Refreshed mC4: **29 trillion characters** across **107 languages** (9.0 billion documents, a 35% size increase).\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nCapping per-language repeats and spreading the rest of the budget uniformly beats temperature sampling for multilingual pretraining, and the advantage holds as models scale.\n\n*Also —* UniMax is a drop-in, hyperparameter-light replacement for temperature sampling, shipped with a refreshed corpus and umT5 checkpoints.","poster_thumb":"posters/061_unimax_fairer_and_more_effective_language_sampli.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/061_unimax_fairer_and_more_effective_language_sampli/061_unimax_fairer_and_more_effective_language_sampli/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/061_unimax_fairer_and_more_effective_language_sampli/061_unimax_fairer_and_more_effective_language_sampli/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/061_unimax_fairer_and_more_effective_language_sampli/061_unimax_fairer_and_more_effective_language_sampli/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/061_unimax_fairer_and_more_effective_language_sampli/061_unimax_fairer_and_more_effective_language_sampli/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/061_unimax_fairer_and_more_effective_language_sampli/061_unimax_fairer_and_more_effective_language_sampli/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/061_unimax_fairer_and_more_effective_language_sampli/061_unimax_fairer_and_more_effective_language_sampli/reel.html"}},{"id":"062","slug":"062_a_theoretical_analysis_of_the_test_error_of_fini","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"A Theoretical Analysis of the Test Error of Finite-Rank Kernel Ridge Regression","authors":"Tin Sum Cheng¹, Aurelien Lucchi¹, Ivan Dokmanić¹, Anastasis Kratsios²³, David Belius⁴","institutes":"¹University of Basel; ²McMaster University; ³Vector Institute; ⁴UniDistance Suisse","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.00987","code_url":"","hook":"The paper derives sharp non-asymptotic high-probability upper AND lower bounds on the finite-rank KRR test error in the under-parameterized regime (sample size N greater than rank M), valid for any ridge λ.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nExisting statistical learning guarantees give loose, often vacuous bounds on the test error of finite-rank kernel ridge regression (KRR), especially in the ridgeless regime and without matching lower bounds.\n\n*Also —* Finite-rank kernels arise naturally when only the final layer of a pre-trained deep network is fine-tuned for transfer learning, so tight guarantees here matter in practice.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nPrior finite-rank analyses either rely on asymptotic, high-dimensional limits, hold only for specific ridge schedules, or bound only the expected error, none of which certify a given finite-sample KRR run.\n\n*Also —* Because deep transfer learning freezes early layers and retrains only the head, the induced kernel is genuinely finite-rank, so a non-asymptotic theory tailored to this regime is needed.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper derives sharp non-asymptotic high-probability upper AND lower bounds on the finite-rank KRR test error in the under-parameterized regime (sample size N greater than rank M), valid for any ridge λ. It (i) gives non-vacuous bounds in the ridgeless λ→0 limit, (ii) provides a lower bound that matches the upper bound as N grows, and (iii) validates the improvement empirically.\n\n*Also —* A key technical move is analyzing the target in the L²ρ basis {ψk} rather than the RKHS basis, decoupling the spectrum from the sampling randomness to obtain sharper constants.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nThey study the finite-rank KRR estimator and decompose its test error via the classical bias-variance split, then bound bias and variance separately with high probability. The proof combines careful algebra in the ridge terms with a sub-Gaussian covariance-matrix concentration result plus a Neumann-series expansion of the matrix inverse, so the bounds hold for any λ chosen independently of N.\n\n**Key idea.** $f_{Z,\\lambda} = \\arg\\min_{f\\in\\mathcal{H}} \\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}(f(x_i)-y_i)^2 + \\lambda\\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}}^2$ ; $R_{Z,\\lambda} = \\mathbb{E}_{x,\\epsilon}\\big[(f_{Z,\\lambda}(x)-\\tilde f(x))^2\\big] = \\mathrm{bias} + \\mathrm{variance}$ ; $\\mathrm{variance} \\le \\sigma^2\\frac{2M}{N}\\Big(1+\\sqrt{\\tfrac{\\log N}{N}}\\Big) + C_2\\frac{\\log N}{N}$\n\n*Also —* The target function f̃ is decomposed into an in-RKHS part f̃≤M and an orthogonal complement γ̃>M ψ>M (an input-dependent noise term); the complementary coefficient γ̃>M captures the irreducible finite-rank error that never vanishes even as N→∞.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nValidation is on two synthetic finite-rank kernels: the truncated Neural Tangent Kernel (tNTK) and a low-rank kernel (LK), with KRR run over a range of sample sizes N (about 10 to 200) and ridge values, repeated over 10 random trials.\n\n*Also —* No natural-image dataset is used; the paper is theoretical and uses controlled kernels where the eigenspectrum and target decomposition are known, so the bounds can be checked directly against measured test error.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nThe derived upper and lower bounds sandwich the empirically measured test error across the full range of N on both the tNTK and LK kernels, and they converge together as N grows, confirming tightness. Against the closest prior bound (Bach's), the new upper bound is dramatically lower, tracking the true error while Bach's bound sits orders of magnitude above it.\n\n*Also —* In the ridgeless limit the bias is controlled solely by the finite-rank error γ̃>M², and the variance is controlled solely by the noise variance σ², with the upper and lower bounds coinciding as N→∞.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nThe analysis is decomposed to show each term's role: the finite-rank error term γ̃>M² is the irreducible floor that never vanishes with more data; the residue terms proportional to ‖f̃‖² and the C₁, C₂ constants decay like √(log N / N) and are negligible for large N. Figure 3 shows that dropping these residue terms makes the simplified bounds fail to bracket the error only for small N.\n\n*Also —* Varying the ridge (Figure 2, right) shows the new bound improves monotonically as 1/λ grows (ridgeless direction), whereas Bach's bound degrades, isolating the ridge-dependence as the main source of the improvement.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Bounds hold with probability at least 1 − 2/N with respect to the random sampling.\n\n*Also —* - New two-sided bounds are the first to supply a high-probability LOWER bound on finite-rank KRR test error (prior works: none).\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nFinite-rank KRR, the model behind last-layer fine-tuning, admits sharp two-sided non-asymptotic test-error bounds that stay tight for any ridge, including ridgeless, giving a much more accurate certificate of generalization than earlier one-sided or asymptotic results.\n\n*Also —* The improvement comes from analyzing the target in the eigenfunction (L²ρ) basis, decoupling spectrum from sampling noise.","poster_thumb":"posters/062_a_theoretical_analysis_of_the_test_error_of_fini.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/062_a_theoretical_analysis_of_the_test_error_of_fini/062_a_theoretical_analysis_of_the_test_error_of_fini/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/062_a_theoretical_analysis_of_the_test_error_of_fini/062_a_theoretical_analysis_of_the_test_error_of_fini/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/062_a_theoretical_analysis_of_the_test_error_of_fini/062_a_theoretical_analysis_of_the_test_error_of_fini/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/062_a_theoretical_analysis_of_the_test_error_of_fini/062_a_theoretical_analysis_of_the_test_error_of_fini/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/062_a_theoretical_analysis_of_the_test_error_of_fini/062_a_theoretical_analysis_of_the_test_error_of_fini/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/062_a_theoretical_analysis_of_the_test_error_of_fini/062_a_theoretical_analysis_of_the_test_error_of_fini/reel.html"}},{"id":"063","slug":"063_a_view_from_somewhere_human_centric_face_represe","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"A View From Somewhere: Human-Centric Face Representations","authors":"Jerone T. A. Andrews¹, Przemysław Joniak², Alice Xiang¹","institutes":"¹Sony AI; ²University of Tokyo","venue":"ICLR 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.17176","code_url":"https://github.com/SonyAI/a_view_from_somewhere","hook":"The authors release AVFS, a dataset of 638,180 human triplet judgments over 4,921 faces annotated with annotator identity and demographics, and introduce a conditional framework that learns a continuous, human-interpretable face embedding s…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nHuman-centric face datasets are typically evaluated with categorical demographic labels, but such labels are often missing, legally restricted, biased when inferred, and fail to capture the continuous nature of human phenotypic diversity.\n\n*Also —* Categorical labels cannot express how similar two same-labeled faces are, and rigid taxonomies erase multi-ethnic individuals and can cause psychological harm through mislabeling.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nFace similarity is inherently continuous and observer-dependent, so a representation aligned with human perception can avoid problematic semantic labels while still supporting diversity analysis.\n\n*Also —* Prior work either cannot embed faces outside the training set or pools all judgments, ignoring the stochasticity within and between individual annotators.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe authors release AVFS, a dataset of 638,180 human triplet judgments over 4,921 faces annotated with annotator identity and demographics, and introduce a conditional framework that learns a continuous, human-interpretable face embedding space.\n\n*Also —* Annotator-specific masks let the model study how each person weighs embedding dimensions, revealing that annotators are not interchangeable and that sociocultural background shapes similarity decisions.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nA conditional ResNet18 convnet maps faces to 128-dimensional embeddings and is trained on odd-one-out (3AFC) triplet judgments. Each annotator gets a learnable sigmoid mask that gates the embedding dimensions, so similarity is conditioned on who made the judgment.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\text{sim}_a(i,j) = \\big(\\sigma(\\phi_a)\\odot \\mathrm{ReLU}(w_i)\\big)^\\top\\big(\\sigma(\\phi_a)\\odot \\mathrm{ReLU}(w_j)\\big)$, $\\hat{p}(k_\\ell\\mid a)=\\dfrac{\\exp(\\text{sim}_a(i_\\ell,j_\\ell))}{\\exp(\\text{sim}_a(i_\\ell,j_\\ell))+\\exp(\\text{sim}_a(i_\\ell,k_\\ell))+\\exp(\\text{sim}_a(j_\\ell,k_\\ell))}$\n\n*Also —* Setting all masks to ones recovers the unconditional MDS objective (AVFS-U); conditional variants are AVFS-C and post-hoc AVFS-CPH. A sparsity plus non-negativity penalty makes the surviving dimensions interpretable, yielding 22 active dimensions.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nAVFS contains 638,180 quality-controlled odd-one-out triplet judgments over 4,921 near-frontal face images, each judgment tagged with the annotator's identifier and self-identified demographic attributes.\n\n*Also —* Evaluation uses held-out same-stimuli triplets (24,060 judgments), novel-stimuli triplets (80,300 judgments), and comparisons against semantically trained baselines on CelebA and FairFace attributes.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nAVFS models predict face similarity more accurately than semantically trained baselines on both same-stimuli and novel-stimuli triplets, while better reflecting human judgment uncertainty (higher Spearman's r on odd-one-out entropy).\n\n*Also —* Annotator-specific masks generalize to triplets of entirely novel faces, indicating the embedding space is more closely aligned with the human mental representation of faces than label-trained spaces.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nRandomly swapping annotator masks across the 80,300 judgments drops AVFS-CPH accuracy from 61.7% to 52.8% ± 0.02%, showing annotators are not interchangeable.\n\n*Also —* Dimension-elimination shows only 6–13 dimensions recover 95–99% of predictive accuracy, while 15–22 dimensions are needed to explain 95–99% of the similarity-matrix variance, evidencing context-dependent similarity.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n638,180 human face-similarity judgments over 4,921 faces; 22 interpretable dimensions retained at 61.9% validation accuracy; annotator-swap accuracy drop 61.7% to 52.8%; continuous attributes recovered without any semantic training labels.\n\n*Also —* 128-dimensional ResNet18 embeddings; 24,060 same-stimuli and 80,300 novel-stimuli evaluation judgments.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nLearning face representations from human similarity judgments, rather than demographic labels, yields a continuous, interpretable, and perception-aligned embedding space that supports diversity analysis without invasive categorical labeling.\n\n*Also —* Because the conditional framework only needs annotator record-keeping, it generalizes to any human decision-making task, not just faces.","poster_thumb":"posters/063_a_view_from_somewhere_human_centric_face_represe.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/063_a_view_from_somewhere_human_centric_face_represe/063_a_view_from_somewhere_human_centric_face_represe/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/063_a_view_from_somewhere_human_centric_face_represe/063_a_view_from_somewhere_human_centric_face_represe/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/063_a_view_from_somewhere_human_centric_face_represe/063_a_view_from_somewhere_human_centric_face_represe/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/063_a_view_from_somewhere_human_centric_face_represe/063_a_view_from_somewhere_human_centric_face_represe/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/063_a_view_from_somewhere_human_centric_face_represe/063_a_view_from_somewhere_human_centric_face_represe/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/063_a_view_from_somewhere_human_centric_face_represe/063_a_view_from_somewhere_human_centric_face_represe/reel.html"}},{"id":"064","slug":"064_comparing_optimization_targets_for_contrast_cons","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Comparing Optimization Targets for Contrast-Consistent Search","authors":"Hugo Fry¹, Seamus Fallows¹, Ian Fan¹, Jamie Wright², Nandi Schoots³","institutes":"¹Independent; ²Oxford University; ³King's College London","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.00488","code_url":"https://github.com/ash-ai-safety-hub/g3-nandi","hook":"(1) Conceptual clarifications correcting two misconceptions about CCS (it classifies via translation-invariant displacement vectors, not a separating hyperplane, and can succeed even when its \"probabilities\" cluster near 0.5).","about_md":"## Problem\n\nContrast-Consistent Search (CCS) recovers a language model's internal \"truth\" direction, but what its loss actually optimizes for, and whether that target is optimal, is poorly understood.\n\n*Also —* CCS is presented as learning truth probabilities via a negation-consistency constraint, yet the mechanism behind its success has not been isolated or explained.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nSafely deploying LLMs requires reliably extracting their latent knowledge of truth; understanding *why* CCS works is a prerequisite for trusting and improving such probes.\n\n*Also —* Prior work framed CCS around clustering activations and learning calibrated probabilities. The authors show both framings are misleading, motivating a cleaner account of CCS's true optimization target.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\n(1) Conceptual clarifications correcting two misconceptions about CCS (it classifies via translation-invariant displacement vectors, not a separating hyperplane, and can succeed even when its \"probabilities\" cluster near 0.5). (2) A heuristic account of CCS's optimization target and a new Midpoint-Displacement (MD) loss derived from it. (3) An empirical comparison of many loss functions across models and datasets showing MD is a good proxy for CCS and can outperform it.\n\n*Also —* Two further loss functions (Mean Absolute, Square Mean Root) are introduced as baselines in the appendix.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nThe authors decompose CCS behavior into two quantities along a unit weight direction θ̂: σ_d² (mean-square separation of contrast-pair displacement vectors u_i = φ⁺_i − φ⁻_i) and σ_m² (mean-square value of contrast-pair midpoints v_i = φ⁺_i + φ⁻_i). They argue CCS's double-saturating sigmoid forces a trade-off — maximizing σ_d² while minimizing σ_m² — so CCS implicitly optimizes a balance of the two. The proposed Midpoint-Displacement (MD) loss makes this trade-off explicit through a single hyper-parameter λ. Two variants are studied: MD-CCS (λ chosen to mimic CCS) and MD-Acc (λ tuned for accuracy).\n\n**Key idea.** $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{MD}} = (\\lambda-1)\\,\\sigma_d^2 + \\lambda\\,\\sigma_m^2,\\quad \\lambda\\in[0,1],\\ \\ |\\theta|=1$ where $\\sigma_d^2 = \\tfrac{1}{n}\\sum_i (\\hat{\\theta}^{T} u_i)^2,\\ u_i = \\phi_i^{+}-\\phi_i^{-}$ and $\\sigma_m^2 = \\tfrac{1}{n}\\sum_i (\\hat{\\theta}^{T} v_i)^2,\\ v_i = \\phi_i^{+}+\\phi_i^{-}$; the original CCS loss is $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{CCS}}(\\theta,b) = \\tfrac{1}{n}\\sum_i \\big(1 - p_{\\theta,b}(\\phi_i^{+}) - p_{\\theta,b}(\\phi_i^{-})\\big)^2 + \\min\\big(p_{\\theta,b}(\\phi_i^{+}), p_{\\theta,b}(\\phi_i^{-})\\big)^2$.\n\n*Also —* Similarity to CCS is measured by cosine similarity between learned weight vectors; accuracy uses ground-truth labels. Probers are compared against MA, SMR, PCA, random, and supervised baselines over four models and five datasets.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nProbes are trained and evaluated on hidden-state activations from four models — UnifiedQA T5-Large (encoder and decoder), DeBERTa, and GPT-Neo — averaged over five datasets (including BoolQ), following the CCS contrast-pair setup.\n\n*Also —* Contrast pairs are formed by appending mutually exclusive answers to a question; the two answer sets are independently normalized so the probe cannot simply detect the answer token.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nMD-CCS attains an average cosine similarity of ≈0.63 with CCS probers, versus a CCS self-similarity of only 0.78 — far above chance (two random 1024-d unit vectors reach 0.63 with probability ≈10⁻²³⁷), establishing MD as a faithful proxy for CCS's optimization target.\n\n*Also —* No other high-accuracy loss (MA, SMR, PCA) comes close to CCS in cosine similarity (averages 0.14–0.17), so the resemblance is specific to MD, not a generic byproduct of accuracy.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nMD-CCS and MD-Acc differ only in the hyper-parameter λ, yet give very different cosine similarities to CCS (0.63 vs 0.38 average) — showing CCS's target is governed by the σ_d²/σ_m² trade-off. Swapping MD-CCS's λ for the accuracy-tuned value raises average test accuracy from 0.7178 to 0.7557.\n\n*Also —* The MD-CCS hyper-parameter is stable across datasets and models, suggesting the identified proxy target is robust.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- MD-CCS ↔ CCS average cosine similarity ≈ **0.63** (CCS self-similarity **0.78**; MA/SMR/PCA only 0.14–0.17).\n\n*Also —* Supervised probes average 0.8674 accuracy, marking the practical ceiling for these activations.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nCCS works because of the displacement information in its contrast-pair data, not because of its specific loss formula — a simple Midpoint-Displacement loss reproduces CCS and, retuned, beats it.\n\n*Also —* The exact loss formulation is largely interchangeable among high-accuracy probes; the trade-off between displacement variance and midpoint variance is what defines CCS's target.","poster_thumb":"posters/064_comparing_optimization_targets_for_contrast_cons.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/064_comparing_optimization_targets_for_contrast_cons/064_comparing_optimization_targets_for_contrast_cons/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/064_comparing_optimization_targets_for_contrast_cons/064_comparing_optimization_targets_for_contrast_cons/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/064_comparing_optimization_targets_for_contrast_cons/064_comparing_optimization_targets_for_contrast_cons/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/064_comparing_optimization_targets_for_contrast_cons/064_comparing_optimization_targets_for_contrast_cons/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/064_comparing_optimization_targets_for_contrast_cons/064_comparing_optimization_targets_for_contrast_cons/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/064_comparing_optimization_targets_for_contrast_cons/064_comparing_optimization_targets_for_contrast_cons/reel.html"}},{"id":"065","slug":"065_spatial_graph_attention_and_curiosity_driven_pol","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Spatial Graph Attention and Curiosity-driven Policy for Antiviral Drug Discovery","authors":"Yulun Wu¹², Mikaela Cashman³², Nicholas Choma⁴², Érica T. Prates³², Verónica Melesse Vergara⁵³, Manesh Shah⁵, Andrew Chen⁴², Austin Clyde⁶, Thomas S. Brettin⁷, Wibe A. de Jong⁴², Neeraj Kumar⁸², Martha S. Head³², Rick L. Stevens⁶⁷, Peter Nugent⁴², Daniel A. Jacobson⁵³², James B. Brown¹⁴²","institutes":"¹University of California, Berkeley; ²National Virtual Biotechnology Laboratory, US Department of Energy; ³Oak Ridge National Laboratory; ⁴Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory; ⁵University of Tennessee, Knoxville; ⁶University of Chicago; ⁷Argonne National Laboratory; ⁸Pacific Northwest National Laboratory","venue":"ICLR 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.02190","code_url":"https://github.com/yulun-rayn/DGAPN","hook":"The paper contributes (1) spatial Graph Attention (sGAT), a GNN that self-attends over node and edge attributes and encodes 3D spatial structure; (2) Graph Attention Policy Network (GAPN), an attentional policy for a dynamic fragment-based…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nAutomated design of graph-structured molecules that optimize target properties is hard: chemical space is discrete and enormous, and compressing connected molecular objects into faithful, learnable feature representations is nontrivial.\n\n*Also —* Manual drug discovery is slow and expensive, motivating AI that can efficiently and effectively navigate a vast, physically constrained molecular domain.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nPrior generative models under-encode molecular information, ignoring spatial structure and rich edge attributes, and rely on inefficient node-by-node action spaces that produce hard-to-synthesize molecules and explore poorly.\n\n*Also —* Spatial structure matters because molecular volume and the geometry of interaction sites govern how well a candidate complements a receptor binding site, which is essential for antiviral efficacy.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper contributes (1) spatial Graph Attention (sGAT), a GNN that self-attends over node and edge attributes and encodes 3D spatial structure; (2) Graph Attention Policy Network (GAPN), an attentional policy for a dynamic fragment-based action space trained with PPO; and (3) a random-network-distillation curiosity bonus, together forming DGAPN.\n\n*Also —* DGAPN reduces the complexity of paths to chemical synthesis while achieving state-of-the-art binding affinity on a SARS-CoV-2 target.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nMolecule generation is a Markov decision process: at each step the CReM chemical library proposes valid fragment-swap candidates, and an attentional policy selects the next molecule. States are embedded by sGAT, which attends over node/edge attributes and adds a spatial convolution on a sparsified inverse distance matrix. The policy is trained with PPO/A2C, and a random network distillation module supplies innovation rewards for exploration.\n\n**Key idea.** Node attention weight $\\alpha_{ij}^{m} = \\mathrm{softmax}_j\\left(\\sigma([h_{n_i}W_{n,m} \\Vert h_{e_{ik}}W_{e,m} \\Vert h_{n_k}W_{n,m}]\\cdot att_m^{T})\\right)$; spatial convolution $H_n'' = \\sigma\\left(\\tilde{D}^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\tilde{G}\\tilde{D}^{-\\frac{1}{2}} + I\\,H_n W_n\\right)$; PPO objective $J^{*}(\\theta) = \\max_\\theta \\mathbb{E}_{D,\\pi_{\\theta_{old}}}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\min\\{r_t(\\theta)A^{\\pi_{\\theta_{old}}}, \\mathrm{clip}_\\epsilon(r_t(\\theta))A^{\\pi_{\\theta_{old}}}\\}$; innovation reward $r_i(s') = \\mathrm{clip}_\\eta\\left(\\|\\hat{f}_\\psi(s') - f^{*}(s')\\| - m_b\\right)/\\sqrt{v_b}$.\n\n*Also —* Transition dynamics are deterministic (action equals selecting the next state), the trajectory length is fixed to keep episodes short and enable parallel docking, and early sGAT layers can be pre-trained via supervised learning and frozen during RL.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nExperiments target designing inhibitors for the SARS-CoV-2 NSP15 protein, using a dataset of purchasable molecules (Kiss et al., 2012) with docking scores against NSP15. The method is also evaluated on the standard QED and penalized LogP optimization tasks.\n\n*Also —* Molecular docking scores are computed with a GPU-accelerated automated docking tool using the protein's 3D structure and the coordinates of a targeted functional site; molecules failing conformer generation get a docking score of 0 under an \"adjusted validity\" criterion.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn single-objective docking against NSP15, DGAPN reaches a −10.07 top and −6.77 mean docking score, significantly beating all five baselines (REINVENT, JTVAE, GCPN, MolDQN, MARS), with a p-value of 8.55 × 10⁻²⁰⁹ over the second-best model (MolDQN) under Welch's t-test.\n\n*Also —* In constrained optimization DGAPN gives the largest property improvements across all similarity thresholds versus MolDQN and MARS; in multi-objective mode (ω = 0.6) it attains QED 0.72 and SA 2.20 while keeping a −5.69 mean docking score better than every single-objective baseline.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nAblations isolate each component: spatial convolution strongly improves supervised molecular representation learning (loss curves over 40 runs); removing innovation rewards (GAPN vs DGAPN) worsens top docking (−9.19 vs −10.07); and DGAPN even beats a CReM greedy oracle that sees intermediate docking rewards.\n\n*Also —* Innovation bonuses improve docking but slightly worsen synthetic accessibility (SA), revealing a docking-vs-SA trade-off; a separate evaluation mode (DGAPN-eval) reaches −10.38 best and −7.73 mean docking.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Best docking score −10.07 (mean −6.77), versus MolDQN's −8.01 best / −5.38 mean.\n\n*Also —* Innovation-reward ablation: DGAPN −10.07 best vs GAPN −9.19 best; DGAPN-eval reaches −10.38 best / −7.73 mean.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy encoding molecular 3D structure with spatial graph attention and driving exploration with a random-network-distillation curiosity bonus over a fragment-based action space, DGAPN generates higher-affinity, more synthesizable drug candidates than prior state-of-the-art generators.\n\n*Also —* The framework generalizes to user-defined objectives and offers a practical single-vs-multi-objective knob to balance binding affinity against drug-likeness and synthesizability.","poster_thumb":"posters/065_spatial_graph_attention_and_curiosity_driven_pol.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/065_spatial_graph_attention_and_curiosity_driven_pol/065_spatial_graph_attention_and_curiosity_driven_pol/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/065_spatial_graph_attention_and_curiosity_driven_pol/065_spatial_graph_attention_and_curiosity_driven_pol/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/065_spatial_graph_attention_and_curiosity_driven_pol/065_spatial_graph_attention_and_curiosity_driven_pol/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/065_spatial_graph_attention_and_curiosity_driven_pol/065_spatial_graph_attention_and_curiosity_driven_pol/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/065_spatial_graph_attention_and_curiosity_driven_pol/065_spatial_graph_attention_and_curiosity_driven_pol/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/065_spatial_graph_attention_and_curiosity_driven_pol/065_spatial_graph_attention_and_curiosity_driven_pol/reel.html"}},{"id":"066","slug":"066_stylemorph_disentangled_3d_aware_image_synthesis","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"StyleMorph: Disentangled 3D-Aware Image Synthesis with a 3D Morphable StyleGAN","authors":"Eric-Tuan Le¹, Edward Bartrum¹², Iasonas Kokkinos¹","institutes":"¹University College London; ²Alan Turing Institute","venue":"ICLR 2023","paper_url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=Ojpb1y8jflw","code_url":"","hook":"StyleMorph learns a 3D morphable model of non-rigid shape variation for an object category purely from 2D image supervision, and introduces Template Object Coordinates (TOCS), a deformable variant of Normalized Object Coordinates that serve…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nExisting 3D-aware GANs produce high-quality images but entangle geometry with appearance, so they cannot separately control shape, camera pose, object texture, and background during synthesis.\n\n*Also —* 3D morphable models (3DMMs) give this disentangled control, but building one classically demands extensive 3D scanning and manual alignment, restricting it to a few categories like faces.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\n3DMMs are the workhorse of VFX and AR because they give creators disentangled control over pose, expression, and appearance; bringing that control to 3D-aware GANs without 3D supervision would unlock editable, category-general synthesis.\n\n*Also —* Prior deformation-based methods either handle a single dynamic scene or assume a pre-existing deformable template already exists for the category, so they do not learn the morphable model itself from scratch.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nStyleMorph learns a 3D morphable model of non-rigid shape variation for an object category purely from 2D image supervision, and introduces Template Object Coordinates (TOCS), a deformable variant of Normalized Object Coordinates that serves as a deformation-equivariant descriptor of 3D shape.\n\n*Also —* Using 2D TOCS maps as a conditioning signal for a style-based deferred neural renderer, it achieves unprecedented joint disentanglement of pose, shape, object appearance, and scene appearance for high-resolution, photorealistic synthesis.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nA \"Morphable Renderer\" warps camera rays from world space into a canonical template space via a SIREN deformation field driven by a shape code, then integrates template coordinates along each ray to render a low-resolution 2D TOCS map that bottlenecks all geometric variation (shape, pose, projection). This TOCS map, plus separate foreground and background appearance codes, conditions a StyleGAN2-based deferred neural renderer that synthesizes and late-fuses foreground and background into a high-resolution image.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\hat{\\mathbf{r}}(t) = f_s(\\mathbf{r}(t)) = \\mathbf{r}(t) + g_s(\\mathbf{r}(t))$ ; $\\mathrm{TOCS}(\\mathbf{r}) = \\int_{t_n}^{t_f} w(\\hat{\\mathbf{r}}(t))\\,\\hat{\\mathbf{r}}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ ; $I = A \\odot I_{fg} + (1-A) \\odot I_{bg}$\n\n*Also —* Training is two-stage: stage 1 trains the deformable volume renderer as a 64² RGBA image generator (with weak silhouette supervision from Labels4free masks); stage 2 freezes it, prunes the RGB block, and trains the DNR at full resolution conditioned only on the 64² TOCS maps. Both stages use a non-saturating GAN objective with R1 and path regularization; stage 2 adds an L1 alpha-consistency loss.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on four standard datasets: FFHQ (70k human faces) and the three AFHQ splits — Cats (5653), Dogs (5239), and Wild (5238) — reporting FID at 256² resolution against 11 state-of-the-art 3D-GAN baselines.\n\n*Also —* FFHQ provides challenging backgrounds and poses; AFHQ tests generalization beyond faces to animal categories with more varied shape and topology.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nStyleMorph reaches FID 7.91 on FFHQ and 4.29 / 3.49 / 13.95 on AFHQ Cats / Wild / Dogs at 256², competitive with the best non-disentangled 3D-GANs while adding full four-way disentanglement and a learned deformable template.\n\n*Also —* Against Disentangled3D — the only prior 3D-GAN that also uses a deformable template — StyleMorph's FFHQ FID of 7.91 dramatically beats its 28.18, and unlike it StyleMorph also models foreground/background separation.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nUnder equal 96-hour training, replacing TOCS with NOCS worsens FID (8.31 → 8.90) and disentanglement, confirming template-space conditioning gives sharper, better-disentangled synthesis; removing the deformable module (direct occupancy MLP) drastically hurts shape/appearance consistency; and early fusion instead of late fusion improves FID slightly but wrecks alpha consistency (88.65% → 85.87%).\n\n*Also —* Directly optimizing a differentiable view-consistency loss (row 5a) improves view consistency (15.84 → 8.12) but trades away image quality, raising FID to 12.31 — a quality-versus-consistency trade-off.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- FFHQ 256² FID: **7.91** (vs Disentangled3D 28.18, the only other template-based method)\n\n*Also —* Trained entirely from unstructured 2D images across 4 datasets with no 3D supervision, pose labels, or predefined template.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy morphing a learned canonical 3D template and rendering it into a purely geometric 2D TOCS map that conditions a StyleGAN renderer, StyleMorph achieves state-of-the-art-quality image synthesis with unprecedented, fully disentangled control over shape, pose, and foreground/background appearance — all learned from 2D images alone.\n\n*Also —* It effectively builds an unsupervised 3D morphable model for general object categories, bringing VFX-style controllability to categories far beyond human faces.","poster_thumb":"posters/066_stylemorph_disentangled_3d_aware_image_synthesis.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/066_stylemorph_disentangled_3d_aware_image_synthesis/066_stylemorph_disentangled_3d_aware_image_synthesis/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/066_stylemorph_disentangled_3d_aware_image_synthesis/066_stylemorph_disentangled_3d_aware_image_synthesis/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/066_stylemorph_disentangled_3d_aware_image_synthesis/066_stylemorph_disentangled_3d_aware_image_synthesis/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/066_stylemorph_disentangled_3d_aware_image_synthesis/066_stylemorph_disentangled_3d_aware_image_synthesis/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/066_stylemorph_disentangled_3d_aware_image_synthesis/066_stylemorph_disentangled_3d_aware_image_synthesis/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/066_stylemorph_disentangled_3d_aware_image_synthesis/066_stylemorph_disentangled_3d_aware_image_synthesis/reel.html"}},{"id":"067","slug":"067_fine_tuning_language_models_with_just_forward_pa","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Fine-Tuning Language Models with Just Forward Passes","authors":"Sadhika Malladi¹, Tianyu Gao¹, Eshaan Nichani¹, Alex Damian¹, Jason D. Lee¹, Danqi Chen¹, Sanjeev Arora¹","institutes":"¹Princeton University","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.17333","code_url":"https://github.com/princeton-nlp/MeZO","hook":"The paper proposes MeZO, an in-place zeroth-order SGD optimizer that fine-tunes language models with the exact memory footprint of inference, and shows it matches backpropagation across model types, scales, and tasks with up to 12x memory s…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nAs language models grow, fine-tuning with backpropagation needs prohibitively large memory to store activations, gradients, and optimizer states, up to 12x the memory required for inference.\n\n*Also —* On a single 80GB A100, inference runs a 30B model, but Adam fine-tuning fits only a 2.7B model, a roughly 11x gap in trainable model size.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nZeroth-order methods can estimate gradients from just two forward passes, but classical theory predicts their convergence slows in proportion to the number of parameters, making them seem hopeless for billion-parameter models.\n\n*Also —* In-context learning and linear probing avoid full fine-tuning but leave accuracy on the table; a truly memory-light optimizer that still matches fine-tuning was missing.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper proposes MeZO, an in-place zeroth-order SGD optimizer that fine-tunes language models with the exact memory footprint of inference, and shows it matches backpropagation across model types, scales, and tasks with up to 12x memory savings.\n\n*Also —* It also provides theory explaining why MeZO converges fast despite huge parameter counts (the rate depends on the loss landscape's effective local rank, not the parameter count), and shows MeZO works with LoRA, prefix tuning, and non-differentiable objectives.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nMeZO estimates gradients with SPSA, perturbing all parameters along a single random Gaussian direction z, computing the loss difference between the plus and minus perturbations, and updating parameters along z scaled by that projected gradient. The key trick is to store only the random seed, not z, and regenerate z on the fly during both perturbation and update, so no extra memory beyond inference is needed.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\widehat{\\nabla}\\mathcal{L}(\\theta;\\mathcal{B}) = \\frac{\\mathcal{L}(\\theta+\\epsilon z;\\mathcal{B}) - \\mathcal{L}(\\theta-\\epsilon z;\\mathcal{B})}{2\\epsilon}\\, z, \\quad z \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, I_d)$ and the update $\\theta_{t+1} = \\theta_t - \\eta\\, \\widehat{\\nabla}\\mathcal{L}(\\theta;\\mathcal{B}_t)$\n\n*Also —* Because each of z's uses is reconstructed from the same seed, MeZO perturbs and resets parameters in place, avoiding the 2x memory that vanilla ZO-SGD incurs from storing the perturbation vector. SPSA needs only two forward passes per step and plugs into SGD (and even Adam) directly.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nExperiments span RoBERTa-large on sentence classification (SST-2, SST-5, SNLI, MNLI, RTE, TREC) in few-shot (k=16) and many-shot (k=512) settings, and OPT models from 1.3B to 66B on SuperGLUE tasks plus multiple-choice (COPA, ReCoRD) and generation (SQuAD, DROP).\n\n*Also —* Comparisons include zero-shot, in-context learning, linear probing, and full Adam fine-tuning, each evaluated with the paper's task prompts.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nMeZO substantially outperforms zero-shot, in-context learning, and linear probing, and matches Adam fine-tuning within roughly 1% on 7 of 11 OPT-13B tasks while using only 1/12 of the memory. On RoBERTa-large it approaches fine-tuning within about 5% at k=512.\n\n*Also —* MeZO also cuts wall-clock cost: it needs only about half as many GPU-hours as Adam fine-tuning for a 30B model in the authors' implementation.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nMeZO is compatible with parameter-efficient tuning: MeZO with LoRA and MeZO with prefix tuning perform on par with full-parameter MeZO. Using n=1 perturbation per step is the most compute-efficient choice.\n\n*Also —* MeZO can directly optimize non-differentiable objectives such as accuracy and F1, and outperforms the prior zeroth-order method BBTv2 by up to 11% absolute.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Up to **12x** memory reduction versus Adam fine-tuning (OPT-13B); same memory as inference.\n\n*Also —* Scales tested up to 66B; beats BBTv2 by up to 11% absolute.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nYou can fine-tune huge language models with just forward passes: MeZO matches backpropagation quality at inference-level memory, unlocking training of models an order of magnitude larger on the same hardware.\n\n*Also —* Contrary to classical zeroth-order pessimism, fine-tuning a well pre-trained model converges in a rate governed by the local effective dimensionality of the loss, not the raw parameter count.","poster_thumb":"posters/067_fine_tuning_language_models_with_just_forward_pa.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/067_fine_tuning_language_models_with_just_forward_pa/067_fine_tuning_language_models_with_just_forward_pa/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/067_fine_tuning_language_models_with_just_forward_pa/067_fine_tuning_language_models_with_just_forward_pa/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/067_fine_tuning_language_models_with_just_forward_pa/067_fine_tuning_language_models_with_just_forward_pa/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/067_fine_tuning_language_models_with_just_forward_pa/067_fine_tuning_language_models_with_just_forward_pa/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/067_fine_tuning_language_models_with_just_forward_pa/067_fine_tuning_language_models_with_just_forward_pa/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/067_fine_tuning_language_models_with_just_forward_pa/067_fine_tuning_language_models_with_just_forward_pa/reel.html"}},{"id":"068","slug":"068_vectormapnet_end_to_end_vectorized_hd_map_learni","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"VectorMapNet: End-to-end Vectorized HD Map Learning","authors":"Yicheng Liu¹², Tianyuan Yuan², Yue Wang³, Yilun Wang⁴, Hang Zhao²¹","institutes":"¹Shanghai Qi Zhi Institute; ²Tsinghua University; ³MIT; ⁴Li Auto","venue":"ICML 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.08920","code_url":"https://github.com/Mrmoore98/VectorMapNet_code","hook":"VectorMapNet is the first end-to-end vectorized HD map learner: it predicts map elements as a sparse set of polylines directly from sensor data, eliminating rasterization and post-processing, and adapts a DETR-style detector to localize def…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nAutonomous driving needs vectorized HD semantic maps, but offline manual annotation does not scale and existing learning-based methods only output dense rasterized segmentations that require heuristic post-processing to vectorize.\n\n*Also —* Rasterized maps lack per-instance information, cannot enforce spatial consistency, and are incompatible with downstream modules that consume instance-level 2D/3D vectorized maps.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nOnline map learning from onboard sensors avoids the localization errors and labor of offline HD maps, and a truly end-to-end system should output the vectorized primitives downstream tasks use directly, without a rasterize-then-vectorize detour.\n\n*Also —* HDMapNet, the prior state of the art, still predicts rasterized semantic/instance/direction maps and stitches them with a hand-crafted post-processing algorithm, which caps its scalability and accuracy.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nVectorMapNet is the first end-to-end vectorized HD map learner: it predicts map elements as a sparse set of polylines directly from sensor data, eliminating rasterization and post-processing, and adapts a DETR-style detector to localize deformable map elements in BEV space.\n\n*Also —* It introduces polylines as a unified, direction-encoding primitive for heterogeneous map elements (boundaries, dividers, crossings) and reformulates map construction as a detection-then-generation problem.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nVectorMapNet has three stages: a BEV feature extractor lifts camera (ResNet + IPM) and LiDAR (PointPillars) features into a shared bird's-eye-view space; a DETR-style map element detector uses learnable element queries and deformable attention to predict element keypoints and class labels; and an autoregressive Transformer polyline generator decodes each detected element into an ordered sequence of vertices.\n\n**Key idea.** $p(V_i^{poly}\\mid A_i, l_i, \\mathcal{F}_{BEV}; \\theta) = \\prod_{n=1}^{2N_v} p(v_{i,n}^{f}\\mid v_{i,<n}^{f}, A_i, l_i, \\mathcal{F}_{BEV})$ ; $\\mathcal{L} = \\mathcal{L}_{det} + \\mathcal{L}_{gen}$ ; $\\mathcal{L}_{gen} = -\\frac{1}{2N_v}\\sum_{n=1}^{2N_v} \\log \\hat{p}(v_{i,n}^{f}\\mid v_{i,<n}^{f}, A_i, l_i, \\mathcal{F}_{BEV})$\n\n*Also —* Vertex coordinates are quantized into discrete tokens and modeled with a categorical distribution (following PolyGen); polylines are generated in parallel, and a two-stage strategy fine-tunes the generator on predicted keypoints to reduce exposure bias.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nExperiments run on nuScenes and Argoverse2, evaluating predicted polylines against ground truth with Chamfer-distance AP and a newly introduced Fréchet-distance AP (which accounts for vertex order) at thresholds of 0.5, 1.0, and 1.5 m.\n\n*Also —* Map elements are pedestrian crossings, lane dividers, and road boundaries; Argoverse2 provides z-axis annotations, enabling 2D and 3D evaluation.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nVectorMapNet sets a new state of the art, surpassing HDMapNet by 14.2 mAP on nuScenes and 14.6 mAP on Argoverse2; on nuScenes it improves camera-only by +17.9 mAP, LiDAR-only by +9.9 mAP, and fusion by +14.2 mAP over HDMapNet.\n\n*Also —* With the two-stage fine-tuning strategy, the fusion model reaches 53.7 mAP on nuScenes (vs. HDMapNet's 31.0), and sensor fusion adds +4.3 mAP over camera-only and +11.2 mAP over LiDAR-only.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nAmong three keypoint representations, the Bounding Box (k=2) design is best, outperforming SME and Extreme Points by 2.0 Fréchet mAP and 7.3 Chamfer mAP; the two-stage teacher-forcing-then-fine-tune training adds +6.9 mAP (camera) and +8.5 mAP (fusion).\n\n*Also —* Feeding VectorMapNet's predicted maps into a downstream motion-forecasting baseline lowers minADE from 0.909 (trajectory only) to 0.826, nearly matching the ground-truth map (0.779) and within 0.2% MR of it.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- +14.2 mAP over HDMapNet on nuScenes; +14.6 mAP on Argoverse2\n\n*Also —* Downstream motion forecasting minADE improves 0.909 → 0.826 with predicted maps.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nFraming HD map construction as detection-then-polyline-generation lets a single end-to-end network predict clean, directional vector maps straight from onboard sensors, beating rasterize-and-post-process pipelines by double-digit mAP.\n\n*Also —* Polylines are a versatile primitive: the same model extends to centerline prediction with no structural changes, and its outputs measurably help downstream motion forecasting.","poster_thumb":"posters/068_vectormapnet_end_to_end_vectorized_hd_map_learni.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/068_vectormapnet_end_to_end_vectorized_hd_map_learni/068_vectormapnet_end_to_end_vectorized_hd_map_learni/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/068_vectormapnet_end_to_end_vectorized_hd_map_learni/068_vectormapnet_end_to_end_vectorized_hd_map_learni/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/068_vectormapnet_end_to_end_vectorized_hd_map_learni/068_vectormapnet_end_to_end_vectorized_hd_map_learni/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/068_vectormapnet_end_to_end_vectorized_hd_map_learni/068_vectormapnet_end_to_end_vectorized_hd_map_learni/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/068_vectormapnet_end_to_end_vectorized_hd_map_learni/068_vectormapnet_end_to_end_vectorized_hd_map_learni/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/068_vectormapnet_end_to_end_vectorized_hd_map_learni/068_vectormapnet_end_to_end_vectorized_hd_map_learni/reel.html"}},{"id":"069","slug":"069_visual_correspondence_hallucination","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Visual Correspondence Hallucination","authors":"Hugo Germain¹, Vincent Lepetit¹, Guillaume Bourmaud²","institutes":"¹LIGM, École des Ponts, Univ Gustave Eiffel, CNRS, Marne-la-Vallée, France; ²IMS, University of Bordeaux, Bordeaux INP, CNRS, Bordeaux, France","venue":"ICLR 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09711","code_url":"","hook":"The paper introduces the task of correspondence hallucination and NeurHal, a network that, given a source/target image pair and source keypoints, outputs for every keypoint a probability distribution over its correspondent's location regard…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nLocal feature matching only locates a keypoint's correspondent when it is visible; occluded or out-of-frame correspondents are treated as noise, so matching fails when few keypoints are covisible.\n\n*Also —* Humans instead reason geometrically to predict, or hallucinate, where non-covisible correspondents lie, a capability prior methods lack.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nState-of-the-art localization relies on keypoint matches, yet these matching methods collapse on low-overlap image pairs where most locations are occluded or outside the field of view.\n\n*Also —* Geometric reasoning has only been used a posteriori (RANSAC-style outlier removal), never to actively predict correspondences at non-covisible locations.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces the task of correspondence hallucination and NeurHal, a network that, given a source/target image pair and source keypoints, outputs for every keypoint a probability distribution over its correspondent's location regardless of visibility, unifying identifying, inpainting, and outpainting.\n\n*Also —* It analyzes the specific features of this novel learning task, which motivate the loss and the non-siamese, cross-attention architecture, and shows the ability benefits absolute camera pose estimation.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nA siamese CNN backbone produces dense descriptor maps for source and target; the target map is padded with a learnable vector λ to represent locations outside the field of view. A cross-attention backbone with positional encoding lets source and target descriptors communicate, producing a per-keypoint feature dS,n and dense target features; each correspondence map CT,n is formed by a 1×1 convolution of the target features using dS,n as filter followed by a 2D softmax.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\mathrm{NRE}(p_S, C_T, R_{TS}, t_{TS}, d_S) := -\\ln C_T(x_T)\\ \\text{where}\\ x_T = K_C\\,\\omega(d_S, p_S, R_{TS}, t_{TS})$\n\n*Also —* Training minimizes a sum of Neural Reprojection Error (NRE) terms by SGD with early stopping; there is no covisibility assumption and no need to label keypoints as visible/occluded/outside — the network only ever outputs correspondence maps. Maps are kept low resolution (e.g. 160×120 for a 640×480 target with stride 8, γ=50%).\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on indoor scenes ScanNet and NYU Depth, and outdoor scenes MegaDepth and ETH-3D; NeurHal is trained on ScanNet for the indoor setting and on MegaDepth for the outdoor setting, then tested on scenes unseen at training time.\n\n*Also —* Absolute camera pose estimation is evaluated on the ScanNet test set over 2,500 source/target image pairs from held-out scenes.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nNeurHal hallucinates correspondences on unseen scenes for both inpainting (occluded) and outpainting (out-of-frame) tasks, with argmax errors concentrated far below the random-prediction average, while state-of-the-art matching methods (LoFTR, DRC-Net, S2DNet) produce poor inpainting and essentially zero outpainting.\n\n*Also —* For absolute pose, NeurHal estimates the camera pose correctly significantly more often than any competitor on low-overlap pairs, because it is the only method able to outpaint correspondences.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nTraining NeurHal on all three tasks (identification + inpainting + outpainting) gives the best pose-estimation robustness; adding outpainting is critical for low-overlap pairs, while adding inpainting brings little improvement.\n\n*Also —* A pose is counted correct when rotation error < 20° and translation error < 1.5 m, evaluated as a function of the maximum image-pair overlap on ScanNet.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Robustness thresholds: rotation error < 20°, translation error < 1.5 m.\n\n*Also —* NeurHal is the only evaluated method capable of outpainting correspondents outside the target field of view.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nA single network can learn to hallucinate keypoint correspondences — visible, occluded, or outside the field of view — and this hallucination makes absolute camera pose estimation markedly more robust on low-overlap image pairs.\n\n*Also —* Correspondence hallucination reframes non-covisible regions as signal to be predicted rather than noise to be discarded.","poster_thumb":"posters/069_visual_correspondence_hallucination.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/069_visual_correspondence_hallucination/069_visual_correspondence_hallucination/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/069_visual_correspondence_hallucination/069_visual_correspondence_hallucination/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/069_visual_correspondence_hallucination/069_visual_correspondence_hallucination/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/069_visual_correspondence_hallucination/069_visual_correspondence_hallucination/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/069_visual_correspondence_hallucination/069_visual_correspondence_hallucination/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/069_visual_correspondence_hallucination/069_visual_correspondence_hallucination/reel.html"}},{"id":"070","slug":"070_towards_reasoning_aware_explainable_vqa","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Towards Reasoning-Aware Explainable VQA","authors":"Rakesh Vaideeswaran¹, Feng Gao², Abhinav Mathur², Govind Thattai²","institutes":"¹University of Illinois, Urbana-Champaign; ²Amazon Alexa AI","venue":"NeurIPS 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.05190","code_url":"","hook":"The paper presents easy-to-implement explanation-generation modules on top of a SOTA VQA framework that maintain accuracy while producing human-readable explanations, and shows via experiments plus human studies that new metrics are needed…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nMost VQA models optimize only answer accuracy and reach the answer as a black box, giving no human-readable evidence for how the answer was derived.\n\n*Also —* Very few models both answer correctly and produce a self-contained explanation that actually corresponds to the correct answer (the paper's \"Model type 3\").\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nAs reasoning demands in VQA grow, interpretability becomes urgent for diagnosing and trusting models, yet explanation quality has no reliable evaluation metric.\n\n*Also —* Prior explainable-VQA work either reuses image captions as weak explanations or relies on external knowledge that gives no direct evidence for the answer.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper presents easy-to-implement explanation-generation modules on top of a SOTA VQA framework that maintain accuracy while producing human-readable explanations, and shows via experiments plus human studies that new metrics are needed to evaluate VQA explanations.\n\n*Also —* It compares LSTM and Transformer-decoder explanation generators and demonstrates concrete failure cases of string-matching metrics.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nThe method builds on the Coarse-to-Fine Reasoning (CFR) VQA backbone, whose information-filtering, multimodal-learning, and semantic-reasoning modules produce a joint embedding; this embedding feeds both an MLP answer predictor and a new explanation generator (LSTM or Transformer decoder) trained end-to-end.\n\n**Key idea.** $a^* = \\arg\\max_a \\mathrm{CFR}_\\theta(a \\mid f_I, p_I, f_Q, p_Q)$, $L = \\alpha L_{ans} + (1-\\alpha) L_{expl}$, $L_{expl} = -\\frac{1}{l\\cdot|V|}\\sum_{i=1}^{l}\\sum_{k=1}^{|V|} y_{i,k}\\,\\log p(w_{i,k})$\n\n*Also —* Faster-RCNN extracts region features and image predicates; questions are encoded with GloVe embeddings plus a GRU, and question predicates are obtained via a stop-word filter. Training balances answer and explanation losses with a factor alpha.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nExperiments use two VQA datasets with annotated explanations: GQA-REX (about 1.04M QA pairs over 82K images, explanations for ~98% of GQA-balanced samples) and VQA-E (explanations for ~40% of VQA2.0 QA pairs).\n\n*Also —* GQA-REX explanations follow a scene-graph reasoning format and are not fully human-readable; VQA-E explanations resemble image captions containing the answer. Both are acknowledged as imperfect.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nThe augmented model maintains near-SOTA answer accuracy while generating explanations, reaching 77.49% VQA score on GQA-REX and 71.48% on VQA-E, essentially matching the explanation-free baseline.\n\n*Also —* For explanation quality, CFRF+LSTM beats the baseline on VQA-E (BLEU-1 0.33 vs 0.268; ROUGE-L 0.325 vs 0.249), though absolute scores remain only satisfactory.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nSweeping the loss balance factor alpha and swapping the explanation generator shows accuracy is robust: on GQA-REX, LSTM scores range 75.08%–77.49% across alpha, and the Transformer decoder (alpha=0.5) reaches 77.06%, all close to the 77.49% baseline.\n\n*Also —* On VQA-E, LSTM scores stay within 71.32%–71.55% across alpha and the Transformer reaches 71.46%, confirming the explanation module barely perturbs answering regardless of architecture or loss weighting.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- 77.49% VQA score on GQA-REX (maintains SOTA while explaining)\n\n*Also —* Human study covered 4735 unique VQA-E validation image-question pairs, 3 annotators each (14,205 responses from 111 subjects); 93.12% of ground-truth explanations were judged valid.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nYou can bolt a simple explanation generator onto a strong VQA model and get human-readable explanations for free, but today's string-matching metrics can't tell good explanations from bad ones, so better evaluation metrics are the real bottleneck.\n\n*Also —* Concrete examples show BLEU/ROUGE rewarding wrong explanations and penalizing valid ones, reinforcing the call for human-grounded or reasoning-aware metrics.","poster_thumb":"posters/070_towards_reasoning_aware_explainable_vqa.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/070_towards_reasoning_aware_explainable_vqa/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/070_towards_reasoning_aware_explainable_vqa/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/070_towards_reasoning_aware_explainable_vqa/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/070_towards_reasoning_aware_explainable_vqa/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/070_towards_reasoning_aware_explainable_vqa/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/070_towards_reasoning_aware_explainable_vqa/reel.html"}},{"id":"071","slug":"071_moccasin_efficient_tensor_rematerialization_for","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Moccasin: Efficient Tensor Rematerialization for Neural Networks","authors":"Burak Bartan¹, Haoming Li², Harris Teague¹, Christopher Lott¹, Bistra Dilkina²","institutes":"¹Qualcomm AI Research; ²University of Southern California","venue":"ICML 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.14463","code_url":"","hook":"Moccasin, a constraint programming formulation with only O(n) integer variables (versus Checkmate's O(n²) Boolean variables), that solves the rematerialization scheduling problem up to an order of magnitude faster on large graphs.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nGiven a neural network compute graph and a fixed local memory budget, choose which tensors to keep versus recompute (rematerialize) so total execution time is minimized while never exceeding the budget.\n\n*Also —* Memory is the primary limiting factor for deploying and training large models on low-memory edge devices; the underlying scheduling problem is PSPACE-complete.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nPrior state-of-the-art (Checkmate) formulates rematerialization as a MILP with O(n²) Boolean variables, which does not scale to graphs with hundreds of nodes and thousands of edges.\n\n*Also —* On-device training and tighter latency targets increase pressure on local memory, so a solver must handle large graphs within an acceptable compile time.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nMoccasin, a constraint programming formulation with only O(n) integer variables (versus Checkmate's O(n²) Boolean variables), that solves the rematerialization scheduling problem up to an order of magnitude faster on large graphs.\n\n*Also —* A retention-interval representation of tensor lifetimes, memory and precedence constraints modeled with CP cumulative and reservoir constraints, and an optional topological-ordering restriction that further shrinks the search space.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nEach node's output tensor is described by up to Cv retention intervals, each an integer start event s_iv and end event e_iv plus a Boolean activity flag a_iv. Minimizing total recompute duration subject to a memory budget is solved with Google OR-Tools CP-SAT.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\min_{s,e,a} \\sum_{v,i} w_v\\, a_{iv}\\quad \\text{s.t.}\\ \\sum_{v,i:\\, s_{iv}\\le t\\le e_{iv}} m_v\\, a_{iv} \\le M,\\ \\forall t;\\quad s_{iv}\\le e_{iv},\\ e_{iv}\\le s_{iv}^{i+1},\\ a_{1v}=1$\n\n*Also —* The memory budget is enforced with the CP cumulative constraint (output sizes as demands, budget M as capacity); data dependencies with the reservoir constraint; distinct compute events with alldifferent. Setting Cv small (Cv = 2 in experiments) keeps only O(Cn) variables.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on random layered graphs G1–G4 spanning 100 to 1000 nodes (up to 5875 edges), on synthetic and real-world compute graphs, with memory budgets set to 80% and 90% of the peak memory of the initial rematerialization-free schedule.\n\n*Also —* A real-world graph with n = 442 nodes and m = 1247 edges is used for the headline solve-time comparison; experiments run on a 16-core workstation with 32 GB RAM.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nMoccasin is up to an order of magnitude faster than Checkmate, especially on large graphs. On graphs G3 (n = 500) and G4 (n = 1000) Checkmate times out with no solution even at a 3-hour limit and exits with out-of-memory, while Moccasin converges to a low-duration-increase solution in under an hour.\n\n*Also —* On G2 (n = 250) with a tight budget Checkmate finds no feasible solution within 30 minutes and takes 10 minutes at the loosest budget, whereas Moccasin finishes in a few seconds.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nEnforcing an input topological ordering (Section 2.3 variant) reduces the search space and solve time relative to the unrestricted formulation. The per-node interval budget is fixed at Cv = 2 (C = 2) throughout the experiments.\n\n*Also —* Table 1 contrasts formulation complexity: Checkmate-MILP uses O(n² + nm) Boolean variables, whereas Moccasin-CP uses O(Cn) integer variables with domain size O(n) and O(Cm) constraints.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Integer/Boolean variables: O(n) for Moccasin vs O(n²) for Checkmate.\n\n*Also —* - Scales to graphs with hundreds of nodes and thousands of edges (e.g. n = 1000, m = 5875) where Checkmate times out / runs out of memory.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nFraming tensor rematerialization as a constraint program over retention intervals cuts the discrete-variable count from quadratic to linear, letting Moccasin solve much larger neural-network compute graphs up to 10× faster while keeping the runtime overhead under 5%.\n\n*Also —* The formulation is hardware-agnostic: once solved, the resulting execution sequence can run on any CPU or GPU.","poster_thumb":"posters/071_moccasin_efficient_tensor_rematerialization_for.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/071_moccasin_efficient_tensor_rematerialization_for/071_moccasin_efficient_tensor_rematerialization_for/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/071_moccasin_efficient_tensor_rematerialization_for/071_moccasin_efficient_tensor_rematerialization_for/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/071_moccasin_efficient_tensor_rematerialization_for/071_moccasin_efficient_tensor_rematerialization_for/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/071_moccasin_efficient_tensor_rematerialization_for/071_moccasin_efficient_tensor_rematerialization_for/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/071_moccasin_efficient_tensor_rematerialization_for/071_moccasin_efficient_tensor_rematerialization_for/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/071_moccasin_efficient_tensor_rematerialization_for/071_moccasin_efficient_tensor_rematerialization_for/reel.html"}},{"id":"072","slug":"072_how_do_nonlinear_transformers_acquire_generaliza","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"How Do Nonlinear Transformers Learn and Generalize in In-Context Learning?","authors":"Hongkang Li¹, Meng Wang¹, Songtao Lu², Xiaodong Cui², Pin-Yu Chen²","institutes":"¹Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute; ²IBM Thomas J. Watson Research Center","venue":"ICML 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.15607","code_url":"","hook":"The paper gives the first training-dynamics analysis of a one-layer Transformer with both nonlinear (softmax) self-attention and nonlinear (ReLU) MLP, proving quantitative ICL generalization guarantees in-domain and out-of-domain, and the f…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nTransformers show strong in-context learning (ICL), yet why training yields ICL and how well the trained model generalizes is largely unknown, because nonlinear self-attention and nonlinear MLP make the training objective nonconvex and hard to analyze.\n\n*Also —* No prior theory characterizes how to train a model to reach ICL under distribution-shifted data, nor how model pruning affects ICL performance.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nPrior ICL theory simplifies away the hard parts: it either drops nonlinear self-attention or uses a linear MLP, studies only linear regression, and cannot explain training under distribution shift or the effect of pruning.\n\n*Also —* As LLMs are increasingly pruned to cut inference cost while preserving ICL, a theoretical account of when pruning is safe for ICL is missing entirely.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper gives the first training-dynamics analysis of a one-layer Transformer with both nonlinear (softmax) self-attention and nonlinear (ReLU) MLP, proving quantitative ICL generalization guarantees in-domain and out-of-domain, and the first theoretical analysis of how magnitude-based pruning affects ICL.\n\n*Also —* It quantifies how the required training data, iterations, and context length depend on feature magnitude and on the fraction of context examples sharing the query's relevant pattern, and it explains the internal mechanism of the trained model.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nA single-head, one-layer Transformer with softmax self-attention and a two-layer ReLU MLP is trained by minimizing hinge loss on prompts drawn from a subset of binary classification tasks. Each input carries in-domain-relevant (IDR) patterns that decide the label plus irrelevant patterns; the analysis tracks the gradient-descent training trajectory of the attention and MLP weights.\n\n**Key idea.** $F(\\Psi;P)=a^{\\top}\\mathrm{ReLU}\\!\\left(W_O\\sum_{i=1}^{l}W_V p_i\\,\\mathrm{attn}(\\Psi;P,i)\\right),\\quad \\mathrm{attn}(\\Psi;P,i)=\\mathrm{softmax}\\big((W_K p_i)^{\\top}W_Q p_{\\text{query}}\\big)$ and the iteration/sample complexity $T=\\Theta\\!\\big(\\eta^{-1}M_1\\,\\alpha^{-2/3}\\beta^{-2/3}\\sqrt{\\log M_1}\\big),\\ N=BT$\n\n*Also —* Generalization is evaluated two ways: in-domain (test query and task from the training distribution) and out-of-domain (test query from a shifted distribution whose relevant patterns are linear combinations of the training patterns). Key quantities are α, the fraction of context examples sharing the query's IDR pattern, and β, the norm of relevant patterns.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nSynthetic binary classification tasks generated from the paper's data model, with M₁ = 6 in-domain-relevant patterns and M₂ = 24 irrelevant patterns; models are the analyzed one-layer Transformer and a 3-layer, 2-head GPT-2.\n\n*Also —* Training uses α = 0.8 and context length l = 20; out-of-domain tests use M₁′ = 3 relevant patterns that are linear combinations of the training patterns. Classification error is measured as the probability of a sign mismatch between prediction and label.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nWith T = Θ(η⁻¹M₁α^(−2/3)β^(−2/3)√(log M₁)) iterations and N = BT samples, the trained one-layer Transformer provably reaches O(ε) in-domain generalization error, and also O(ε) out-of-domain error when the shifted relevant patterns are linear combinations (coefficient sum ≥ 1) of the training patterns.\n\n*Also —* Experimentally, out-of-domain classification error drops below 0.01 once S₁ ≥ 1 (verifying the linear-combination condition), and required iterations and context length scale as predicted with α.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nVarying the relevant-pattern fraction shows required context length scales as α⁻¹ (training) and α′⁻¹ (testing), and iterations and samples scale as α^(−2/3): a larger fraction gives faster convergence and shorter needed context.\n\n*Also —* Comparing ICL against logistic regression, Gaussian/linear-kernel SVM, and 1-/3-nearest-neighbor: at α′ = 0.8 ICL's advantage is modest, but at the harder α′ = 0.6 ICL is the most sample-efficient, showing it filters irrelevant data and resists label noise better than classical methods.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Iterations and samples scale as **α^(−2/3)**; required context length scales as **α⁻¹** (train) / **α′⁻¹** (test).\n\n*Also —* - Pruning the small-magnitude MLP neurons keeps error at **O(ε + M₁^(−1/2))** (near-lossless); experimentally, magnitude-based pruning up to **~15%** of W_O neurons does not hurt out-of-domain generalization.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nA one-layer Transformer with genuinely nonlinear attention and MLP can be provably trained to perform in-context learning that generalizes both in-domain and under distribution shift, with training cost governed by the fraction of relevant context, and its low-magnitude MLP neurons can be pruned almost for free.\n\n*Also —* The mechanism: attention concentrates on context examples sharing the query's relevant pattern, and the ReLU MLP promotes their label embeddings, so magnitude-based pruning that keeps the large neurons preserves ICL.","poster_thumb":"posters/072_how_do_nonlinear_transformers_acquire_generaliza.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/072_how_do_nonlinear_transformers_acquire_generaliza/072_how_do_nonlinear_transformers_acquire_generaliza/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/072_how_do_nonlinear_transformers_acquire_generaliza/072_how_do_nonlinear_transformers_acquire_generaliza/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/072_how_do_nonlinear_transformers_acquire_generaliza/072_how_do_nonlinear_transformers_acquire_generaliza/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/072_how_do_nonlinear_transformers_acquire_generaliza/072_how_do_nonlinear_transformers_acquire_generaliza/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/072_how_do_nonlinear_transformers_acquire_generaliza/072_how_do_nonlinear_transformers_acquire_generaliza/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/072_how_do_nonlinear_transformers_acquire_generaliza/072_how_do_nonlinear_transformers_acquire_generaliza/reel.html"}},{"id":"073","slug":"073_characterizing_the_optimal_0_1_loss_for_multi_cl","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Characterizing the Optimal 0-1 Loss for Multi-class Classification with a Test-time Attacker","authors":"Sihui Dai¹, Wenxin Ding², Arjun Nitin Bhagoji², Daniel Cullina³, Ben Y. Zhao², Haitao Zheng², Prateek Mittal¹","institutes":"¹Princeton University; ²University of Chicago; ³Pennsylvania State University","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.10722","code_url":"https://github.com/inspire-group/multiclass_robust_lb","hook":"(1) Extends the conflict-graph framework to multi-class, expressing the optimal 0-1 loss as a linear program on a conflict hypergraph; (2) develops efficient upper and lower bounds that determine the range of the optimal loss; (3) an extens…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nNo method existed to compute the lowest achievable 0-1 loss under a test-time (adversarial) attacker for multi-class classification; prior optimal-loss results covered only the binary case.\n\n*Also —* Without this benchmark, practitioners cannot tell whether a robust model is near-optimal or how much headroom remains, keeping them stuck in the attack-defense arms race.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nA dataset-and-attacker-specific lower bound on robust loss is a diagnostic tool: it lets practitioners measure progress for defenses against an absolute optimum instead of only against other defenses.\n\n*Also —* Prior lower bounds restricted the hypothesis class to binary classifiers, so the far more common multi-class regime, where higher-order interactions between examples appear, was uncharacterized.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\n(1) Extends the conflict-graph framework to multi-class, expressing the optimal 0-1 loss as a linear program on a conflict hypergraph; (2) develops efficient upper and lower bounds that determine the range of the optimal loss; (3) an extensive empirical analysis of the gap between adversarially trained classifiers and the optimum.\n\n*Also —* The bounds are cast as classifier-attacker games, connecting truncated-hypergraph lower bounds, aggregated binary lower bounds, and a generalized Caro-Wei upper bound.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nFor a discrete distribution, build a conflict hypergraph whose vertices are data points and whose hyperedges connect same-neighborhood examples from different classes. The optimal robust loss is then a linear program over achievable correct-classification probabilities; its dual is a fractional covering of vertices by hyperedges that yields the adversarial strategy.\n\n**Key idea.** $L^*(P, N, \\mathcal{H}) = 1 - \\sup_{h\\in\\mathcal{H}} \\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\\sim P}\\big[\\inf_{\\tilde{x}\\in N(x)} h(\\tilde{x})_y\\big]$ and its LP form $1 - L^*(P, N, \\mathcal{H}_{\\text{soft}}) = \\max_{q} \\; p^{\\top} q \\quad \\text{s.t.} \\quad q \\ge 0,\\; Bq \\le 1$\n\n*Also —* To scale, the hypergraph is truncated to a maximum hyperedge degree m (giving lower bounds L*(m)), binary 1-vs-1 optimal losses are aggregated via maximum-weight coupling (a looser but cheap lower bound L*_CO(2)), and a generalized Caro-Wei bound on the independent-set number gives an upper bound L_CW.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nOptimal losses are computed for MNIST and CIFAR-10 under an L2-constrained test-time attacker at varying strengths ε, in both a 3-class setting (1000 samples per class) and the full 10-class setting.\n\n*Also —* 3-class MNIST uses classes 1, 4, 7; 3-class CIFAR-10 uses plane, bird, ship. Adversarially trained baselines use TRADES (3-layer CNN on MNIST, WRN-28-10 on CIFAR-10) evaluated with APGD-CE from AutoAttack.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nThe gap between adversarially trained classifiers and the optimal loss is large even at small ε, and far larger than in the binary case. For 3-class CIFAR-10, TRADES cannot beat about 0.6 loss at a budget where the optimal loss is near 0.\n\n*Also —* In the 10-class setting the truncated-hypergraph lower bounds and the Caro-Wei upper bound match closely at practical ε, tightly localizing the true optimal loss and confirming the AT gap holds against both bounds.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nAdding higher-degree hyperedges barely changes the lower bound at small ε: L*(2), L*(3), and L*(4) nearly coincide until loss exceeds ~0.4, despite millions of higher-degree hyperedges being present.\n\n*Also —* For CIFAR-10 at ε=3 there are about 3 million degree-3 and 10 million degree-4 hyperedges, yet they leave the computed bound unchanged; aggregating binary bounds (L*_CO(2)) is cheapest but much looser; larger architectures help only slightly at low ε and not at all at high ε.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- 3-class CIFAR-10: TRADES stuck near 0.6 loss where the optimal loss is ~0\n\n*Also —* - CIFAR-10 at ε=3: ~3M degree-3 and ~10M degree-4 hyperedges with zero effect on the bound\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nThere is a large, quantifiable gap between current robust classifiers and the optimal achievable loss in multi-class settings, and edge-only conflict-hypergraph bounds efficiently pin down that optimum in the practical regime.\n\n*Also —* The framework gives practitioners a fast diagnostic for how much robustness headroom remains, refocusing effort on closing the gap rather than on the attack-defense arms race.","poster_thumb":"posters/073_characterizing_the_optimal_0_1_loss_for_multi_cl.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/073_characterizing_the_optimal_0_1_loss_for_multi_cl/073_characterizing_the_optimal_0_1_loss_for_multi_cl/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/073_characterizing_the_optimal_0_1_loss_for_multi_cl/073_characterizing_the_optimal_0_1_loss_for_multi_cl/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/073_characterizing_the_optimal_0_1_loss_for_multi_cl/073_characterizing_the_optimal_0_1_loss_for_multi_cl/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/073_characterizing_the_optimal_0_1_loss_for_multi_cl/073_characterizing_the_optimal_0_1_loss_for_multi_cl/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/073_characterizing_the_optimal_0_1_loss_for_multi_cl/073_characterizing_the_optimal_0_1_loss_for_multi_cl/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/073_characterizing_the_optimal_0_1_loss_for_multi_cl/073_characterizing_the_optimal_0_1_loss_for_multi_cl/reel.html"}},{"id":"074","slug":"074_deep_demonstration_tracing_learning_generalizabl","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Deep Demonstration Tracing: Learning Generalizable Imitator Policy for Runtime Imitation from a Single Demonstration","authors":"Xiong-Hui Chen¹², Junyin Ye¹², Hang Zhao³², Yi-Chen Li¹², Xu-Hui Liu¹², Haoran Shi², Yu-Yan Xu², Zhihao Ye¹², Si-Hang Yang¹², Yang Yu¹², Anqi Huang⁴², Kai Xu³, Zongzhang Zhang¹","institutes":"¹Nanjing University; ²Polixir Technologies; ³National University of Defense Technology; ⁴Nanjing University of Science and Technology","venue":"ICML 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.05285","code_url":"https://github.com/xionghuichen/Deep-Demonstration-Tracing","hook":"The paper (1) advances the OSIL setting with a strong gap between demonstration collection and deployment, backed by a new demo-navigation benchmark; (2) proposes a demonstration transformer architecture that traces demonstrations; and (3)…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nOne-shot imitation learning (OSIL) trains an agent to perform a task from a single demonstration, but existing methods assume the deployment environment closely resembles demonstration collection and break down when unforeseen changes occur.\n\n*Also —* After a demonstration is collected, unexpected obstacles, disturbances, or pedestrians can push the agent into states never shown, where naive replay of demonstrated actions fails.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nHumans imitating a route can detour around an unexpected obstacle and then rejoin the original path; agents need this same adaptive tracing ability rather than blind replay.\n\n*Also —* Prior OSIL work embeds a demonstration as a free context vector and clones actions, offering no guarantee of sensible behavior in unseen states.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper (1) advances the OSIL setting with a strong gap between demonstration collection and deployment, backed by a new demo-navigation benchmark; (2) proposes a demonstration transformer architecture that traces demonstrations; and (3) casts OSIL as context-based meta-RL, with a theoretical condition for imitating from one trajectory.\n\n*Also —* It introduces Valet Parking Assist in Maze (VPAM) as a benchmark suite and shows DDT scales log-linearly with data and model size.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nDDT builds the imitator policy from a demonstration transformer that, given the current state as a query and demonstration state-action pairs as keys and values, identifies relevant demonstration states (attention weighting), analyzes expert behavior (point-wise multiplication), and traces back to take the action. It trains this policy with context-based meta-RL using a stationary OSIL reward, optimized by Soft Actor-Critic.\n\n**Key idea.** $R_{\\mathrm{OSIL}}(s,a) := W_{\\mathrm{OSIL}}((\\bar{s},\\bar{a}),(s,a)) + \\alpha R_{\\omega}(s,a)$ and the meta-RL objective $\\max_{\\Pi}\\; \\mathbb{E}_{M_{\\omega}\\sim P(M_{\\mathrm{train}})}\\, \\mathbb{E}_{M_{\\omega},\\Pi}\\!\\left[\\sum_{i=0}^{\\infty}\\gamma^{i} R_{\\mathrm{OSIL}}(s_i,a_i)\\right]$ with imitator policy $\\Pi := \\pi(a\\mid s,\\phi(\\tau_{\\omega}))$\n\n*Also —* The expert-state and visited-state encoders share weights; the imitator policy Π := π(a|s, φ(τ_ω)) integrates the context-based policy and a task-information extractor into one architecture, giving inductive bias that improves generalization to unseen demonstrations.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nThe authors build VPAM (Valet Parking Assist in Maze), a demo-navigation benchmark where a point agent travels from a start to a target in a maze using only l-step local views (8 rays × 5 steps), with randomly generated obstacles that may not exist in the demonstration. Eight tasks vary single- vs multi-map, obstacle presence, and coordinate availability.\n\n*Also —* DDT is further evaluated on Meta-World, Gymnasium (Reacher, Pusher), and a MuJoCo robot manipulation task with grasping, stacking, and clutter, plus partially observable VPAM variants.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn VPAM test settings DDT reaches success rates of 0.86 (Train), 0.84 (Non-Obstacle), and 0.73 (Unforeseen Obstacle), beating DCRL (0.71/0.62/0.57), CbMRL (0.62/0.47/0.36), and Trans4OSIL (0.33/0.28/0.16) across all settings.\n\n*Also —* Going from Train to Unforeseen Obstacle, DDT degrades only -15% versus -20%, -33%, and -52% for the baselines — at least 5% better performance retention. On Meta-World with disturbance, DDT reaches 0.61 on unseen demos versus ≤0.12 for baselines.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nReplacing the demonstration transformer with a standard transformer (DDT using transformer) significantly lowers asymptotic performance, and removing the OSIL reward (DDT w/o OSIL reward, learning only from the ending reward) sharply reduces learning efficiency — showing both components are essential.\n\n*Also —* Both ablations are run on multi-map imitation without obstacles and tested on unseen maps (Fig. 8a).\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- 0.73 DDT success rate under Unforeseen Obstacles (best baseline 0.57)\n\n*Also —* On mixed-up demonstrations DDT reaches 0.84 / 0.88 / 0.90 unseen-demo success across 2 / 4 / 8 environments; a single demonstration (|T_ω| = 1) suffices under the Ω-recoverable condition.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy teaching an imitator to adaptively trace a single demonstration through a purpose-built transformer trained with meta-RL, DDT achieves robust one-shot imitation that survives unforeseen environmental changes where prior methods collapse.\n\n*Also —* Its log-linear scaling suggests DDT could serve as a skeleton for larger generalist decision-making agents.","poster_thumb":"posters/074_deep_demonstration_tracing_learning_generalizabl.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/074_deep_demonstration_tracing_learning_generalizabl/074_deep_demonstration_tracing_learning_generalizabl/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/074_deep_demonstration_tracing_learning_generalizabl/074_deep_demonstration_tracing_learning_generalizabl/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/074_deep_demonstration_tracing_learning_generalizabl/074_deep_demonstration_tracing_learning_generalizabl/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/074_deep_demonstration_tracing_learning_generalizabl/074_deep_demonstration_tracing_learning_generalizabl/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/074_deep_demonstration_tracing_learning_generalizabl/074_deep_demonstration_tracing_learning_generalizabl/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/074_deep_demonstration_tracing_learning_generalizabl/074_deep_demonstration_tracing_learning_generalizabl/reel.html"}},{"id":"075","slug":"075_neupl_neural_population_learning","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"\"NeuPL: Neural Population Learning\"","authors":"Siqi Liu¹², Luke Marris¹², Daniel Hennes², Josh Merel², Nicolas Heess², Thore Graepel¹","institutes":"¹University College London; ²DeepMind","venue":"ICLR 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.07415","code_url":"","hook":"NeuPL represents an entire policy population inside one opponent-conditioned neural network, unifying self-play, fictitious play, and PSRO as different interaction graphs, with convergence guarantees to an N-step best-response.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nLearning in strategy games requires a diverse population of policies, usually grown by iteratively best-responding to existing ones — an approach that breaks down under real-world budgets.\n\n*Also —* Approximate best-responses must be truncated on hand-crafted schedules, leaving under-trained \"good-responses\" in the population, and each iteration wastefully relearns basic skills from scratch.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nPopulation size and per-iteration training budget are handcrafted knobs in prior work; NeuPL instead lets the meta-game solver decide the effective population size while sharing skills across policies.\n\n*Also —* Empirical successes like StarCraft leagues used close to a thousand agents at enormous cost, motivating a far more sample- and skill-efficient alternative.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nNeuPL represents an entire policy population inside one opponent-conditioned neural network, unifying self-play, fictitious play, and PSRO as different interaction graphs, with convergence guarantees to an N-step best-response.\n\n*Also —* The framework delegates the effective population size to the meta-graph solver (no handcrafted truncation), enables transfer learning across policies, and admits cyclic interaction graphs beyond PSRO's scope.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nA single network Πθ(·|o, σi) represents all policies, each conditioned on a meta-strategy σi drawn from an interaction graph Σ; policies are trained jointly by RL to best-respond to their assigned opponent mixtures, with a learned payoff estimator φω connecting the population to its empirical payoff matrix.\n\n**Key idea.** $J_{\\sigma_i} = \\mathbb{E}_{\\sigma_j \\sim P(\\sigma_i)}\\, \\mathbb{E}_{a\\sim\\Pi_\\theta(\\cdot|o_{\\le t},\\sigma_i),\\, a'\\sim\\Pi_\\theta(\\cdot|o'_{\\le t},\\sigma_j)} \\left[\\textstyle\\sum_t r_t \\gamma^t\\right]$ and the payoff-estimator loss $L_{ij} = \\mathbb{E}_{o\\sim D}\\big[(\\phi_\\omega(\\sigma_i,\\sigma_j) - \\mathbb{E}_{a\\sim\\Pi_\\theta(\\cdot|o,\\sigma_i)}[Q_\\theta(o,a,\\sigma_i,\\sigma_j)])^2\\big]$\n\n*Also —* Interaction graphs may be static (Σ fixed, recovering fictitious play) or adaptive (Σ ← F(U) from a meta-graph solver such as FPSRO-N, recovering PSRO-Nash); lower-triangular graphs yield the sequence of N-step best-responses, and identical rows σi = σj collapse to identical policies so the effective population size emerges automatically.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on three domains of increasing complexity: the normal-form game rock-paper-scissors, the spatiotemporal partially-observed game running-with-scissors, and the large-scale 2-vs-2 MuJoCo Football Game-of-Skills.\n\n*Also —* Running-with-scissors gives each player only a 4×4 first-person grid view and requires inferring the opponent's hidden inventory; MuJoCo Football couples continuous motor control with team coordination and latent strategic cycles.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nA NeuPL population of size 8 exploits PSRO baselines representing the same 8 policies even when those baselines received twice as many gradient updates, and its rising performance tracks its effective population size growing from 5 to 8.\n\n*Also —* Populations become less exploitable as they expand; effective population size plateaus at 12 across maximum sizes, and increasing the cap beyond 8 gives only marginal exploitability benefit.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nTransfer ablations show that agents transferring encoder and memory from a NeuPL network trained to epoch 1,000 learn exploiters to strong Nash mixtures (n=4 and n=7 policies) that randomly-initialized agents fail to counter even with prolonged training.\n\n*Also —* Against an easy mixture (n=2) even the random-init agent eventually succeeds, only slower; the gap widens sharply against competent opponents, and each transfer experiment is repeated five times.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- NeuPL population size **8** exploits PSRO populations of **8** policies while PSRO used **2×** the gradient updates.\n\n*Also —* Evaluated on 3 domains (rock-paper-scissors, running-with-scissors, 2-vs-2 MuJoCo Football); a population cap greater than 8 yields only marginal exploitability benefit.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy holding an entire policy population inside one opponent-conditioned network, NeuPL turns population learning from a sequence of wasteful from-scratch iterations into a single transfer-rich training run — where new strategies become more accessible as the population grows.\n\n*Also —* The same framework recovers self-play, fictitious play, and PSRO by swapping the interaction graph, and scales from rock-paper-scissors up to MuJoCo Football.","poster_thumb":"posters/075_neupl_neural_population_learning.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/075_neupl_neural_population_learning/075_neupl_neural_population_learning/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/075_neupl_neural_population_learning/075_neupl_neural_population_learning/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/075_neupl_neural_population_learning/075_neupl_neural_population_learning/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/075_neupl_neural_population_learning/075_neupl_neural_population_learning/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/075_neupl_neural_population_learning/075_neupl_neural_population_learning/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/075_neupl_neural_population_learning/075_neupl_neural_population_learning/reel.html"}},{"id":"076","slug":"076_timesiam_a_pre_training_framework_for_siamese_ti","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"TimeSiam: A Pre-Training Framework for Siamese Time-Series Modeling","authors":"Jiaxiang Dong¹, Haixu Wu¹, Yuxuan Wang¹, Yunzhong Qiu¹, Li Zhang¹, Jianmin Wang¹, Mingsheng Long¹","institutes":"¹School of Software, BNRist, Tsinghua University","venue":"ICML 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.02475","code_url":"https://github.com/thuml/TimeSiam","hook":"The paper proposes TimeSiam, a simple but effective Siamese pre-training framework that captures correlations among temporally distanced subseries; introduces learnable lineage embeddings to represent different past-to-current distances; an…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nTime series pre-training borrows masked modeling and contrastive learning from vision and language, but randomly masking a series or computing series-wise similarity distorts or neglects the temporal correlations that are intrinsic and crucial to time series.\n\n*Also —* Random masking can make reconstruction too hard to guide useful learning, while contrastive methods lean heavily on carefully chosen augmentations that are especially hard to design for time series.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nAn enormous, growing volume of unlabeled time series is collected from IoT sensors and wearables; a pre-training method that explicitly emphasizes temporal correlation modeling could unlock this data for downstream forecasting and classification.\n\n*Also —* Prior work treats each subseries in isolation and overlooks the chronological relationship between temporally distanced observations, leaving the time-dependent structure of the data unused.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper proposes TimeSiam, a simple but effective Siamese pre-training framework that captures correlations among temporally distanced subseries; introduces learnable lineage embeddings to represent different past-to-current distances; and demonstrates consistent state-of-the-art fine-tuning across forecasting and classification in both in- and cross-domain settings.\n\n*Also —* TimeSiam is backbone-agnostic (validated on iTransformer, PatchTST, and TCN) and supports both fixed-input and extended-input fine-tuning via multiple lineage embeddings.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nTimeSiam randomly samples a past subseries and a current subseries (\"Siamese subseries\") from the same time series, masks the current one via simple augmentation, and uses shared-weight Siamese encoders plus a cross-attention decoder to reconstruct the masked current series from the past observation. This past-to-current reconstruction forces the model to learn intrinsic temporal correlations.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{reconstruction}} = \\lVert x^{\\text{curr}} - \\hat{x}^{\\text{curr}} \\rVert_2^2$ ; $h_e = \\mathrm{Average}(h_{e,0}, h_{e,1}, \\dots, h_{e,n}),\\ \\text{where}\\ h_{e,i} = \\mathrm{Encoder}(\\mathrm{Embed}(x) \\oplus e^{\\text{lineage}}_i)$\n\n*Also —* Learnable lineage embeddings, indexed by the relative distance between past and current subseries, are added to the encoded representation so one model can capture correlations across different time distances; at fine-tuning these lineages are averaged (fixed input) or concatenated per segment (extended input).\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nExperiments span 13 benchmarks: eleven established datasets (ETT four subsets, Weather, Electricity, Traffic, Exchange for forecasting; AD, TDBrain, PTB for classification) plus two newly constructed large-scale multi-domain datasets, TSLD-500M and TSLD-1G, for cross-domain pre-training.\n\n*Also —* TSLD-1G contains 13.9M examples merged from non-overlapping domains, enabling large-scale cross-domain transfer studies.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn in-domain forecasting TimeSiam lowers average MSE by 5.7% (PatchTST) and 2.5% (iTransformer) over random initialization, and on in-domain classification it raises average accuracy by 11.5%, consistently outperforming eight strong self-supervised baselines in both in- and cross-domain settings.\n\n*Also —* In cross-domain transfer from TSLD-1G, TimeSiam sometimes exceeds even the in-domain result (e.g., TSLD-1G → ETTh1/ETTm1), underscoring the value of large, diverse pre-training data.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nAblations on Traffic (in-domain forecasting) show masked past-to-current reconstruction outperforms self-reconstruction, a moderate 25% masking ratio works best (15% oversimplifies, 75% is too hard), and lineage embeddings give consistent gains over random initialization.\n\n*Also —* Increasing the number of lineage embeddings improves ECL and Traffic performance up to a point, confirming that modeling multiple temporal distances helps.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- 5.7% / 2.5% average MSE reduction on in-domain forecasting (PatchTST / iTransformer) vs random init.\n\n*Also —* TSLD-1G pre-training dataset contains 13.9M examples across multiple domains.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy reframing time series pre-training as past-to-current reconstruction between Siamese subseries with learnable lineage embeddings, TimeSiam captures the temporal correlations that masking and contrastive methods miss, delivering simple, general, state-of-the-art transfer across tasks and domains.\n\n*Also —* The framework is drop-in for modern backbones and scales its benefits with larger, more diverse pre-training data.","poster_thumb":"posters/076_timesiam_a_pre_training_framework_for_siamese_ti.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/076_timesiam_a_pre_training_framework_for_siamese_ti/076_timesiam_a_pre_training_framework_for_siamese_ti/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/076_timesiam_a_pre_training_framework_for_siamese_ti/076_timesiam_a_pre_training_framework_for_siamese_ti/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/076_timesiam_a_pre_training_framework_for_siamese_ti/076_timesiam_a_pre_training_framework_for_siamese_ti/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/076_timesiam_a_pre_training_framework_for_siamese_ti/076_timesiam_a_pre_training_framework_for_siamese_ti/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/076_timesiam_a_pre_training_framework_for_siamese_ti/076_timesiam_a_pre_training_framework_for_siamese_ti/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/076_timesiam_a_pre_training_framework_for_siamese_ti/076_timesiam_a_pre_training_framework_for_siamese_ti/reel.html"}},{"id":"077","slug":"077_a_geometric_framework_for_understanding_memoriza","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"A Geometric Framework for Understanding Memorization in Generative Models","authors":"Brendan Leigh Ross¹, Hamidreza Kamkari¹, Tongzi Wu¹, Rasa Hosseinzadeh¹, Zhaoyan Liu¹, George Stein¹, Jesse C. Cresswell¹, Gabriel Loaiza-Ganem¹","institutes":"¹Layer 6 AI","venue":"ICLR 2025","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.00113","code_url":"https://github.com/layer6ai-labs/dgm_geometry","hook":"The paper introduces the manifold memorization hypothesis (MMH), which characterizes memorization by comparing the local intrinsic dimension (LID) of the model manifold to that of the ground-truth manifold, and formally separates overfittin…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nDeep generative models memorize and reproduce training datapoints, creating privacy and copyright risks, yet the field lacks a unified, formal language to define and reason about \"how memorized\" a sample is.\n\n*Also —* Prior definitions rely on pixel-space distance to a specific training point, which cannot capture reconstructive memorization (copied layout/style, not the whole image) and does not scale to LAION-2B / Stable Diffusion.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nA geometric view grounded in the manifold hypothesis promises tractable, scalable memorization detection where probability-mass-based theory does not, and can unify the many scattered empirical \"memorization phenomena\" reported in the literature.\n\n*Also —* Earlier probabilistic definitions require access to the full training set and huge numbers of samples, which is intractable at scale; a manifold-dimension perspective admits estimators that remain tractable for Stable Diffusion.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces the manifold memorization hypothesis (MMH), which characterizes memorization by comparing the local intrinsic dimension (LID) of the model manifold to that of the ground-truth manifold, and formally separates overfitting-driven memorization (OD-Mem) from data-driven memorization (DD-Mem).\n\n*Also —* Using the MMH it unifies prior observations (duplication, low complexity, specific conditioning), empirically validates the hypothesis from 2D synthetic data up to Stable Diffusion, and derives new tools for detecting memorized images even without captions and for mitigating memorization at sample time.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nFor a point x, the local intrinsic dimension LID(x) is the dimensionality of the manifold at x — equivalently the degrees of freedom, so that ℓ active constraints give LID(x) = d − ℓ. The MMH compares LIDθ(x) on the learned manifold Mθ to LID*(x) on the ground-truth manifold M*: OD-Mem occurs when the model is over-constrained (LIDθ(x) < LID*(x), a generalization failure), while DD-Mem occurs when LIDθ(x) is intrinsically low even though LIDθ(x) = LID*(x).\n\n**Key idea.** $\\mathrm{LID}(x) = d - \\ell$ ; $\\text{OD-Mem}:\\ \\mathrm{LID}_\\theta(x) < \\mathrm{LID}_*(x)$ ; $s_\\theta^{\\mathrm{CFG}}(x;t,c) = s_\\theta(x;t,\\varnothing) + \\lambda\\big(s_\\theta(x;t,c) - s_\\theta(x;t,\\varnothing)\\big)$ ; $\\mathrm{LID}_*(x_0 \\mid c) \\le \\mathrm{LID}_*(x_0)$\n\n*Also —* Because no estimator of LID* scales to Stable Diffusion, three tractable proxies for the model-side LIDθ are used at scale — unconditional LIDθ via FLIPD, conditional LIDθ(·|c), and the classifier-free-guidance (CFG) vector norm — and the paper shows conditioning can only lower LID (LID*(x₀|c) ≤ LID*(x₀)), which is why specific prompts encourage memorization. Mitigation backpropagates a differentiable memorization metric to attribute memorization to individual tokens, then uses GPT-4 to rephrase those tokens.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nValidation spans 2D synthetic von Mises mixtures, StyleGAN2-ADA and iDDPM trained on CIFAR10, and Stable Diffusion v1.5 on large-scale image data; for Stable Diffusion, 86 \"matching verbatim\" memorized LAION images (from Webster, 2023) are contrasted against non-memorized images.\n\n*Also —* The non-memorized pool mixes 2000 images from LAION-Aesthetics 6.5+, 2000 from COCO, and all 251 images from the Tuxemon dataset; the normal-bundle (NB) and FLIPD estimators are used for LID.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nAcross every scale, estimates of LID are strongly predictive of memorization: memorized images consistently receive small LIDθ values (and large CFG vector norms), directly confirming the MMH.\n\n*Also —* Crucially, the unconditional LIDθ detects memorized training images well even without access to their captions — a novel capability that the CFG-vector-norm technique of prior work cannot provide, since it requires the conditioning caption.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nFor mitigation, sweeping the number of perturbed tokens k over {1,2,3,4,6,8} traces the trade-off between memorization (SSCD similarity, lower is better) and fidelity (CLIP score, higher is better); attribution-based token selection beats a uniform-random-selection ablation.\n\n*Also —* New differentiable attribution metrics based on the CFG-adjusted score norm and on FLIPD perform on par with the original CFG-vector-norm metric of Wen et al. (2023); all attribution-based variants keep higher CLIP score at equal memorization reduction than random token perturbation.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nValidated from 2-dimensional synthetic data up to Stable Diffusion v1.5; 86 verbatim-memorized LAION images vs. ~4251 non-memorized images (2000 LAION-Aesthetics + 2000 COCO + 251 Tuxemon).\n\n*Also —* Three LIDθ proxies evaluated (unconditional LIDθ, conditional LIDθ(·|c), CFG vector norm); mitigation swept over k ∈ {1,2,3,4,6,8} tokens with 5 repetitions per prompt.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nMemorization in generative models is fundamentally about geometry: when the model's local intrinsic dimension is too low, the model memorizes — so measuring and raising LID both detects and mitigates memorization, even at Stable Diffusion scale.\n\n*Also —* The MMH cleanly separates overfitting-driven from data-driven memorization, unifies disparate prior observations, and enables caption-free detection of memorized training images.","poster_thumb":"posters/077_a_geometric_framework_for_understanding_memoriza.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/077_a_geometric_framework_for_understanding_memoriza/077_a_geometric_framework_for_understanding_memoriza/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/077_a_geometric_framework_for_understanding_memoriza/077_a_geometric_framework_for_understanding_memoriza/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/077_a_geometric_framework_for_understanding_memoriza/077_a_geometric_framework_for_understanding_memoriza/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/077_a_geometric_framework_for_understanding_memoriza/077_a_geometric_framework_for_understanding_memoriza/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/077_a_geometric_framework_for_understanding_memoriza/077_a_geometric_framework_for_understanding_memoriza/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/077_a_geometric_framework_for_understanding_memoriza/077_a_geometric_framework_for_understanding_memoriza/reel.html"}},{"id":"078","slug":"078_rvs_what_is_essential_for_offline_rl_via_supervi","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"\"RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?\"","authors":"Scott Emmons¹, Benjamin Eysenbach², Ilya Kostrikov¹, Sergey Levine¹","institutes":"¹UC Berkeley; ²Carnegie Mellon University","venue":"ICLR 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.10751","code_url":"https://github.com/scottemmons/rvs","hook":"The paper unifies existing goal- and reward-conditioned methods under a single RvS framework, then empirically isolates the essential design choices: model capacity, regularization, and the conditioning variable.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nSupervised learning can solve offline RL without temporal-difference learning, but it is unclear when this works and which algorithmic components are actually necessary for good performance.\n\n*Also —* Prior RvS methods disagree on whether advantage weighting or Transformer sequence models are needed, leaving practitioners without a clear recipe.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nValue-based offline RL is powerful in theory but hard to use, needing stabilization tricks and delicate tuning; converting RL into conditional imitation learning promises a simpler alternative.\n\n*Also —* If a minimal supervised recipe matches complex methods, it gives practitioners a dependable field guide and exposes where such methods still fail.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper unifies existing goal- and reward-conditioned methods under a single RvS framework, then empirically isolates the essential design choices: model capacity, regularization, and the conditioning variable.\n\n*Also —* It shows a two-layer MLP maximizing likelihood is competitive with state-of-the-art TD and Transformer methods, and it exposes RvS weakness on random data as an open problem.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nRvS trains an outcome-conditioned policy πθ(a | s, ω) with a two-layer feedforward MLP by maximizing the log-likelihood of observed actions, using hindsight relabeling so each action is a demonstration for the outcome it achieved.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\max_{\\theta} \\sum_{\\tau \\in \\mathcal{D}} \\sum_{1 \\le t \\le |\\tau|} \\mathbb{E}_{\\omega \\sim f(\\omega \\mid \\tau_{t:H})}\\big[\\log \\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t, \\omega)\\big]$ with goal outcomes $f(\\omega \\mid \\tau_{t:H}) = \\mathrm{Unif}(s_{t+1}, \\dots, s_H)$ (RvS-G) and reward outcomes $\\omega = \\tfrac{1}{H-t+1}\\sum_{t'=t}^{H} r(s_{t'}, a_{t'})$ (RvS-R)\n\n*Also —* Outcomes are either a future goal state (RvS-G) or an average future return (RvS-R), formed by concatenating the outcome onto the input state; no advantage weighting or Transformer is used.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on three D4RL suites (AntMaze v2, Gym Locomotion v2, Franka Kitchen v0) plus the GCSL goal-conditioned suite (FourRooms, Door, Pusher, Lander, Claw), spanning navigation, locomotion, and manipulation.\n\n*Also —* D4RL datasets range from random to medium-expert quality; GCSL tasks are adapted for offline RL using random-policy data, and scores are normalized to the [0, 100] range.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nA simple two-layer MLP is state-of-the-art on AntMaze (RvS-G 53.5 average vs 50.6 for the best baseline), Kitchen (RvS-G 54.0), and GCSL (RvS-G 62.0 vs 58.0 for online GCSL), and on Gym RvS-R matches Decision Transformer using only an MLP.\n\n*Also —* RvS-G surprisingly matches dynamic-programming methods on subtrajectory-stitching tasks (mixed Kitchen, all AntMaze), where value-based methods were thought essential.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nTwo design choices are decisive. Larger network width improves performance (up to 1024 hidden units), and dropout regularization helps or hurts depending on the dataset (boosts kitchen-complete, hurts hopper-medium-expert and antmaze-medium-play).\n\n*Also —* A categorical distribution over discretized actions matches or beats a unimodal Gaussian across the GCSL suite, again favoring higher policy capacity; validation loss only loosely predicts final performance.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- RvS-G AntMaze average: **53.5** (best baseline CQL-p: 50.6) — state-of-the-art\n\n*Also —* - RvS-R Gym average: **54.7**, matching Decision Transformer (57.0) with only an MLP\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nA plain two-layer MLP trained to maximize likelihood is a state-of-the-art offline RL method, provided you carefully tune capacity and regularization and choose the right conditioning variable (goals vs rewards).\n\n*Also —* RvS remains comparatively weak on random data, where TD learning still wins, marking a clear open problem.","poster_thumb":"posters/078_rvs_what_is_essential_for_offline_rl_via_supervi.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/078_rvs_what_is_essential_for_offline_rl_via_supervi/078_rvs_what_is_essential_for_offline_rl_via_supervi/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/078_rvs_what_is_essential_for_offline_rl_via_supervi/078_rvs_what_is_essential_for_offline_rl_via_supervi/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/078_rvs_what_is_essential_for_offline_rl_via_supervi/078_rvs_what_is_essential_for_offline_rl_via_supervi/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/078_rvs_what_is_essential_for_offline_rl_via_supervi/078_rvs_what_is_essential_for_offline_rl_via_supervi/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/078_rvs_what_is_essential_for_offline_rl_via_supervi/078_rvs_what_is_essential_for_offline_rl_via_supervi/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/078_rvs_what_is_essential_for_offline_rl_via_supervi/078_rvs_what_is_essential_for_offline_rl_via_supervi/reel.html"}},{"id":"079","slug":"079_near_real_time_monitoring_of_global_ocean_carbon","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Near-real-time monitoring of global ocean carbon sink","authors":"Piyu Ke¹²³, Xiaofan Gui³, Wei Cao³, Dezhi Wang⁴, Ce Hou⁵⁶, Lixing Wang¹, Xuanren Song¹, Yun Li⁷, Biqing Zhu⁸, Jiang Bian³, Stephen Sitch², Philippe Ciais⁹¹⁰, Pierre Friedlingstein², Zhu Liu¹¹¹","institutes":"¹Tsinghua University; ²University of Exeter; ³Microsoft Research; ⁴Lanzhou University; ⁵Hong Kong University of Science and Technology; ⁶Peking University; ⁷KU Leuven; ⁸International Institute for Applied Systems Analysis (IIASA); ⁹Laboratoire des Sciences du Climat et de l'Environnement (LSCE); ¹⁰The Cyprus Institute; ¹¹The University of Hong Kong","venue":"ICLR 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.01637","code_url":"https://github.com/kepiyu/CMO-NRT","hook":"The paper presents Carbon Monitor Ocean (CMO-NRT), a near-real-time, monthly grid-based dataset of global surface ocean fCO2 and air-sea CO2 flux, and the deep-learning framework that produces it.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nOfficial estimates of the global ocean carbon sink from the annual Global Carbon Budget lag actual conditions by roughly one year, so recent changes in ocean CO2 uptake cannot be monitored in time.\n\n*Also —* The delay stems from the computational cost of ocean biogeochemical models and from the roughly one-year lag of the underlying SOCAT surface-ocean CO2 observations that data products depend on.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nThe global stocktake under the Paris Agreement and intensifying climate mitigation efforts demand accurate ocean carbon-sink data with much lower latency than a one-year delay allows.\n\n*Also —* Existing fCO2 and air-sea flux databases (GOBMs, data products) are geographically detailed but always historical, leaving a gap for timely, near-present monitoring of the sink.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper presents Carbon Monitor Ocean (CMO-NRT), a near-real-time, monthly grid-based dataset of global surface ocean fCO2 and air-sea CO2 flux, and the deep-learning framework that produces it.\n\n*Also —* It updates the 10 GOBMs and 8 data products of the Global Carbon Budget 2022 to a near-real-time framework, releases the data publicly on Figshare and at carbonsink.microsoft.com, and provides open code.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nA CNN-plus-linear model is trained with semi-supervised learning to learn the non-linear relationship between GOBM/data-product estimates and observed predictors (year, month, latitude, longitude, and nine environmental factors such as SST, ICE, CHL, MLD, CO2, SSS, SSH, SLP, wind). Global 180×360 grids are cut into 18×18 patches; a supervised RMSE loss on labeled data is combined with an unsupervised consistency loss between weakly (10% features masked) and strongly (30% masked) augmented predictions.\n\n**Key idea.** $L = w\\,L_u + L_s,\\quad L_s = \\mathrm{RMSE}(y,\\hat{y}),\\quad L_u = \\mathrm{RMSE}(\\hat{y}_{\\text{weak}},\\hat{y}_{\\text{strong}})$\n\n*Also —* Predictions are ensembled via K-fold cross-validation; near-real-time xCO2 is extended by a separate model achieving a test RMSE of 1.74 (~0.5% error). The CNN stacks hidden dimensions 9→64→64 and linear layers 64→64→1 with GELU activation and dropout p=0.25.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nCMO-NRT provides monthly gridded global surface-ocean fCO2 and air-sea CO2 flux from January 2022 to July 2023, derived from the 10 GOBMs and 8 data products of the Global Carbon Budget 2022.\n\n*Also —* Inputs include ESA GlobColour chlorophyll-a, sea-surface temperature, sea ice, mixed-layer depth, salinity, sea-surface height, sea-level pressure and wind; the dataset is released on Figshare (doi:10.6084/m9.figshare.24658494) and updated on the project website.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nValidating on held-out 2020-2021 data, CMO-NRT predictions match the original GOBM and data-product outputs closely, with most per-model correlations exceeding R²=0.9 and global-aggregate monthly fCO2 correlations mostly above R²=0.85.\n\n*Also —* For global monthly averages, predicted values were only slightly higher than originals, with most differences under 3 µatm, and GOBMs showed more stable, tightly clustered agreement than data products.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nPer-model evaluation shows GOBMs achieve higher and more consistent skill (e.g., MRI-ESM2-1 R²=0.97, CESM2 R²=0.96) than several data products, where occasional points deviate strongly from the fit line (e.g., UOEX-Watson R²=0.80, MPI-SOMFFN R²=0.53 in global-average comparison).\n\n*Also —* Spatial-difference maps confirm agreement is largely uniform, with the largest prediction-original discrepancies concentrated in the Arctic Ocean and the equatorial Pacific.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nReduces the ~1-year reporting latency of the ocean carbon sink to near-real-time (monthly), covering January 2022-July 2023; correlation R² > 0.9 for most of the 10 GOBMs and 8 data products; global-aggregate R² > 0.85; global monthly fCO2 differences mostly < 3 µatm; near-real-time xCO2 model RMSE 1.74 (~0.5%).\n\n*Also —* 18 source estimates (10 GOBMs + 8 data products) updated; 180×360 global grid processed as 18×18 patches; 9 environmental predictors.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy using CNNs and semi-supervised learning to update biogeochemical models and data products, CMO-NRT turns the once year-delayed global ocean carbon sink into a near-real-time, monthly, gridded monitor.\n\n*Also —* This gives scientists and policymakers a timelier, spatially detailed constraint on ocean CO2 uptake for the global carbon budget and climate-response decisions.","poster_thumb":"posters/079_near_real_time_monitoring_of_global_ocean_carbon.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/079_near_real_time_monitoring_of_global_ocean_carbon/079_near_real_time_monitoring_of_global_ocean_carbon/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/079_near_real_time_monitoring_of_global_ocean_carbon/079_near_real_time_monitoring_of_global_ocean_carbon/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/079_near_real_time_monitoring_of_global_ocean_carbon/079_near_real_time_monitoring_of_global_ocean_carbon/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/079_near_real_time_monitoring_of_global_ocean_carbon/079_near_real_time_monitoring_of_global_ocean_carbon/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/079_near_real_time_monitoring_of_global_ocean_carbon/079_near_real_time_monitoring_of_global_ocean_carbon/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/079_near_real_time_monitoring_of_global_ocean_carbon/079_near_real_time_monitoring_of_global_ocean_carbon/reel.html"}},{"id":"080","slug":"080_uni_mask_unified_inference_in_sequential_decisio","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Uni[MASK]: Unified Inference in Sequential Decision Problems","authors":"Micah Carroll¹, Orr Paradise¹, Jessy Lin¹, Raluca Georgescu², Mingfei Sun², David Bignell², Stephanie Milani³, Katja Hofmann², Matthew Hausknecht², Anca Dragan¹, Sam Devlin²","institutes":"¹UC Berkeley; ²Microsoft Research; ³Carnegie Mellon University","venue":"NeurIPS 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.10869","code_url":"https://github.com/micahcarroll/uniMASK","hook":"The paper introduces Uni[MASK], a framework that expresses inference tasks in sequential decision problems as input masking schemes, letting one model be trained on many tasks at once.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nSequential decision problems are usually solved one task at a time, with a separate model trained for behavior cloning, offline RL, dynamics prediction, or goal conditioning, despite their shared structure.\n\n*Also —* This siloing wastes the fact that all these inferences operate over the same trajectory of states, actions, and returns and could share representations.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nMasked language modeling shows that predicting randomly masked tokens yields representations transferable across many downstream tasks; the authors observe decision-making inferences are just different maskings of a trajectory.\n\n*Also —* Predicting a masked last action given prior states and actions is exactly a behavior-cloning inference, so the whole family of tasks fits one bidirectional masked-prediction objective.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces Uni[MASK], a framework that expresses inference tasks in sequential decision problems as input masking schemes, letting one model be trained on many tasks at once.\n\n*Also —* It shows a single Uni[MASK] model matches or beats single-task models across tasks, and consistently outperforms them after fine-tuning; it also introduces an improved GPT baseline, Decision-GPT.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nA trajectory is tokenized into per-timestep state, action, and property (return-to-go) tokens; a masking scheme specifies which input tokens are shown and which outputs must be predicted, and a bidirectional BERT-style transformer encoder predicts the masked tokens.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\tau = \\{(s_0,a_0,p_0),\\dots,(s_T,a_T,p_T)\\}, \\quad \\hat{R}_t = \\sum_{t'=t}^{T} r_{t'}$ and the behavior-cloning inference $P(a_t \\mid s_{0:t}, a_{0:t-1})$, with the general masked inference example $P(a_2 \\mid s_{0:2,T}, a_{0:1}, \\hat{R}_0)$.\n\n*Also —* Four training regimes are compared: single-task, multi-task (random scheme per snippet), random-mask (arbitrary token masking), and fine-tune (random-mask pretraining then task-specific fine-tuning). Positional encoding replaces timestep encoding and only the first RTG token is fed in.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nTwo environments: a MiniGrid gridworld navigating to a goal behind a locked door, and the MuJoCo-physics Maze2D (D4RL) continuous-control maze.\n\n*Also —* MiniGrid data is used for the qualitative and validation-loss task comparisons; Maze2D reports reward from 1000 rollouts across 5 seeds against feedforward, Decision Transformer, and Decision-GPT baselines at context lengths 5 and 10.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nIn MiniGrid, random-mask training outperforms single-task on all tasks, and random-mask pretraining plus fine-tuning gives the best performance, beating single-task on every task except behavior cloning.\n\n*Also —* In Maze2D, fine-tuning is critical: multi-task-plus-fine-tune and random-mask-plus-fine-tune Uni[MASK] models beat all baselines at context length 5, reaching reward around 2.73 to 2.74 versus 1.13 to 1.49 for Decision Transformer.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nComparing the four training regimes isolates fine-tuning as the key ingredient, and comparing single-task Uni[MASK] against Decision-GPT isolates BERT-style versus GPT-style backbones.\n\n*Also —* At context length 10, BERT-style Uni[MASK] models degrade and are outperformed by the GPT-based Decision-GPT, exposing a difficulty of BERT-like architectures with longer-sequence generation; feeding only the first RTG token beat feeding it at every timestep.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Maze2D reward (context length 5, BC): Uni[MASK] multi-task + finetune 2.73 vs Decision Transformer 1.13\n\n*Also —* - Results averaged over 6 seeds (MiniGrid) and 5 seeds / 1000 rollouts (Maze2D)\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nCasting sequential-decision tasks as masking schemes lets one masked-prediction model do the job of many specialized models, and random-mask pretraining plus fine-tuning generally beats task-specific training.\n\n*Also —* BERT-style backbones excel at short contexts but struggle with longer-sequence generation, pointing to GPT-plus-random-masking as promising future work.","poster_thumb":"posters/080_uni_mask_unified_inference_in_sequential_decisio.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/080_uni_mask_unified_inference_in_sequential_decisio/080_uni_mask_unified_inference_in_sequential_decisio/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/080_uni_mask_unified_inference_in_sequential_decisio/080_uni_mask_unified_inference_in_sequential_decisio/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/080_uni_mask_unified_inference_in_sequential_decisio/080_uni_mask_unified_inference_in_sequential_decisio/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/080_uni_mask_unified_inference_in_sequential_decisio/080_uni_mask_unified_inference_in_sequential_decisio/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/080_uni_mask_unified_inference_in_sequential_decisio/080_uni_mask_unified_inference_in_sequential_decisio/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/080_uni_mask_unified_inference_in_sequential_decisio/080_uni_mask_unified_inference_in_sequential_decisio/reel.html"}},{"id":"081","slug":"081_dichotomy_in_compositional_reasoning_scaling_and","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Do Large Language Models Have Compositional Ability? An Investigation into Limitations and Scalability","authors":"Zhuoyan Xu¹, Zhenmei Shi¹, Yingyu Liang¹","institutes":"¹University of Wisconsin-Madison","venue":"COLM 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.15720","code_url":"https://github.com/OliverXUZY/LLM_Compose","hook":"The paper (1) builds a test suite of linguistic and logical composite tasks with simple tasks as in-context examples, (2) empirically reveals a dichotomy in compositional behavior across LLM families and scales, and (3) gives a theoretical…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nCan an LLM that learns two simple tasks in-context automatically solve their composition, an unseen composite task, without ever seeing composite examples? This basic generalization is trivial for humans but largely untested for LLMs.\n\n*Also —* Compositional ability, solving unseen complex tasks that combine simpler ones, is viewed as essential for general intelligence, yet how LLMs handle composite tasks not seen in pretraining remains open and underexplored.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nLLMs excel at individual in-context tasks, but real reasoning demands fusing skills. It is unknown whether their in-context learning integrates known task abilities or fails to compose them.\n\n*Also —* Prior work studies compositional generalization narrowly and lacks a theoretical account of when and why composition succeeds; even GPT-4 and Claude 3 fail simple asterisk-and-parenthesis composite tasks despite solving each part.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper (1) builds a test suite of linguistic and logical composite tasks with simple tasks as in-context examples, (2) empirically reveals a dichotomy in compositional behavior across LLM families and scales, and (3) gives a theoretical analysis in a linear self-attention model explaining when composition emerges.\n\n*Also —* It connects the nature of a task, whether its sub-tasks act on separate input parts, to whether scaling helps, offering practical guidance on which composite tasks LLMs can be trusted to solve.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nUnder standard in-context learning (K=10 simple-task examples plus one test input), the authors evaluate four settings per composite task: each simple task alone, composite (simple-task demos, composite test), and composite in-context (composite demos, the gold-standard upper bound). They test Llama-1/2/3 and GPT families across scales on word-level and arithmetic tasks. Theoretically, they analyze a one-layer single-head linear self-attention network and show composition succeeds when the two simple tasks have \"confined support\", occupying separate feature subspaces of the input embedding.\n\n**Key idea.** $f_{\\mathrm{LSA},\\theta}(E) = E + W^{PV} E \\cdot \\dfrac{E^{\\top} W^{KQ} E}{N}$\n\n*Also —* Separable composite tasks (sub-tasks on distinct input parts) are contrasted with compose-by-step tasks (chained multi-step reasoning that shares embedding support). The theory formalizes compositional ability and links per-task accuracy gains under scale to the separable case.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nA custom test suite of composite tasks pairing simple building-block tasks. Linguistic tasks include Capitalization, Swap, and translation tasks (Phrase Recombination with Longer Chain; Passive-to-Active with Object-to-Subject). Logical tasks include arithmetic operations (Two Sum, Modular, Plus One) composed with word-level ones.\n\n*Also —* Tasks are grouped into separable composites (e.g., Capitalization & Plus One) versus compose-by-step composites (e.g., Capitalization & Swap, Modular & Two Sum Plus). Exact-match accuracy is used for logical tasks; word error rate (WER) for translation tasks.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nLLMs show divergent behavior. On separable composite tasks they compose well and improve with scale, approaching the composite in-context gold standard. On compose-by-step tasks they largely fail: Llama models solve each simple task at ~90% accuracy but drop to ~20% or below on the composite, and scaling up does not help.\n\n*Also —* Supplying composite in-context examples restores near-simple-task accuracy, showing the models have the representation power but fail to compose it from simple-task demonstrations alone. The same composition failure appears in GPT-4 and Claude 3.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nVarying model scale within families cleanly separates the two regimes: on separable tasks accuracy rises with scale, while on compose-by-step tasks it stays flat or degrades. Switching from composite to composite-in-context demonstrations recovers performance, isolating composition (not capability) as the bottleneck.\n\n*Also —* Across word-level, arithmetic, and translation task families the same separable-vs-step dichotomy holds, and larger models improve on the composite task only when the underlying simple tasks improve, matching the confined-support scaling theory.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Simple tasks (capitalization / swap): ~90% accuracy for Llama families.\n\n*Also —* In-context example count K=10; evaluated across Llama-1 (7b-65b), Llama-2 (3b-70b), Llama-3, and GPT (0.74b-6b) model scales.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nLLMs can compose two skills only when the sub-tasks act on separate parts of the input; for tasks needing chained multi-step reasoning they fail and scaling does not rescue them.\n\n*Also —* Whether more compute helps a composite reasoning task is predictable from the task's structure: separable (confined-support) tasks scale, entangled multi-step tasks do not.","poster_thumb":"posters/081_dichotomy_in_compositional_reasoning_scaling_and.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/081_dichotomy_in_compositional_reasoning_scaling_and/081_dichotomy_in_compositional_reasoning_scaling_and/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/081_dichotomy_in_compositional_reasoning_scaling_and/081_dichotomy_in_compositional_reasoning_scaling_and/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/081_dichotomy_in_compositional_reasoning_scaling_and/081_dichotomy_in_compositional_reasoning_scaling_and/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/081_dichotomy_in_compositional_reasoning_scaling_and/081_dichotomy_in_compositional_reasoning_scaling_and/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/081_dichotomy_in_compositional_reasoning_scaling_and/081_dichotomy_in_compositional_reasoning_scaling_and/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/081_dichotomy_in_compositional_reasoning_scaling_and/081_dichotomy_in_compositional_reasoning_scaling_and/reel.html"}},{"id":"082","slug":"082_data_efficient_large_vision_models_through_seque","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Data-efficient Large Vision Models through Sequential Autoregression","authors":"Jianyuan Guo¹, Zhiwei Hao², Chengcheng Wang³, Yehui Tang³, Han Wu¹, Han Hu², Kai Han³, Chang Xu¹","institutes":"¹University of Sydney; ²Beijing Institute of Technology; ³Huawei Noah's Ark Lab","venue":"ICML 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.04841","code_url":"https://github.com/ggjy/DeLVM","hook":"The paper introduces DeLVM, a data-efficient autoregressive vision model, and shows that (1) data augmentation rebalances long-tailed multi-task data as effectively as adding new data, and (2) knowledge distillation transfers a large teache…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nAutoregressive large vision models (LVMs) generalize across tasks but rely on colossal models (3B+ parameters) and enormous visual corpora (~400B tokens, 1.64B images), making them costly and hard to deploy.\n\n*Also —* Vision tasks follow a long-tailed distribution, so naively mixing benchmarks lets data-rich tasks (segmentation) swamp data-poor ones (keypoint detection), which the model then fails to learn.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nVision lacks the massive uniform corpora that NLP enjoys, so the one-epoch, data-abundant training recipes of large language models cannot transfer directly to data-limited vision tasks.\n\n*Also —* Prior LVM work chases ever-larger models and datasets; the compression techniques (augmentation, distillation) routine elsewhere in vision remain unexplored for autoregressive LVMs.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces DeLVM, a data-efficient autoregressive vision model, and shows that (1) data augmentation rebalances long-tailed multi-task data as effectively as adding new data, and (2) knowledge distillation transfers a large teacher's ability into a compact student LVM for both single- and multi-task settings.\n\n*Also —* It further demonstrates a practical 80M-parameter model that, with augmentation plus distillation, even reaches 83% top-1 accuracy on ImageNet classification, hinting that generation and understanding can be learned jointly.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nImages are tokenized by an off-the-shelf VQGAN into 256 discrete tokens per image and assembled into visual sentences; a causal LLaMA transformer is trained by next-token prediction. Data augmentation (random crop/flip, tokenized and mixed in) rebalances scarce tasks, and knowledge distillation guides a small student LLaMA-300M/80M with a LLaMA-1B teacher.\n\n**Key idea.** $s_i = \\{x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{L-1}, x_L\\}$, $s_o = \\{x_2, x_3, \\ldots, x_L, \\varnothing\\}$, $\\mathcal{L} = \\mathrm{CrossEntropy}(s_o, \\hat{s}_o)$\n\n*Also —* At inference, several input-output image pairs form a prompt that defines the task; concatenated with a tokenized test image, the model autoregressively generates the next 256 tokens, which the VQGAN decoder turns back into an image.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nTraining and evaluation span three vision tasks, image segmentation (subsets of SA-1B), human pose estimation (COCO-Pose), and image deraining (Rain13K), plus foreground segmentation on Pascal-5i and image classification on ImageNet.\n\n*Also —* Held-out SA-1B subsets, MPII, and Test2800 serve as validation sets; metrics include validation loss, pixel accuracy, perplexity, and mIoU. The VQGAN encoder is trained on Laion.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nBalancing the three-task mixture by augmentation gives the best overall performance, and knowledge distillation consistently improves the compact student on every task in both single-task and multi-task settings; the distilled LLaMA-300M outperforms its from-scratch counterpart on Pascal-5i foreground segmentation.\n\n*Also —* Increasing SA-1B data from 1% to 10% (0.34B to 3.43B tokens) cuts validation loss by 0.19 and perplexity by 22.4; augmentation reproduces this trend without any new data.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nPrompt background color strongly shapes outputs (black-background prompts ease post-processing); without data shuffling the model catastrophically forgets, performing well only on its most recently trained task; and distillation helps even at 80M parameters.\n\n*Also —* Table 5 shows continual (unshuffled) training drives perplexity on earlier tasks to 1000+, confirming catastrophic forgetting; Table 6 shows the LLaMA-80M student improves on all three tasks with KD.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Compact student **LLaMA-300M / 80M** vs. the ~**3B**-parameter, ~**400B**-token LVM baseline.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nClassical data augmentation and knowledge distillation make autoregressive large vision models data- and parameter-efficient, delivering strong multi-task performance from compact models trained on limited data.\n\n*Also —* A tiny 80M model reaching 83% ImageNet accuracy suggests a promising path toward unified, deployable generalist vision models that jointly learn generation and understanding.","poster_thumb":"posters/082_data_efficient_large_vision_models_through_seque.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/082_data_efficient_large_vision_models_through_seque/082_data_efficient_large_vision_models_through_seque/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/082_data_efficient_large_vision_models_through_seque/082_data_efficient_large_vision_models_through_seque/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/082_data_efficient_large_vision_models_through_seque/082_data_efficient_large_vision_models_through_seque/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/082_data_efficient_large_vision_models_through_seque/082_data_efficient_large_vision_models_through_seque/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/082_data_efficient_large_vision_models_through_seque/082_data_efficient_large_vision_models_through_seque/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/082_data_efficient_large_vision_models_through_seque/082_data_efficient_large_vision_models_through_seque/reel.html"}},{"id":"083","slug":"083_information_gain_propagation_a_new_way_to_graph","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"\"Information Gain Propagation: A New Way to Graph Active Learning with Soft Labels\"","authors":"Wentao Zhang¹, Yexin Wang¹, Zhenbang You¹, Meng Cao², Ping Huang², Jiulong Shan², Zhi Yang¹³, Bin Cui¹³⁴","institutes":"¹Peking University; ²Apple; ³National Engineering Laboratory for Big Data Analysis and Applications; ⁴Institute of Computational Social Science, Peking University (Qingdao)","venue":"ICLR 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.01093","code_url":"","hook":"IGP is the first graph active-learning framework built on relaxed binary queries and soft labels, paired with a new node-selection criterion that explicitly maximizes information-gain propagation across the graph.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nGNNs need many labeled nodes, and existing graph active-learning methods assume an oracle can name the exact class of every selected node, an expensive multi-class query that exceeds expert capability when categories are many.\n\n*Also —* Exact labeling is costly, especially out-of-domain (e.g. ogbn-papers100M has 172 classes), so hard-label queries do not scale with the number of categories.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nJudging the exact class is much harder than confirming a guess. A relaxed yes/no query is cheaper per node, and prior uncertainty-based criteria ignore that a labeled node propagates information to its k-hop neighbors in a GNN.\n\n*Also —* An exact multi-class query costs roughly c−1 times a binary query for c classes; prior criteria maximize single-node uncertainty, not graph-wide information gain.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nIGP is the first graph active-learning framework built on relaxed binary queries and soft labels, paired with a new node-selection criterion that explicitly maximizes information-gain propagation across the graph.\n\n*Also —* It works on any GNN backbone (SGC, APPNP, GCN, MVGRL) and consistently beats state-of-the-art AL baselines under equal labeling cost.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nFor each candidate node the oracle answers only \"is the predicted label correct?\" (yes/no), producing a normalized soft label. IGP estimates each node's influence magnitude on its neighbors, weights the information gain by that influence, and selects the budget-constrained node set that maximizes total propagated information gain.\n\n**Key idea.** $IGP(v_j,v_i,k)=H\\!\\Big(\\sum_{v_m\\in V_l} I_f(v_j,v_m,k)\\hat{y}_m\\Big)-H\\!\\Big(\\sum_{v_m\\in V_l\\cup\\{v_i\\}} I_f(v_j,v_m,k)\\hat{y}'_m\\Big)$ and the selection objective $\\max_{V_l} F(V_l)=\\sum_{v_i\\in V_l}\\sum_{v_j\\in RF(v_i)} IGP(v_j,v_i,k),\\;\\; s.t.\\;|V_l|=B$\n\n*Also —* Pipeline loops Model Training (GNN on soft labels) → Node Selection (maximize IGP = influence magnitude × information gain) → Node Labeling (relaxed query → normalized label) → update. Influence magnitude uses feature-gradient / random-walk propagation over a k-layer GNN.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on three citation networks (Cora, Citeseer, PubMed), one large social network (Reddit), and one OGB graph (ogbn-arxiv), under a fixed labeling-cost budget rather than a fixed label count.\n\n*Also —* Budget is measured as query cost (money), with an exact query costing c−1× a relaxed query; budgets ranged from 2C to 20C labels (C = number of classes).\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nUnder equal labeling budget (20 labels/class, GCN), IGP reaches 86.4% on Cora, 75.8% on Citeseer, and 83.6% on PubMed, beating the best baseline GRAIN by 1.6–2.2% on citation networks, 0.9% on Reddit, and 0.6% on ogbn-arxiv.\n\n*Also —* IGP's accuracy rises fastest as budget grows, and it stays ahead across every budget size. On PubMed it beats ALG and GRAIN by more than 1.8% across four GNN backbones (SGC, APPNP, GCN, MVGRL).\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nRemoving any component hurts accuracy. Dropping informative selection costs up to −3.2% (PubMed), informative training up to −2.9%, information quantity up to −2.1%, and normalized label up to −2.4%.\n\n*Also —* Informative selection is the single most important component (−2.5% on Cora vs smaller gaps for others), confirming that propagation-aware selection, not just soft-label training, drives the gains.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n86.4% Cora accuracy; +2.2% over the best baseline on Cora; consistent SOTA across 5 datasets and 4 GNN backbones; binary yes/no query replaces multi-class labeling.\n\n*Also —* +1.6–2.2% on citation networks; >1.8% over ALG/GRAIN on PubMed across backbones.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nAsk a cheaper yes/no question and pick nodes whose information gain propagates farthest, and graph active learning gets both more accurate and much cheaper to label.\n\n*Also —* Relaxed queries plus propagation-aware selection is a general recipe that plugs into any GNN.","poster_thumb":"posters/083_information_gain_propagation_a_new_way_to_graph.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/083_information_gain_propagation_a_new_way_to_graph/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/083_information_gain_propagation_a_new_way_to_graph/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/083_information_gain_propagation_a_new_way_to_graph/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/083_information_gain_propagation_a_new_way_to_graph/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/083_information_gain_propagation_a_new_way_to_graph/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/083_information_gain_propagation_a_new_way_to_graph/reel.html"}},{"id":"084","slug":"084_re_graph_edit_networks","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"[Re] Graph Edit Networks","authors":"Vid Stropnik¹, Maruša Oražem¹","institutes":"¹Faculty of Computer and Information Science, University of Ljubljana","venue":"ReScience C 2022","paper_url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6574701","code_url":"https://github.com/MarusaOrazem/reproducibility_challenge","hook":"The authors re-implement and re-run GEN, exhaustively document the five synthetic data-generating processes, propose an alternative risk-estimation protocol with proper train/test separation, and critically evaluate whether the benchmarks a…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nPredicting how a graph evolves over time is hard: most graph neural networks output node or edge scores, not the explicit, interpretable sequence of structural changes needed to turn one graph into the next.\n\n*Also —* Graph Edit Networks (GEN) address this with an output layer that emits edit scripts, but the original claims rest on lightly-described synthetic benchmarks and untested scaling arguments.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nThe original GEN paper reports strong wins over baselines and favourable sub-quadratic and linear scaling, but the synthetic data generators and runtime claims were never described in enough detail to verify independently.\n\n*Also —* Reproducibility matters most exactly where results look impressive on small, custom benchmarks whose difficulty is hard to judge from the paper alone.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe authors re-implement and re-run GEN, exhaustively document the five synthetic data-generating processes, propose an alternative risk-estimation protocol with proper train/test separation, and critically evaluate whether the benchmarks actually test the model's expressive power.\n\n*Also —* They also debug and help fix a section of the original authors' code and contribute a more rigorous scaling analysis of forward and backward passes.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nGEN is an output layer for GNNs that, given the current graph and a teaching signal derived from a reference mapping, predicts an edit script, a finite sequence of node insertions, deletions, replacements and edge insertions/deletions, that transforms the current graph into the next under a Markovian assumption.\n\n**Key idea.** $G_{t+1} = \\psi_t(G_t);\\quad \\psi_t := \\delta_t^1 \\circ \\delta_t^2 \\circ \\cdots \\circ \\delta_t^n;\\quad \\forall \\delta_t^i \\in \\bar{\\delta}_t$\n\n*Also —* Reference pair mappings are obtained via graph-edit-distance approximators (exact distance is NP-hard), then converted into per-node teaching signals; the reproduction trains GEN variants with adapted hinge and cross-entropy losses, plus flexible and fixed edge filtering for large graphs, against a modified Variational Graph Autoencoder baseline.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nFive synthetic data-generating processes are used: Edit Cycles, Degree Rules and Game of Life (dynamical graph systems), plus Boolean Formulae and Peano Addition (tree dynamical systems); scaling is tested on the arXiv HEP-Th citation network of 27,700 papers.\n\n*Also —* From the citation network, 1554 rolling-window sub-graphs with node counts NG ∈ [100, 2786] are extracted; the DGPs admit widely differing numbers of unique graphs (9 for Edit Cycles up to 34,353 for Peano Addition).\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nThe reproduction confirms claims (i)–(iii): GEN out-performs the modified VGAE on all three dynamical graph DGPs, reaches near-perfect accuracy on the tree DGPs, and forward passes scale sub-quadratically. It refutes claim (iv): backward passes do not scale linearly.\n\n*Also —* Most reproduced metrics fall within a standard deviation of the originally reported values; the largest deviation was an increase in VGAE deletion precision and insertion recall on the Edit Cycles task, which did not overturn GEN's win.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nSwapping random-graph initialisation for the Erdős–Rényi and Configuration models leaves all metrics within 0.05 of the reported values, and the stricter risk-estimation protocol with a 100-graph held-out test set shifts scores only by δ ∈ [−0.1, 0.05].\n\n*Also —* A diagnostic sampling study shows the tree DGPs rarely produce simplifiable trees, only 13% for Boolean Formulae and 26% for Peano Addition, casting doubt on how much the benchmarks actually test the model.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Forward-pass log-log scaling slopes: 1.38 ± 0.02 (flexible filtering), 1.31 ± 0.02 (constant filtering) — sub-quadratic.\n\n*Also —* - Boolean Formulae accuracy 0.98 ± 0.02; Peano Addition perfect (1.0).\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nGraph Edit Networks reproduce well and are genuinely elegant and interpretable, but their backward passes scale super-linearly rather than linearly, and several benchmarks reward memorisation more than true expressive power.\n\n*Also —* The reproduction's careful documentation of the data generators is arguably as valuable as its verdict on the claims.","poster_thumb":"posters/084_re_graph_edit_networks.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/084_re_graph_edit_networks/084_re_graph_edit_networks/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/084_re_graph_edit_networks/084_re_graph_edit_networks/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/084_re_graph_edit_networks/084_re_graph_edit_networks/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/084_re_graph_edit_networks/084_re_graph_edit_networks/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/084_re_graph_edit_networks/084_re_graph_edit_networks/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/084_re_graph_edit_networks/084_re_graph_edit_networks/reel.html"}},{"id":"085","slug":"085_revisiting_robustness_in_graph_machine_learning","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Revisiting Robustness in Graph Machine Learning","authors":"Lukas Gosch¹, Daniel Sturm¹, Simon Geisler¹, Stephan Günnemann¹","institutes":"¹Department of Computer Science & Munich Data Science Institute, Technical University of Munich","venue":"ICLR 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.00851","code_url":"","hook":"The paper introduces a semantics-aware notion of adversarial robustness for node classification and defines over-robustness, unwanted robustness against perturbations that have already changed the ground-truth label.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nIt is unclear whether the small, low-budget structure perturbations used to attack graph neural networks actually preserve a node's semantic content, the core assumption behind any adversarial example.\n\n*Also —* Graphs cannot be manually inspected like images, so the de facto ℓ₀-pseudonorm budgets on inserted/deleted edges may silently change a node's true label rather than leave it intact.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nBecause most real-world graph nodes have low degree, small ℓ₀ budgets can already remove a node from its entire original neighbourhood, so unnoticeability heuristics like degree distribution do not guarantee unchanged semantics.\n\n*Also —* Prior defenses assume every prediction change under a small budget is an adversarial failure; if the semantics actually changed, that framing mismeasures robustness.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces a semantics-aware notion of adversarial robustness for node classification and defines over-robustness, unwanted robustness against perturbations that have already changed the ground-truth label.\n\n*Also —* Using Contextual Stochastic Block Models it quantifies how often common threat models violate semantics, shows all tested GNNs are over-robust, demonstrates that label propagation reduces over-robustness, and proves there is no robustness-accuracy tradeoff for inductively classifying a newly added node.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nThe authors use a Bayes optimal classifier g on Contextual Stochastic Block Models as a reference for the true semantics, then define an over-robust example at node v as one where f and g agree on the clean graph, the perturbation changes g's prediction (semantics), yet f keeps its original prediction.\n\n**Key idea.** $R_{over} = 1 - R(f,g)/R(f)$ where $R(f,g)$ is semantics-aware robustness against a reference classifier $g$ and $R(f) := R(f,y)$ is conventional degree-corrected robustness; the Bayes optimal reference is $f^*(X',A',y)_v = \\arg\\max_{\\hat y \\in \\{0,1\\}} P[y'_v = \\hat y \\mid X', A', y]$.\n\n*Also —* Over-robustness is quantified as the fraction of conventional adversarial robustness that cannot be explained by semantics-preserving robustness; label propagation (label spreading) is combined with GNN soft-predictions to inject the training graph's label structure into inference.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nExperiments use Contextual Stochastic Block Models (CSBMs) with n = 1000 nodes parametrized to match CORA (p = 0.63%, q = 0.15%, d = 21), plus the real-world Cora-ML graph and a Contextual Barabási-Albert model with community structure (CBA).\n\n*Also —* The signal strength K is varied from K = 0.1 (almost no discriminative features) to K = 5 (structure unnecessary); for each K, 10 training graphs are sampled and 1000 nodes are inductively added at test time, with an 80/20 train/validation node split.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nFor a majority of nodes, common threat models include many perturbed graphs that violate unchanged semantics, and every assessed GNN (GCN, SGC, APPNP, GAT, GATv2, GraphSAGE) shows over-robustness, robustness beyond the point of semantic change.\n\n*Also —* For K = 1.0 with the B_deg+2 threat model, 99.4% of target nodes admit a perturbed graph with changed semantic content; an MLP shows 43% over-robustness at K = 0.1 under ℓ₂-weak, and all GNNs sit close to this upper bound.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nAdding label propagation on top of a GNN sharply reduces over-robustness: GCN+LP at K = 0.5 drops to R_over = 20.9%, and label propagation alone achieves the lowest over-robustness of all methods.\n\n*Also —* Adding LP does not decrease test accuracy and often increases adversarial robustness while structure matters; under stronger attacks over-robustness persists (Nettack leaves a GCN at K = 0.5 with 11.4% over-robustness, and an MLP under Nettack at K = 2 still shows 19.2%).\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- 99.4% of target nodes (K = 1.0, B_deg+2 threat model) admit a perturbed graph with changed semantic content.\n\n*Also —* - GCN+LP over-robustness falls to 20.9% at K = 0.5.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nMuch of what looks like graph neural network robustness is actually over-robustness, staying fixed after a node's semantics have already changed, and injecting the training graph's label structure via label propagation reduces it while helping accuracy and true robustness.\n\n*Also —* With a semantics-aware notion of robustness, inductively classifying a newly added node has no robustness-accuracy tradeoff, reframing how graph adversarial robustness should be measured.","poster_thumb":"posters/085_revisiting_robustness_in_graph_machine_learning.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/085_revisiting_robustness_in_graph_machine_learning/085_revisiting_robustness_in_graph_machine_learning/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/085_revisiting_robustness_in_graph_machine_learning/085_revisiting_robustness_in_graph_machine_learning/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/085_revisiting_robustness_in_graph_machine_learning/085_revisiting_robustness_in_graph_machine_learning/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/085_revisiting_robustness_in_graph_machine_learning/085_revisiting_robustness_in_graph_machine_learning/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/085_revisiting_robustness_in_graph_machine_learning/085_revisiting_robustness_in_graph_machine_learning/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/085_revisiting_robustness_in_graph_machine_learning/085_revisiting_robustness_in_graph_machine_learning/reel.html"}},{"id":"086","slug":"086_pixiu_a_comprehensive_benchmark_instruction_data","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"PIXIU: A Large Language Model, Instruction Data and Evaluation Benchmark for Finance","authors":"Qianqian Xie¹, Weiguang Han¹, Xiao Zhang², Yanzhao Lai³, Min Peng¹, Alejandro Lopez-Lira⁴, Jimin Huang⁵","institutes":"¹Wuhan University; ²Sun Yat-Sen University; ³Southwest Jiaotong University; ⁴University of Florida; ⁵ChanceFocus AMC","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05443","code_url":"https://github.com/chancefocus/PIXIU","hook":"PIXIU introduces FIT, the first multi-task multi-modal financial instruction dataset (136K samples, 5 tasks, 9 datasets); FinMA, the first openly released instruction-following financial LLM; and FLARE, the first benchmark spanning financia…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nThere are no publicly available financial large language models, financial instruction tuning datasets, or holistic evaluation benchmarks, which blocks open-source progress in financial AI.\n\n*Also —* The one existing financial LLM, BloombergGPT (50B), is proprietary, is not instruction-tuned, and releases neither model nor data.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nInstruction tuning is critical for zero-shot ability on downstream financial tasks, and real-world finance needs prediction tasks such as stock movement that existing NLP-only benchmarks ignore.\n\n*Also —* Prior benchmarks (FLUE, BBT-CFLEB) cover only financial NLP tasks and omit prediction tasks aligned with real trading scenarios.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nPIXIU introduces FIT, the first multi-task multi-modal financial instruction dataset (136K samples, 5 tasks, 9 datasets); FinMA, the first openly released instruction-following financial LLM; and FLARE, the first benchmark spanning financial NLP and prediction tasks.\n\n*Also —* The model, instruction data, benchmark datasets, and implementation are all open-sourced to encourage transparency and future research.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nPIXIU collects open financial data across five tasks, wraps each sample with domain-expert-written task-specific instructions to build FIT, then fine-tunes LLaMA (7B and 30B) with multi-task instruction tuning to produce FinMA.\n\n*Also —* Data spans multiple modalities (text plus tabular reports and historical stock-price time series) and text types (reports, news, tweets, filings); variants include FinMA-7B, FinMA-30B (NLP tasks), and FinMA-7B-full (NLP + prediction).\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nFIT holds 136,609 instruction samples across 5 tasks and 9 datasets; FLARE evaluates on those 9 datasets covering sentiment (FPB, FiQA-SA), headline classification, NER, question answering (FinQA, ConvFinQA), and stock movement prediction (BigData22, ACL18, CIKM18).\n\n*Also —* Metrics are task-specific: weighted F1 and accuracy for sentiment, average F1 for headlines, entity F1 for NER, exact-match accuracy for QA, and accuracy plus Matthews correlation coefficient for stock prediction.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nFinMA significantly outperforms BloombergGPT, ChatGPT, and GPT-4 on most FLARE NLP tasks; on the FPB sentiment dataset FinMA-30B beats GPT-4 by 10% F1 and BloombergGPT by 37% F1, and achieves SOTA on 3 NLP tasks plus 1 prediction dataset.\n\n*Also —* FinMA underperforms on question answering (FinQA/ConvFinQA) due to LLaMA's weak quantitative reasoning, and all LLMs remain limited on stock movement prediction.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nScaling from FinMA-7B to FinMA-30B gives no consistent gain on most NLP or prediction tasks, showing data quality and diversity matter more than parameter count; FinMA-7B-full (trained on NLP + prediction) achieves the best result on the ACL18 stock dataset.\n\n*Also —* On complex quantitative QA (ConvFinQA), larger LLaMA-based FinMA does help, tracking LLaMA's better math performance at scale, but still trails GPT-4.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- 136,609 (136K) instruction samples in FIT across 5 tasks and 9 datasets\n\n*Also —* FPB weighted F1 reaches 0.88 (FinMA-30B) versus 0.78 (GPT-4); Headline Avg F1 up to 0.98 for FinMA.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nPIXIU delivers the first open financial LLM (FinMA), instruction dataset (FIT), and benchmark (FLARE) together, showing domain instruction tuning beats far larger general models on financial NLP while exposing quantitative-reasoning and prediction as open challenges.\n\n*Also —* Everything is open-sourced, providing a reproducible foundation for future financial AI research.","poster_thumb":"posters/086_pixiu_a_comprehensive_benchmark_instruction_data.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/086_pixiu_a_comprehensive_benchmark_instruction_data/086_pixiu_a_comprehensive_benchmark_instruction_data/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/086_pixiu_a_comprehensive_benchmark_instruction_data/086_pixiu_a_comprehensive_benchmark_instruction_data/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/086_pixiu_a_comprehensive_benchmark_instruction_data/086_pixiu_a_comprehensive_benchmark_instruction_data/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/086_pixiu_a_comprehensive_benchmark_instruction_data/086_pixiu_a_comprehensive_benchmark_instruction_data/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/086_pixiu_a_comprehensive_benchmark_instruction_data/086_pixiu_a_comprehensive_benchmark_instruction_data/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/086_pixiu_a_comprehensive_benchmark_instruction_data/086_pixiu_a_comprehensive_benchmark_instruction_data/reel.html"}},{"id":"087","slug":"087_subtractive_mixture_models_via_squaring_represen","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Subtractive Mixture Models via Squaring: Representation and Learning","authors":"Lorenzo Loconte¹, Aleksanteri M. Sladek², Stefan Mengel³, Martin Trapp², Arno Solin², Nicolas Gillis⁴, Antonio Vergari¹","institutes":"¹School of Informatics, University of Edinburgh; ²Department of Computer Science, Aalto University; ³University of Artois, CNRS, CRIL; ⁴Department of Mathematics and Operational Research, Universite de Mons","venue":"ICLR 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.00724","code_url":"https://github.com/april-tools/squared-npcs","hook":"The paper introduces a general framework to represent non-monotonic mixtures via squaring inside tensorized probabilistic circuits (NPC²s), shows they subsume square-root-of-density, PSD, and Born-machine models, and proves an exponential e…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nClassical mixture models can only add non-negative components, so approximating distributions with \"holes\" or sharp structure needs a large, wasteful number of components.\n\n*Also —* Allowing components to subtract mass can drastically cut the count, but learning such subtractive mixtures while guaranteeing a valid non-negative density is hard.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nA subtractive mixture that can cancel mass models complex distributions with far fewer components, but naively negative mixture weights can make the function negative and no longer a valid distribution.\n\n*Also —* Prior fixes (energy-based exponentiation) lose tractable normalization; several disconnected communities reinvented squaring-style tricks without a unified, tractable treatment.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper introduces a general framework to represent non-monotonic mixtures via squaring inside tensorized probabilistic circuits (NPC²s), shows they subsume square-root-of-density, PSD, and Born-machine models, and proves an exponential expressiveness advantage over monotonic circuits.\n\n*Also —* It derives a simple tractable squaring algorithm over layers and provides broad empirical evidence on continuous, discrete, UCI, and language-model-distillation tasks.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nA shallow subtractive mixture uses unconstrained real weights and is squared to guarantee non-negativity; squaring a K-component mixture yields a product-of-experts form that can be renormalized tractably. This is lifted to deep tensorized structured-decomposable circuits by recursively squaring each layer, so deep squared non-monotonic PCs (NPC²s) remain tractable for marginalization and inference.\n\n**Key idea.** $c^2(X) = \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{K} w_i c_i(X)\\right)^2 = \\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\sum_{j=1}^{K} w_i w_j\\, c_i(X) c_j(X),\\quad w_i \\in \\mathbb{R}$ ; $Z = \\int c^2(x)\\,dx = \\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\sum_{j=1}^{K} w_i w_j \\int c_i(x) c_j(x)\\,dx$ ; $\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal{D}} \\log\\!\\big(c^2(x)/Z\\big) = -|\\mathcal{D}|\\log Z + 2\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal{D}} \\log|c(x)|$\n\n*Also —* Squared layers hold a quadratic number of units but still output vectors, so the partition function Z is computed once per batch, keeping the model efficient to train with gradient-based MLE.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluation spans 2D synthetic continuous and discrete densities, five UCI multivariate datasets (Power, Gas, Hepmass, MiniBooNE, BSDS300), and distillation of sentences sampled from the GPT2 language model.\n\n*Also —* Baselines include full-covariance Gaussian, normalizing flows (RealNVP, MADE, MAF, NSF), monotonic PCs (EiNet-LRS), and tensor-train density estimators (TTDE).\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nWith the same model size, NPC²s achieve higher log-likelihoods than monotonic PCs across the density-estimation tasks, and negative parameters (not just squaring) are what capture complex \"holes\" in the target densities.\n\n*Also —* On GPT2 distillation, NPC²s scale better than monotonic PCs and reach log-likelihoods closer to GPT2's own, better approximating the intractable model.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nComparing NPC²s against squared monotonic PCs (MPC²) isolates the effect of negative parameters from squaring alone; negative parameters give the extra expressiveness, while binary-tree region graphs generally beat linear-tree ones.\n\n*Also —* On continuous data, splines as input layers help NPC²s most; on discrete data, embeddings/Binomials matter more than categorical inputs, and on some image-mass tasks the advantage narrows.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nSquaring a K-component mixture encodes (K+1 choose 2) components within the same K-parameter budget; the class of squared non-monotonic circuits is proven exponentially more compact than any structured-decomposable monotonic PC.\n\n*Also —* NPC²s attain the best average test log-likelihoods among tractable models on several UCI datasets (e.g. Power 0.62, Gas 10.98, Hepmass -20.41, MiniBooNE -26.68); on GPT2-sampled data they approach GPT2's reference log-likelihood of about -52 where monotonic PCs plateau.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nSquaring turns subtractive mixtures into valid, tractable probabilistic circuits that need exponentially fewer components than additive mixtures for the same expressiveness.\n\n*Also —* This unifies several negative-parameter models under one property-driven framework and makes subtractive mixtures a practical tool for tractable probabilistic modeling.","poster_thumb":"posters/087_subtractive_mixture_models_via_squaring_represen.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/087_subtractive_mixture_models_via_squaring_represen/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/087_subtractive_mixture_models_via_squaring_represen/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/087_subtractive_mixture_models_via_squaring_represen/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/087_subtractive_mixture_models_via_squaring_represen/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/087_subtractive_mixture_models_via_squaring_represen/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/087_subtractive_mixture_models_via_squaring_represen/reel.html"}},{"id":"088","slug":"088_geomformer_a_general_architecture_for_geometric","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"GeoMFormer: A General Architecture for Geometric Molecular Representation Learning","authors":"Tianlang Chen¹, Shengjie Luo², Di He², Shuxin Zheng³, Tie-Yan Liu³, Liwei Wang²⁴","institutes":"¹School of EECS, Peking University; ²National Key Laboratory of General Artificial Intelligence, School of Intelligence Science and Technology, Peking University; ³Microsoft Research AI4Science; ⁴Center for Machine Learning Research, Peking University","venue":"ICML 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.16853","code_url":"https://github.com/c-tl/GeoMFormer","hook":"GeoMFormer, a Transformer-based architecture with two streams (invariant and equivariant) bridged by cross-attention, that learns both representation types; the paper shows many prior architectures are special instances of it.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nMolecular modeling requires networks that respect physical laws, invariance and equivariance to coordinate rotation and translation, yet no general framework learns both invariant and equivariant features well at once.\n\n*Also —* Many applications need strong invariant prediction (e.g. energy) and equivariant prediction (e.g. atomic positions/forces) from the same model simultaneously.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nMost prior geometric networks are designed heuristically with costly, specialized modules that either scale poorly or lack expressive power, so a general and flexible design principle is needed.\n\n*Also —* Equivariant operations are often limited in number because of the equivariance constraint, leading to complex architectures that are hard to scale and reuse.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nGeoMFormer, a Transformer-based architecture with two streams (invariant and equivariant) bridged by cross-attention, that learns both representation types; the paper shows many prior architectures are special instances of it.\n\n*Also —* Built entirely from standard Transformer modules, and validated with new state-of-the-art results across invariant and equivariant molecular benchmarks plus comprehensive ablations.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nTwo parallel Transformer streams process invariant and equivariant tokens; each stream has self-attention, a cross-attention module bridging to the other stream, and an FFN. Equivariant attention uses a modified score over the 3D-vector Query/Key so equivariance is preserved.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\alpha_{ij} = \\sum_{k=1}^{d} {Q^{E}_{[i,:,k]}}^{\\top} K^{E}_{[j,:,k]}, \\quad Q^{E}, K^{E}, V^{E} = \\psi^{E}(Z^{E})$\n\n*Also —* Invariant-cross-Equivariant and Equivariant-cross-Invariant attention specify Query/Key/Value differently per stream; standard Layer Normalization and structural encodings are incorporated. Existing models arise as special cases by restricting the modules.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on OC20 (IS2RE invariant, IS2RS equivariant; 460,328 complexes), PCQM4Mv2 (~3.37M molecules), Molecule3D (~2.34M, random & scaffold splits), N-body simulation (3,000 training trajectories), and MD17 force-field modeling.\n\n*Also —* Tasks span invariant prediction (energy, HOMO-LUMO gap) and equivariant prediction (relaxed structure, particle positions, forces), covering adsorbate-catalyst complexes, simple molecules, and particle systems.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nGeoMFormer sets state-of-the-art or highly competitive results across all benchmarks: it outperforms baselines on OC20 IS2RE and IS2RS, achieves the lowest MAE among quadratic-complexity models on PCQM4Mv2, and beats all baselines on Molecule3D and the N-body simulation.\n\n*Also —* On N-body simulation the MSE drops 33.8% versus the previous best; on Molecule3D it cuts MAE by 16.3% (random) and 11.6% (scaffold); on PCQM4Mv2 it reduces MAE 6.7% relative to the previous best quadratic model, all with O(n²) complexity.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nRemoving the cross-attention modules degrades performance substantially, confirming they are essential to bridging the two streams; each of the four attention modules contributes.\n\n*Also —* On MD17 energy prediction, adding cross-attention yields 18.7% (Inv-Cross-Attn), 9.8% (Equ-Cross-Attn), and 20.8% (both) relative improvement; on MD17 force prediction the gains are 28.0%, 43.9%, and 60.8%; on N-body they are 7.8%, 13.0%, and 17.5%.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- PCQM4Mv2 Valid MAE 0.0734, the best among O(n²) models (6.7% relative reduction).\n\n*Also —* - OC20 IS2RE energy MAE 0.4141 eV average and EwT 8.59%, outperforming Graphormer-3D.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nTwo standard Transformer streams, one invariant and one equivariant, bridged by cross-attention, form a single general architecture that learns both representation types and beats specialized geometric models across the board.\n\n*Also —* Because prior architectures emerge as special cases, GeoMFormer offers a unifying, scalable design principle for geometric molecular representation learning.","poster_thumb":"posters/088_geomformer_a_general_architecture_for_geometric.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/088_geomformer_a_general_architecture_for_geometric/088_geomformer_a_general_architecture_for_geometric/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/088_geomformer_a_general_architecture_for_geometric/088_geomformer_a_general_architecture_for_geometric/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/088_geomformer_a_general_architecture_for_geometric/088_geomformer_a_general_architecture_for_geometric/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/088_geomformer_a_general_architecture_for_geometric/088_geomformer_a_general_architecture_for_geometric/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/088_geomformer_a_general_architecture_for_geometric/088_geomformer_a_general_architecture_for_geometric/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/088_geomformer_a_general_architecture_for_geometric/088_geomformer_a_general_architecture_for_geometric/reel.html"}},{"id":"089","slug":"089_flab_benchmarking_deep_learning_methods_for_anti","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"\"FLAb: Benchmarking deep learning methods for antibody fitness prediction\"","authors":"Michael Chungyoun¹, Jeffrey Ruffolo¹, Jeffrey Gray¹","institutes":"¹Johns Hopkins University","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.01.13.575504v1","code_url":"https://github.com/Graylab/FLAb","hook":"The authors present FLAb, the largest therapeutic antibody design benchmark to date, curating 17 mutational landscapes with 13,384 fitness measurements across six developability properties, and use it to benchmark six pretrained protein mod…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nExisting protein fitness benchmarks (CAFA, TAPE, FLIP) either exclude antibody data or use very little of it, so there is no systematic way to evaluate how well deep learning models capture therapeutic antibody fitness.\n\n*Also —* Antibody design methods are still judged by weak proxies like native sequence recovery, which do not indicate real therapeutic potential.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nTherapeutic antibodies must balance six developability properties with intricate trade-offs, so reliable model-based scoring could filter design candidates far more efficiently than wet-lab experiments.\n\n*Also —* As deep learning approaches for antibodies proliferate, the field needs a shared, antibody-specific benchmark to converge on before trusting any model's predictions.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe authors present FLAb, the largest therapeutic antibody design benchmark to date, curating 17 mutational landscapes with 13,384 fitness measurements across six developability properties, and use it to benchmark six pretrained protein models against physics-based Rosetta.\n\n*Also —* All FLAb data are released openly for community contribution, and the study dissects how wild-type origin, architecture, training-data composition, parameter size, and evolutionary signal each affect performance.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nEach antibody variable-region sequence (or structure) is scored by a model's (pseudo-)perplexity, averaged over all heavy- and light-chain residues; a well-calibrated model should assign low perplexity (high confidence) to high-fitness antibodies. These likelihoods are then correlated against experimentally measured fitness using Pearson (r), Spearman (ρ), and Kendall tau (τ) coefficients.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\mathrm{PPL}(s)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\log p(x_i \\mid x_{<i})\\right)$\n\n*Also —* Six pretrained models are tested with no fine-tuning: decoder-only (ProGen2 suite, IgLM, ProtGPT2), encoder-only (AntiBERTy), and inverse-folding (ESM-IF, ProteinMPNN), all compared to physics-based Rosetta energy.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nFLAb compiles 17 mutational landscapes of distinct antibody families totaling 13,384 fitness metrics from eight studies, spanning six therapeutic properties: expression, thermostability, immunogenicity, aggregation, polyreactivity, and binding affinity.\n\n*Also —* Each property has its own experimental unit (expression in µg/mL, thermostability as melting temperature, immunogenicity as % anti-drug-antibody response, binding as dissociation constant KD, aggregation as wavelength shift, polyreactivity as retention time).\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nNo model correlates well with all six properties, and performance varies across datasets of the same property. ProGen2-Small achieved the most top performances (best on seven datasets), with ProGen2-Medium, ProGen2-OAS, ESM-IF, and Rosetta Energy tied for second (top on six datasets each).\n\n*Also —* Sequence-based models on average outperform structure-based models across all six landscapes, with thermostability showing the largest gap.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nParameter size affects performance more than architecture or training-data composition: encoder-only AntiBERTy and decoder-only IgLM (both trained on the same 558M OAS sequences) perform similarly, while scaling ProGen2 from 151M to 6.4B parameters noticeably improved only polyreactivity and thermostability prediction.\n\n*Also —* Some models are biased toward evolutionary signal: AntiBERTy, IgLM, and the ProGen2 suite assign higher confidence to wild-type golimumab than to more thermostable mutants, whereas physics-based Rosetta correctly ranks the stabilized mutants.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n13,384 fitness measurements; 17 mutational landscapes; 6 developability properties; 6 deep learning models benchmarked against Rosetta.\n\n*Also —* Intrinsic properties average |PCC| > 0.6 versus extrinsic properties (binding < 0.4, expression < 0.42, immunogenicity < 0.5); intra-family thermostability |PCC| 0.77 versus inter-family 0.12.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nNo current deep learning model reliably predicts antibody fitness across all developability properties, so much more work is needed before these models can dependably filter therapeutic antibody candidates.\n\n*Also —* Intrinsic biophysical properties are captured far better than extrinsic ones, and promising paths forward include adding structure, antigen context, and physics-based priors.","poster_thumb":"posters/089_flab_benchmarking_deep_learning_methods_for_anti.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/089_flab_benchmarking_deep_learning_methods_for_anti/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/089_flab_benchmarking_deep_learning_methods_for_anti/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/089_flab_benchmarking_deep_learning_methods_for_anti/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/089_flab_benchmarking_deep_learning_methods_for_anti/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/089_flab_benchmarking_deep_learning_methods_for_anti/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/089_flab_benchmarking_deep_learning_methods_for_anti/reel.html"}},{"id":"090","slug":"090_generation_probabilities_are_not_enough_improvin","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Generation Probabilities are Not Enough: Improving Error Highlighting for AI Code Suggestions","authors":"Helena Vasconcelos¹, Gagan Bansal², Adam Fourney², Q. Vera Liao², Jennifer Wortman Vaughan²","institutes":"¹Stanford University; ²Microsoft Research","venue":"NeurIPS 2022","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.07248","code_url":"","hook":"A preregistered, mixed-methods study with 30 participants comparing three code-completion conditions (no highlights, generation-probability highlights, and edit-model highlights), plus a new \"edit model\" that predicts which tokens a user is…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nAI code-completion models produce erroneous suggestions that can inject bugs, and users, prone to automation bias, must first detect those errors, yet it is unclear how to surface uncertainty in generative output where each suggestion holds hundreds of token-level decisions.\n\n*Also —* Prior uncertainty and explanation work targets single-decision support (one classification or diagnosis) and does not translate cleanly to token-by-token generative scenarios.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nToken-level highlighting, like in-line spell-check, could draw operator attention to code regions that most need human oversight, but it is unknown which signal to highlight or whether highlighting helps at all.\n\n*Also —* The existing strategy, highlighting the model's lowest-probability tokens, has been proposed in past work and ships in OpenAI's Playground, yet its assumption that low-probability equals likely-error was untested against human editing behavior.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nA preregistered, mixed-methods study with 30 participants comparing three code-completion conditions (no highlights, generation-probability highlights, and edit-model highlights), plus a new \"edit model\" that predicts which tokens a user is likely to edit.\n\n*Also —* The work shows edit-likelihood is a stronger highlighting signal than raw generation probability and argues the approach can generalize by learning from large-scale telemetry such as Copilot edit data.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nAll three tools use the same OpenAI Davinci-002 Codex model and differ only in how completions are presented. The Generation-probability tool highlights the most uncertain tokens (probability threshold 71%) as output by Codex; the Edit-model tool highlights tokens most likely to be edited (threshold 66%) according to a learned edit model; the Prediction-only tool shows the raw completion with no highlights.\n\n*Also —* The edit model was trained on data from nine coders in a preliminary phase who edited Codex output until each task was solved correctly. Highlighting thresholds were tuned so the total number of highlights shown across all three tasks was equal in every condition.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\n30 participants, all employees of a large US technology company with Python experience, each completed three coding tasks drawn from LeetCode's \"easy\" setting under a 10-minute-per-task limit, in ~1-hour paid interview sessions.\n\n*Also —* Task order and tool-to-task assignment were randomized; participants could run their code and provided unit tests for debugging. The edit model's training data came from a separate preliminary collection with nine coders.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nParticipants finished fastest with edit-model highlighting (µ = 8.59 min) and slowest with generation-probability highlighting (µ = 9.61 min), with prediction-only in between (µ = 9.27 min); the gap between the two highlighting strategies is highly significant (p = 0.003).\n\n*Also —* The trend between the edit condition and prediction-only was promising but weaker (p = 0.06), indicating that the choice of highlighting strategy, not merely the presence of highlights, drives the benefit.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nA token-survival analysis shows the edit model steers people to more precise edits: non-highlighted tokens survive more often under the edit model (0.87) than under generation probability (0.81) or prediction-only (0.79), while highlighted tokens survive far less under the edit model (0.35) than under generation probability (0.74), all p < .0001.\n\n*Also —* This confirms the edit model is a much stronger signal of what people will actually change, whereas generation-probability highlights are only weakly aligned with real edits.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nTask time: 8.59 min (edit model) vs 9.61 min (generation probability), p = 0.003. Highlighted-token survival: 0.35 (edit) vs 0.74 (generation probability), p < .0001. Preference rating: 3.94 (edit) vs 2.88 (generation probability), p = 0.001.\n\n*Also —* N = 30 participants; 3 LeetCode \"easy\" tasks; highlighting thresholds 66% (edit) and 71% (generation probability); edit model trained from 9 preliminary coders.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nHighlighting the tokens a person is likely to edit, rather than the tokens the model found improbable, makes programmers faster, more surgical in their edits, and is strongly preferred, generation probabilities alone are not enough.\n\n*Also —* Because products like Copilot already track edits to AI generations as a performance metric, that telemetry could train an open-world edit model to bring these benefits to real-world code assistance.","poster_thumb":"posters/090_generation_probabilities_are_not_enough_improvin.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/090_generation_probabilities_are_not_enough_improvin/090_generation_probabilities_are_not_enough_improvin/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/090_generation_probabilities_are_not_enough_improvin/090_generation_probabilities_are_not_enough_improvin/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/090_generation_probabilities_are_not_enough_improvin/090_generation_probabilities_are_not_enough_improvin/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/090_generation_probabilities_are_not_enough_improvin/090_generation_probabilities_are_not_enough_improvin/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/090_generation_probabilities_are_not_enough_improvin/090_generation_probabilities_are_not_enough_improvin/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/090_generation_probabilities_are_not_enough_improvin/090_generation_probabilities_are_not_enough_improvin/reel.html"}},{"id":"091","slug":"091_a_rate_distortion_view_of_uncertainty_quantifica","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"A Rate-Distortion View of Uncertainty Quantification","authors":"Ifigeneia Apostolopoulou¹, Benjamin Eysenbach², Frank Nielsen³, Artur Dubrawski¹","institutes":"¹Machine Learning Department, Auton Lab, Carnegie Mellon University; ²Computer Science Department, Princeton University; ³Sony Computer Science Laboratories Inc., Tokyo, Japan","venue":"ICML 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.10775","code_url":"https://github.com/ifiaposto/Distance_Aware_Bottleneck","hook":"The paper formulates uncertainty quantification as computing a rate-distortion function that compresses the training set into a codebook of prototype distributions, yielding the Distance Aware Bottleneck (DAB), a single-model, deterministic…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nDeep neural networks usually cannot tell how far a new input lies from their training data, so they give overconfident predictions on out-of-distribution or hard examples.\n\n*Also —* Probabilistic models like Gaussian Processes are naturally distance-aware, but reliable, efficient uncertainty estimation for real-world deep learning is still missing.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nExisting single-pass Deterministic Uncertainty Methods rely on architectural constraints (e.g. spectral normalization) to avoid feature collapse, which can hurt calibration and complicate integration into large pre-trained models.\n\n*Also —* Ensembles and Bayesian methods are distance-aware but need many forward passes, making them expensive to deploy at scale.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper formulates uncertainty quantification as computing a rate-distortion function that compresses the training set into a codebook of prototype distributions, yielding the Distance Aware Bottleneck (DAB), a single-model, deterministic, single-forward-pass uncertainty estimator.\n\n*Also —* It contributes a practical alternating-minimization learning algorithm, a \"meta-probabilistic\" distortion defined over distributions of embeddings, and a post-hoc variant that adds distance awareness to large pre-trained feature extractors.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nDAB builds on the Information Bottleneck but replaces its rate term with an achievable rate from a Rate Distortion Finite Cardinality problem: it learns a codebook of k centroid distributions (codes) that quantize the encoders of all training points. A new example's uncertainty is its expected statistical distance (KL divergence) from the codebook, given by one forward pass.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{DAB}} \\triangleq -I(Z,Y;\\theta) + \\beta I(P_X, Q;\\theta,\\phi) + \\alpha\\beta\\, \\mathbb{E}_{P_X,Q}\\big[D\\big(p(z\\mid x;\\theta), q_\\kappa(z;\\phi)\\big)\\big]$ ; $\\mathrm{uncertainty}(x_{\\text{test}}) = \\mathbb{E}\\big[D\\big(p(z\\mid x_{\\text{test}};\\theta), q_\\kappa(z;\\phi)\\big)\\big]$\n\n*Also —* Training alternates between gradient updates of the encoder/decoder and analytic updates of the soft assignments and centroids, mirroring a Blahut-Arimoto style scheme. DAB is analogous to a Gaussian Process, with the codebook playing the role of inducing points and statistical distance replacing Euclidean distance.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on CIFAR-10 (in-distribution) with SVHN as far-OOD and CIFAR-100 as near-OOD, on CIFAR-10 misclassification prediction, and on large-scale ImageNet-1K with ImageNet-O as the OOD set.\n\n*Also —* DAB uses a narrow 8-dimensional latent bottleneck with only 10 distributional codes; baselines include Deep Ensembles, DDU, DUQ, DUE, SNGP, and vanilla VIB.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn CIFAR-10, DAB outperforms all baselines on both OOD tasks, reaching AUROC 0.986 / AUPRC 0.994 on SVHN and AUROC 0.922 / AUPRC 0.915 on CIFAR-100, beating even a 5-model Deep Ensemble.\n\n*Also —* It achieves this with a single forward pass and ~36.5M parameters versus the ensemble's ~182M, at comparable 95.9% accuracy.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nFor misclassification prediction on CIFAR-10, DAB reaches Calibration AUROC 0.930, closing the gap to Deep Ensembles (0.951) and far surpassing other single-pass DUMs such as DDU (0.632), DUE (0.856), DUQ (0.889), and SNGP (0.897).\n\n*Also —* A codebook visualization shows each of the 10 centroids progressively attracting test points of a single class over training epochs, confirming the codes meaningfully represent the data.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nCIFAR-10 → SVHN OOD: AUROC 0.986 (best). CIFAR-10 → CIFAR-100 OOD: AUROC 0.922 (best). CIFAR-10 misclassification: Calibration AUROC 0.930.\n\n*Also —* ImageNet-1K: DAB (fine-tuned ResNet-50) beats a 5-model ensemble on misclassification (Calibration AUROC 0.868 vs 0.861) and OOD detection (ImageNet-O AUROC 0.743 vs 0.642) with far fewer trainable parameters (~36.6M vs ~117.7M).\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nCasting uncertainty as compressing training data into a learned codebook lets a single deterministic network measure how far new inputs are from what it has seen, matching or beating expensive ensembles.\n\n*Also —* DAB provides a unified, GP-like notion of uncertainty for both classification and regression that can even be attached post-hoc to large pre-trained models.","poster_thumb":"posters/091_a_rate_distortion_view_of_uncertainty_quantifica.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/091_a_rate_distortion_view_of_uncertainty_quantifica/091_a_rate_distortion_view_of_uncertainty_quantifica/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/091_a_rate_distortion_view_of_uncertainty_quantifica/091_a_rate_distortion_view_of_uncertainty_quantifica/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/091_a_rate_distortion_view_of_uncertainty_quantifica/091_a_rate_distortion_view_of_uncertainty_quantifica/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/091_a_rate_distortion_view_of_uncertainty_quantifica/091_a_rate_distortion_view_of_uncertainty_quantifica/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/091_a_rate_distortion_view_of_uncertainty_quantifica/091_a_rate_distortion_view_of_uncertainty_quantifica/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/091_a_rate_distortion_view_of_uncertainty_quantifica/091_a_rate_distortion_view_of_uncertainty_quantifica/reel.html"}},{"id":"092","slug":"092_cabinet_content_relevance_based_noise_reduction","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"CABINET: Content Relevance-Based Noise Reduction for Table Question Answering","authors":"Sohan Patnaik¹², Heril Changwal¹³, Milan Aggarwal¹, Sumit Bhatia¹, Yaman Kumar Singla¹, Balaji Krishnamurthy¹","institutes":"¹MDSR Lab, Adobe; ²IIT Kharagpur; ³IIT Roorkee","venue":"ICLR 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.01155","code_url":"https://github.com/Sohanpatnaik106/CABINET_QA","hook":"CABINET, a framework that suppresses irrelevant table content via a differentiable Unsupervised Relevance Scorer plus a weakly-supervised parsing-statement module that highlights question-relevant cells, trained end-to-end with the QA LLM.","about_md":"## Problem\n\nIn table question answering, only a few cells matter for a given question; the rest act as noise, and LLMs are vulnerable to this irrelevant content, producing sub-optimal answers.\n\n*Also —* Degradation worsens on large tables where even more irrelevant data is present, amplifying the distraction for the QA model.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nPrior in-context methods like DATER hard-decompose a table into a sub-table, which can discard useful cells and lock in errors when the wrong sub-table is selected.\n\n*Also —* Explicitly removing table content is brittle; a soft relevance-weighting scheme keeps all information available while still de-emphasizing noise.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nCABINET, a framework that suppresses irrelevant table content via a differentiable Unsupervised Relevance Scorer plus a weakly-supervised parsing-statement module that highlights question-relevant cells, trained end-to-end with the QA LLM.\n\n*Also —* It establishes new state of the art on WikiTQ, FeTaQA, and WikiSQL, and demonstrates markedly greater robustness to table perturbations and to increasing table size.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nThe table is linearized and embedded with the question; an Unsupervised Relevance Scorer (a transformer encoder trained via variational inference with clustering, separation, and sparsification losses) assigns each table token a relevance score that multiplies its embedding before the QA LLM decodes the answer.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\eta_p^{uns} = \\mathrm{sigmoid}(z_p),\\; z_p = \\mu_p + s\\cdot\\sigma_p$; $\\mathcal{L} = \\mathcal{L}_{CE} + \\lambda_{clu}\\mathcal{L}_{clu} + \\lambda_{sep}\\mathcal{L}_{sep} + \\lambda_{sparse}\\mathcal{L}_{sparse}$; $\\eta_p = \\lambda_{uns}\\,\\eta_p^{uns} + \\lambda_{cell}\\,\\eta_p^{cell}$\n\n*Also —* In parallel, a Parsing Statement Generator (Flan T5-xl, bootstrapped from ~300 annotations) produces criteria for relevant rows/columns, and a cell highlighter yields a cell-based score; the unsupervised and cell-based scores are linearly fused into the final relevance weight. The QA backbone is OmniTab (560M).\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on three table QA benchmarks: WikiTableQuestions (WikiTQ) and WikiSQL, scored by exact-match accuracy, and FeTaQA, scored by Sacre-BLEU for long free-form answers.\n\n*Also —* WikiTQ is among the most complex table QA datasets requiring compositional reasoning; the authors also release a dataset of ~300 manually written parsing statements to bootstrap the weakly-supervised module.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nCABINET sets new state of the art on all three datasets: 69.1% accuracy on WikiTQ, 40.5 Sacre-BLEU on FeTaQA, and 89.5% accuracy on WikiSQL, all with a 560M-parameter model.\n\n*Also —* On WikiTQ it beats OmniTab, DATER, and fine-tuned Flan T5-xl by 6.4%, 3.2%, and 4.7% absolute respectively, and outperforms GPT-3 / Codex-based in-context learning methods.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nRemoving all three URS structuring losses drops WikiTQ accuracy from 65.6% to 60.8%; the three losses (clustering, separation, sparsification) must act together to help. Fusing unsupervised and cell-based relevance (λuns=0.7, λcell=0.3) beats either alone.\n\n*Also —* Using cell-based relevance alone (λuns=0, λcell=1) collapses performance (WikiTQ 37.6%), showing the unsupervised scorer is the primary signal and the parsing-statement module is a complementary aid.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- WikiTQ: 69.1% accuracy (new SoTA, +6.4% over OmniTab)\n\n*Also —* More robust under table perturbations (row/column permutation, cell replacement) and maintains its lead as table size grows; ~11.5% smaller performance drop than OmniTab under perturbation on WikiTQ.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nSoftly weighting table content by learned relevance, rather than hard-decomposing tables, lets an LLM focus on the cells that matter and sets new state of the art on table QA with a compact model.\n\n*Also —* Combining an unsupervised, differentiable relevance scorer with a weakly-supervised parsing-statement cell highlighter is both more accurate and more robust to noise than prior removal-based approaches.","poster_thumb":"posters/092_cabinet_content_relevance_based_noise_reduction.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/092_cabinet_content_relevance_based_noise_reduction/092_cabinet_content_relevance_based_noise_reduction/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/092_cabinet_content_relevance_based_noise_reduction/092_cabinet_content_relevance_based_noise_reduction/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/092_cabinet_content_relevance_based_noise_reduction/092_cabinet_content_relevance_based_noise_reduction/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/092_cabinet_content_relevance_based_noise_reduction/092_cabinet_content_relevance_based_noise_reduction/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/092_cabinet_content_relevance_based_noise_reduction/092_cabinet_content_relevance_based_noise_reduction/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/092_cabinet_content_relevance_based_noise_reduction/092_cabinet_content_relevance_based_noise_reduction/reel.html"}},{"id":"093","slug":"093_sparse_parameterization_for_epitomic_dataset_dis","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Sparse Parameterization for Epitomic Dataset Distillation","authors":"Xing Wei¹, Anjia Cao¹, Funing Yang¹, Zhiheng Ma²","institutes":"¹School of Software Engineering, Xi'an Jiaotong University; ²Shenzhen Institute of Advanced Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/hash/9e8889198d16fb79926e71adbe38cae4-Abstract-Conference.html","code_url":"https://github.com/MIV-XJTU/SPEED","hook":"SPEED is a universal sparse parameterization framework for epitomic dataset distillation that introduces Spatial-Agnostic Epitomic Tokens (SAETs), Sparse Coding Matrices (SCMs), and a Feature-Recurrent Network (FReeNet) to remove spatial re…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nDataset distillation compresses a large dataset into a small synthetic one, but prior methods obsess over the matching objective and use naive image-independent parameterization, ignoring spatial redundancy within and between synthetic images.\n\n*Also —* This redundancy wastes the tiny storage budget, capping how much informative synthetic data can be packed in, and hurts most on high-resolution real-world images.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nNatural images contain heavy spatial redundancy that a shared, sparse representation can exploit; a few prior parameterization methods use cross-image relationships but none address redundancy irrespective of spatial location.\n\n*Also —* Concentrating the storage budget on a compact shared dictionary plus sparse per-image codes lets SPEED synthesize far more informative images than the naive one-image-per-parameter scheme.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nSPEED is a universal sparse parameterization framework for epitomic dataset distillation that introduces Spatial-Agnostic Epitomic Tokens (SAETs), Sparse Coding Matrices (SCMs), and a Feature-Recurrent Network (FReeNet) to remove spatial redundancy within and between synthetic images.\n\n*Also —* It is compatible with a wide range of matching objectives (gradient, distribution, trajectory), consistently boosting them, and achieves state-of-the-art distillation, especially on high-resolution ImageNet subsets.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nEach synthetic image is factorized as X̃ᵢ = Φ_ϕ(E, Aᵢ): a shared multi-head dictionary of epitomic tokens E and a per-image sparse coding matrix Aᵢ are combined by multi-head sparse coding, then a recurrent transformer-style FReeNet Φ_ϕ (shared SAETs/SCMs across R blocks, MLP + residual + layer-norm) synthesizes hierarchical image patches. Training minimizes a matching objective plus an ℓ1 sparsity penalty on the codes; after training, each SCM is pruned to its top-k elements and stored in sparse COO/uint8 format.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\tilde{X}_i = \\Phi_\\phi(E, A_i)$ ; $\\arg\\min\\ \\mathbb{E}_{\\theta\\sim\\Theta}\\, D\\!\\left(\\phi(\\mathcal{T},\\theta), \\phi(\\mathcal{S},\\theta)\\right) + \\lambda \\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\sum_{h=1}^{H} \\lVert A_i^{h} \\rVert_1$ ; $\\bar{A} = B \\odot A,\\quad B[i,j] = \\mathbb{1}\\big[A[i,j] \\in \\mathrm{topk}(\\lvert A\\rvert)\\big]$\n\n*Also —* Sharing SAETs and FReeNet across all images while keeping only sparse per-image codes cuts SCM storage complexity from O(NHKJ) to O(NHk) with k ≪ KJ, so a tight budget (e.g. CIFAR100 IPC 1) can hold ~11 synthetic images per class instead of one.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on three standard distillation benchmarks — CIFAR10 and CIFAR100 (32×32) and TinyImageNet (64×64) — and six high-resolution 128×128 ImageNet subsets (ImageNette, ImageWoof, ImageFruit, ImageMeow, ImageSquawk, ImageYellow), each with 10 classes, plus CIFAR100-C for corruption robustness.\n\n*Also —* Storage is measured as parameters-per-class under IPC 1/10/50 budgets; the default backbone is a ConvNet with 128 channels and trajectory matching is the default objective, with cross-architecture eval on MLP, ResNet18, and ViT.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nSPEED sets new state-of-the-art on all three standard benchmarks and all six ImageNet subsets. At IPC 1 storage it reaches 40.0% on CIFAR100 (+6.0%) and 26.9% on TinyImageNet (+10.9%) over the prior best, and averages +11.2% across ImageNet subsets versus the previous state-of-the-art at the same budget.\n\n*Also —* Its IPC 1 results rival prior methods' IPC 10 results using only ~10% of their parameters; on ImageSquawk IPC 10 it hits 71.8% (+15.0%). SPEED also holds the best accuracy at every step of continual learning and is far more robust to corruption, nearly doubling ResNet18 accuracy under every corruption type.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nFeature sparsification is nearly free: pruning the SCM to k=48 non-zero elements (307K params) meets the storage budget while keeping ConvNet accuracy at 73.5%, versus 74.0% for the full 15M-parameter model. FReeNet depth R=2, three heads (H=3), and moderate token count/dimension are the best settings.\n\n*Also —* A moderate k improves cross-architecture generalization; too-small k (12, 106K) collapses accuracy to 57.8%, and Figure 4 shows synthetic samples look almost identical before and after sparsification down to 0.3% density.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- CIFAR100 IPC 1: 40.0% test accuracy, +6.0% over prior SOTA\n\n*Also —* - ImageSquawk IPC 10: 71.8% (+15.0%)\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nHow you parameterize the synthetic dataset matters as much as the matching objective: a shared epitomic-token dictionary with sparse per-image codes and a recurrent synthesizer removes spatial redundancy, delivering state-of-the-art distillation at a fraction of the storage, especially on high-resolution data.\n\n*Also —* Sparse parameterization also improves generalization to unseen architectures and robustness to corruption, suggesting sparsity is a general lever for efficient, transferable synthetic data.","poster_thumb":"posters/093_sparse_parameterization_for_epitomic_dataset_dis.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/093_sparse_parameterization_for_epitomic_dataset_dis/093_sparse_parameterization_for_epitomic_dataset_dis/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/093_sparse_parameterization_for_epitomic_dataset_dis/093_sparse_parameterization_for_epitomic_dataset_dis/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/093_sparse_parameterization_for_epitomic_dataset_dis/093_sparse_parameterization_for_epitomic_dataset_dis/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/093_sparse_parameterization_for_epitomic_dataset_dis/093_sparse_parameterization_for_epitomic_dataset_dis/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/093_sparse_parameterization_for_epitomic_dataset_dis/093_sparse_parameterization_for_epitomic_dataset_dis/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/093_sparse_parameterization_for_epitomic_dataset_dis/093_sparse_parameterization_for_epitomic_dataset_dis/reel.html"}},{"id":"094","slug":"094_extending_kernel_pca_through_dualization_sparsit","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Extending Kernel PCA through Dualization: Sparsity, Robustness and Fast Algorithms","authors":"Francesco Tonin¹, Alex Lambert¹, Panagiotis Patrinos¹, Johan A.K. Suykens¹","institutes":"¹ESAT-STADIUS, KU Leuven","venue":"ICML 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05815","code_url":"https://github.com/taralloc/dc-kpca","hook":"The paper derives a general dual formulation of Kernel PCA as a difference of convex functions that (1) enables SVD-free gradient-based solvers, including for infinite-dimensional feature spaces, and (2) unifies robust and sparse variants t…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nClassical Kernel PCA solves an n×n singular value decomposition of the Gram matrix, an O(n³) operation that is prohibitively slow and blocks large-scale use.\n\n*Also —* Adding desirable properties like robustness to outliers or sparsity has long required ad-hoc, per-property reformulations and heuristics, with no unifying framework.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nKernel PCA is naturally a variance-maximization problem under orthonormality constraints, which places it in the well-studied family of difference-of-convex (DC) problems, but prior DC treatments handled only linear PCA or a single component.\n\n*Also —* Infimal convolution is known to design robust and sparse losses cleanly in duality settings, suggesting one dual framework could unify these extensions for the nonlinear, multi-component kernel case.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper derives a general dual formulation of Kernel PCA as a difference of convex functions that (1) enables SVD-free gradient-based solvers, including for infinite-dimensional feature spaces, and (2) unifies robust and sparse variants through Moreau-envelope objectives in a single framework.\n\n*Also —* It provides the dualization theory (nuclear-norm gradient, critical-point characterization), practical L-BFGS and DC algorithms, and closed-form proximal operators for Huber and ε-insensitive losses.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nKernel PCA is cast as inf g(W) − f(ΓW) over a Stiefel manifold, then dualized so the finite-dimensional dual variable H replaces the infinite-dimensional directions; the indicator of the Stiefel manifold yields a nuclear-norm term π(H) = Tr√(H⊤GH) whose gradient is computed via a small s×s SVD.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\inf_{H\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times s}} \\tfrac{1}{2}\\,\\mathrm{Tr}(H^\\top H) - \\pi(H),\\quad \\pi(H) = \\mathrm{Tr}\\sqrt{H^\\top G H}$ ; $\\inf_{H\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times s}} \\tfrac{1}{2}\\lVert H\\rVert_F^2 + \\Psi^\\star(H) - \\pi(H)$ ; $H_\\kappa := \\tfrac{1}{2}\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_F^2 \\,\\square\\, \\kappa\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert$\n\n*Also —* The standard KPCA dual is solved with L-BFGS; robust/sparse variants add a Moreau-envelope term Ψ⋆(H) and are optimized with a difference-of-convex (DCA) algorithm, using closed-form proximal operators for the Huber and ε-insensitive losses. Only s×s SVDs are ever needed, never the n×n one.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEfficiency is measured on Synth 1 (n=7000), Protein (n=14895), RCV1 (n=20242), and CIFAR-10 (n=60000); robustness on a contaminated Iris dataset; sparsity via reconstruction error under ℓ²_ε and ℓ^∞_ε losses.\n\n*Also —* Solvers are compared using a relative dual-cost residual stopping criterion at tolerances δ = 10⁻² and 10⁻⁴; a Laplace kernel is used for the timing study, a Gaussian kernel for the robustness study, and results are averaged over 5 runs.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nAt tolerance δ = 10⁻², the proposed L-BFGS solver is faster than every baseline and at least 3× faster than randomized SVD, reaching a 9.17× speedup on CIFAR-10 (n=60000); full SVD exceeded 30 minutes on the larger datasets.\n\n*Also —* The solver is largely insensitive to the decay rate of G's eigenspectrum, whereas randomized SVD needs sharply more oversamples as the spectrum decays slowly (a common real-world regime).\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nVarying the Huber parameter κ (Figure 3) shows the H²_κ and H¹_κ losses systematically lower MSE on contaminated Iris versus the plain squared loss; varying the number of components s (Figure 2) shows L-BFGS keeps its time advantage over RSVD until very large s, where the per-iteration s×s SVD begins to bite.\n\n*Also —* The eigenspectrum-decay study (Figure 1) isolates why the method scales well: RSVD's required oversamples grow steeply as decay slows, while L-BFGS iterations stay flat.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- **9.17×** speedup over randomized SVD on CIFAR-10 (n=60000) at tolerance δ = 10⁻²\n\n*Also —* Evaluated on datasets up to n = 60000; full SVD exceeded a 30-minute cap on RCV1 and CIFAR-10.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nDualizing Kernel PCA as a difference of convex functions gives one framework that is simultaneously faster (SVD-free, up to 9× over RSVD), robust, and sparse, all controlled by the choice of a Moreau-envelope objective.\n\n*Also —* The dual variable is always finite-dimensional, so the approach handles even infinite-dimensional feature maps that classical SVD-based KPCA cannot.","poster_thumb":"posters/094_extending_kernel_pca_through_dualization_sparsit.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/094_extending_kernel_pca_through_dualization_sparsit/094_extending_kernel_pca_through_dualization_sparsit/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/094_extending_kernel_pca_through_dualization_sparsit/094_extending_kernel_pca_through_dualization_sparsit/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/094_extending_kernel_pca_through_dualization_sparsit/094_extending_kernel_pca_through_dualization_sparsit/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/094_extending_kernel_pca_through_dualization_sparsit/094_extending_kernel_pca_through_dualization_sparsit/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/094_extending_kernel_pca_through_dualization_sparsit/094_extending_kernel_pca_through_dualization_sparsit/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/094_extending_kernel_pca_through_dualization_sparsit/094_extending_kernel_pca_through_dualization_sparsit/reel.html"}},{"id":"095","slug":"095_scaling_spherical_cnns","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Scaling Spherical CNNs","authors":"Carlos Esteves¹, Jean-Jacques Slotine², Ameesh Makadia¹","institutes":"¹Google Research; ²MIT","venue":"ICML 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05420","code_url":"https://github.com/google-research/spherical-cnn","hook":"The paper presents a systematic approach to scale spherical CNNs one order of magnitude larger: a TPU-optimized JAX implementation of spin-weighted spherical harmonic transforms, new general-purpose layers and activations, and application-s…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nSpherical CNNs compute convolutions in the spectral domain, which is far costlier than planar convolution, so applications were confined to small, low-resolution, low-capacity problems.\n\n*Also —* No spherical CNN existed at the scale of common planar architectures like VGG-19, blocking their use on large scientific datasets.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nMolecular property prediction and weather forecasting are naturally spherical and rotation-related, so rotation-equivariant spherical CNNs should excel, but only if they can scale to datasets like QM9 (134K molecules) and high-resolution ERA5 weather grids.\n\n*Also —* These fields were dominated by equivariant graph neural networks and transformers; prior spherical CNNs were limited to tiny benchmarks like QM7 (7,165 molecules).\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper presents a systematic approach to scale spherical CNNs one order of magnitude larger: a TPU-optimized JAX implementation of spin-weighted spherical harmonic transforms, new general-purpose layers and activations, and application-specific input representations for molecules and weather.\n\n*Also —* Naively increasing depth and width is shown insufficient; scaling required redesigning the nonlinearity, normalization, and residual block, each improving both efficiency and accuracy.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nBuilding on spin-weighted spherical CNNs, the authors introduce a phase collapse nonlinearity (which uses the modulus to collapse phase and recover rotation invariance), spectral batch normalization, spectral pooling, and an efficient residual block whose skip connection lives in Fourier space (Figure 2). Fourier transforms are computed as dense matrix multiplications (DFT) rather than FFTs for speed on TPUs.\n\n**Key idea.** $(f * k)(x) = \\int_{g \\in SO(3)} f(g\\nu)\\, k(g^{-1}x)\\, dg$ and the phase collapse activation $x_0 \\leftarrow W_1 x_0 + W_2 |x| + b$\n\n*Also —* The complete rewrite in JAX runs distributed across up to 32 TPUs; molecules are encoded as sets of spherical functions built from physically-based, power-law interactions between atom pairs (Figure 3).\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nQM9 (134,000 molecules, up to 29 atoms, 12 regression targets) for molecular property regression, and WeatherBench / ERA5 reanalysis data for short and medium-range weather forecasting.\n\n*Also —* Weather tasks span WeatherBench (Z500, T850, T2M at 3 and 5 days), global extreme temperature forecasting up to 28 days, and iterative high-resolution forecasting following Keisler (2022).\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nThe scaled spherical CNN reaches state of the art on QM9, outperforming the previously dominant graph neural networks and transformers on 8 of 12 targets in Split 1 and 9 of 12 targets in Split 2.\n\n*Also —* On WeatherBench it outperforms the baseline on all metrics in the 2-predictor setting and even beats models pre-trained on large simulated datasets on several temperature metrics, showing for the first time that spherical CNNs are viable neural weather models.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nTable 1 isolates each contribution on a QM9 model: phase collapse cuts RMSE by 8.0%, spectral batch norm by a further 1.4%, and the efficient residual block by another 2.4%, while the JAX/DFT/symmetry choices deliver large steps-per-second gains.\n\n*Also —* Table 6 confirms the phase collapse activation, spectral pooling, and the new spherical molecule representation each outperform prior alternatives, reaching 15.25 meV MAE on QM9 enthalpy.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- Scales spherical CNNs by one order of magnitude in operations and feature resolution\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nWith the right redesign of core components and a hardware-tuned implementation, spherical CNNs can finally scale to reach state of the art on molecular property prediction and become competitive neural weather models.\n\n*Also —* The released JAX implementation is intended as a platform for further spherical CNN research on real-world scientific problems.","poster_thumb":"posters/095_scaling_spherical_cnns.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/095_scaling_spherical_cnns/095_scaling_spherical_cnns/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/095_scaling_spherical_cnns/095_scaling_spherical_cnns/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/095_scaling_spherical_cnns/095_scaling_spherical_cnns/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/095_scaling_spherical_cnns/095_scaling_spherical_cnns/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/095_scaling_spherical_cnns/095_scaling_spherical_cnns/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/095_scaling_spherical_cnns/095_scaling_spherical_cnns/reel.html"}},{"id":"096","slug":"096_truly_scale_equivariant_deep_nets_with_fourier_l","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Truly Scale-Equivariant Deep Nets with Fourier Layers","authors":"Md Ashiqur Rahman¹, Raymond A. Yeh¹","institutes":"¹Department of Computer Science, Purdue University","venue":"NeurIPS 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.02922","code_url":"https://github.com/ashiq24/Scale_Equivarinat_Fourier_Layer","hook":"They (1) formulate down-scaling in the discrete domain as ideal downsampling with anti-aliasing, (2) propose a family of truly scale-equivariant deep nets built from Fourier layers with a simple frequency-dependency condition, and (3) show…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nExisting scale-equivariant CNNs are not *truly* scale-equivariant in practice: they achieve equivariance through weight-sharing and kernel resizing but incur non-negligible equivariance error.\n\n*Also —* They derive resizing in the continuous domain, so they never account for anti-aliasing that a discrete down-scaling operation demands.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen you down-scale a discrete signal, the Nyquist theorem requires an anti-aliasing filter; ignoring it lets high frequencies alias into low ones, breaking equivariance.\n\n*Also —* Prior continuous-domain formulations have no notion of anti-aliasing, which is precisely the gap that produces their residual error.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThey (1) formulate down-scaling in the discrete domain as ideal downsampling with anti-aliasing, (2) propose a family of truly scale-equivariant deep nets built from Fourier layers with a simple frequency-dependency condition, and (3) show absolute zero end-to-end equivariance error with competitive accuracy on MNIST-scale and STL-10.\n\n*Also —* They design scale-equivariant versions of every module (convolution, non-linearity, pooling), plus a per-scale classifier and a consistency loss tailored to scale-consistent prediction.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nDown-scaling is defined as ideal downsampling D_R = subsampling after an ideal low-pass (anti-aliasing) filter. The core condition (Claim 1) is that a net is scale-equivariant iff each output frequency Y[k] depends only on equal-or-lower input frequencies X[-k:k]. Every module (a spatially-local Fourier convolution, a frequency-aware non-linearity, and a Fourier pooling) is built to satisfy this condition.\n\n**Key idea.** $D_R(x) \\triangleq \\mathrm{Sub}_R(h \\circledast x)$ ; $Y[k] = \\tilde{G}_k\\big(X[-k:k]\\big)\\ \\forall k \\implies g(D_R(x)) = D_R(g(x))$ ; $\\sum_{k \\in R(x)} \\max\\big(L(\\hat{y}[k], y) - L(\\hat{y}[k-1], y),\\, 0\\big)$\n\n*Also —* A per-scale classifier shares an MLP across scales via Fourier zero-padding, and a hinge consistency loss encourages high-resolution predictions to beat their down-scaled versions. All operations run in the Fourier domain, giving O(|A| N log N + K N) per-layer cost.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nEvaluated on MNIST-scale (resolutions 8×8 to 28×28) and STL10-scale (resolutions 48 to 97), the standard benchmarks for scale-equivariant networks, under both ideal and non-ideal (Gibbs-ringing) downsampling.\n\n*Also —* MNIST-scale uses 10k / 2k / 50k train/val/test; STL10-scale uses 7k / 1k / 5k. Data-efficiency is probed at 5k, 2.5k, and 1k training samples.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn MNIST-scale (ideal, all scales) the model reaches 0.9889 accuracy with 0.9716 scale-consistency and 0.00 equivariance error, the best on every metric. On STL10-scale it reaches 0.7332 accuracy versus 0.5844 for the next best (Fourier CNN), a ~15-point gain, again with zero equivariance error.\n\n*Also —* It stays best under non-ideal downsampling (0.9880 accuracy, 0.9760 scale-consistency, 0.05 error) and is the most data-efficient, reaching 0.9606 accuracy at just 1k training samples versus DISCO's 0.9457.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nRemoving the consistency loss lowers both accuracy and scale-consistency at every training-set size (e.g. at 5k samples scale-consistency drops from 0.9296 to 0.9150; at 2.5k from 0.8906 to 0.8633), validating the loss.\n\n*Also —* Frequency-domain non-linearities were also tried but empirically degraded classification, motivating the spatial-domain scale-equivariant non-linearity σ_s used instead.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- **0.00** end-to-end scale-equivariance error (absolute zero, by construction) on MNIST-scale and STL10-scale.\n\n*Also —* - **0.7332** STL10-scale accuracy vs **0.5844** next-best, a **~15-point** gain.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy formulating down-scaling as ideal (anti-aliased) downsampling and enforcing a frequency-dependency rule via Fourier layers, the network becomes *exactly* scale-equivariant, zero error, not merely approximately, while staying accurate and data-efficient.\n\n*Also —* Getting the signal-processing right, treating down-scaling as a discrete anti-aliased operation, is what turns approximate equivariance into provable, absolute equivariance.","poster_thumb":"posters/096_truly_scale_equivariant_deep_nets_with_fourier_l.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/096_truly_scale_equivariant_deep_nets_with_fourier_l/096_truly_scale_equivariant_deep_nets_with_fourier_l/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/096_truly_scale_equivariant_deep_nets_with_fourier_l/096_truly_scale_equivariant_deep_nets_with_fourier_l/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/096_truly_scale_equivariant_deep_nets_with_fourier_l/096_truly_scale_equivariant_deep_nets_with_fourier_l/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/096_truly_scale_equivariant_deep_nets_with_fourier_l/096_truly_scale_equivariant_deep_nets_with_fourier_l/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/096_truly_scale_equivariant_deep_nets_with_fourier_l/096_truly_scale_equivariant_deep_nets_with_fourier_l/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/096_truly_scale_equivariant_deep_nets_with_fourier_l/096_truly_scale_equivariant_deep_nets_with_fourier_l/reel.html"}},{"id":"097","slug":"097_improving_equilibrium_propagation_without_weight","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Improving equilibrium propagation without weight symmetry through Jacobian homeostasis","authors":"Axel Laborieux¹, Friedemann Zenke¹²","institutes":"¹Friedrich Miescher Institute for Biomedical Research, Basel; ²University of Basel","venue":"ICLR 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.02214","code_url":"","hook":"A comprehensive analysis isolating the two bias sources (finite nudge vs. Jacobian asymmetry), an extension of holomorphic EP to asymmetric complex-differentiable systems, a new homeostatic loss reducing Jacobian asymmetry, and a demonstrat…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nEquilibrium propagation (EP) needs weight symmetry and infinitesimal nudges for unbiased gradients, both hard to realize in physical or neuromorphic hardware, so how weight asymmetry causes bias was unknown.\n\n*Also —* In practice asymmetry's contribution to bias is masked by the finite nudge, so the two effects had never been analytically separated.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nPhysical neural substrates (brains, analog hardware) could train networks at far lower energy, but backprop's linear backward pass and transposed weights are biologically implausible, motivating EP alternatives that must survive imperfect, asymmetric connectivity.\n\n*Also —* Generalized (asymmetric) EP had only been shown on MNIST and fails on CIFAR-10, so its poor scaling was an open, unexplained failure mode.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nA comprehensive analysis isolating the two bias sources (finite nudge vs. Jacobian asymmetry), an extension of holomorphic EP to asymmetric complex-differentiable systems, a new homeostatic loss reducing Jacobian asymmetry, and a demonstration that hEP scales to ImageNet 32×32.\n\n*Also —* The homeostatic loss acts on the Jacobian rather than the weights, so it also works for architectures with no reciprocal connections at all.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nGeneralized holomorphic EP computes the exact neuronal error vector with finite nudges via a Cauchy integral over an oscillating teaching signal β(t), removing finite-nudge bias even for asymmetric Jacobians and allowing continuous-time estimation without separate phases. A new homeostatic loss penalizes the skew-symmetric part A of the Jacobian at the free fixed point, improving functional symmetry without imposing weight symmetry.\n\n**Key idea.** $\\frac{du^*}{d\\beta}\\Big|_{\\beta=0}=\\frac{1}{2i\\pi}\\oint_\\gamma \\frac{u^*_\\beta}{\\beta^2}\\,d\\beta=\\frac{1}{T|\\beta|}\\int_0^T u^*_{\\beta(t)}\\,e^{-2i\\pi t/T}\\,dt$ ; $\\mathcal{L}_{homeo}:=\\mathbb{E}_{\\varepsilon\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I)}\\left[\\lVert J_F\\varepsilon\\rVert^2-\\varepsilon^\\top J_F^2\\varepsilon\\right]$ ; $d_\\beta u^*=\\delta-2S^{-1}A\\delta+o(S^{-1}A\\delta)$\n\n*Also —* Bias is shown to scale with A: dβu* = δ − 2S⁻¹Aδ + o(·). The homeostatic loss is estimated with the Hutchinson trace estimator using Gaussian noise samples, and is added to cross-entropy scaled by λ_homeo.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nFashion MNIST (bias-isolation MLP experiments), and CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and ImageNet 32×32 for the recurrent convolutional architecture with asymmetric feedback weights.\n\n*Also —* Predictive coding networks (Appendix D) further confirm the homeostatic loss generalizes beyond reciprocal architectures.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn CIFAR-10 the asymmetric hEP network jumps from 60.4% (no homeostatic loss) to 84.3% Top-1 with the homeostatic loss, only 4.3 points below the symmetric architecture, and for the first time hEP trains on ImageNet 32×32 (31.4% Top-1 / 55.2% Top-5).\n\n*Also —* The homeostatic loss increases the Jacobian symmetry measure and the layer-wise alignment between hEP's and backprop's neuronal error vectors over training, while having no measurable effect when networks are already trained with RBP.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nOn Fashion MNIST the Cauchy-integral estimate removes finite-nudge bias: at large nudge |β|=0.5, classic one-sided EP degrades to 38.4% error while the N=6 estimate reaches 14.3%, matching the true derivative (14.7%).\n\n*Also —* Using N=2 instead of the exact derivative costs an extra 2.9 points on CIFAR-10, isolating the residual finite-nudge contribution; an antisymmetric output-to-first-layer architecture shows the same benefits, confirming the loss is more general than weight alignment.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- CIFAR-10 Top-1: 60.4% → 84.3% with homeostatic loss (asymmetric hEP)\n\n*Also —* All CIFAR results average three seeds; the ImageNet 32×32 result is the first reported for equilibrium propagation on this benchmark.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nEquilibrium propagation does not need symmetric weights: eliminating finite-nudge bias with a Cauchy integral and penalizing Jacobian asymmetry with a homeostatic loss lets asymmetric networks scale to ImageNet-level tasks with only a small gap to the ideal symmetric case.\n\n*Also —* Because functional (Jacobian) symmetry is weaker than weight symmetry, this points to homeostatic processes as a biologically plausible route to training physical neural substrates.","poster_thumb":"posters/097_improving_equilibrium_propagation_without_weight.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/097_improving_equilibrium_propagation_without_weight/097_improving_equilibrium_propagation_without_weight/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/097_improving_equilibrium_propagation_without_weight/097_improving_equilibrium_propagation_without_weight/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/097_improving_equilibrium_propagation_without_weight/097_improving_equilibrium_propagation_without_weight/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/097_improving_equilibrium_propagation_without_weight/097_improving_equilibrium_propagation_without_weight/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/097_improving_equilibrium_propagation_without_weight/097_improving_equilibrium_propagation_without_weight/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/097_improving_equilibrium_propagation_without_weight/097_improving_equilibrium_propagation_without_weight/reel.html"}},{"id":"098","slug":"098_fast_scalable_warm_start_semidefinite_programmin","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Fast, Scalable, Warm-Start Semidefinite Programming with Spectral Bundling and Sketching","authors":"Rico Angell¹, Andrew McCallum¹","institutes":"¹Manning College of Information and Computer Sciences, University of Massachusetts Amherst","venue":"ICML 2024","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.11801","code_url":"https://github.com/rangell/usbs","hook":"USBS is a unified spectral bundle method for general SDPs with both equality and inequality constraints that supports warm-starting, is provably convergent, and, with optional matrix sketching, scales to massive instances while sustaining c…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nSketching-based scalable SDP solvers scale to huge problems but need more iterations as size grows, and their iteration-dependent parameter schedules block effective warm-starting.\n\n*Also —* Standard SDP solvers pay a cubic per-iteration cost from projecting onto the semidefinite cone via full eigendecomposition, so practitioners dismiss SDPs as intractable at scale.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nMany applications with incrementally-arriving data or mixed-integer subproblems solve a sequence of closely related SDPs, so being able to warm-start from the previous solution is essential for speed.\n\n*Also —* Spectral bundle methods have low per-iteration cost and fast empirical convergence, but prior versions handled only equality or only inequality constraints and lacked an efficient standalone implementation for massive SDPs.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nUSBS is a unified spectral bundle method for general SDPs with both equality and inequality constraints that supports warm-starting, is provably convergent, and, with optional matrix sketching, scales to massive instances while sustaining convergence speed.\n\n*Also —* The authors prove non-asymptotic convergence rates, let the user trade per-iteration complexity against convergence speed via the model parameters, and release a standalone pure-JAX implementation that runs on CPU, GPU, and TPU.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nUSBS recasts the dual SDP as an unconstrained penalized objective and minimizes it with a proximal bundle method, building a low-dimensional spectral model from current and past eigenvectors and taking descent or null steps based on the model's predicted decrease.\n\n**Key idea.** $f(y) := \\alpha\\,[\\lambda_{\\max}(C - \\mathcal{A}^* y)]_+ + \\langle b, y\\rangle + \\iota_{\\mathcal{Y}}(y)$ ; proximal step $\\tilde{y}_{t+1} \\leftarrow \\arg\\min_{y}\\ \\hat{f}_t(y) + \\tfrac{\\rho}{2}\\lVert y - y_t\\rVert^2$ ; descent test $\\beta\\,(f(y_t) - \\hat{f}_t(\\tilde{y}_{t+1})) \\le f(y_t) - f(\\tilde{y}_{t+1})$\n\n*Also —* The model spans a subspace parameterized by an accumulated matrix X̄ₜ and k orthonormal vectors Vₜ (kc current eigenvectors plus kp past ones); each step solves a small minimax problem for the candidate iterate, and optional sketching stores only a low-rank sketch of the primal matrix to control memory.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nUSBS is evaluated on three problem families: MaxCut on ten DIMACS10 graphs (n from 16K to 3.7M), the quadratic assignment problem on QAPLIB and TSPLIB instances (n from 136 to 198), and interactive entity resolution with ∃-constraints on three author-coreference datasets (PubMed, QIAN, SCAD-zbMATH).\n\n*Also —* The MaxCut graphs reach 25.9M nonzeros in the Laplacian and over 10¹³ decision variables (333SP); the QAP relaxation has O(n⁴) decision variables, reaching 1.5 billion at n=198; warm starts are built by dropping the last 1% of MaxCut vertices, the final QAP row/column, or the newest ∃-constraint.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nAgainst CGAL with sketching, the previous state-of-the-art scalable solver, USBS reaches an accurate solution on all ten MaxCut instances within 28 hours while CGAL fails on 7 of 10 within 72 hours, and it delivers a 500x speedup on an instance with over 2 billion decision variables.\n\n*Also —* On QAP and entity-resolution tasks USBS consistently reaches lower relative gaps and cumulative solve times, and the performance gap over CGAL widens as problem size grows; USBS also leverages warm starts far more reliably than CGAL.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nWarm-starting yields more than a 100x speedup in convergence over cold-starting for USBS, while CGAL frequently cannot reliably exploit a warm start; larger kc (current eigenvectors) improves convergence whereas kp (past spectral vectors) helps little and can even hurt.\n\n*Also —* The default hyperparameters (r=10, ρ=0.01, β=0.25, kc=10, kp=1) reflect these findings; unlike the original Helmberg–Rendl method that fixes kc=1, USBS benefits from a larger current-eigenvector budget.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\n- **500x** speedup over CGAL on a MaxCut instance with over 2 billion decision variables.\n\n*Also —* - Scales to SDPs with over **10¹³** decision variables (333SP MaxCut, n≈3.7M).\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nUSBS is a unified, provably correct spectral bundle method that makes massive general SDPs practical, sustaining fast convergence at billion-plus-variable scale and reliably exploiting warm starts where prior scalable solvers cannot.\n\n*Also —* With a standalone pure-JAX implementation runnable on CPU/GPU/TPU, it turns SDPs from \"intractable at real-world scale\" into a usable tool for incremental and interactive optimization pipelines.","poster_thumb":"posters/098_fast_scalable_warm_start_semidefinite_programmin.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/098_fast_scalable_warm_start_semidefinite_programmin/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/098_fast_scalable_warm_start_semidefinite_programmin/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/098_fast_scalable_warm_start_semidefinite_programmin/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/098_fast_scalable_warm_start_semidefinite_programmin/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/098_fast_scalable_warm_start_semidefinite_programmin/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/098_fast_scalable_warm_start_semidefinite_programmin/reel.html"}},{"id":"099","slug":"099_do_you_remember_overcoming_catastrophic_forgetti","source":"p2p","source_label":"Paper2Poster benchmark","source_url":"https://paper2poster.github.io/","title":"Do You Remember? Overcoming Catastrophic Forgetting for Fake Audio Detection","authors":"Xiaohui Zhang¹², Jiangyan Yi¹, Jianhua Tao³, Chenglong Wang¹⁴, Chuyuan Zhang¹","institutes":"¹Institute of Automation, Chinese Academy of Sciences; ²Beijing Jiaotong University; ³Tsinghua University; ⁴University of Science and Technology of China","venue":"ICML 2023","paper_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.03300","code_url":"https://github.com/Cecile-hi/Regularized-Adaptive-Weight-Modification","hook":"The paper proposes RAWM, a replay-free continual learning method with two parts: adaptive weight modification that steers the update direction by the genuine-versus-fake ratio, and a regularization term that forces the model to remember the…","about_md":"## Problem\n\nFake audio detectors reach strong accuracy on their training dataset but degrade sharply on audio from a different dataset; naively fine-tuning on new data causes catastrophic forgetting of the old.\n\n*Also —* Prior cross-dataset fixes (ensemble learning, domain adaptation) need samples from the old dataset, which are often unavailable, for example when a released pre-trained model cannot be re-fine-tuned on the vendor's private data.\n\n\n\n## Motivation\n\nOrthogonal Weight Modification (OWM) overcomes forgetting without replay but ignores that genuine audio often shares a similar feature distribution across datasets, wasting an exploitable regularity.\n\n*Also —* Conversely, a few datasets contain genuine audio recorded under very different acoustic conditions, so treating all classes identically skews the feature distribution and hurts retention.\n\n\n\n## Contribution\n\nThe paper proposes RAWM, a replay-free continual learning method with two parts: adaptive weight modification that steers the update direction by the genuine-versus-fake ratio, and a regularization term that forces the model to remember the old inference distribution.\n\n*Also —* The method generalizes beyond fake audio detection to speech emotion recognition and image recognition (CLEAR benchmark), and is validated across four fake audio datasets averaged over seven runs.\n\n\n\n## Method\n\nRAWM builds on OWM's projector P (orthogonal to the subspace of previous inputs) and adds a second projector Q scaled by the genuine-to-fake ratio β; the final modification direction R combines normalized P and Q so updates adapt per batch while a distillation-style regularization preserves the old distribution.\n\n**Key idea.** $Q = \\beta\\,[\\,I - P(P^{\\top}P)^{-1}P\\,]$, $R = P_{\\text{norm}} + m\\,Q_{\\text{norm}}$, $\\beta = \\dfrac{N_g + 1}{N_f + 1}$, $L_{\\text{reg}}(\\hat{y}_o, \\hat{y}_n) = -\\,\\hat{y}_o \\cdot \\log \\hat{y}_n$\n\n*Also —* Weights update as W ← W + R·G for new tasks, where G is the scaled back-prop gradient; the regularization replicates the pre-trained model into a frozen teacher and a fine-tuned student, matching softened softmax outputs via a modified cross-entropy loss weighted by coefficient η.\n\n\n\n## Dataset / Benchmark\n\nFour fake audio datasets are used in sequence, trained on ASVspoof2019LA (S) then fine-tuned on ASVspoof2015 (T1), VCC2020 (T2), and In-the-Wild (T3); performance is measured by Equal Error Rate (EER, %).\n\n*Also —* Generalization is tested on speech emotion recognition (MSP-Podcast → IEMOCAP, 4 emotions, accuracy) and image recognition (CLEAR-10 benchmark, 10 experiences, accuracy). Features come from a pre-trained Wav2vec 2.0 model for audio.\n\n\n\n## Key Result\n\nOn sequence training between two datasets, RAWM's forgetting is one-tenth that of naive fine-tuning and its EER on the new dataset is half that of fine-tuning, beating EWC, LwF, OWM, and DFWF on both old and new sets.\n\n*Also —* For four-dataset sequence training at the best setting η = 0.50, RAWM reaches EER of 1.508 (S), 0.641 (T1), 3.850 (T2), 3.163 (T3), versus a baseline that degrades to 24.5 / 46.5 / 91.5 on T1/T2/T3.\n\n\n\n## Ablation Study\n\nRemoving regularization (–REG) or removing adaptive weight modification (–AWM) shows each part helps: adaptive modification dominates when feature distributions are similar or languages differ, while regularization dominates when acoustic conditions differ sharply.\n\n*Also —* In four-dataset sequence training, dropping adaptive modification (–REG to –AWM) degrades EER more than dropping regularization (RAWM to –REG), indicating adaptive weight modification is the larger contributor overall.\n\n\n\n## Headline Numbers\n\nRAWM forgetting ≈ one-tenth of fine-tuning; new-dataset EER ≈ half of fine-tuning; best η = 0.50.\n\n*Also —* Few-sample (100 samples) two-dataset EER: RAWM 0.923 (S) / 0.312 (T1) vs Fine-tune 7.951 / 0.617 and DFWF 1.975 / 0.733. Speech emotion recognition accuracy: RAWM 41.995% (MSP-Podcast) / 54.229% (IEMOCAP), best among continual learning methods.\n\n\n\n## Takeaway\n\nBy adapting the weight-update direction to the genuine-versus-fake ratio and regularizing against the old distribution, RAWM overcomes catastrophic forgetting in fake audio detection without replaying any past data, and the idea transfers to other classification tasks.\n\n*Also —* The recipe is practical for released pre-trained detectors that cannot access their original training data.","poster_thumb":"posters/099_do_you_remember_overcoming_catastrophic_forgetti.jpg","w":900,"h":540,"has":{"poster_pdf":true,"pptx":true,"video":true,"video_nosub":true,"blog_zh":true,"blog_en":true,"reel":true},"rel":{"poster_pdf":"paper2poster/runs/099_do_you_remember_overcoming_catastrophic_forgetti/099_do_you_remember_overcoming_catastrophic_forgetti/poster.pdf","video":"paper2poster/runs/099_do_you_remember_overcoming_catastrophic_forgetti/099_do_you_remember_overcoming_catastrophic_forgetti/video.mp4","video_nosub":"paper2poster/runs/099_do_you_remember_overcoming_catastrophic_forgetti/099_do_you_remember_overcoming_catastrophic_forgetti/video_no_subtitles.mp4","blog_zh":"paper2poster/runs/099_do_you_remember_overcoming_catastrophic_forgetti/099_do_you_remember_overcoming_catastrophic_forgetti/blog_zh.docx","blog_en":"paper2poster/runs/099_do_you_remember_overcoming_catastrophic_forgetti/099_do_you_remember_overcoming_catastrophic_forgetti/blog_en.docx","reel":"paper2poster/runs/099_do_you_remember_overcoming_catastrophic_forgetti/099_do_you_remember_overcoming_catastrophic_forgetti/reel.html"}}];
